(301, 1, '2019-10-31 14:10:06', '2019-10-31 14:10:06', '<!-- wp:paragraph {\"backgroundColor\":\"very-light-gray\"} -->\n<p class=\"has-background has-very-light-gray-background-color\">By Chris Biose</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>First Published in the Daily Times, Wednesday, January 2, 1974 Page 27</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The decree on Nigerian Enterprises Promotion was promulgated in 1972 to bring about increased indigenous control of the economy.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The success of the indigenization scheme cannot\nhowever, be measured simply by paper transfer of ownership, or sales of shares.\nIt must bring about effective control by Nigerians and ultimately, improved\nstandards of living for the people.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The question then arises as to how far the success\nof the scheme can be reasonably ensured. It is gratifying that the Nigerian\nBank for Commerce and Industry has been set up to provide necessary finance. In\naddition, a good number of much needed qualified personnel are produced yearly\nby our universities. </p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Nevertheless, Nigeria’s social problems are not\nonly educational and economic; they also pertain to moral as well.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>In addition to what can be casually observed,\nempirical research done on the business-habits of Nigerians had shown very\ndiscouraging results. The ‘squandermania-complex’ of the Nigerian ‘elite’ is\never on the increase. There is minimal enterprising spirit. There is an\noverwhelming element of distrust among Nigerians because they are still\nparochial in their thinking. So there is very little pooling of resources.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Also, in the country today, employment is based on\nkinship, Nigerians are yet to learn to employ the most capable hands in place\nof nearest relatives.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>However, it should be remembered that individuals\noperate within the context of the social environment.&nbsp; Since the individual and for that matter, an\norganization is a system of roles, the environment tends to impinge on the\nsystem in terms of expectations, defined values, and norms of behaviour.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The government should therefore take steps to\nrelate important policy decisions to this social fact. That is to say, economic\nmeasures must be related to social values, norms, and established behavior\npatterns of the people.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>It is difficult to change only a given aspect of\nsociety while holding others constant. Such a change is likely to be resisted\nor neutralised by other inter-related organs of society. The approach must be\ntotal.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Great projects have been known to fail because of\nsome errors in planning, some important though not too obvious factors ignored.\nThe ideas, beliefs, and values of a people must be congruent with the social\ninstitutions which they establish.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>And of course people are genuinely convinced of the\nuprightness of their cultural upbringing. They are used to thinking and acting\nin a particular manner. They cannot therefore easily rise above their thinking\nhabits and examine themselves in an unbiased way.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Nevertheless these questions relating to the\nbusiness habits of the average Nigerian, deriving from certain social forces\nneed not be disquieting. The government can seek the service of our social\nscientists to attack these problems of our age. Every citizen should be\ninvolved in the indigenization and should share in its profits.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>CHRISTOPHER BIOSE, University of Lagos.</strong> </p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->', 'Indigenization must improve living standards', '', 'publish', 'open', 'closed', '', 'indigenization-must-improve-living-standards', '', '', '2019-10-31 15:36:04', '2019-10-31 15:36:04', '', 0, 'https://boldtimes.com.ng/?p=301', 0, 'post', '', 0),
(302, 1, '2019-10-31 14:09:25', '2019-10-31 14:09:25', 'Indigenization in Nigeria', 'Indigenization', '', 'inherit', 'open', 'closed', '', 'indigenization', '', '', '2019-10-31 14:09:44', '2019-10-31 14:09:44', '', 301, 'http://boldtimes.com.ng-content/uploads/2019/10/Indigenization.jpg', 0, 'attachment', 'image/jpeg', 0),
(305, 1, '2019-10-31 14:23:50', '2019-10-31 14:23:50', '<!-- wp:paragraph {\"textColor\":\"vivid-red\",\"backgroundColor\":\"very-light-gray\"} -->\n<p class=\"has-text-color has-background has-vivid-red-color has-very-light-gray-background-color\">By Chris Biose</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>First Published in the Nigerian Tribune, Tuesday, 9 June, 1987  Page 6 </p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph {\"backgroundColor\":\"very-light-gray\"} -->\n<p class=\"has-background has-very-light-gray-background-color\"><em>To <strong>CHRIS BIOSE</strong>, </em><em>a\nfree-lance writer, implementing late Chief Awolowo’s four cardinal programmes,\nnationwide, is the highest honour to the memory of one who did so much to\nimprove the life of his compatriots</em><em>.</em></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>As\nif to atone for their mistake in denying him the chance to occupy the highest\npolitical office in the land as he well deserved, to enable him transform the\ncountry for the benefit of the majority, and in appreciation of the invaluable\nservices he rendered with the limited opportunity he had while yet with us,\nNigerians are resolved to bestow adequate individual, group and national\nhonours on the late leader. Hundreds of thousands of Nigerians have thus\noffered useful suggestions as to how best to honour our departed leader.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The\nFederal Government responded positively and has participated fully in the funeral\narrangements spear-headed by some of the deceased’s lieutenants and the LOOBO\nstates. It also gladdens the heart that the Federal Government has named one of\nthe nation’s institutions of higher learning – UNIFE – after the great leader.\nThese are, indeed, heartwarming and spontaneous expressions of the highest\nregard and the late leader’s family and disciples must feel most grateful.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Seen,\nhowever, from the point of view of the under privilege classes of the country,\none can express the view that these agreeably laudable actions so far taken are\nlargely cosmetic and symbolic insofar as they do not pertain to the root causes\nand key issues which ranked high in the thinking of this political giant. As\nthe head of the state himself correctly pointed out, not very long ago, Chief\nAwolowo had been the main issue in Nigerian politics for the past 35 years. The\ntrue test must be: if Chief Awolowo were to reappear and request to make a\nwish, would be start talking of a funeral and names of schools and stadia? I\ntrust not. Attractive as these might be, there were certain things which Awo\nconsistently advocated in his life time, and for which people now openly\nexpress their admiration.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>In\nthis connection, it is most interesting to find that a good number of highly\nplaced government functionaries at both federal and state levels have publicly\nacknowledged the wisdom of the four cardinal action programmes based on the\nlate leader’s socialist political strategy for the country’s development and\nthis, I submit, is where our major interest must lie. There seems to be a\npervasive rediscovery of the rectitude of the socialist policies for which he\nalways fought.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>If\nthis is truly so, and if one is not to be tempted to agree with those who see\nthe unrestrained cutburst of expressions of admiration as hypocritical, then\none should be encouraged to humbly suggest that the best way to honour Awo is\nfor the federal authorities to act now to see that some core principles of the\nlate leader’s socialist programmes are translated into concrete action. It is\nmy humble view that the best way to honour a man is to do his wish. Let the\nauthorities implement at least one Awo programme and name it as such. </p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:image {\"id\":306,\"align\":\"center\"} -->\n<div class=\"wp-block-image\"><figure class=\"aligncenter\"><img src=\"https://boldtimes.net.ng-content/uploads/2019/10/Awolowo.jpg\" alt=\"\" class=\"wp-image-306\"/><figcaption><strong><em>Chief Obafemi Awolowo</em></strong></figcaption></figure></div>\n<!-- /wp:image -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>To\ngive honour where it is due, one must acknowledge with due appreciation the\nintegrated rural development programme of the federal administration. There is\nat present a noticeable effort to improve access to the rural areas by a\n“massive programme of rural road construction and rehabilitation.” This is\ncommendable but roads alone do not a civilization make.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Those\nwho knew the late leader would confirm that he was a pragmatist who spoke\nmainly in action. In this respect, anyone of two under mentioned Awo programmes\ncould be implemented as a specific mark of honour for the late leader. </p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>1.\nWith immediate effort, and as a minimum measure, the government should declare\na free and compulsory secular education at primary and secondary levels\nthroughout the country followed by a year of compulsory national services which\nwill include military training for all Nigerian youths before going into the\nlabour market or for tertiary education. </p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>It\nshould be borne in mind that this educational policy is not an end in itself.\nIt is an essential instrument for promoting national political consciousness\nand eventual economic and technological take-off of the country. This could be\nspecifically christened - The Awo Programme.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>2.\nOn a more holistic note, the administration could take the bull by the horn and\ndeclare Nigeria a socialist state and put a halt to all policies which increase\nand perpetuate inequalities; while instituting and vigorously pursuing policies\nwhich promote egalitarianism.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>In\nthe words of Papa himself, ‘it is abundantly clear that the evils of the capitalist\nsystem are naked self-interest, greed, and the gross and irremediable\ninjustices arising from the forces of supply and demand and the margin. Some of\nthese evils are inherent in, and inseparable from, the capitalist system and\nsince they tend, inexorably, to make the weak progressively weaker relatively,\nand the underdeveloped country more systematically enslaved as an economic\nentity, every underdeveloped country will be wise resolutely to avoid the\ncapitalist system. </p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>“The\nsocialist system is preferable to the capitalist system, because it is devoid\nof the evils on the later, and is positively superior to it. This then, in my\nconsidered judgment is the path to economic freedom in underdeveloped\ncountries.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>“But\nit must be emphasized that there is an imperative and indispensable need for\nthorough and detailed planning, and stern discipline of body and mind to make\nthe socialist system, and to ensure under it, effective and efficient\nmobilization, co-ordination, deployment and development of all the natural and\nhuman resources.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>“..\nmy own considered verdict is that the path to economic freedom in developing\ncountries is socialism.”</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>If\nany of these policy decisions is implemented with immediate effect, as a\nspecific mark of honour for the revered elder statesman, then Awo,s name would\nbe specially associated with the take-off of the Third Republic. If it is not\ndone, the struggle will start afresh.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>It\nhas been said: “if you love me, keep my commandments.” Those who love </p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Awo\nshould imitate his stern discipline, egalitarian programmes and work ethic.\nThis will be the best way to honour the man Awo. </p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->', 'Awo: The ultimate honour', '', 'publish', 'open', 'closed', '', 'awo-the-ultimate-honour', '', '', '2019-10-31 15:35:50', '2019-10-31 15:35:50', '', 0, 'https://boldtimes.com.ng/?p=305', 0, 'post', '', 0),
