(314, 1, '2019-10-31 14:56:32', '2019-10-31 14:56:32', '<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>\n\nBy Chris Biose\n\n</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>First Published in The Guardian, December 31, 1992, Page 13 </p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>There has been a certain amount of confusion\nregarding the duties of the National Assembly recently inaugurated by the Head\nof State. This arises mainly from the provisions of Degree 53 which purports to\nforbid the Assembly members from discussing the most pressing national issues\nsuch as formation of political parties, criteria of revenue allocation, S.A.P.,\nquota system, secular status of the state, census, return to true Federalism\nand corruption which has become a way of life in high places. Rather, the\nAssemblymen are limited to discuss sterile matters pertaining to archives,\nantiquities, maps, cinemas and other relatively irrelevant topics.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>It is my respectful submission that any attempt to\nlimit the powers of the National Assembly by means other than those enshrined\nin the Nigerian constitution itself, is not only inimical to the democratisation\nand transition process, but ipso facto unconstitutional and therefore invalid <em>ab initio</em>.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>I further submit that it is time for Nigerians to\nhave a say, indeed to take the initiative in the democratization process,\nthrough their elected representatives. Why should we continue to blame general\nBabangida for doing this or doing that when the people have been too timid to\ndo something for themselves? We have not expressed our disapproval or anger in\nany serious manner (repressive and inhuman decrees notwithstanding). Rather, we\nwatch with amazement as our duly elected Governors, Council Chairmen,\naccompanied by certain traditional rulers, contrary to the wishes of the\npeople, make unholy pilgrimages to Abuja just to praise the president for every\nact, however distasteful to the public. What greater evidence of a leadership\nthat loses touch with its mentors too soon. And yet, the people are handicapped\nsince they are not organised to say NO SIR!</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>We pray the National Assembly members not to join the\nGovernors, Council Chairmen, and traditional rulers as chorus boys of the\ndexterious General. They must not give the adroit General the impression that\nhe can dictate the tune all the time. They should do precisely what they were\nelected to do. They should have the courage to be our true representatives and\nsay: “we the people say as follows… and that’s just how it is going to be”.\nAnyone opposing them, except in accordance with the constitution is an enemy of\nthe people since such a person would be subverting the wishes of the\nelectorate.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The pitiable plight of our country is largely due\nto the irresponsible attitude of those we place in positions of authority. The\nconfusion of the Nigerian elite arises from the fact that they are largely\nselfish, cantankerous and devoid of positive ideas. It would be tragic to\ncontemplate a National Assembly that is bereft of constructive ideas at this\nmoment in our history. One would not blame the Assembly members if they are products\nof a command mentality. But ithey must learn to grow if they are to serve any\nuseful purpose in the present Nigerian situation. Assemblymen can take an\ninitiative if only it is abortive.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The destiny of Nigeria in the next few months will\nbe shaped not only by what the Assemblymen choose to do, but more importantly\nby what they fail to do. What Nigerians need today is not a set of timid and\ndocile senators and Assemblymen who only wait to be commanded as if they are\npart of the Army or who opt to be “settled” instead of taking the unwilling political\nbull by the horn. If they choose the path of acquisence, then the fault will\nnot be that of General Babangida who is only doing his own thing in his own\nway. We cannot agree less with Alexis De Tocqueville when he observed: “To\ncommit violent and unjust acts, it is not enough for a government to have the\nwill, or even the power; the habits, ideas and passions of the time to their\ncommittal” We urge the Assemblymen not to lend themselves to further\ndehumanisation and pauperisation of the Nigerian masses.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Whatever views we may harbour regarding the unusual\nmanner in which the National Assembly was convened, it is incontestable that\nthere is no other body in Nigeria today that can claim superior legitimacy than\nthe National Assembly. By virtue of their legislative powers, Assemblymen can\nproceed to regularize any anomaly attendant upon their present position. They\nneed to co-operate with the existing undemocratic Executive Arm of Government\nbut they do not need to be subservient to it. They do not need to take\npermission from anyone to do their job as they deem fit. That permission is in\nthe constitution and above all, it is inherent in the will of the people which\nis the supreme law. We do not want a litany of excuses or stories of hands\nbeing tied. The real unforgivable sin is not to try.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>If the National Assembly fails the nation, without\neven trying, then the excuse that General Babangida did not allow them will not\nbe accepted by the present generation of </p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Niger ians or by posterity.National Assemblymen must have some conception of what they are\nentitled to do for Nigeria. They should go ahead and do just that. Nigerians expect\nthem to discuss and legislate on all issues assigned to them under the Nigerian\nconstitution. If at this point in time they still do not know what to do and\nare merely waiting to be instructed, then they should disband at once and go\nhome so we all know where we stand.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Just as it is with individuals, nations grow and\nmature through crisis and incentive. We need a vibrant National Assembly that\ndares to be itself. Assemblymen should go ahead and discuss crucial issues and\nmake laws. They should appoint committees to monitor and check the various\nMinistries for accountability. It is their duty to check the excesses of the\nExecutive in accordance with the doctrine of checks and balances and control of\npublic funds in accordance with Section E, Para 78-87 of the Constitution.\nPresidential veto can be overridden by the power vested in the National\nAssembly to pass a bill by two-thirds majority. Even if they are harassed by\nthe military as is happening in Togo, they must not be cowed.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>If in the process of performing their legitimate\nfunctions, the National Assembly provokes a constitutional crisis, we may find\nthat they would be doing Nigeria a great historical good. That would create an\nopportunity for the Supreme Court to play a crucial role and decide where\nlegitimacy resides, whether in an elected National Assembly or in an unelected\nExecutive. In the final analysis, there is the tribunal of the people which\ncomes last and in a decisive manner to clear any doubts about such issues.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Democratic movements should expect strong\nopposition from entrenched beneficiaries of the present decadent system and\nother vested interest. As the most visible and nationally acceptable democratic\ninstitution (the political parties having been effectively neutralized)\nAssemblymen need to organize effectively, in terms of logistics, planning and\nexecution of their strategy.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>In the first week of sitting, I expect the National\nAssembly to repeal all Decrees that are inconsistent with their own status and\nexistence, employing the power of two-thirds majority where necessary. They\nshould immediately repeal all obnoxious, anti-democratic and anti-human rights\ndecrees they can find. They should repeal as well all provisions ousting the\njurisdiction of the court particularly those contained in Decrees 52 and 53.\nThey should give room for freedom of expression, association and the growth of\ndemocratic ideals.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>In the discharge of their duties, National Assembly\nmembers must act in concert, as a coherent body. They must discuss issues, and\nagree or disagree but never make them a laughing stock. Let the fate of the\npolitical parties and the presidential aspirants are an object lesson for them.\nIn this matter, all Assembly of both parties shares the same political destiny.\nThis should be jealously guarded.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Meanwhile, the people are waiting. National\nAssemblymen members should never permit themselves to be diverted in their\nassignment by petty party differences, religious fanaticism, tribal or class\nbiases or lack of clear vision and will-power. They must not fail in this\nhistoric mission. We expect them to move our dear country Nigeria ahead into\nthe 21<sup>st</sup> century, as a united, strong, democratic, proud and\neconomically emergent African nation. We are entitled to nothing less.&nbsp; </p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->', 'FOR A VIBRANT NATIONAL ASSEMBLY', '', 'publish', 'open', 'closed', '', 'for-a-vibrant-national-assembly', '', '', '2019-10-31 15:33:41', '2019-10-31 15:33:41', '', 0, 'https://boldtimes.com.ng/?p=314', 0, 'post', '', 0),
(315, 1, '2019-10-31 14:56:13', '2019-10-31 14:56:13', '', 'NASS-22', '', 'inherit', 'open', 'closed', '', 'nass-22', '', '', '2019-10-31 14:56:13', '2019-10-31 14:56:13', '', 314, 'http://boldtimes.com.ng-content/uploads/2019/10/NASS-22.jpg', 0, 'attachment', 'image/jpeg', 0),
(317, 1, '2019-10-31 15:00:56', '2019-10-31 15:00:56', '<!-- wp:paragraph {\"backgroundColor\":\"very-light-gray\"} -->\n<p class=\"has-background has-very-light-gray-background-color\">  By Chris O. O. Biose  </p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>First Published in the Sunday Tribune, January, 1993 Page 11, Another view  </p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The contribution of Mr. Ade Ade-Adekoye entitled\n“Triumph of Machiavellism?” (Sunday Tribune, 10/1/93), is a one-sided\npresentation of the political situation of the country. It is heavily bias military.\nThis undisguised attempt to to cast the political class without exception in\nthe toga of political devils while presenting the military class as political\nsaints should not go uncorrected.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Reference to Machiavelli, Aristotle, Agar, Bacon,\nAddison, and Rousseau gives the impression that the writer is conversant with\nthe history of political ideas. The educated elite have a moral obligation to\nbe objective and impartial in their assessment of critical national issues. To\ndo otherwise would be dishonest and a negation of any claim to intellectual (if\nnot political and economic), leadership.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The purpose of this rejoinder is not to refute the\nills of most Nigerian politicians. It is to reject the misleading conclusions\narising from unfair comparison. The image of the military as saviours must be\ntotally rejected in the Nigerian experience. If there must be comparative\nanalysis, it is necessary to place the facts side by side, not in a lop-sided\nmanner that ignores the tenets of fair comparison.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>“Events of the past four months,” according to\nAde-Adekoya “show that the politicians want the presidency come rain or sun,\nshine, snow or frost.” This must be viewed from the vantage point that the\nmilitary have held power by force for 24 years out of the 32 years of Nigerian\nindependence. Military obsession with power has several times taken such bloody\ndimensions that that hundreds of soldiers have lost their lives planning and\nexecuting <em>coups-de-tat</em> just to gain\npower, contrary to the letter and spirit of the law and the constitution of the\nstate.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Since 1964, both the military and political leaders\nhave been engaged in the struggle for political power. The striking difference\nis that while political leaders employ the use of money and persuasion to\ninduce voters, military leaders rely on the barrel of the gun and absolute\ncontrol and monopoly of the use of force, It is no wonder then that the\nsoldiers have the upper hand in this struggle.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Things have come to a stage where the military\nclass dictates the tune entirely and the disadvantaged political classes have\nfound no answer to the power of the gun. The initial corrective justification\nof military interventionism has long since been debunked as self serving.\nFollowing this development, the military power structure has matured from\nwholesale reliance on force to sophisticated weapons of propaganda.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>What is really bothersome is that there are still\nsome Nigerian civilian elite who have been completely taken in by the massive\npropaganda against politicians aimed at justifying perpetuation of military\nrule. The logic of this propaganda is that since politicians have not learnt to\nplay politics as required by the military interventionists, the only\nalternative is for the military to remain in power. This is a classic example\nof a falsehood that is repeated so often that even the victim of the lie\naccepts it as the whole truth. The trap here is the tacit assumption that the\nmilitary has any business taking over power in the first place. It must be made\nclear that military takeover of government is the fundamental indiscipline. It\nis the original political sin. </p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Mr. Ade-Adekoya accused the politicians of “dishing\nout uncertainties, lies and fear.” What greater uncertainties have we\nexperienced in our national life than the interminable transition programme of\nthe past seven years. Strikes have assumed epidemic proportions. Doctors,\nnurses, university teachers, airways men, railway men, primary and secondary\nschool teachers, all were at one time or the other forced to down tools to draw\nattention to their unbearable suffering. Even lawyer had to abandon the courts\nin Lagos and other states to protest executive disobedience of judicial\ndecisions.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The economic indiscipline of the Shargari\nadministration compares well with what the military has done to our economy\nbefore and after Shargari. Renowned economist, Dr. Pius Okigbo recently\nprovided figures which illuminate the genesis of our economic profligacy.\nBetween 1974 and 1979, under various corrective regimes, our external debt rose\nby 325% (from S1.6 billion to S6.8 billion). Shargari “improved” on this record\nby yet tripling the debt from S6.8 billion to S18.5 billion in only 4 years.\nFrom January, 1984 and 1991, the debt stock had risen to S33.4 billion. Place\nthis side by side with outflow of resources from the country running at over\nN60.5 billion per annum since 1986 and the value of the naira has fallen by\nalmost 100% since 1984. Given this scenario, wealthy Nigerians keep their\nwealth abroad. Chief Arthur Nzeribe showed some years ago that if wealthy\nNigerians can be persuade to repatriate their wealth in foreign lands, our\neconomic problem would be reduced to a manageable level. But this cannot take\nplace because of political insecurity at home. Mr. Ade-Adekoya justifies the\ncontinued unwelcome military stay in power on the grounds that politicians have\nnot learnt their lessons. “The political class fails to make the best use of\nthe opportunity given it,” he avers. The question is who is teaching what, to\nwho? </p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>“How can a professional group trained in the\nmanagement of violence, whose best known professional ethic can be summarized\nas “command, obey, force”, teach people to be democratic? Anyone educated in\nthe simplest rudiments of democracy will realise that this is an absolute\nimpossibility. No one can give what he himself does not have.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Think of an unelected Executive teaching democracy\nto politicians in one breath and in another breath banning elected members of\nthe National Assembly from discussing crucial national issues, telling them\nwhat topics to discuss and legislate upon and what they must not discuss. What\na way to teach democracy! Add to this the castration of the judiciary through a\nlong list of obnoxious decrees which oust the jurisdiction of the courts and\nprevent citizens from obtaining redress when they are injured by the state. </p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>We must agree in full with Albert Schweitzer when\nhe said that “example is not the main thing in influencing others, it is the\nonly thing.” Honestly now, where are we to get any example worthy of emulation\nin our political life today? </p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The truth is that far from being saviours,&nbsp; &nbsp;the\nmilitary has become, in the words of Major General David Jemibewon, one of the\nnation’s major problems. And I venture to add, far greater problem than the\npoliticians.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Mr. Ade-Adekoya is sure as he puts it that “posterity\nwill not forgive this generation of politicians”. Let him rest assured that the\npresent generation of Nigerians is already unwilling to forgive the military\npoliticians for drawing the hand of the democratic clock back in Nigeria and\nthe African continent.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; </p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->', 'Triumph of Machiavellism? - Another view', '', 'publish', 'open', 'closed', '', 'triumph-of-machiavellism', '', '', '2019-10-31 15:32:33', '2019-10-31 15:32:33', '', 0, 'https://boldtimes.com.ng/?p=317', 0, 'post', '', 0),
