(341, 1, '2019-11-09 13:49:38', '2019-11-09 13:49:38', '<!-- wp:paragraph {\"backgroundColor\":\"very-light-gray\"} -->\n<p class=\"has-very-light-gray-background-color has-background\">REVIEW BY<br>\nDR. EZOEM, NWANZE IGNATIUS<br>\nPROVOST EMERITUS<br>\nFCE (T) ASABA<br>\nDELTA STATE</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph {\"align\":\"justify\",\"backgroundColor\":\"very-light-gray\"} -->\n<p class=\"has-text-align-justify has-very-light-gray-background-color has-background\">Book Title:               HISTORY AND TRADITION S OF<br>                               IDUMUJE CLAN<br> Author:                  Chris O. O. Biose<br> Publishers:            BOLD Publishers Ltd, 8 Fadipe Street,<br>                                 Ijaye- Ogba, Ikeja, Lagos State.<br> Year Published:     September, 2019.<br> Pages:                    503<br>ISBN:                     13978-978-49936-8-5 <br>EAN:                      9789784993685<br>COVER PRICE:     N10,000.00</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>Introduction</strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>If one does not know where he is coming from, it will be difficult for him to know exactly where he is going to because he definitely does not belong to where he is. James Kegyir Aggrey, Ghanaian renowned scholar once said “I am proud of my colour, whoever is not proud of his colour is not fit to live. In the whole wide world, Africa my Africa comes first”. The book we are about to have a glimpse of, is a veritable historical discourse that showcased the obstinate reality of the origins and cultural evolution of the people of Idumuje Clan in Aniocha North Local Government Area of Delta State.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>The Book</strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The compendium with 503 pages has three\nparts that were clustered into 50 chapters. In Chapter One, the author gave a\nmethodological overview of carrying out his study and data collection. In the\nsecond chapter, we are enlightened with the cradle, the geographical location\nand historical origin of Enuani people, of which Idumuje Clan is a part.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>In this chapter, we are meant to know\nthat Ikeke was the progenitor while Ikeke and Isagbeme formed Imeogbe Village\nas early as the 13<sup>th</sup> century. This chapter also showed how other\nvillages and cultural practices evolved.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>In Chapters Three and Four, the book\nconcentrated on the cultural set up and practice of Imeogbe-Ihunmor, Idumude-Unor\nand Imeogbe-Isagbeme village. The history of how Isagbeme came to meet Ikeke\nand they started what is today popularly known as Idumuje-Ikeke was well\nestablished. Their sojourn from <em>Iduu,</em>\ntheir cultural practices and Apotheosis were also well covered.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>From Chapter Five, the book opened with\na discourse into the spiritual foundation of Idumuje civilization. Here we see\nhow Ikeke arrived his settlement with two deities - the Ohai (male) and Nnemonicha\n(female). Shrines were erected where the god and goddess were kept and worshipped.\nThese and others latter became sacred places in the land. Sacred places and\ntrees were later set up by these two founders. The deities had their separate\npriests.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>In\nChapter Six, the emergence of Okwunye Village was showcased. Okwunye arrived in\nthe 14<sup>th</sup> century. Although it is indisputable that Imeogbe-Ihunmor\nis the cradle of Idumuje, the arrival of Okwunye in the 14<sup>th</sup> century\nand his interaction with Ba-Iyi descendant of Ikeke, eventually proved to be\nthe most momentous event in the historiography of Idumuje people. Ba-Iyi and\nOkwunye were the founders of Idumuje as a distinct political entity. Ba-Iyi had\nspiritual powers while Okwunye held sway at the political administration of the\nevolving kingdom.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>In Chapter Seven, we see the origins of\nmonarchical Institution in Idumuje Clan rulership by a king. Idumuje land was a\nstate-less society for over a hundred years. There was no centralized political\nauthority but only a few homesteads. The union of Ba-Iyi and Okwunye signaled\nthe commencement of monarchical authority in Idumuje that then became a\nkingdom. As core founders of Idumuje monarchy, two symbolic trees <em>(egbo),</em> one for Ba-Iyi and one for Okwunye\nmarked the <em>Ihu Ani Ukwu Idumuje</em>; literary,\nthe face of the great land of Idumuje.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>A royal palace was put in place, shrines\nalso set up. There was the famous <em>Okwute\nUwatuwa</em> surrounded by three <em>egbo</em>\ntrees at the King’s Palace in Idumuje-Unor..</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Chapter Eight gave a historical pact between\nBa-Iyi and Okwunye which became the cradle of cultural development of Idumuje Clan.\nThe author also gave a brief history of the administrative organogram of the\nclan.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Chapter Nine gave a history of the\nIdumuje Unor Royal Family. It is indisputable that Okwunye is the sole Royal\nfamily in Idumuje Unor. The royal lineage from Eze Okwunye up to Obi Anyasi III\nis captured and discussed in this chapter. At present, the Obi of the town is\nObi Charles C. Anyasi III; traditionally enthroned on Sunday 5<sup>th</sup>\nMay, 2013.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Chapter Ten gave a history of the\norigin of Idumobu village and dates back to the late 15th to early 16<sup>th</sup>\ncentury. The founder, Obu, was reputed to possess magical powers. He was\ninvited by the King of Idumuje to help defend the territorial integrity of the\nfledging kindom against external invaders. Obu came and later settled in\nIdumuje with the gift of a wife by the King for his (Obu’s) exploits. He was\nalso given a land to settle. Kindred’s later sprang up from this union. The\ntraditional title bequeathed to Idumobu village is Iyase - next to the king.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Chapter Eleven is on the origin of\nAtuma Village. The kindreds that constitute Atuma are believed to have arrived\nfrom various places at different times. A lot of versions exist as to the original\nfounders of the village. The chieftaincy title of Odogwu-Atuma belongs to\nIdumu-Okohu. Odogwu-Atuma is next in rank to Odogwu-Ani (Odogwu of the whole\ntown) which is held by Imeogbe-Isaagbeme and Imeogbe-Ihunmor in rotation.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>In Chapter Twelve, the history of the\nsixth village, Ogbeakwu, is told. The founder was Isede who migrated from\nOnicha-Ugbo to join Obu’s descendants at Idumobu. Ikoko Iyala is the cradle of\nthis new village. Uwolo is the chieftaincy title for this new village: this\nvillage was also reputed as the first to embrace Christianity in Idumuje Unor in\nthe early 20<sup>th</sup> century. The first church in Idumuje Unor is St.\nMatthew’s Catholic Church founded in 1905 while the second is St. Luke’s\nAnglican Church founded in 1907 all in Ogbeakwu. </p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Chapter Thirteen gave a story of Aniofu\nvillage. This is a village founded during the reign of Obi Adonoje who\nretreated into the forest due to escalation of Ekwumekwu war of resistance to\nwhite domination. When the war ended after many years, the Obi retuned but the\ncluster of people from diverse backgrounds and villages brought by the Obi\nremained behind. The head of the village is known as Diopka – the eldest man.\nIt is worthy to note that the villages and kindreds in Idumuje-Unor, are\nreplicated in Aniofu.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Chapters Fourteen to Nineteen gave a\nhistory of how one Nwoko, a descendant of Erhabor kindred in Imeogbe-Isagbeme,\nIdumuje-Unor, founded Idumuje-Ugboko with his family members in the later\ndecade of the 18<sup>th</sup> century. Nwoko was later excused from paying\nhomage to the King of Idumuje-Unor and became autonomous. He later acquired and\nreplicated the major shrines of Idumuje-Unor in Idumuje-Ugboko. The Royal\nfamily of Idumuje-Ugboko was also discussed in chapter fifteen. The present Obi\nof Idumuje-Ugboko is HRM, Obi Chukwunonso Nwoko IV. The administration,\ncultural practices and chieftaincy development of Idumuje-Ugboko are contained\nin the chapters.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Chapter Twenty marked the beginning of\nPart Two of the book. Here the cultural identitiy, significant cultural\nsymbols, structural pattern of the traditional government, traditional functions\nof kings, as well as succession/installation of Obis and chiefs, traditional\nprivileges and symbol of unity in Idumuje Clan are captured and x-rayed in\ndetail.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>In Chapter Twenty-Five, the institution\nand function of Omu as &nbsp;well as the Omu\nsociety is discussed. Chapter Twenty-Six talked about the chieftaincy\ninstitution as well as the distribution of traditional cchieftaincies in\nvarious villages in Idumuje Clan.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>In chapters Twenty-Seven and\nTwenty-Eight, the book discussed various title-making societies and special\ngroups such as; the <em>Ikpala (Ichi-Nmor)</em>.\nIt discussed the process of <em>Iba-Nzu </em>and\nsome paraphernalia used during the ceremony, including <em>Okpulukpu, Okwa Nzu</em> and a host of other paraphernalia of office of <em>okpala </em>title holders. It also discussed\nspecial groups such as <em>Ndi-Ogbu</em> and <em>Ndi-Idelima</em>.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Chapter Twenty-Nine wrote on the\ntraditional rights and obligations of indigenes in Idumuje Clan. In Chapter\nThirty, we read about <em>Okika</em> (traditional\ncalendar) in Idumuje Clan.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Chapter Thirty-One talked about <em>Iba-Nzu, Iwaji, Ogbainbge</em> and <em>Ine </em>festivals. All these are traditional\nfestivals of purification, social solidarity and merry-making.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The family institution in Idumuje Clan\nis captured in Chapter Thirty-Two. Here we see forms of traditional marriages\nand marriage rites up to child birth and naming as well as processes. The\nhistory of Idumuje Ontology/Religious institutions are well documented in\nChapter Thirty-Three. Funeral rites <em>(Ini-Ozu)\n</em>in Idumuje Clan – types and process, dominated the discussion in Chapter\nThirty-Four.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>In Chapter Thirty-Five, economic\nphilosophy of Idumuje people is discussed - how their resources, technology and\nenvironment are harnessed to satisfy basic need of life.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Chapters Thirty-Six and Thirty-Seven\nare on educational institution in Idumjuje Clan. Traditional forms of learning,\nApprenticeship, Technology, Arts and Crafts of the people of Idumuje were\ndiscussed.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Chapter Thirty-Nine wrote on traditional\nprocess and social control. Here you see the place of the philosophy of\nindigenous legal system and traditional approach to settlement of disputes.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Chapter Forty wrote on the <em>Egwugwu</em> (masquerade) institution in\nIdumuje Clan, its origin, attributions and its over-riding efficacy in social\ncontrol.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>In Chapter Forty-One, the book wrote on\nesoteric traditions of Idumuje Clan. Some of these institutions are not usually\nfor public consumption and their modus operandi is shrouded in secrecy. Some of\nthese are <em>Ime-Ohai</em> and <em>Igba-Afa </em>(Divination), rain makers,\nincantation, <em>Ikpai,</em> to mention but a\nfew.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Part Three of the book starting from\nChapter Forty-Two, gave an account of the advent of colonialism in Idumuje Clan\nand the Ekwumeku onslaught. Arrival of colonial masters, missionaries,\nresistance against foreign domination and reasons for resistance, incessant\narrest of natives as well as the impact of forced rule on the political and\njudicial Institution in Idumuje Clan from colonial, post-colonial and the\nmilitary era were chronicled. The book gave an account of the impact of these\nhistorical forces on social institutions in the clan. In the view of the\nauthor, these external influences changed drastically the nature and\nconstituents of traditional institutions in Idumuje Clan. The colonialists\nintroduced special organs of government specifically devoted to distinct aspect\nof administration of the society.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>In Chapter Forty-Four, fundamental\nchanges in the prestigious culture of Idumuje Clan were documented. Many\ntraditional sites/trees/practices/rites have become seriously brutalized if not\ncompletely obliviated.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>In Chapter Forty-Five, the book\nchronicled the impact of foreign rule on the economic structure of Idumuje Clan.\nThe exogenous factors in cultural change, traditional festivals and family\nvalues constitute the focus of Chapter Forty-Six while Chapter Forty-Seven\ndwelt on paradise lost - changes in social relations, class structure and\ncultural values. These changes created an emerging chaotic society.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Part Four of the book contains Chapters\nForty-Eight to Fifty. Chapter Forty-Eight dwelt on advances in education and\nhealth in Idumuje Clan. The first formal educational institution is now known\nas Nkeze Primary school, founded by the CMS in 1902 at Idumuje- Ugboko and St.\nMatthew’s Primary School, Idumuje-Unor established by the Catholic Mission in\n1905.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The first and most notable among the Developmental\nUnions in Idumuje Clan, is Idumuje Patriotic Union of Nigeria (IPUN) founded in\n1936. The union later establish the Patriotic School in 1945 and by Januarys\n17, 1955, the school was taken over by Western Region Government Universal\nPrimary Education (UPE) Programme. The Idumuje-Ugboko Development Union (IUDU)\nwas the next. The IUDU helped in establishing educational and health centers in\nthe clan. From 1970, communities in the clan contributed funds to provide\nelectricity and with the giant strides of the town unions, the communities have\nbeen linked to the National Grid. </p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Chapter Forty-Nine dwelt on defence of\nindigenous culture. Traditional religion was compared and contrasted with\nborrowed religion in this chapter.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The concluding Chapter of the book\n(Chapter Fifty) talked about the need for self-re-discovery in Idumuje Clan and\nrevival of traditional values and practices that have gone moribund if not into\noblivion. For ages, long before the coming of Europeans with their version\nof&nbsp; of civilization, our fore\nfathers/ancestors established, cherished and treasured their independence and\ncommunal living. The displacement of indigenous culture created a situation\nwhereby we are neither birds nor animals (confused generation). There is need\nfor serious mental re-orientation and attitudinal re-programming.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>Conclusion</strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The book which\nis written by no less a scholar, an intellectual giant, literary icon and\nebullient historiographer, Chief Chris O. O. Biose, is a must read for all\nIdumuje sons and daughters. It is also for all inhabitants of Idumuje-Odiani\nclans, Aniocha North Local Government Area, Anioma and even Nigeria scholars as\na whole. It is a good source of reference materials also for students of History\nand Anthropology. The readership-friendly language used by the author makes the\nbook a must-read to all and sundry. The book also contains a list of discoveries\nneeded by future researchers in related literatures and studies. I am very\nconvinced that this well researched book will stand the test of time as it\nremains an intellectual mahogany for good people of Idumuje Clan and scholars\nat large.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>Dr Ezoem N. Ignatius (FCIA, AAA, Minae)</strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>Provost Emeritus, FCE (T) Asaba, Delta State, Nigeria..</strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->', 'REVIEW OF THE  BOOK “HISTORY AND TRADITIONS OF  IDUMUJE CLAN” – Authored by Chief Chris O. O. Biose', '', 'publish', 'open', 'closed', '', 'review-of-the-book-history-and-traditions-of-idumuje-clan-authored-by-chief-chris-o-o-biose', '', '', '2022-11-29 09:21:47', '2022-11-29 09:21:47', '<!-- wp:paragraph {\"backgroundColor\":\"very-light-gray\"} -->\n<p class=\"has-background has-very-light-gray-background-color\">REVIEW BY<br>\nDR. EZOEM, NWANZE IGNATIUS<br>\nPROVOST EMERITUS<br>\nFCE (T) ASABA<br>\nDELTA STATE</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph {\"align\":\"justify\",\"backgroundColor\":\"very-light-gray\"} -->\n<p class=\"has-background has-text-align-justify has-very-light-gray-background-color\">Book Title:               HISTORY AND TRADITION S OF<br>                               IDUMUJE CLAN<br> Author:                  Chris O. O. Biose<br> Publishers:            BOLD Publishers Ltd, 8 Fadipe Street,<br>                                 Ijaye- Ogba, Ikeja, Lagos State.<br> Year Published:     September, 2019.<br> Pages:                    503<br>ISBN:                     13978-978-49936-8-5 <br>EAN:                      9789784993685<br>COVER PRICE:     N10,000.00</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>Introduction</strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>If one does not know where he is coming from, it will be difficult for him to know exactly where he is going to because he definitely does not belong to where he is. James Kegyir Aggrey, Ghanaian renowned scholar once said “I am proud of my colour, whoever is not proud of his colour is not fit to live. In the whole wide world, Africa my Africa comes first”. The book we are about to have a glimpse of, is a veritable historical discourse that showcased the obstinate reality of the origins and cultural evolution of the people of Idumuje Clan in Aniocha North Local Government Area of Delta State.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>The Book</strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The compendium with 503 pages has three\nparts that were clustered into 50 chapters. In Chapter One, the author gave a\nmethodological overview of carrying out his study and data collection. In the\nsecond chapter, we are enlightened with the cradle, the geographical location\nand historical origin of Enuani people, of which Idumuje Clan is a part.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>In this chapter, we are meant to know\nthat Ikeke was the progenitor while Ikeke and Isagbeme formed Imeogbe Village\nas early as the 13<sup>th</sup> century. This chapter also showed how other\nvillages and cultural practices evolved.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>In Chapters Three and Four, the book\nconcentrated on the cultural set up and practice of Imeogbe-Ihunmor, Idumude-Unor\nand Imeogbe-Isagbeme village. The history of how Isagbeme came to meet Ikeke\nand they started what is today popularly known as Idumuje-Ikeke was well\nestablished. Their sojourn from <em>Iduu,</em>\ntheir cultural practices and Apotheosis were also well covered.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>From Chapter Five, the book opened with\na discourse into the spiritual foundation of Idumuje civilization. Here we see\nhow Ikeke arrived his settlement with two deities - the Ohai (male) and Nnemonicha\n(female). Shrines were erected where the god and goddess were kept and worshipped.\nThese and others latter became sacred places in the land. Sacred places and\ntrees were later set up by these two founders. The deities had their separate\npriests.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>In\nChapter Six, the emergence of Okwunye Village was showcased. Okwunye arrived in\nthe 14<sup>th</sup> century. Although it is indisputable that Imeogbe-Ihunmor\nis the cradle of Idumuje, the arrival of Okwunye in the 14<sup>th</sup> century\nand his interaction with Ba-Iyi descendant of Ikeke, eventually proved to be\nthe most momentous event in the historiography of Idumuje people. Ba-Iyi and\nOkwunye were the founders of Idumuje as a distinct political entity. Ba-Iyi had\nspiritual powers while Okwunye held sway at the political administration of the\nevolving kingdom.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>In Chapter Seven, we see the origins of\nmonarchical Institution in Idumuje Clan rulership by a king. Idumuje land was a\nstate-less society for over a hundred years. There was no centralized political\nauthority but only a few homesteads. The union of Ba-Iyi and Okwunye signaled\nthe commencement of monarchical authority in Idumuje that then became a\nkingdom. As core founders of Idumuje monarchy, two symbolic trees <em>(egbo),</em> one for Ba-Iyi and one for Okwunye\nmarked the <em>Ihu Ani Ukwu Idumuje</em>; literary,\nthe face of the great land of Idumuje.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>A royal palace was put in place, shrines\nalso set up. There was the famous <em>Okwute\nUwatuwa</em> surrounded by three <em>egbo</em>\ntrees at the King’s Palace in Idumuje-Unor..</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Chapter Eight gave a historical pact between\nBa-Iyi and Okwunye which became the cradle of cultural development of Idumuje Clan.\nThe author also gave a brief history of the administrative organogram of the\nclan.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Chapter Nine gave a history of the\nIdumuje Unor Royal Family. It is indisputable that Okwunye is the sole Royal\nfamily in Idumuje Unor. The royal lineage from Eze Okwunye up to Obi Anyasi III\nis captured and discussed in this chapter. At present, the Obi of the town is\nObi Charles C. Anyasi III; traditionally enthroned on Sunday 5<sup>th</sup>\nMay, 2013.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Chapter Ten gave a history of the\norigin of Idumobu village and dates back to the late 15th to early 16<sup>th</sup>\ncentury. The founder, Obu, was reputed to possess magical powers. He was\ninvited by the King of Idumuje to help defend the territorial integrity of the\nfledging kindom against external invaders. Obu came and later settled in\nIdumuje with the gift of a wife by the King for his (Obu’s) exploits. He was\nalso given a land to settle. Kindred’s later sprang up from this union. The\ntraditional title bequeathed to Idumobu village is Iyase - next to the king.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Chapter Eleven is on the origin of\nAtuma Village. The kindreds that constitute Atuma are believed to have arrived\nfrom various places at different times. A lot of versions exist as to the original\nfounders of the village. The chieftaincy title of Odogwu-Atuma belongs to\nIdumu-Okohu. Odogwu-Atuma is next in rank to Odogwu-Ani (Odogwu of the whole\ntown) which is held by Imeogbe-Isaagbeme and Imeogbe-Ihunmor in rotation.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>In Chapter Twelve, the history of the\nsixth village, Ogbeakwu, is told. The founder was Isede who migrated from\nOnicha-Ugbo to join Obu’s descendants at Idumobu. Ikoko Iyala is the cradle of\nthis new village. Uwolo is the chieftaincy title for this new village: this\nvillage was also reputed as the first to embrace Christianity in Idumuje Unor in\nthe early 20<sup>th</sup> century. The first church in Idumuje Unor is St.\nMatthew’s Catholic Church founded in 1905 while the second is St. Luke’s\nAnglican Church founded in 1907 all in Ogbeakwu. </p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Chapter Thirteen gave a story of Aniofu\nvillage. This is a village founded during the reign of Obi Adonoje who\nretreated into the forest due to escalation of Ekwumekwu war of resistance to\nwhite domination. When the war ended after many years, the Obi retuned but the\ncluster of people from diverse backgrounds and villages brought by the Obi\nremained behind. The head of the village is known as Diopka – the eldest man.\nIt is worthy to note that the villages and kindreds in Idumuje-Unor, are\nreplicated in Aniofu.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Chapters Fourteen to Nineteen gave a\nhistory of how one Nwoko, a descendant of Erhabor kindred in Imeogbe-Isagbeme,\nIdumuje-Unor, founded Idumuje-Ugboko with his family members in the later\ndecade of the 18<sup>th</sup> century. Nwoko was later excused from paying\nhomage to the King of Idumuje-Unor and became autonomous. He later acquired and\nreplicated the major shrines of Idumuje-Unor in Idumuje-Ugboko. The Royal\nfamily of Idumuje-Ugboko was also discussed in chapter fifteen. The present Obi\nof Idumuje-Ugboko is HRM, Obi Chukwunonso Nwoko IV. The administration,\ncultural practices and chieftaincy development of Idumuje-Ugboko are contained\nin the chapters.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Chapter Twenty marked the beginning of\nPart Two of the book. Here the cultural identitiy, significant cultural\nsymbols, structural pattern of the traditional government, traditional functions\nof kings, as well as succession/installation of Obis and chiefs, traditional\nprivileges and symbol of unity in Idumuje Clan are captured and x-rayed in\ndetail.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>In Chapter Twenty-Five, the institution\nand function of Omu as &nbsp;well as the Omu\nsociety is discussed. Chapter Twenty-Six talked about the chieftaincy\ninstitution as well as the distribution of traditional cchieftaincies in\nvarious villages in Idumuje Clan.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>In chapters Twenty-Seven and\nTwenty-Eight, the book discussed various title-making societies and special\ngroups such as; the <em>Ikpala (Ichi-Nmor)</em>.\nIt discussed the process of <em>Iba-Nzu </em>and\nsome paraphernalia used during the ceremony, including <em>Okpulukpu, Okwa Nzu</em> and a host of other paraphernalia of office of <em>okpala </em>title holders. It also discussed\nspecial groups such as <em>Ndi-Ogbu</em> and <em>Ndi-Idelima</em>.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Chapter Twenty-Nine wrote on the\ntraditional rights and obligations of indigenes in Idumuje Clan. In Chapter\nThirty, we read about <em>Okika</em> (traditional\ncalendar) in Idumuje Clan.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Chapter Thirty-One talked about <em>Iba-Nzu, Iwaji, Ogbainbge</em> and <em>Ine </em>festivals. All these are traditional\nfestivals of purification, social solidarity and merry-making.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The family institution in Idumuje Clan\nis captured in Chapter Thirty-Two. Here we see forms of traditional marriages\nand marriage rites up to child birth and naming as well as processes. The\nhistory of Idumuje Ontology/Religious institutions are well documented in\nChapter Thirty-Three. Funeral rites <em>(Ini-Ozu)\n</em>in Idumuje Clan – types and process, dominated the discussion in Chapter\nThirty-Four.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>In Chapter Thirty-Five, economic\nphilosophy of Idumuje people is discussed - how their resources, technology and\nenvironment are harnessed to satisfy basic need of life.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Chapters Thirty-Six and Thirty-Seven\nare on educational institution in Idumjuje Clan. Traditional forms of learning,\nApprenticeship, Technology, Arts and Crafts of the people of Idumuje were\ndiscussed.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Chapter Thirty-Nine wrote on traditional\nprocess and social control. Here you see the place of the philosophy of\nindigenous legal system and traditional approach to settlement of disputes.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Chapter Forty wrote on the <em>Egwugwu</em> (masquerade) institution in\nIdumuje Clan, its origin, attributions and its over-riding efficacy in social\ncontrol.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>In Chapter Forty-One, the book wrote on\nesoteric traditions of Idumuje Clan. Some of these institutions are not usually\nfor public consumption and their modus operandi is shrouded in secrecy. Some of\nthese are <em>Ime-Ohai</em> and <em>Igba-Afa </em>(Divination), rain makers,\nincantation, <em>Ikpai,</em> to mention but a\nfew.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Part Three of the book starting from\nChapter Forty-Two, gave an account of the advent of colonialism in Idumuje Clan\nand the Ekwumeku onslaught. Arrival of colonial masters, missionaries,\nresistance against foreign domination and reasons for resistance, incessant\narrest of natives as well as the impact of forced rule on the political and\njudicial Institution in Idumuje Clan from colonial, post-colonial and the\nmilitary era were chronicled. The book gave an account of the impact of these\nhistorical forces on social institutions in the clan. In the view of the\nauthor, these external influences changed drastically the nature and\nconstituents of traditional institutions in Idumuje Clan. The colonialists\nintroduced special organs of government specifically devoted to distinct aspect\nof administration of the society.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>In Chapter Forty-Four, fundamental\nchanges in the prestigious culture of Idumuje Clan were documented. Many\ntraditional sites/trees/practices/rites have become seriously brutalized if not\ncompletely obliviated.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>In Chapter Forty-Five, the book\nchronicled the impact of foreign rule on the economic structure of Idumuje Clan.\nThe exogenous factors in cultural change, traditional festivals and family\nvalues constitute the focus of Chapter Forty-Six while Chapter Forty-Seven\ndwelt on paradise lost - changes in social relations, class structure and\ncultural values. These changes created an emerging chaotic society.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Part Four of the book contains Chapters\nForty-Eight to Fifty. Chapter Forty-Eight dwelt on advances in education and\nhealth in Idumuje Clan. The first formal educational institution is now known\nas Nkeze Primary school, founded by the CMS in 1902 at Idumuje- Ugboko and St.\nMatthew’s Primary School, Idumuje-Unor established by the Catholic Mission in\n1905.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The first and most notable among the Developmental\nUnions in Idumuje Clan, is Idumuje Patriotic Union of Nigeria (IPUN) founded in\n1936. The union later establish the Patriotic School in 1945 and by Januarys\n17, 1955, the school was taken over by Western Region Government Universal\nPrimary Education (UPE) Programme. The Idumuje-Ugboko Development Union (IUDU)\nwas the next. The IUDU helped in establishing educational and health centers in\nthe clan. From 1970, communities in the clan contributed funds to provide\nelectricity and with the giant strides of the town unions, the communities have\nbeen linked to the National Grid. </p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Chapter Forty-Nine dwelt on defence of\nindigenous culture. Traditional religion was compared and contrasted with\nborrowed religion in this chapter.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The concluding Chapter of the book\n(Chapter Fifty) talked about the need for self-re-discovery in Idumuje Clan and\nrevival of traditional values and practices that have gone moribund if not into\noblivion. For ages, long before the coming of Europeans with their version\nof&nbsp; of civilization, our fore\nfathers/ancestors established, cherished and treasured their independence and\ncommunal living. The displacement of indigenous culture created a situation\nwhereby we are neither birds nor animals (confused generation). There is need\nfor serious mental re-orientation and attitudinal re-programming.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>Conclusion</strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The book which\nis written by no less a scholar, an intellectual giant, literary icon and\nebullient historiographer, Chief Chris O. O. Biose, is a must read for all\nIdumuje sons and daughters. It is also for all inhabitants of Idumuje-Odiani\nclans, Aniocha North Local Government Area, Anioma and even Nigeria scholars as\na whole. It is a good source of reference materials also for students of History\nand Anthropology. The readership-friendly language used by the author makes the\nbook a must-read to all and sundry. The book also contains a list of discoveries\nneeded by future researchers in related literatures and studies. I am very\nconvinced that this well researched book will stand the test of time as it\nremains an intellectual mahogany for good people of Idumuje Clan and scholars\nat large.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>Dr Ezoem N. Ignatius (FCIA, AAA, Minae)</strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>Provost Emeritus, FCE (T) Asaba, Delta State, Nigeria..</strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->', 0, 'https://boldtimes.com.ng/?p=341', 0, 'post', '', 2),
