(408, 1, '2019-12-17 12:59:46', '2019-12-17 12:59:46', '<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong><em>“If per chance you come across a human being who is\nunable to tell his particular bearing relatively to one or more of the\ninnumerable points of life’s compass, you would not hesitate to write him off\nas a child, an idiot, or an aimless wanderer.</em></strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong><em>“In the same way, if you meet a politician who is\nunwilling or unable to declare and, as precisely as possible, describe his\nposition in relation to the cardinal points of the political compass, you will\nfeel strongly tempted to denounce him as a fraud, a total misfit, or a hopeless\ndrifter.</em></strong><strong>”&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; </strong>- Obafemi Awolowo (1909-1987)&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; </p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>When it is said\nthat Nigeria is a backward country, as measured by economic and development\nindices, most people tend to compare tall skyscrapers in Manhattan District of\nNew York with those at the Central Business District in the Nigerian capital, Abuja,\nas indices of development. We think of spiraling inflation, heavy debt burden\nand increasing worthlessness of the Nigerian currency triggered by retired\nGeneral Ibrahim Babangida’s Structural Adjustment Programme (SAP) in 1986. We\nruminate over huge deficit in core economic infrastructure such as electric\npower as well as social infrastructural deficit in education and health. All\nthese result in shorter life span and overall low quality of life for Nigerians.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>But there is a more\ntelling dimension of backwardness which is much more invidious, yet less\namenable to public perception or social measurement. I refer to the moral\nstatus of the Nigerian elite, lack of commitment to stated goals of the society\nby the elite, lackadaisical attitude to duty and generally, warped thinking\nabout public affairs. </p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>Fake Human Beings</strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Most\npeople are aware of fake products - things that are not what they purports to\nbe. For example, if one buys salt from the market and uses it for his food but\nthe food tastes bland and unsalted, then it is obvious that the salt is fake\neven though it has the appearance of salt. Common examples of fake products in\nNigeria are fake wine, fake fruit drinks, fake cream, imitation jewelries and\nfake electronic devices. </p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>In\nAugust 2014, the Standard Organisation of Nigeria (SON) revealed that 80 per\ncent of computer, accessories and allied technology products and ICT devices sold\nat the largest computer market in Nigeria, Computer Village Market, Ikeja, were\ncounterfeit. According to the Managing Director of SON, Dr. Joseph Odumudu, <strong>“80% of their activities are illicit.”</strong>\nHe said that the activities of counterfeiters in the industry led to the\n“closure of many legitimate companies with their work force thrown into the\nlabour market”. (Prince Osuagwu, SON declares 80% of market fake, CAPDAN kicks,\nadmit 30%, Vanguard, August 13, 2014). Nigeria is a place where fake products\ndrive away genuine ones.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Nigeria\nis not only dominated by fake products, it is also peopled by fake human\nbeings. By fake human beings, I do not mean dolls or robots because these are\nnot human beings at all. Fake human beings as used here are people who pretend\nto be what they are not. Some persons assume positions but refuse to exhibit\nqualities and skills requisite for such positions.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>For example, some\nmarried women are merely women married to their men but not wives. To be a\nwife, a woman must play the role of a wife as defined by society and culture.\nIn such a case, a man has a woman who has children for him but not a wife. In\nthe same way, some married men deny their wives of love and care; they neglect\nor refuse to play the role of husband and may therefore be seen as married men\nand legal father of children but not husbands to their wives.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>A young person who\nregisters to study in an institution of learning but does not attend classes or\nengage in any form of learning is most likely a loafer, certainly not a student.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>To be brief,\neverything has a function and it is the fulfillment of such function that makes\nit what it claims to be. For considerations of space, let us\ntake four examples in the public space, namely, fake religionists, fake\nTraditional Rulers, fake security operatives and the father of all fakes - fake\npoliticians.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>Fake Religionists</strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Of\nall categories of fake human beings in contemporary Nigeria, the most common\ncategory is fake religionists that dot every nook and cranny of our villages\nand cities. They operate under the camouflage of Godliness and since most\nNigerians claim to be religious, fake religionists have free passage however dubious\ntheir activities may be. </p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Fake\nreligionists are not restricted to Nigeria, neither is it a new phenomenon in\nthe society. About 2,000 years ago, the Lord Jesus was constrained to drive\naway those who were buying and selling in the temple in Jerusalem and who made\nthe temple of God “a den of thieves” (Matthew 21:12-13). In our day, every Tom,\nDick and Harry claims to be a prophet, performing miracles at every corner and\non television, bombarding homes with varied exhortations. They measure their\nsuccess in terms of Naira, hard currency, expensive cars and jet planes.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Like their\ncounterparts of 2000 years ago, most of these are thieves, albeit of the more\nsubtle type that lawyers classify as confidence tricksters, “receiving under\nfalse pretence” which is a punishable act under Section 419 of the Criminal\nCode. Today, these impostors have evolved highly sophisticated techniques of\nplying their vicious trade through trendy dressing and fantastic claims on\nradio and television, coupled with a domineering attitude, all designed to\ncheat and exploit. </p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>I once asked\none of my friends why he made himself a Bishop soon after opening his church in\nBenin City. He told me that as a Bishop, he would receive special recognition\nor mention and sit at the high table or front row at any public event organized\nby the government. He said that he would ask the Governor to <strong>“kneel down, let me pray for you.”</strong> He\nsaid that as a Bishop, he has the privilege to lay his hands on the head of any\nprominent personality in the society, thereby gaining authority over them. It\nis the fastest way to social recognition and wealth, he asserted with gusto. He\ninvited me to take a high position in his church and I politely declined.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The prodigious\nand horrific spiritual harlotry by Nigerian potentates, spiritual and temporal,\nwho remorselessly use the name of God for lucre, is one of the saddest chapters\nin the history of this beleaguered country. The Nigerian situation exemplifies\nwhat Professor Isaac Sagay once described as <strong>“religious schizophrenia - outwardly religious but inwardly diabolical.”\n</strong>(Nigeria: Serving God and Mammon, Sunday Vanguard, December 17, 2000, page\n9). The Lord Jesus admonished us to “<strong>beware\nof false prophets, which come to you in sheep’s clothing, but inwardly, are\nravening wolves.” &nbsp;</strong>(Matthew 7:15)</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>Fake Traditional\nRulers</strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Another category of\nfakes that present in flowing robes and &nbsp;glamorous attires are called “Traditional\nRulers” or “Monarchs”. Monarchy is one of the oldest forms of government in this\npart of the world. Most Nigerian communities revered their kings. When British colonialists\ncame, they demoted indigenous kings to “chiefs” because they would not\ncontemplate any other king than the British crown. Therefore, to be relevant, a\nking must pander to the government and the practice continued since then. Kings\nlook to government for recognition and financial support rather than their\ncommunities. </p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The second factor that\ncontributed to disconnect between some kings and their people is the religion\nintroduced by the colonisers. Some kings think that to be modern, they must\ndemonise indigenous culture as the colonialists did. They deviate from\npromoting, projecting and defending indigenous cultural values while some of\nthem took on the business of active demolition of indigenous cultural heritage\nof their communities. The institution turned into a government appointment and kings\nglorify in government recognition and financial perquisites rather than\nsustenance of traditions of their communities. Consequently, the institution has\nbeen largely trivialized and commercialized if not bastardised, not belonging\nto tradition or government. </p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>There is no doubt\nthat some kings hold fast to the culture and traditions of their people and\neven improving on it. Notable examples are the Benin and Itsekiri kingdoms.\nHowever, the experience of many communities in Southern parts of Nigeria is\nthat their kings have abandoned their traditions and embraced alien cultures in\ntheir bid to appear “modern”.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Like fake products,\nthey adorn themselves with traditional attires but in their conduct and actions\nin their communities, they are anti-tradition. Tradition means that which is\nlaid down from the past. It does not mean church. It existed before colonialism\nand churchianity. No traditional ruler is installed on the basis of foreign\nreligion. But after coronation, some of them throw the tradition of their\npeople to the dustbin and embark on anti-tradition. Such kings are fakes; they\nmay be recognized as traditional rulers by the government but like unsalted\nsalt, they are fake.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>Fake Security\nOperatives</strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Security\nis an indispensable need in any society. That is probably why it is enshrined\nin Chapter ll, Section 14 (2) (b) of the Constitution of the Federal Republic\nof Nigeria, 1999, as amended that <strong>“security\nand welfare of the people shall be the primary purpose of government.”</strong> The same\nConstitution also made provision for establishment of agencies to ensure\nsecurity of life and property of citizens. These include the Nigeria Police\nForce, the Armed Forces and other security and para-military agencies.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>However,\nsome individuals in uniform such as Customs and Excise, Nigeria Police Force and\nArmed Forces use their positions contrary to that provided by the law that set\nthem up. There is nowhere that the law says that Nigerians citizens should be\nheld in detention without trial, held in underground cells or subjected to torture\nand other degrading and inhuman treatment but these are practices that come to\nlight from time to time in the country.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The\nmost rampant and open display of lawlessness by law enforcement officers in the\npublic domain is extortion. Large numbers of policemen line up at roads,\nparticularly in South-East of the country, extorting money from hapless\nmotorists while fully armed, pretending to be performing police duties. If they\nare collecting money from motorists by force of arms, are they not better\ndescribed as armed robbers than policemen? Is it part of police duties to line\nthe roads and forcefully collect money from motorists?</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>Fake Politicians</strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>In\nspite of several categories of fake human beings that undermine the moral\nvigour of the society, a few of which are enumerated above, the most socially\ndangerous fakes in Nigeria are fake politicians. This is because of the wide\ndemonstration effect of the behavior of this class of fakes. They lay down\nexamples, for good or for ill, for other sectors of the society</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The\nstock in trade of this category of fakes is violence and deception. How could\nsomeone who feeds young people with drugs and gives them guns to dislodge his\npolitical opponents be described as a politician? Such a person is a brigand of\nthe most horrible order. It would be a misnomer to address such a person as a\npolitician of the democratic breed.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Unfortunately,\nlike fake products, fake politicians drive legitimate and honest ones out of\ncirculation.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Worse\nstill, the violent tactics of military-political elite permeated the entire\nsocial fabric and produced an increasingly violent generation and glorifies\nviolence as a way of life.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>A True Public\nServant</strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>It\ngladdens the heart to realize that Nigeria is not entirely dominated by fakes. There\nare some shining lights that demonstrate noble traits that are the best in\nhumanity, even though they are few and far between.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Before\nProfessor Dora Akunyili (1954-2014) mounted her vigorous campaign against fake\nand counterfeit drugs in Nigeria in her capacity as Director-General of\nNational Agency for Food and Drug Administration and Control (NAFDC), fake and\ncounterfeit drugs dominated the Nigerian market. She took the bull by the horn.\nAccording to Akunyili, <strong>“People there\n(NAFDAC) did not believe that I could confront the mafia who had been\nunchallenged for almost three decades. It was a rude shock to the\ncounterfeiters that we refused any discussion or negotiation for business to\ncontinue as usual.” </strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:image {\"id\":409,\"sizeSlug\":\"large\"} -->\n<figure class=\"wp-block-image size-large\"><img src=\"https://boldtimes.net.ng-content/uploads/2019/12/images.jpg\" alt=\"\" class=\"wp-image-409\"/></figure>\n<!-- /wp:image -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>She\npersonally led raids to dislodge notorious drug counterfeiters in open drug\nmarkets in Onitsha, and other places and seized fake drugs worth billions of\nNaira.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Akunyili is from\nAnambra State and Onitsha in her home state is generally recognized as the\nheadquarters of fake and counterfeit drugs in Nigeria. She did not say, “they\nare my people, let me be soft on them.”</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>When the approach which\nthe counterfeiters made to her failed, they resorted to harassment and\nintimidation, thinking that as a woman, she would succumb to their antics. But\nshe was unyielding in pursuit of her assignment. In December 2003, she narrowly\nescaped death when assassins attacked her car near Agulu in Anambra State. The\nbullets shattered the rear windscreen of her car, pierced her headscarf and\nburnt her scarf <strong>“like hot water” </strong>and\nwent through the front screen. She was naturally shaken but would not bow to\nintimidation.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>In\nMarch 2008, she sealed off the Onitsha Head Bridge drug market completely when\nthe drug dealers were found to be intransigent and incorrigible. The market was\nonly reopened when the former Governor of the State, Mr. Peter Obi, personally\nintervened and assured her that he would help police the market very closely\nand rid it of dangerous drug dealers.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>When\nshe was appointed Minister of Information in December 2008, the Drug Dealers at\nOnitsha Head Bridge Market openly celebrated her departure from NAFDAC. They\nabandoned their stalls and paraded nearby streets, popping champagne and\ndancing that the iron lady was off their neck.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Akunyili\ntook her legendary distaste for underhand tactics to her new post. When highly\nplaced officials at the Presidency were playing hide and seek over the health\nstatus of late President Umaru Musa Yar’Adua, Akunyili was the odd man out,\nmaking her famous statement, <strong>“I am fed\nup with lies about Yar’Adua.”</strong> </p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Writing\nin May 2006, Annes Harding, a world-acclaimed writer on health and scientific\ntopics stated that <strong>“since she became\nDirector-General in 2001, the proportion of drugs sold in Nigeria that are fake\nhas dropped from 70% to 10%.”</strong> In its Bulletin Volume 84, No. 9, published\nin September 2006, titled <em>Nigeria leads\nfight against “killer” counterfeit drugs,</em> the World Health Organisation\n(WHO) declared that <strong>“Nigeria has been at\nthe forefront of global efforts to fight counterfeit drugs since Dora Akunyili\ntook over the NAFDAC in 2001.”</strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Akunyili\nis an outstanding example of a true Minister (servant) of the people of\nNigeria. </p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>Away with Fake\nPoliticians</strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The\nFederal Government and National Assembly make a big issue about “fake news” and\ntheir desire to legislate on it. But informed opinion is that preoccupation\nwith so called fake news is only an attempt to cover up the wide range of fakes\nin Nigerian public life, most of whom belong to the class of power elite.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Whatever\nthe resources available in a state, if the human beings occupying that space\nare fake, unwilling to perform their duties creditably or or morally debased,\nthe place is thereby debased. The effect is more crippling when the debased\nconstitute the ruling class. A morally and spiritually debased or fake ruling\nclass is the greatest curse any society may experience.\n\nNigeria cannot achieve\ndevelopment with fake religionists, fake kings, fake security officers and fake\npoliticians. Most people are afraid to speak out about fakes that predominate\nin the society because of their vindictiveness and the vicious manner in which\nthey deploy their power against perceived threats. However, for the health,\nsurvival and development of the country, all hands must be on deck to expose\nand dislodge fakes that are acting as worms eating down this potentially great\ncountry. It is time to do away with fakes in our body politic.\n\n\n\n</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->', 'NIGERIA: AWAY WITH FAKE POLITICIANS', '', 'publish', 'open', 'closed', '', 'nigeria-away-with-fake-politicians', '', '', '2019-12-17 12:59:49', '2019-12-17 12:59:49', '<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong><em>“If per chance you come across a human being who is\nunable to tell his particular bearing relatively to one or more of the\ninnumerable points of life’s compass, you would not hesitate to write him off\nas a child, an idiot, or an aimless wanderer.</em></strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong><em>“In the same way, if you meet a politician who is\nunwilling or unable to declare and, as precisely as possible, describe his\nposition in relation to the cardinal points of the political compass, you will\nfeel strongly tempted to denounce him as a fraud, a total misfit, or a hopeless\ndrifter.</em></strong><strong>”&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; </strong>- Obafemi Awolowo (1909-1987)&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; </p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>When it is said\nthat Nigeria is a backward country, as measured by economic and development\nindices, most people tend to compare tall skyscrapers in Manhattan District of\nNew York with those at the Central Business District in the Nigerian capital, Abuja,\nas indices of development. We think of spiraling inflation, heavy debt burden\nand increasing worthlessness of the Nigerian currency triggered by retired\nGeneral Ibrahim Babangida’s Structural Adjustment Programme (SAP) in 1986. We\nruminate over huge deficit in core economic infrastructure such as electric\npower as well as social infrastructural deficit in education and health. All\nthese result in shorter life span and overall low quality of life for Nigerians.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>But there is a more\ntelling dimension of backwardness which is much more invidious, yet less\namenable to public perception or social measurement. I refer to the moral\nstatus of the Nigerian elite, lack of commitment to stated goals of the society\nby the elite, lackadaisical attitude to duty and generally, warped thinking\nabout public affairs. </p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>Fake Human Beings</strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Most\npeople are aware of fake products - things that are not what they purports to\nbe. For example, if one buys salt from the market and uses it for his food but\nthe food tastes bland and unsalted, then it is obvious that the salt is fake\neven though it has the appearance of salt. Common examples of fake products in\nNigeria are fake wine, fake fruit drinks, fake cream, imitation jewelries and\nfake electronic devices. </p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>In\nAugust 2014, the Standard Organisation of Nigeria (SON) revealed that 80 per\ncent of computer, accessories and allied technology products and ICT devices sold\nat the largest computer market in Nigeria, Computer Village Market, Ikeja, were\ncounterfeit. According to the Managing Director of SON, Dr. Joseph Odumudu, <strong>“80% of their activities are illicit.”</strong>\nHe said that the activities of counterfeiters in the industry led to the\n“closure of many legitimate companies with their work force thrown into the\nlabour market”. (Prince Osuagwu, SON declares 80% of market fake, CAPDAN kicks,\nadmit 30%, Vanguard, August 13, 2014). Nigeria is a place where fake products\ndrive away genuine ones.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Nigeria\nis not only dominated by fake products, it is also peopled by fake human\nbeings. By fake human beings, I do not mean dolls or robots because these are\nnot human beings at all. Fake human beings as used here are people who pretend\nto be what they are not. Some persons assume positions but refuse to exhibit\nqualities and skills requisite for such positions.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>For example, some\nmarried women are merely women married to their men but not wives. To be a\nwife, a woman must play the role of a wife as defined by society and culture.\nIn such a case, a man has a woman who has children for him but not a wife. In\nthe same way, some married men deny their wives of love and care; they neglect\nor refuse to play the role of husband and may therefore be seen as married men\nand legal father of children but not husbands to their wives.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>A young person who\nregisters to study in an institution of learning but does not attend classes or\nengage in any form of learning is most likely a loafer, certainly not a student.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>To be brief,\neverything has a function and it is the fulfillment of such function that makes\nit what it claims to be. For considerations of space, let us\ntake four examples in the public space, namely, fake religionists, fake\nTraditional Rulers, fake security operatives and the father of all fakes - fake\npoliticians.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>Fake Religionists</strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Of\nall categories of fake human beings in contemporary Nigeria, the most common\ncategory is fake religionists that dot every nook and cranny of our villages\nand cities. They operate under the camouflage of Godliness and since most\nNigerians claim to be religious, fake religionists have free passage however dubious\ntheir activities may be. </p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Fake\nreligionists are not restricted to Nigeria, neither is it a new phenomenon in\nthe society. About 2,000 years ago, the Lord Jesus was constrained to drive\naway those who were buying and selling in the temple in Jerusalem and who made\nthe temple of God “a den of thieves” (Matthew 21:12-13). In our day, every Tom,\nDick and Harry claims to be a prophet, performing miracles at every corner and\non television, bombarding homes with varied exhortations. They measure their\nsuccess in terms of Naira, hard currency, expensive cars and jet planes.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Like their\ncounterparts of 2000 years ago, most of these are thieves, albeit of the more\nsubtle type that lawyers classify as confidence tricksters, “receiving under\nfalse pretence” which is a punishable act under Section 419 of the Criminal\nCode. Today, these impostors have evolved highly sophisticated techniques of\nplying their vicious trade through trendy dressing and fantastic claims on\nradio and television, coupled with a domineering attitude, all designed to\ncheat and exploit. </p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>I once asked\none of my friends why he made himself a Bishop soon after opening his church in\nBenin City. He told me that as a Bishop, he would receive special recognition\nor mention and sit at the high table or front row at any public event organized\nby the government. He said that he would ask the Governor to <strong>“kneel down, let me pray for you.”</strong> He\nsaid that as a Bishop, he has the privilege to lay his hands on the head of any\nprominent personality in the society, thereby gaining authority over them. It\nis the fastest way to social recognition and wealth, he asserted with gusto. He\ninvited me to take a high position in his church and I politely declined.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The prodigious\nand horrific spiritual harlotry by Nigerian potentates, spiritual and temporal,\nwho remorselessly use the name of God for lucre, is one of the saddest chapters\nin the history of this beleaguered country. The Nigerian situation exemplifies\nwhat Professor Isaac Sagay once described as <strong>“religious schizophrenia - outwardly religious but inwardly diabolical.”\n</strong>(Nigeria: Serving God and Mammon, Sunday Vanguard, December 17, 2000, page\n9). The Lord Jesus admonished us to “<strong>beware\nof false prophets, which come to you in sheep’s clothing, but inwardly, are\nravening wolves.” &nbsp;</strong>(Matthew 7:15)</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>Fake Traditional\nRulers</strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Another category of\nfakes that present in flowing robes and &nbsp;glamorous attires are called “Traditional\nRulers” or “Monarchs”. Monarchy is one of the oldest forms of government in this\npart of the world. Most Nigerian communities revered their kings. When British colonialists\ncame, they demoted indigenous kings to “chiefs” because they would not\ncontemplate any other king than the British crown. Therefore, to be relevant, a\nking must pander to the government and the practice continued since then. Kings\nlook to government for recognition and financial support rather than their\ncommunities. </p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The second factor that\ncontributed to disconnect between some kings and their people is the religion\nintroduced by the colonisers. Some kings think that to be modern, they must\ndemonise indigenous culture as the colonialists did. They deviate from\npromoting, projecting and defending indigenous cultural values while some of\nthem took on the business of active demolition of indigenous cultural heritage\nof their communities. The institution turned into a government appointment and kings\nglorify in government recognition and financial perquisites rather than\nsustenance of traditions of their communities. Consequently, the institution has\nbeen largely trivialized and commercialized if not bastardised, not belonging\nto tradition or government. </p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>There is no doubt\nthat some kings hold fast to the culture and traditions of their people and\neven improving on it. Notable examples are the Benin and Itsekiri kingdoms.\nHowever, the experience of many communities in Southern parts of Nigeria is\nthat their kings have abandoned their traditions and embraced alien cultures in\ntheir bid to appear “modern”.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Like fake products,\nthey adorn themselves with traditional attires but in their conduct and actions\nin their communities, they are anti-tradition. Tradition means that which is\nlaid down from the past. It does not mean church. It existed before colonialism\nand churchianity. No traditional ruler is installed on the basis of foreign\nreligion. But after coronation, some of them throw the tradition of their\npeople to the dustbin and embark on anti-tradition. Such kings are fakes; they\nmay be recognized as traditional rulers by the government but like unsalted\nsalt, they are fake.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>Fake Security\nOperatives</strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Security\nis an indispensable need in any society. That is probably why it is enshrined\nin Chapter ll, Section 14 (2) (b) of the Constitution of the Federal Republic\nof Nigeria, 1999, as amended that <strong>“security\nand welfare of the people shall be the primary purpose of government.”</strong> The same\nConstitution also made provision for establishment of agencies to ensure\nsecurity of life and property of citizens. These include the Nigeria Police\nForce, the Armed Forces and other security and para-military agencies.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>However,\nsome individuals in uniform such as Customs and Excise, Nigeria Police Force and\nArmed Forces use their positions contrary to that provided by the law that set\nthem up. There is nowhere that the law says that Nigerians citizens should be\nheld in detention without trial, held in underground cells or subjected to torture\nand other degrading and inhuman treatment but these are practices that come to\nlight from time to time in the country.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The\nmost rampant and open display of lawlessness by law enforcement officers in the\npublic domain is extortion. Large numbers of policemen line up at roads,\nparticularly in South-East of the country, extorting money from hapless\nmotorists while fully armed, pretending to be performing police duties. If they\nare collecting money from motorists by force of arms, are they not better\ndescribed as armed robbers than policemen? Is it part of police duties to line\nthe roads and forcefully collect money from motorists?</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>Fake Politicians</strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>In\nspite of several categories of fake human beings that undermine the moral\nvigour of the society, a few of which are enumerated above, the most socially\ndangerous fakes in Nigeria are fake politicians. This is because of the wide\ndemonstration effect of the behavior of this class of fakes. They lay down\nexamples, for good or for ill, for other sectors of the society</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The\nstock in trade of this category of fakes is violence and deception. How could\nsomeone who feeds young people with drugs and gives them guns to dislodge his\npolitical opponents be described as a politician? Such a person is a brigand of\nthe most horrible order. It would be a misnomer to address such a person as a\npolitician of the democratic breed.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Unfortunately,\nlike fake products, fake politicians drive legitimate and honest ones out of\ncirculation.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Worse\nstill, the violent tactics of military-political elite permeated the entire\nsocial fabric and produced an increasingly violent generation and glorifies\nviolence as a way of life.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>A True Public\nServant</strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>It\ngladdens the heart to realize that Nigeria is not entirely dominated by fakes. There\nare some shining lights that demonstrate noble traits that are the best in\nhumanity, even though they are few and far between.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Before\nProfessor Dora Akunyili (1954-2014) mounted her vigorous campaign against fake\nand counterfeit drugs in Nigeria in her capacity as Director-General of\nNational Agency for Food and Drug Administration and Control (NAFDC), fake and\ncounterfeit drugs dominated the Nigerian market. She took the bull by the horn.\nAccording to Akunyili, <strong>“People there\n(NAFDAC) did not believe that I could confront the mafia who had been\nunchallenged for almost three decades. It was a rude shock to the\ncounterfeiters that we refused any discussion or negotiation for business to\ncontinue as usual.” </strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:image {\"id\":409,\"sizeSlug\":\"large\"} -->\n<figure class=\"wp-block-image size-large\"><img src=\"https://boldtimes.net.ng-content/uploads/2019/12/images.jpg\" alt=\"\" class=\"wp-image-409\"/></figure>\n<!-- /wp:image -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>She\npersonally led raids to dislodge notorious drug counterfeiters in open drug\nmarkets in Onitsha, and other places and seized fake drugs worth billions of\nNaira.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Akunyili is from\nAnambra State and Onitsha in her home state is generally recognized as the\nheadquarters of fake and counterfeit drugs in Nigeria. She did not say, “they\nare my people, let me be soft on them.”</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>When the approach which\nthe counterfeiters made to her failed, they resorted to harassment and\nintimidation, thinking that as a woman, she would succumb to their antics. But\nshe was unyielding in pursuit of her assignment. In December 2003, she narrowly\nescaped death when assassins attacked her car near Agulu in Anambra State. The\nbullets shattered the rear windscreen of her car, pierced her headscarf and\nburnt her scarf <strong>“like hot water” </strong>and\nwent through the front screen. She was naturally shaken but would not bow to\nintimidation.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>In\nMarch 2008, she sealed off the Onitsha Head Bridge drug market completely when\nthe drug dealers were found to be intransigent and incorrigible. The market was\nonly reopened when the former Governor of the State, Mr. Peter Obi, personally\nintervened and assured her that he would help police the market very closely\nand rid it of dangerous drug dealers.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>When\nshe was appointed Minister of Information in December 2008, the Drug Dealers at\nOnitsha Head Bridge Market openly celebrated her departure from NAFDAC. They\nabandoned their stalls and paraded nearby streets, popping champagne and\ndancing that the iron lady was off their neck.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Akunyili\ntook her legendary distaste for underhand tactics to her new post. When highly\nplaced officials at the Presidency were playing hide and seek over the health\nstatus of late President Umaru Musa Yar’Adua, Akunyili was the odd man out,\nmaking her famous statement, <strong>“I am fed\nup with lies about Yar’Adua.”</strong> </p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Writing\nin May 2006, Annes Harding, a world-acclaimed writer on health and scientific\ntopics stated that <strong>“since she became\nDirector-General in 2001, the proportion of drugs sold in Nigeria that are fake\nhas dropped from 70% to 10%.”</strong> In its Bulletin Volume 84, No. 9, published\nin September 2006, titled <em>Nigeria leads\nfight against “killer” counterfeit drugs,</em> the World Health Organisation\n(WHO) declared that <strong>“Nigeria has been at\nthe forefront of global efforts to fight counterfeit drugs since Dora Akunyili\ntook over the NAFDAC in 2001.”</strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Akunyili\nis an outstanding example of a true Minister (servant) of the people of\nNigeria. </p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>Away with Fake\nPoliticians</strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The\nFederal Government and National Assembly make a big issue about “fake news” and\ntheir desire to legislate on it. But informed opinion is that preoccupation\nwith so called fake news is only an attempt to cover up the wide range of fakes\nin Nigerian public life, most of whom belong to the class of power elite.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Whatever\nthe resources available in a state, if the human beings occupying that space\nare fake, unwilling to perform their duties creditably or or morally debased,\nthe place is thereby debased. The effect is more crippling when the debased\nconstitute the ruling class. A morally and spiritually debased or fake ruling\nclass is the greatest curse any society may experience.\n\nNigeria cannot achieve\ndevelopment with fake religionists, fake kings, fake security officers and fake\npoliticians. Most people are afraid to speak out about fakes that predominate\nin the society because of their vindictiveness and the vicious manner in which\nthey deploy their power against perceived threats. However, for the health,\nsurvival and development of the country, all hands must be on deck to expose\nand dislodge fakes that are acting as worms eating down this potentially great\ncountry. It is time to do away with fakes in our body politic.\n\n\n\n</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->', 0, 'https://boldtimes.com.ng/?p=408', 0, 'post', '', 1),
(409, 1, '2019-12-17 12:57:01', '2019-12-17 12:57:01', '', 'images', '', 'inherit', 'open', 'closed', '', 'images', '', '', '2019-12-17 12:57:01', '2019-12-17 12:57:01', '', 408, 'http://boldtimes.com.ng-content/uploads/2019/12/images.jpg', 0, 'attachment', 'image/jpeg', 0);
INSERT INTO `wp5g_posts` (`ID`, `post_author`, `post_date`, `post_date_gmt`, `post_content`, `post_title`, `post_excerpt`, `post_status`, `comment_status`, `ping_status`, `post_password`, `post_name`, `to_ping`, `pinged`, `post_modified`, `post_modified_gmt`, `post_content_filtered`, `post_parent`, `guid`, `menu_order`, `post_type`, `post_mime_type`, `comment_count`) VALUES
(416, 1, '2020-05-25 10:50:42', '2020-05-25 10:50:42', '<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>First published inANIOMA VOICE,Vol. 1, No. 2, August 29-September 4, 2006, page 16</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Undoubtedly, one of the most visible policy thrusts of the Obansanjo administration is his anti-corruption crusade. The initial focus of the crusade was the military looters and their bureaucratic collaborators. Due to the staggering and mind-boggling size of these loots, public attention was understandably arrested.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>This short piece, however, explores one of the stupefying dimensions of moral cracks in several less visible but significant facets of the Nigerian society. I refer to an aspect of the social system where fraud is least expected - the educational system.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Like other developing countries of Africa, the Nigerian educational system is confronted with numerous problems. These include infrastructural decay, inadequate conditions of service for teachers and resultant teachers’ apathy, youth violence, cultism and psychedelic culture. Most students prefer to lay about, averse and indifferent to learning. Parents fail to buy books while governments fail to provide basic infrastructure such as classrooms, chairs, desks, register, and chalk.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>Dimensions of the problem</strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>For samplers, let us take a few facts from primary schools, examination bodies and tertiary institutions.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>For up to 50 percent of primary school leavers, the primary school system fails to achieve the objectives of inculcating in them, basic literacy and numeracy. Inability to read at the end of primary school course is a great source of frustration in secondary and tertiary schools. Such students therefore seek deviant means of achieving academic success.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>In the past decade, secondary school examination bodies in Nigeria have announced the cancellation of over one million results. In the process of cancellation of results of examination cheats, some innocent students inevitably suffer.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>According to the Pro-Chancellor of the University of Lagos, Chief Afe Babalola, SAN, students, teachers, invigilators and school proprietors collude to cheat during examinations conducted by Joint Admissions and Matriculations Board (JAMB). The result, said Chief Babalola, was that dullards scored 300 and above while brilliant, hardworking student hover around 250. Chief Babalola said that during post-JAMB screening at the University of Lagos, almost all students whom scored 280 and above failed to show up for the test.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>In April and May 2001, the authorities of Ambrose Alli University, Ekpoma, Edo State, identified over 10,000 students who were found to be unqualified to be students in that University.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>In all probability, these undergraduates did not start their academic fraud in their various universities. They had their apprenticeship at lower levels of educational ladder. Therefore, any realistic and systematic analysis of examination malpractice must look further to unravel the genesis of the problem.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Let us consider for a moment the vast array of human and material resources devoted to education by government, voluntary agencies and parents. Consider fees paid by students, salaries paid to teachers to teach them. Consider infrastructure and other resources wasted. It is disheartening to contemplate that all these could translate into one huge and grand social deceit.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>What are we saying? The educational system and policies tend to replicate within its structure and administration, social values, political structures and trends. Therefore, it would have been a great miracle if the educational system had been devoid of the massive force and fraud, which characterized the political structures in Nigeria. A certificate fails to certify what it portends when it is acquired through examination malpractice.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>A voice in the wilderness</strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>In spite of the degenerate social system, it is heartwarming to observe a growing number of high government officials who are joining the crusade for transparency, justice and discipline in our national life. All patriots, well-meaning citizens and organizations should maintain keen and abiding interest in the efficient administration of the state and advancement of the well-being of citizens.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>In this day, which is characterized by grave decline in integrity among public office holders, when leadership is best known for its evil influence on the lives of citizens, an age that breeds villains rather than heroes, patriots must take it upon themselves to identify and encourage any voice of courage in the wilderness of misrule. Much as we condemn evil without reservation, it would be a great injustice if we fail to recognize meritorious service and give honor to whom it is due.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Over the past few years, the Delta State Commissioner for Primary and Secondary Education, Dr. Veronica Ogbuagu made bold and determined efforts to bring some measures of discipline into the Delta State education system and restore sanity to schools.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>She started by tackling the twin problem of trading inside the classroom and truancy among teachers both in primary and secondary schools.&nbsp; Without doubt, the primary school is the foundation of the educational system. Whatever affects the foundation inevitably affects the entire structure.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Dr. Ogbuagu successfully brought an end to the practice whereby some teachers brought such items as bangles, trinkets, pants,<em> egusi</em> (melon) and<em> agbono</em> &nbsp;<em>(irvingia gabonensis)</em> nuts, also known as African mango, for sale in the school.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>She was alarmed that some teachers found time to make regular trips to Dubai, Cotonu, Onitsha and other international markets to carry out their business while remaining on the pay roll of the Unified Teaching Service of Delta State. The problem of widespread truancy among teachers was particularly troubling to this notable educationist.&nbsp; But Ogbuagu did not merely bemoan these ugly practices. She took bold steps to stem them.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>She embarked on sporadic and unscheduled visits to schools, including those located in the most out of the way places, and was able to ‘catch’ a large number of these truants red-handed. <strong>“Take for example”,</strong> lamented Ogbuagu, <strong>“a school that has a staff of 46, you found only 10 on their duty post.”</strong> (The Pointer, December 16, 2003, page 2). Her sporadic visits to schools served to deter most of the errant teachers in these respects.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Teachers who were rarely present in class to teach could not be expected to cover the syllabus for the year. And how would teachers expect students to pass examinations set on the basis of topics in syllabuses that were never taught? In such a situation, both students and teachers had a vested interest in examination malpractice.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Dr. Ogbuagu was not satisfied merely to mouth her disapproval of examination malpractice. She went ahead to take firm action.  She ensured that questions were set on the approved scheme for each category of students. Then teachers from different schools swapped in invigilating examinations.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>                                         </p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:image {\"id\":421,\"sizeSlug\":\"medium\"} -->\n<figure class=\"wp-block-image size-medium\"><img src=\"https://boldtimes.net.ng-content/uploads/2020/05/VERO-1-300x180.jpg\" alt=\"\" class=\"wp-image-421\"/></figure>\n<!-- /wp:image -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Dr. Veronica Ogbuagu</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Former Commissioner for Education, Delta State</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>There was hue and cry among corrupt teachers and some school administrators, these agents of destruction of early lives of our children. This was not surprising since we live in a culture of bad examples where most people are addicted to complacency. In the situation, people are tempted to condemn the lone good example as aberrant, even deviant. But Dr. Ogbuagu was unyielding on the path of discipline and probity.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>To discourage teachers from awarding marks to children for topics not taught, she went ahead to ensure that teachers did not mark scripts of their own class children. Teachers from other schools marked scripts, based on the marking scheme.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Finally, she set up examination marshals, complete with uniforms and organized workshops and seminars to enable them effectively monitor the implementation of her examination ethics programme.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>Heroes of the 4<sup>th</sup> Republic&nbsp;</strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>We will get a glimpse of the extent of Dr. Ogbuagu’s contribution to the building of a sane Nigerian society if we realize that the school is a training ground, not only of knowledge and skills, but more importantly, of values and attitudes.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>It is clear from recent Nigerian history that Dollars and Naira cannot save our country from further decay. We must now be diffident of the efficacy of raw political power to ensure a just and progressive society. Even religion as panacea to human problem has failed as advocate of <strong>“political sharia”</strong> (apologies to President Olusegun Obasanjo), have made plain for all to see. &nbsp;But the right kind of education will certainly go a long way.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Dr. Ogbuagu has joined the league of dedicated public office holders,&nbsp; apostles of transparency and anti-corruption crusaders in the mold of the famous Professor Dora Akunyili of NAFDAC, Alhaji Nuhu Ribadu of EFCC, Mallam Nasir el-Rufai, Minister of Federal Capital Territory and Dr. Mrs. Ngozi Okonjo-Iweala, former Minister of Finance. These are truly leading lights of a new Nigeria. These are the heroes of the 4<sup>th</sup> Republic.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Before Dr. Ogbuagu’s appointment, most teachers actively contributed to the destruction of valued social standards. Today, the Delta State school system is waking up to join the civilized world in making our schools truly places to prepare our children for a greater tomorrow. Unfortunately, there is as yet no respite in this respect in many departments of the Delta State Public Service.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The importance of citing the case of Dr. Ogbuagu is that Nigeria and indeed Delta State need more of such personalities who would say a decisive “no” to indiscipline and fraudulent machinery in any arm of government.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>In spite of brain and brawn, in spite of inestimable resources both human and material, in spite of petro-dollars, as long as Nigeria remains a moral cripple, there is no way it can take a bold leap into greatness.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>All said, the future of Nigeria is at stake in what goes on in our primary and secondary schools across the country today.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->', 'ONE GOOD EXAMPLE IN DELTA STATE', '', 'publish', 'open', 'closed', '', 'one-good-example-in-delta-state', '', '', '2020-05-25 10:50:46', '2020-05-25 10:50:46', '<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>First published inANIOMA VOICE,Vol. 1, No. 2, August 29-September 4, 2006, page 16</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Undoubtedly, one of the most visible policy thrusts of the Obansanjo administration is his anti-corruption crusade. The initial focus of the crusade was the military looters and their bureaucratic collaborators. Due to the staggering and mind-boggling size of these loots, public attention was understandably arrested.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>This short piece, however, explores one of the stupefying dimensions of moral cracks in several less visible but significant facets of the Nigerian society. I refer to an aspect of the social system where fraud is least expected - the educational system.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Like other developing countries of Africa, the Nigerian educational system is confronted with numerous problems. These include infrastructural decay, inadequate conditions of service for teachers and resultant teachers’ apathy, youth violence, cultism and psychedelic culture. Most students prefer to lay about, averse and indifferent to learning. Parents fail to buy books while governments fail to provide basic infrastructure such as classrooms, chairs, desks, regi