(917, 1, '2020-12-18 07:58:45', '2020-12-18 07:58:45', '<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>By Chris O.O. Biose</strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>“In the beginning of a malady, it is easy to cure but difficult to detect but in the course of time, not having been either detected or treated in the beginning, it becomes easy to detect but difficult to cure.”</strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:list -->\n<ul><li>&nbsp;Niccolo Machiavelli (1469-1527)</li></ul>\n<!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Nigeria is only mildly affected by the deadly Covid-19 pandemic relative to many other countries of the world. It is however afflicted by an equally invisible pandemic of frightening proportions. This unrecognized social virus has to do with the climate of moral turpitude enveloping all classes of the Nigerian society, particularly the elite.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>It is absolutely necessary to understand the damaging effect of this disease because without that recognition, citizens cannot even begin to fight it.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>This disease is infinitely higher in its incidence and destructive capacity than Covid19 pandemic.&nbsp; It manifests in socially disruptive behavior that tears down the social fabric. It eats away or undermines the foundation of a peaceful, organized, sane, civilized and progressive society. From small matters such as obeying traffic rules to real big public issue of security of life and property, it promotes anti-social conduct that stunts development.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>Increasing Lawlessness</strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Perhaps, the most visible effect of the social virus identified above is increasing lawlessness that pervades the entire spectrum of the Nigerian society. In the Year of Our Lord 2020, the most populous Black Country on earth is in total disarray due to habitual disregard of law by its ruling elite. Powerful men behave as if the law does not exist. Where then do rulers expect followers to get good example? As the German-born French polymath, theologian, writer, humanitarian philosopher and physician, Dr Albert Schweitzer (1875-1965), who also won the 1952 Nobel Peace Prize for his Philosophy of Relevance, said: <strong>“Example is not the main thing in influencing others, it is the only thing.”</strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:gallery {\"ids\":[923],\"linkTo\":\"none\"} -->\n<figure class=\"wp-block-gallery columns-1 is-cropped\"><ul class=\"blocks-gallery-grid\"><li class=\"blocks-gallery-item\"><figure><img src=\"https://boldtimes.net.ng-content/uploads/2020/12/2658562-1024x709-2.jpg\" alt=\"\" data-id=\"923\" data-full-url=\"https://boldtimes.net.ng-content/uploads/2020/12/2658562-1024x709-2.jpg\" data-link=\"https://boldtimes.net.ng/moral-morbidity-of-nigerian-elite/2658562-1024x709-2/\" class=\"wp-image-923\"/><figcaption class=\"blocks-gallery-item__caption\"> Dr. Albert Schweitzer, a renowned medical missionary</figcaption></figure></li></ul></figure>\n<!-- /wp:gallery -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Disorder has become the rule rather than the exception on Nigerian roads. The irony is that law-enforcement operatives are prime culprits. They are above the Road Traffic Law of the country. On frequent occasions when there is a minor hitch in flow of traffic, most vehicle drivers stay on the queue. Suddenly, one sees a military, police or VIP vehicle with armed escort drive furiousiy on the wrong lane to beat the traffic. Then immediately, commercial vehicle drivers and other “smart” drivers follow the example of the powerful men. Shortly thereafter, there is traffic chaos as both lanes of the roads are blocked by those who want <strong>“to be like”</strong> the men in power. In Nigeria, to be lawless is to be powerful. The issue then is: when fish rots from its head, it is futile to expect the body to survive.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>Filthy and Noisy Environment</strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Another easily observable consequence of the social virus afflicting Nigeria is that citizens are overwhelmed with filth and noise. Why is it impossible to have a clean and healthy environment in the <strong>“Giant of Africa”</strong>? Local Government Councils routinely ignore constitutional roles assigned to them under the FOURTH SCHEDULE of&nbsp;&nbsp; Decree 24, 1999, particularly Section (h) on <strong>“provision and maintenance of public conveniences, sewages and refuse disposal”. </strong>Nigeria is said to hold the gold medal for open defecation in the world. Local Government Councils, 774 of them, exist for the purpose of sharing money from proceeds of sale of oil and gas from the Niger Delta and that is exactly what they do.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>A Nigerian city is defined not only by filth but also by noise. It is a place where someone could suddenly fix microphones by the roadside, block the road altogether forcing road users to seek alternative routes. One frequently sees youths loaded like cattle on trucks that are fitted with loudspeakers, disturbing the entire social space with loud noise. Nobody dares to complain especially if those causing the nuisance are calling on the Holy names of God.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Soon, marketers of fake and counterfeit drugs start imitating the noisy men of God, blaring cacophony of noises in city centres and neighbourhoods. Nigeria is a cauldron of unbearable noise. There is no control, no order, nobody is in charge.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Most State Governors are not bothered about traffic chaos, noise pollution or filthy condition of their state capitals because it does not affect their capacity to share money. There is nothing to bring Nigerian governors together or tears them apart other than sharing proceeds of sale of oil and gas from the Niger Delta. The same goes for National Assembly members. Other than sharing of money, everything else is insignificant.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>Increasing Poverty</strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Worse still, following intensified liberalization, deregulation and commercialisation policy of the administration, most people find that however hard one works, unless one cheats in one form or another, one can scarcely taste “success” in the Nigerian sense of unearned wealth.&nbsp; Government policy essentially serves to engender mass penury, pauperization of the professional class and artisans, massive unemployment and under employment, accentuating fraudulent tendencies, looting of public resources and criminal predations at all levels.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>In consequence of all the above, people are disillusioned and despondent.&nbsp; Most young people not only lose hope in the country, they lose faith in themselves.&nbsp; Hence, the greatest ambition of most Nigerian youths today is to escape from the huge system of deceit and suffering called Nigeria.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>Perverse Role of Security Operatives</strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Aside from issues of traffic, filth, noise and poverty, most Nigerians are under constant threat to their lives and property. Insecurity is rising by leaps and bounds in the country mainly because of the greedy attitude and extortionist tactics of law enforcement operatives who want to be rich “like” politicians that they guard. Since they do not have opportunity to award huge contracts and obtain heavy cuts like Council chairmen and Governors, they use what they have (their guns) to get what they want. For this reason, most crimes against commoners are not reported because citizens do not trust the Nigeria Police to act on credible complaints without inducement.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>By Section 4 of the Police Act Cap P19 Laws of the Federation, 2004, the Nigeria Police is charged with <strong>“prevention and detection of crime, the apprehension of offenders, the preservation of law and order, the protection of life and property and the due enforcement of all laws and regulations with which they are directly charged, and shall perform such military duties within or outside Nigeria as may be required of them by, or under the authority of, this or any other Act.”</strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>However, The Human Rights Violation Investigation Commission of Nigeria, popularly referred to as Oputa Panel Report, submitted to former President Olusegun Obasanjo in June 2002, Volume 7 (Summary, Conclusions and Recommendations, para 50 and 56) states that <strong>“Nigerians see the Police, not as a friend but as offenders and agents of human rights violations in the country…constituting a vital link in the chain of conspiracy against justice in many parts of the country.”&nbsp; </strong>The same Panel Report describes the Directorate of Military Intelligence (DMI) as a <strong>“gross violator of human rights”</strong>.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>If only men of the Nigeria police do their work without extortion, brutality and extra-judicial executions, they would be the darling of the Nigerian public.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Nigerians suffer in silence under their Police Force because of collusion of the authorities.&nbsp; It took #EndSARS protests for hundreds of victims of police brutality in the country to start coming out in trickles to make complaints at Judicial Tribunals set up in several states in the country. Prior to this, government successfully suppressed information about police brutality in the country.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Hundreds of Nigerians are killed regularly by state and non-state actors while security agencies claim that they are doing their best.The Federal Government claims that it <strong>“technically defeated</strong>” the Islamist terrorist sect, Boko Haram, six years ago, yet the sect continues to slaughter Nigerians in their numbers on a regular basis.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Security chiefs in the country often self-adulate that they are <strong>“on top of the situation”</strong> regarding myriad of security challenges in the country. However, by virtue of failure of security, Nigeria ranks as one of the most dangerous and unsafe countries in the world.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The legendary porosity of Nigeria’s borders with neighbouring countries speaks volumes of the failure of entire security apparatus –Army, Navy, Air Force, Police, Immigration, Customs, and over 20 other security agencies of the country. Where is the wisdom of a great man who fails to fix doors in his house and yet believes his household to be secure?</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>Deception in High Places</strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Nigerian leaders deceive themselves and citizens in matters both big and small. Neither the constitution of the country nor its name reflects its reality.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Nigeria does not have a Constitution made by citizens or their Representatives. The country operates a Military Decree (Decree 24, 1999) signed by its last military dictator on 5<sup>th</sup> May, 1999 as its Constitution. The document was never subjected to referendum for validation or adoption by the people. Neither the President nor National Assembly is prepared to even commence the process of enabling citizens to give themselves a genuine Constitution as long as the leaders enjoy the perquisites of their offices.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The country goes the name “Federal Republic” but it is run as a unitary autocracy where power flows from one man and federating units can do next to nothing without approval by the central power. This power dominance by one man is apparent from the fact that any policy at any level of government that is not approved by the President will not see the light of day. This is the foundation of failure of security in the country as the Federal Government insists on monopoly of policing and frustrates any suggestion for establishment of police forces by states. &nbsp;&nbsp;</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Illustrating one seemingly trivial but fundamental aspect of the flawed Nigerian version of federalism, former Governor of Rivers State, Mr Rotimi Chibuike Amaechi pointed to a ridiculous situation whereby staff employed by state governments are under fiscal control of the Federal Government. Former Governor Amaechi asked rhetorically,<strong> “How can I employ workers, yet it is FG that will fix salaries for the workers. I have never seen a federal system where this is obtainable, except here.”</strong> (Charles Kumolu, Constitution Amendment: N/Assembly moves to restructure Nigeria, SUNDAY Vanguard, January 13, 2013, p 50-51)</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Selfish and unpatriotic labour leaders who are only interested in check-off dues which governments collect for them from workers are solidly in support of the anomalous federal structure. They are not interested in a workable federal system that will release federating units from the stranglehold of a central Leviathan.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Furthermore, Nigeria is a funny type of Republic where religious rulers bear sway. Chapter 1, Part ll, Section 19 of Decree 24, 1999, (Prohibition of State Religion) states that <strong>“the government of the Federation or of a State shall not adopt any religion as State Religion.”</strong> The same Military Decree then goes on to provide for religious courts in Chapter Vll, Section E (The Sharia Court of Appeal of the Federal Capital Territory, Abuja) and Part ll, Section B (Sharia Court of Appeal of a State). The leaders pretend to be unaware of the stark contradictions between Republicanism and Theocracy.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>Reign of Predators</strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Nigeria is a place where an ethno-religious class of feudalists successfully recruited support from selfish elite from several ethnic groups and sectors of the country to establish a class of political and economic predators. This class includes politicians, top&nbsp; public servants, big businessmen, traditional rulers and indeed all those perceived as <strong>“government men”</strong> enjoy immunity from interference in their nefarious activities by law enforcement agents.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The pervasive presence of this class gave rise to a situation where the reign of the predators is accepted asnormal. One cannot understand the Nigerian society unless one understands the social-psychological process whereby criminality is perceived as heroism. The elite are drunk with power and money. Like drug use, the addict considers himself a hero for doing what others do not do. They act the gods and pretend to godly power.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The lascivious criminal performs criminal acts in a lustful manner and defines criminality positively. The Nigerian power elite actually celebrate and glamorize criminality. In the face of acts that threaten the general good; they band together in solidarity.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>It is within this context of high level indoctrination in attitudes of disrespect for law that juvenile deviance and law violation by adults become the norm. Mischief making is a pernicious but socially accepted game. Hence, there are no villains in the political hierarchy of any party in power in Nigeria. What would be ranked in conventional society as more audacious criminal escapades in high places only makes a Nigerian elite a greater hero in government circles.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Stubborn ruling elite has dug in deeply in the country and crushes sources of protest by ruthless official and unofficial use of force. Citizens refuse to give their loyalty to the rule of force. There is suppressed tension and ceaseless tension as people live in constant fear of men in power. This presents an extremely dangerous state of affairs, leading to chaotic scenes constantly breaking out at most unexpected times and places.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Nigerians often condemn the phenomenon of gangs of youths otherwise called <strong>‘area boys’</strong> or <strong>“hoodlums”</strong> who organize to grab what does not belong to them from unsuspecting members of the public. Why then do the same citizens fail to condemn in equally categorical terms, the real big grabbers that promote the worst instincts of human nature? The political, economic and traditional area boys that institute a reign of terror and a cloud of fear on the entire populace are accepted as leaders!</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Decree 24, 1999, by which Nigeria is ruled creates authoritarian and suffocating political, administrative and economic structures which have consistently generated social tension in the country. Yet, political leaders are insensitive enough to insist on the status quo. They pay deaf ears to wise counsel of several well-informed and patriotic Nigerians. For example, legal icon, Afe Babalola, SAN, recently counseled:</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>“The only change that can change the country and pave way for nationhood is the change that changes the structure of project Nigeria. No amount of sermon from the pulpit can change the country.” </strong>(Aare Afe Babalola, Nigeria in search of a nation: Excerpts from author’s address to Yourba Summit (2), Vanguard, September 20, 2017, p 40).</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Leaders lack moral fibre and patriotism to commence the process of building a nation-state out of the conglomeration of ethnic groups that make up the country. Instead of nationalism and deepening democracy, they engage in fighting over “zoning”, euphemism for sharing high political positions.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>Nigerians Take Cue from Their Worst</strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Unlike Covid-19, which is transmitted by close contact with an infected person, lawless conduct is transmitted by merely seeing a person doing a wrong thing, hearing or reading of criminals who became wealthy. Moral morbidity is therefore infinitely more infectious than the deadly Covid-19.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Indeed, the hurtful behavious patternof the Nigerian elite lays eggs aplenty. For example, a government that is founded on electoral violence or fraud and sustained by political manipulation and corrupt influence can only reap violence and fraud together with like profit.&nbsp; Things beget their kind. It is therefore no surprise that governments established by violence and fraud beget even greater violence and fraud.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Once an evil person gains access to political power in a local government area, state or the country, he dominates the environment with bad examples. Everyone comes to accept foul means as a standard of conduct.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Those who refuse to deploy violence and fraud to win elections or who insist on living according to their legitimate earnings are mocked as<strong> “mumu”</strong> or <strong>“suffer-head”.</strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>When one conjoins the social philosophy of unmitigated greed with total absence of any iota of loyalty to nation, then one understands the crudest form of banditry by officials of state, unrivalled in the annals of man. The level of greed is out of hand. In the bureaucracy, politics, professions, business houses, educational institutions, religious bodies, traditional institutions, even in the underworld of big time crime. The super dogs are bleeding the under dogs. Nigeria is one huge theatre of the war of all against all.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>In democratic societies, politics and economics are instruments of development. In Nigeria, they are instruments for impoverishing the masses. This is the connection between a perverse form of political power that destroys the economy, aborts development and alienates citizens who then view government as its enemy rather than an embodiment of its wishes and aspirations.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>At the present time, thereisno social force, strong enough to neutralize the debilitating influences of the deadly social virus that afflicts Nigeria. The development of the country is stunted by the moral morbidity of its elite.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->', '<strong>MORAL MORBIDITY </strong><strong>OF NIGERIAN ELITE</strong>', '', 'publish', 'open', 'closed', '', 'moral-morbidity-of-nigerian-elite', '', '', '2020-12-18 08:21:50', '2020-12-18 08:21:50', '<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>By Chris O.O. Biose</strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>“In the beginning of a malady, it is easy to cure but difficult to detect but in the course of time, not having been either detected or treated in the beginning, it becomes easy to detect but difficult to cure.”</strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:list -->\n<ul><li>&nbsp;Niccolo Machiavelli (1469-1527)</li></ul>\n<!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Nigeria is only mildly affected by the deadly Covid-19 pandemic relative to many other countries of the world. It is however afflicted by an equally invisible pandemic of frightening proportions. This unrecognized social virus has to do with the climate of moral turpitude enveloping all classes of the Nigerian society, particularly the elite.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>It is absolutely necessary to understand the damaging effect of this disease because without that recognition, citizens cannot even begin to fight it.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>This disease is infinitely higher in its incidence and destructive capacity than Covid19 pandemic.&nbsp; It manifests in socially disruptive behavior that tears down the social fabric. It eats away or undermines the foundation of a peaceful, organized, sane, civilized and progressive society. From small matters such as obeying traffic rules to real big public issue of security of life and property, it promotes anti-social conduct that stunts development.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>Increasing Lawlessness</strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Perhaps, the most visible effect of the social virus identified above is increasing lawlessness that pervades the entire spectrum of the Nigerian society. In the Year of Our Lord 2020, the most populous Black Country on earth is in total disarray due to habitual disregard of law by its ruling elite. Powerful men behave as if the law does not exist. Where then do rulers expect followers to get good example? As the German-born French polymath, theologian, writer, humanitarian philosopher and physician, Dr Albert Schweitzer (1875-1965), who also won the 1952 Nobel Peace Prize for his Philosophy of Relevance, said: <strong>“Example is not the main thing in influencing others, it is the only thing.”</strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:gallery {\"ids\":[923],\"linkTo\":\"none\"} -->\n<figure class=\"wp-block-gallery columns-1 is-cropped\"><ul class=\"blocks-gallery-grid\"><li class=\"blocks-gallery-item\"><figure><img src=\"https://boldtimes.net.ng-content/uploads/2020/12/2658562-1024x709-2.jpg\" alt=\"\" data-id=\"923\" data-full-url=\"https://boldtimes.net.ng-content/uploads/2020/12/2658562-1024x709-2.jpg\" data-link=\"https://boldtimes.net.ng/moral-morbidity-of-nigerian-elite/2658562-1024x709-2/\" class=\"wp-image-923\"/><figcaption class=\"blocks-gallery-item__caption\"> Dr. Albert Schweitzer, a renowned medical missionary</figcaption></figure></li></ul></figure>\n<!-- /wp:gallery -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Disorder has become the rule rather than the exception on Nigerian roads. The irony is that law-enforcement operatives are prime culprits. They are above the Road Traffic Law of the country. On frequent occasions when there is a minor hitch in flow of traffic, most vehicle drivers stay on the queue. Suddenly, one sees a military, police or VIP vehicle with armed escort drive furiousiy on the wrong lane to beat the traffic. Then immediately, commercial vehicle drivers and other “smart” drivers follow the example of the powerful men. Shortly thereafter, there is traffic chaos as both lanes of the roads are blocked by those who want <strong>“to be like”</strong> the men in power. In Nigeria, to be lawless is to be powerful. The issue then is: when fish rots from its head, it is futile to expect the body to survive.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>Filthy and Noisy Environment</strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Another easily observable consequence of the social virus afflicting Nigeria is that citizens are overwhelmed with filth and noise. Why is it impossible to have a clean and healthy environment in the <strong>“Giant of Africa”</strong>? Local Government Councils routinely ignore constitutional roles assigned to them under the FOURTH SCHEDULE of&nbsp;&nbsp; Decree 24, 1999, particularly Section (h) on <strong>“provision and maintenance of public conveniences, sewages and refuse disposal”. </strong>Nigeria is said to hold the gold medal for open defecation in the world. Local Government Councils, 774 of them, exist for the purpose of sharing money from proceeds of sale of oil and gas from the Niger Delta and that is exactly what they do.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>A Nigerian city is defined not only by filth but also by noise. It is a place where someone could suddenly fix microphones by the roadside, block the road altogether forcing road users to seek alternative routes. One frequently sees youths loaded like cattle on trucks that are fitted with loudspeakers, disturbing the entire social space with loud noise. Nobody dares to complain especially if those causing the nuisance are calling on the Holy names of God.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Soon, marketers of fake and counterfeit drugs start imitating the noisy men of God, blaring cacophony of noises in city centres and neighbourhoods. Nigeria is a cauldron of unbearable noise. There is no control, no order, nobody is in charge.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Most State Governors are not bothered about traffic chaos, noise pollution or filthy condition of their state capitals because it does not affect their capacity to share money. There is nothing to bring Nigerian governors together or tears them apart other than sharing proceeds of sale of oil and gas from the Niger Delta. The same goes for National Assembly members. Other than sharing of money, everything else is insignificant.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>Increasing Poverty</strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Worse still, following intensified liberalization, deregulation and commercialisation policy of the administration, most people find that however hard one works, unless one cheats in one form or another, one can scarcely taste “success” in the Nigerian sense of unearned wealth.&nbsp; Government policy essentially serves to engender mass penury, pauperization of the professional class and artisans, massive unemployment and under employment, accentuating fraudulent tendencies, looting of public resources and criminal predations at all levels.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>In consequence of all the above, people are disillusioned and despondent.&nbsp; Most young people not only lose hope in the country, they lose faith in themselves.&nbsp; Hence, the greatest ambition of most Nigerian youths today is to escape from the huge system of deceit and suffering called Nigeria.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>Perverse Role of Security Operatives</strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Aside from issues of traffic, filth, noise and poverty, most Nigerians are under constant threat to their lives and property. Insecurity is rising by leaps and bounds in the country mainly because of the greedy attitude and extortionist tactics of law enforcement operatives who want to be rich “like” politicians that they guard. Since they do not have opportunity to award huge contracts and obtain heavy cuts like Council chairmen and Governors, they use what they have (their guns) to get what they want. For this reason, most crimes against commoners are not reported because citizens do not trust the Nigeria Police to act on credible complaints without inducement.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>By Section 4 of the Police Act Cap P19 Laws of the Federation, 2004, the Nigeria Police is charged with <strong>“prevention and detection of crime, the apprehension of offenders, the preservation of law and order, the protection of life and property and the due enforcement of all laws and regulations with which they are directly charged, and shall perform such military duties within or outside Nigeria as may be required of them by, or under the authority of, this or any other Act.”</strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>However, The Human Rights Violation Investigation Commission of Nigeria, popularly referred to as Oputa Panel Report, submitted to former President Olusegun Obasanjo in June 2002, Volume 7 (Summary, Conclusions and Recommendations, para 50 and 56) states that <strong>“Nigerians see the Police, not as a friend but as offenders and agents of human rights violations in the country…constituting a vital link in the chain of conspiracy against justice in many parts of the country.”&nbsp; </strong>The same Panel Report describes the Directorate of Military Intelligence (DMI) as a <strong>“gross violator of human rights”</strong>.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>If only men of the Nigeria police do their work without extortion, brutality and extra-judicial executions, they would be the darling of the Nigerian public.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Nigerians suffer in silence under their Police Force because of collusion of the authorities.&nbsp; It took #EndSARS protests for hundreds of victims of police brutality in the country to start coming out in trickles to make complaints at Judicial Tribunals set up in several states in the country. Prior to this, government successfully suppressed information about police brutality in the country.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Hundreds of Nigerians are killed regularly by state and non-state actors while security agencies claim that they are doing their best.The Federal Government claims that it <strong>“technically defeated</strong>” the Islamist terrorist sect, Boko Haram, six years ago, yet the sect continues to slaughter Nigerians in their numbers on a regular basis.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Security chiefs in the country often self-adulate that they are <strong>“on top of the situation”</strong> regarding myriad of security challenges in the country. However, by virtue of failure of security, Nigeria ranks as one of the most dangerous and unsafe countries in the world.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The legendary porosity of Nigeria’s borders with neighbouring countries speaks volumes of the failure of entire security apparatus –Army, Navy, Air Force, Police, Immigration, Customs, and over 20 other security agencies of the country. Where is the wisdom of a great man who fails to fix doors in his house and yet believes his household to be secure?</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>Deception in High Places</strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Nigerian leaders deceive themselves and citizens in matters both big and small. Neither the constitution of the country nor its name reflects its reality.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Nigeria does not have a Constitution made by citizens or their Representatives. The country operates a Military Decree (Decree 24, 1999) signed by its last military dictator on 5<sup>th</sup> May, 1999 as its Constitution. The document was never subjected to referendum for validation or adoption by the people. Neither the President nor National Assembly is prepared to even commence the process of enabling citizens to give themselves a genuine Constitution as long as the leaders enjoy the perquisites of their offices.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The country goes the name “Federal Republic” but it is run as a unitary autocracy where power flows from one man and federating units can do next to nothing without approval by the central power. This power dominance by one man is apparent from the fact that any policy at any level of government that is not approved by the President will not see the light of day. This is the foundation of failure of security in the country as the Federal Government insists on monopoly of policing and frustrates any suggestion for establishment of police forces by states. &nbsp;&nbsp;</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Illustrating one seemingly trivial but fundamental aspect of the flawed Nigerian version of federalism, former Governor of Rivers State, Mr Rotimi Chibuike Amaechi pointed to a ridiculous situation whereby staff employed by state governments are under fiscal control of the Federal Government. Former Governor Amaechi asked rhetorically,<strong> “How can I employ workers, yet it is FG that will fix salaries for the workers. I have never seen a federal system where this is obtainable, except here.”</strong> (Charles Kumolu, Constitution Amendment: N/Assembly moves to restructure Nigeria, SUNDAY Vanguard, January 13, 2013, p 50-51)</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Selfish and unpatriotic labour leaders who are only interested in check-off dues which governments collect for them from workers are solidly in support of the anomalous federal structure. They are not interested in a workable federal system that will release federating units from the stranglehold of a central Leviathan.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Furthermore, Nigeria is a funny type of Republic where religious rulers bear sway. Chapter 1, Part ll, Section 19 of Decree 24, 1999, (Prohibition of State Religion) states that <strong>“the government of the Federation or of a State shall not adopt any religion as State Religion.”</strong> The same Military Decree then goes on to provide for religious courts in Chapter Vll, Section E (The Sharia Court of Appeal of the Federal Capital Territory, Abuja) and Part ll, Section B (Sharia Court of Appeal of a State). The leaders pretend to be unaware of the stark contradictions between Republicanism and Theocracy.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>Reign of Predators</strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Nigeria is a place where an ethno-religious class of feudalists successfully recruited support from selfish elite from several ethnic groups and sectors of the country to establish a class of political and economic predators. This class includes politicians, top&nbsp; public servants, big businessmen, traditional rulers and indeed all those perceived as <strong>“government men”</strong> enjoy immunity from interference in their nefarious activities by law enforcement agents.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The pervasive presence of this class gave rise to a situation where the reign of the predators is accepted asnormal. One cannot understand the Nigerian society unless one understands the social-psychological process whereby criminality is perceived as heroism. The elite are drunk with power and money. Like drug use, the addict considers himself a hero for doing what others do not do. They act the gods and pretend to godly power.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The lascivious criminal performs criminal acts in a lustful manner and defines criminality positively. The Nigerian power elite actually celebrate and glamorize criminality. In the face of acts that threaten the general good; they band together in solidarity.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>It is within this context of high level indoctrination in attitudes of disrespect for law that juvenile deviance and law violation by adults become the norm. Mischief making is a pernicious but socially accepted game. Hence, there are no villains in the political hierarchy of any party in power in Nigeria. What would be ranked in conventional society as more audacious criminal escapades in high places only makes a Nigerian elite a greater hero in government circles.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Stubborn ruling elite has dug in deeply in the country and crushes sources of protest by ruthless official and unofficial use of force. Citizens refuse to give their loyalty to the rule of force. There is suppressed tension and ceaseless tension as people live in constant fear of men in power. This presents an extremely dangerous state of affairs, leading to chaotic scenes constantly breaking out at most unexpected times and places.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Nigerians often condemn the phenomenon of gangs of youths otherwise called <strong>‘area boys’</strong> or <strong>“hoodlums”</strong> who organize to grab what does not belong to them from unsuspecting members of the public. Why then do the same citizens fail to condemn in equally categorical terms, the real big grabbers that promote the worst instincts of human nature? The political, economic and traditional area boys that institute a reign of terror and a cloud of fear on the entire populace are accepted as leaders!</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Decree 24, 1999, by which Nigeria is ruled creates authoritarian and suffocating political, administrative and economic structures which have consistently generated social tension in the country. Yet, political leaders are insensitive enough to insist on the status quo. They pay deaf ears to wise counsel of several well-informed and patriotic Nigerians. For example, legal icon, Afe Babalola, SAN, recently counseled:</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>“The only change that can change the country and pave way for nationhood is the change that changes the structure of project Nigeria. No amount of sermon from the pulpit can change the country.” </strong>(Aare Afe Babalola, Nigeria in search of a nation: Excerpts from author’s address to Yourba Summit (2), Vanguard, September 20, 2017, p 40).</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Leaders lack moral fibre and patriotism to commence the process of building a nation-state out of the conglomeration of ethnic groups that make up the country. Instead of nationalism and deepening democracy, they engage in fighting over “zoning”, euphemism for sharing high political positions.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>Nigerians Take Cue from Their Worst</strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Unlike Covid-19, which is transmitted by close contact with an infected person, lawless conduct is transmitted by merely seeing a person doing a wrong thing, hearing or reading of criminals who became wealthy. Moral morbidity is therefore infinitely more infectious than the deadly Covid-19.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Indeed, the hurtful behavious patternof the Nigerian elite lays eggs aplenty. For example, a government that is founded on electoral violence or fraud and sustained by political manipulation and corrupt influence can only reap violence and fraud together with like profit.&nbsp; Things beget their kind. It is therefore no surprise that governments established by violence and fraud beget even greater violence and fraud.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Once an evil person gains access to political power in a local government area, state or the country, he dominates the environment with bad examples. Everyone comes to accept foul means as a standard of conduct.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Those who refuse to deploy violence and fraud to win elections or who insist on living according to their legitimate earnings are mocked as<strong> “mumu”</strong> or <strong>“suffer-head”.</strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>When one conjoins the social philosophy of unmitigated greed with total absence of any iota of loyalty to nation, then one understands the crudest form of banditry by officials of state, unrivalled in the annals of man. The level of greed is out of hand. In the bureaucracy, politics, professions, business houses, educational institutions, religious bodies, traditional institutions, even in the underworld of big time crime. The super dogs are bleeding the under dogs. Nigeria is one huge theatre of the war of all against all.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>In democratic societies, politics and economics are instruments of development. In Nigeria, they are instruments for impoverishing the masses. This is the connection between a perverse form of political power that destroys the economy, aborts development and alienates citizens who then view government as its enemy rather than an embodiment of its wishes and aspirations.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>At the present time, thereisno social force, strong enough to neutralize the debilitating influences of the deadly social virus that afflicts Nigeria. The development of the country is stunted by the moral morbidity of its elite.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->', 0, 'https://boldtimes.com.ng/?p=917', 0, 'post', '', 0),
(918, 1, '2020-12-18 07:56:50', '2020-12-18 07:56:50', '', '2658562-1024x709', '', 'inherit', 'open', 'closed', '', '2658562-1024x709', '', '', '2020-12-18 07:56:50', '2020-12-18 07:56:50', '', 917, 'http://boldtimes.com.ng-content/uploads/2020/12/2658562-1024x709-1.jpg', 0, 'attachment', 'image/jpeg', 0);
INSERT INTO `wp5g_posts` (`ID`, `post_author`, `post_date`, `post_date_gmt`, `post_content`, `post_title`, `post_excerpt`, `post_status`, `comment_status`, `ping_status`, `post_password`, `post_name`, `to_ping`, `pinged`, `post_modified`, `post_modified_gmt`, `post_content_filtered`, `post_parent`, `guid`, `menu_order`, `post_type`, `post_mime_type`, `comment_count`) VALUES
(920, 1, '2020-12-18 08:14:38', '2020-12-18 08:14:38', '<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>By Chris O.O. Biose</strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>“Not gold but only men can make a nation great and strong.&nbsp; Men who for truth and honour’s sake stand fast and suffer long, brave men who work while others sleep, who dare while others shy.&nbsp; They build a nation’s pillars and lift them to the sky.”</strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:image {\"id\":925,\"sizeSlug\":\"large\",\"linkDestination\":\"none\",\"className\":\"is-style-default\"} -->\n<figure class=\"wp-block-image size-large is-style-default\"><img src=\"https://boldtimes.net.ng-content/uploads/2020/12/download-1.jpg\" alt=\"\" class=\"wp-image-925\"/></figure>\n<!-- /wp:image -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>- Ralph Waldo Emerson (1803-1882)</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>An important question that arises from Part One of this discourse (Moral Morbidity of the Nigerian elite) is how the virus of illicit behaviour that afflicts Nigerian elite and which trickles down to the masses, may be eliminated or reduced to a tolerable level so as to restore the health of social fabric. How can the debasing elite be cleansed by those who have themselves be debased?</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>My answer is that Nigeria needs a prime motivator at the core or source of its debasement. &nbsp;In this respect, the good news is that unlike Covid-19, the virus of anti-social conduct is a state of mind over which the individual could take control. The unique personality (prime motivator) must therefore be morally and intellectually fortified. He must be strong enough to neutralize the debilitating influence of the social virus that surrounds him and in which he is immersed. His approach to public issues is founded on robust ideological foundation, free from religious, ethnic and class bias which burden most men.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Just as Nigeria habours a well organised and merciless battalion of political crooks, so also it has a large army of patriotic citizens who are at present overwhelmed by anti-social forces.&nbsp; This large army awaits a commander-in-chief.&nbsp; The new political commander must awaken this sleeping army of patriotic soldiers of the right and enkindle their innate patriotic zeal.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>Men of Conscience</strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>In this respect, it is important to state that Nigeria no longer needs strong men because strong men have ruined the country and destroyed its social institutions. Equally, Nigeria does not need religionists because most of those that destroyed the country are ardent religionists who plead God for their virulent conduct. Nigeria does not need ethnic jingoists who take their tribe as the country.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>. The country needs men of conscience and not of power. The prime motivator needs to be armed with empathy and ideologically driven. With such men, Nigerians can breathe again. When released from bondage of strong men, there is no height that the country cannot attain.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Instead of deepening democracy, they are fighting over zoning, euphemism for sharing posts.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The prime motivator will lead a corps of counter elite to commence the process of building a nation-state out of the conglomeration of ethnic groups that make up the country. He will serve as a kick-start to re-invent Nigeria. He will liberate citizens from the virus of moral morbidity that stunts its development, threatens its health and ultimately, its existence.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>Ideological Foundation for Re-Inventing Nigeria</strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>In the Nigerian situation where super-abundant material resources constitute stumbling blocks rather than assets, the Number One imperative consists of attitudinal and institutional changes which could only be brought about by an uplifting ideology. Within this context, progressives need to sit down to fix objectives, set targets and work out plans to achieve such objectives. Rational progressivism is an alternative ideology to counter and correct the failures of unbridled egotist fascism, camouflaging as democracy in Nigeria.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>A large majority of Nigerian entrepreneurs function as mere sales managers of foreign products, boosting industrial production in foreign countries while destroying the basis of industrial production at home.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>What a poor country like Nigeria needs is to check the drift towards further inequality and embrace ideologies and programmes that lay a sound basis for development.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>Essence of Progressive Ideology</strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>One of the corrupt tactics of Nigerian political elite is to adopt names and titles that do not reflect their realities. It was shown in this discourse that the titles “Federal” and “Republic” do not justifiably apply to contemporary Nigeria. People also falsely assume titles such as Excellency, Distinguished, Honorable, Progressive, and so forth. It is quite apparent from the Nigerian experience that an individual or political formation is not progressive merely because he or it adopts the title. <strong>“Ye shall know them by their fruits.” </strong>(Matthew 7: 16)</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>To progress is to move forward towards an aim.&nbsp; In every society, there are those who defend the status quo and those who organise to bring about positive change.&nbsp; Progressives form a category of people who constitute themselves into a moving force to champion progressive change.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Progressivism entails secular rationalism involving a transition from act-of-God to act-of-man explanations of social phenomena, thus widening the effective field of the recognition of suffering. It is not concerned with theological concepts of gods and devils.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Toil, sickness, strife, deprivation of liberty and man’s inhumanity to man have been part of the human condition for thousands of years.&nbsp; The progressive mind is concerned to bring about the type of changes that eliminate those conditions that cause human suffering.&nbsp;</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>.As a result of this recognition, progressives take rational, planned action at the level of human affairs as major causative agents of the human condition.&nbsp; They seek to break the rigidities of existing power structures and bring more social forces into relevance, thereby engendering new and refreshing social realities.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Man is not destined to live under any particular political or economic system. Neither is man fated to any particular social order.&nbsp; Progressives accept the burden of building a progressive society as a matter of public policy, supplemented by private initiative.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Being primarily concerned with human welfare as its top priority in social planning, it seeks to institutionalise compassion through socially prescribed channels rather than voluntary private philanthropy. Progressivism is humanitarian.&nbsp; It is extremely sensitive to the plight of the vulnerable and the needy which are contrary to the priorities of feudalists, capitalists, militarists and fascists.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>By way of emphasis, it should be made clear that progressivism is not based on the whims and caprices of “big men” or individual rulers as to what constitutes progress.&nbsp; It is not based on visions and dreams such as the late Ugandan military dictator, Field Marshall Idi Amin was reputed to constantly have about his country.&nbsp;</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>It is not based on prayers and fasting by rulers and their henchmen.&nbsp; It is not based on claims to divine authority by theocratic leaders neither is based on alleged divine right of monarchs. It is not based on militaristism or dictation by an all-knowing military strong man.&nbsp;</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Progressive socialist analysis makes us understand that men, including their thinking and social systems, are products of circumstances and circumstances are liable to change.&nbsp; Society is made, not given.&nbsp; With this realisation, any system that sees itself as the summit and close of history is waging a losing battle against inevitable historical change.&nbsp; Progressives prefer planned change to unplanned cataclysmic social revolts.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>For a movement to be progressive, it must seek to redistribute economic power among the producing agents in a way that no one cheats the other. It must bring economic justice to the lower classes.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Secondly, it must bring about political power shift. This means that the poorer sections of the populace must play a wider and wider role in the state, thereby making egalitarianism a genuine reality.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>It is also important to make clear that a measure is not progressive merely because it offers free money or services to poorer sections of the society. Thus, for example, sharing money to impoverished citizens does not qualify as a progressive policy because the N5,000 or N10,000 being shared does nothing to remove the victims of an oppressive society from their unacceptable condition. A measure is progressive if it aims at bringing an end to exploitative exchange relations with a view to ending exploitation by redistribution of economic and political power in a state.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Neither is one a progressive merely because he earns a low income or because he is a worker, is poor or puts on rags. To be a progressive does not require a person to be shabby or live in a slum. There is no contradiction whatsoever when individuals in a progressive society earns high income. Indeed, the objective of such a state is <strong>“life more abundant” </strong>for all, as famously formulated by Chief Obafemi Awolowo when he launched the Action Group at Owo on April 28, 1951.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Finally, a progressive movement must aim at cultural and economic independence without which political independence is a farce. An economy in which 90 per cent of the resources are under foreign control is nothing but serfdom.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>It is Possible</strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The greater part of the Nigerian problem is leadership. It is the X-factor in the country’s development. The country bears the heavy burden of parasitic ruling elite - selfish, greedy, unpatriotic, lawless and often ignorant. In progressive societies, it is the elite that fashion out strategy and tactics for&nbsp; achieving welfare of the society.&nbsp;</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The founding fathers of the Nigerian state had a dream <strong>\"to build a nation where no man is oppressed\"</strong>so that <strong>“with peace and plenty, Nigeria may be blessed”</strong>, as envisioned in the Independence National Anthem. It is evident that these aspirations of the founding fathers have not been realised to a reasonable degree.<strong></strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>To actualize this key vision, Nigeria needs a leader that is wholeheartedly dedicated to the cause of nationalism. He must be conversant with crucial problems of economic transformation of a backward country. Such a leader will pilot the country to rediscover and actualise the&nbsp; vision of the founding fathers of the Nigerian state.<strong> &nbsp;&nbsp;</strong>.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>In the past 20 years of civilian rule in Nigeria (1999-2020, there has arisen one humble, unassuming, principled and patriotic Nigerian that fits the bill envisaged in this Chapter. A former Governor of a non-oil producing state, he paid off more than two years outstanding salaries and pensions of teachers in his state. He undertook ambitious provision of social infrastructure such as educational and health infrastructure. Before his tenure, his state was No. 26 in ranking in examinations conducted by the West African Examinations Council (WAEC) and National Examination Council (NECO). Within two years of his tenure, his state took first position in SSCE examinations conductedby both examination bodies.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>He undertook massive investment in core economic infrastructure in his state, particularly roads, office buildings.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>During his tenure, he refused to ride in jeeps orarmoured cars. His convoy consisted of a maximum of four 406 Peugeot vehicles. He never used chartered aircraft. He always flew economy class and lodged in fairly priced hotels when he is not in his state.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>At the end of his tenure of office after eight years, he did not owe workers’ salaries, pensioners or contractors. Rather, he handed over savings of over N75 billion which stood in the name of his state government in various Nigerian banks which he christened “ <strong>“Future Generation Fund”</strong>.&nbsp; As far as I know, this outstanding leader is the only former State Governor in Nigeria who refused to collect pensions or other perquisites from his state.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Most Nigerians are said to be <strong>“prayer warriors”.</strong> In their private capacities, (not as government officials), it is time to shun religious and tribal sentiment, stand firm for what is good for the country, and as a complement, pray with Josiah Gilbert:</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; <strong>“God, give us men: A time like this demands</strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Strong mind, great hearts, true faith</strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; and ready hands;</strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Men whom the lust of office</strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; does not kill;</strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Men who possess opinions</strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; and a will;</strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Men who have honour, men who will not lie;</strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Men who can stand before a demagogue</strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; And damn his treacherous flatteries</strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; without winking;</strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Tall men, sun-crowned, who live above the fog</strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; In public duty and in private thinking.”</strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->', '<strong>ANTIDOTE TO MORAL MORBIDITY</strong><strong>OF THE NIGERIAN ELITE</strong>', '', 'publish', 'closed', 'closed', '', 'antidote-to-moral-morbidityof-the-nigerian-elite', '', '', '2020-12-18 08:14:41', '2020-12-18 08:14:41', '<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>By Chris O.O. Biose</strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>“Not gold but only men can make a nation great and strong.&nbsp; Men who for truth and honour’s sake stand fast and suffer long, brave men who work while others sleep, who dare while others shy.&nbsp; They build a nation’s pillars and lift them to the sky.”</strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:image {\"id\":925,\"sizeSlug\":\"large\",\"linkDestination\":\"none\",\"className\":\"is-style-default\"} -->\n<figure class=\"wp-block-image size-large is-style-default\"><img src=\"https://boldtimes.net.ng-content/uploads/2020/12/download-1.jpg\" alt=\"\" class=\"wp-image-925\"/></figure>\n<!-- /wp:image -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>- Ralph Waldo Emerson (1803-1882)</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>An important question that arises from Part One of this discourse