roblem about which they set out to complain.<br>Mittee likened late President Yar’Adua’s amnesty programme for Niger Delta militants to “a situation where people protest over a bad road, cut a tree to block the road because government ignored them. The government comes to bribe them to remove the tree. So, the road is not repaired but vehicles are still allowed to drive through. This is the situation in the region.” The government “equates peace in the Region with flow of oil.” (Jimitola Onoyume, Niger Delta crisis: how government missed it – Mittee, Vanguard, June 13, 2016).</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The point is that the late President had no intention whatsoever of helping the long-suffering agitators in any way; the only motive was to get the militants of the way so that he could continue collecting proceeds of sale of crude oil and natural gas to line the pockets of his political class. It was a grand scam, oiled by grand propaganda. This explains the attitude of Nigerian politicians to citizens across board.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Any leader that pretends that he wants presidency on behalf of his tribe by means of zoning is most likely a liar and a scammer. If he is honest, he will simply say, “I want to use my tribe to acquire power for myself. Please help me in my selfish desire.”</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The situation in multi-ethnic regions is more bothersome. For example, it is totally inconceivable that Northern leaders who are always insisting that presidency be zoned to “North” would ever grant the presidency to a person from Zangon-Kataf in Southern Kaduna who are under constant threat of annihilation by their dominant tribal neighburs. When will Northern leaders zone presidency to the long-suffering Berom of Plateau State or Tiv of Benue State?</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>This situation of seeming perpetual domination exists in most states in the country. It is therefore in the best interest of minorities and all patriotic citizens to seek a truly equitable system where individuals can canvass and contest positions in the economic and political affairs of the country on their merits without regard to ethnic or religious affiliation.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Asking for presidency for an ethnic group or geographical area is deceitful in the extreme because each zone is amorphous and it would only enable the dominant group to use the under-privileged as its footstool to fester its own nest.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>After nearly 110 years of the amalgamation of the Northern Protectorate with the Colony and Protectorate of Southern Nigeria in 1914, present-day Nigerians are still fixated in the Lugardian view of the areas he conquered for his country. Nigerians seem unable to grow out of arbitrary, self-serving colonial demarcations of their country. There is no intrinsic North designed by Omneity, as an eternal verity; there is no South, East or West in the sense erected by colonialists – only temporarily manageable administrative divisions that do not reflect authentic social and cultural realities of the areas lumped together as Districts, Divisions, Provinces and Regions.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>In contemporary Nigeria, there are only oppressors and oppressed, privileged and under-privileged citizens. The furore about zoning continually staged by political contractors is designed to use the masses to gain political power. The key challenge of leadership is to engender national unity, bridge the widening gap between rich and poor and make the good things of life accessible to all without class, religious or ethnic discrimination.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Insurmountable Challenge of Zoning<br>It has been argued in some quarters that zoning of political positions is not unconstitutional because it is not expressly prohibited by the Constitution. Indeed, the letter and spirit of Section 14(3) of Decree 24, 1999, which is also called Constitution of Nigeria, 1999, envisages equitable sharing of positions under the principle of “Federal Character”. The Decree states:</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>“The composition of the government of the federation or any of its agencies and the conduct of its affairs shall be carried out in such a manner as to reflect the federal character of Nigeria and the need to promote national unity and to command national loyalty thereby ensuring that there is no predominance of persons from few states or from a few ethnic or other sectional groups in that government or any of its agencies.”</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>It is submitted that this provision relates to distribution of positions by the executive arm of government. It is not intended to override the basic principle of constitutional democracy based on free and fair elections.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Section 14 of the same Decree states:</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>(1) “The Federal Republic of Nigeria shall be a state based on principles of democracy and social justice.<br>(2) It is hereby accordingly declared that (a) sovereignty belongs to the people of Nigeria from whom government through this constitution derives its powers and authority.”</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Elections constitute the primary method of recruiting political leaders in a democratic state. Most modern states regard free and fair elections as the best procedure for setting up new governments and ensuring orderly succession of governments. In a functional social democracy, power resides in the electorate and not with a cabal that unilaterally arogates to itself, the power to zone elective offices.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Decree 24, 1999, does not allocate a duty to any individual or group to undertake sharing of electoral positions in advance of voting or party primaries.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The fundamental and insurmountable challenge posed by the zoning doctrine is that those who unilaterally take it upon themselves to do zoning invariably share positions to themselves vide their ethnic groups and social class connections.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The only authority legally and constitutionally recognised to choose political leaders in a democratic system of government is the electorate. The process of making this choice is through the party system. This entails internal democracy in making choices within political parties.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->', 0, 'https://boldtimes.com.ng/?p=993', 0, 'post', '', 0),
