(1043, 1, '2022-11-01 08:21:14', '2022-11-01 08:21:14', 'https://boldtimes.net.ng/wp-content/uploads/2021/05/cropped-1200px-Eo_circle_blue_letter-b.svg_-1.png', 'cropped-1200px-Eo_circle_blue_letter-b.svg_-1.png', '', 'inherit', 'open', 'closed', '', 'cropped-1200px-eo_circle_blue_letter-b-svg_-1-png', '', '', '2022-11-01 08:21:14', '2022-11-01 08:21:14', '', 0, 'http://boldtimes.com.ng/wp-content/uploads/2021/05/cropped-1200px-Eo_circle_blue_letter-b.svg_-1.png', 0, 'attachment', 'image/png', 0),
(1078, 1, '2022-12-13 11:01:42', '2022-12-13 11:01:42', '<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>By Chris O. O. Biose,</strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong><a href=\"mailto:chrisoobiose@gmail.com\">chrisoobiose@gmail.com</a></strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>There is a tendency for citizens to try to understand events in the society as they occur without linking them to other events or to events that took place earlier. It is important to recognize the basic</strong><strong> fact that the present is an outgrowth of the past.&nbsp; To understand the present and build effectively upon it, we have to understand the past.&nbsp; In this way, we gain insight and perspective, which enable us to assume a sensible position on issues of the day.</strong><strong></strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>Bearing this in mind, I hereby present a synopsis of the historical background of the Muslim-Muslim presidential ticket adopted by Asiwaju Bola Ahmed Tinubu, presidential candidate of the All Progressives Congress (APC) for the 2023 presidential election. This presentation is a summary of</strong><strong> an important essay entitled <em>Nigeria: Neither an Islamic nor a Christian Country,</em> published online as NIGERIA WORLD FEATURE on March 7, 2001, in which late Professor Omo-Omoruyi delineated the process of renewed Islamisation, not merely of the defunct Northern Region, but of the Nigerian state, as follows:</strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>1973 -&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; The Third Military Dictator of Nigeria, General Murtala Muhammed (1938-1976) enrolled Nigeria as a member of the OIC with Observer Status.</strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>1977/78 -&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Paul Unongo led opposition against Sharia in the Constitution Drafting Committee (CDC) that produced the 1979 Constitution for the country. The Middle Belt Caucus at the Constituent Assembly worked out a deal with the Southern minorities to deal with the Sharia issue.</strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>1986 -&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Nigeria’s Sixth Military Dictator, General Ibrahim Babangida (retd.) converted Nigeria’s involvement in OIC from that of Observer Status to Full Member.&nbsp; He organised a mission led by late Sultan of Sokoto, Alhaji Ibrahim Dasuki, to formally admit Nigeria into the OIC at Fez, Moroco.&nbsp; The grand plan was to make Nigeria an Islamic Republic in the fashion of Libya.&nbsp;</strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>1988 - &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; In January 1988, General Babangida (retd.) unilaterally made Nigeria a shareholder in the Islamic Development Bank (IDB).</strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>1997 - &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; On June 15, 1997, the Seventh Military Dictator of Nigeria, late General Sani Abacha joined other Muslim countries to establish “Eight Islamic Developing Nations” (D-8 for short), an organisation of eight Islamic countries (Egypt, Malaysia, Indonesia, Iran, Turkey, Pakistan, Bangladesh and Nigeria).</strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>2000 - &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Under President Olusegun Obasanjo, 12 northern states adopted the Shari’a as state religion.</strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>2001 - &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; On February 24, 2001, former President Olusegun Obasanjo attended the 3<sup>rd</sup> Summit of the D-8 in Egypt.</strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>The role of religion in Nigerian politics is central to the phenomenon of dominion and exclusion which is one of the perennial challenges of the Nigerian state. It reared its ugly head in the years before independence, forcing the Colonial Government to appoint the Minorities Commission, also known as the Willinks Commission in 1957.</strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>The constitutional guarantees against social discrimination enshrined in the Constitution of the Federation, 1960 and the Constitution of the Federal Republic of Nigeria, 1963 failed to resolve the challenge. Rather than abate, it multiplied beyond exclusion of ethnic minorities in the country to religious exclusion, gender exclusion and generational exclusion, resulting in the #EndSARS youth protest against police brutality in October 2022.</strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>But the most far-reaching source of control of power in Nigerian history is religion which has far wider implications than the much advertised ethnic and gender exclusion. The promoters of religious domination are far more systematic, consistent, determined and have been extremely nimble in the manner they go about it.</strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>The power of religion to control and manipulate societies is evident throughout Nigerian history. The first and most far-reaching example is the Fulani jihad (1804-1809). The jihadists were relatively fewer in number than their Hausa victims but they vanquished the Hausa and made them subservient to Emirs appointed by their conquerors.</strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>The most profound factors that changed the direction of the history of the Igbo nation in Nigeria were religious. In his book, Things Fall Apart (1958), Chinua Achebe (1930-2013) describes how Christian missionaries from Europe were able to destroy the culture of the Igbo nation by undermining the religion of the people and replacing it with a foreign religion.</strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>A relatively few Christian missionaries from far off lands were able to convert millions of indigenous peoples whom they dubbed as “pagan” or “heathen” in what later became Nigeria. The missionaries made indigenous peoples not only to abandon their culture and embrace foreign culture; some indigenes joined foreigners to taunt fellow Africans as “heathen” and therefore despised them.</strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>The third example is how the Fulani who constitute 6 per cent of the Nigerian population, according to the CIA World Factbook, gained and maintained control of the apparatus of government in Nigeria politics since 1960 and are still using it to establish a hegemony.</strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>The interconnection of religious beliefs with all aspects of human activities is indisputable. However, the republican system of government removes direct control of governance from religious leaders and places decision making about public affairs in the hands of the electorate. By deploying religious power to subvert democracy, some leaders sow seeds of discord and destruction of the state.</strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>Therefore, to try to turn Nigeria into a theocracy as illustrated by late Professor Omo-Omoruyi in the essay cited above and the Sharianisation of 12 northern states since 2000 or distribute public positions on basis of religious affiliation as evident in Nigeria since 2015, is inimical to the emergence of a united and prosperous Nigerian nation-state.</strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>Nigerians have a date with destiny to elect a President free from the virus of religious bias in February 2023. Happily, this positive movement is spearheaded by vibrant section of Nigerian youth population.</strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>It is absolutely impossible for Nigeria to start the struggle for progress of its people as long as it focuses on religion as a basis of decision making. In fact, further religionisation of the Nigerian state will result in utter chaos and perdition such as is the fate of Afghanistan. Even a theocratic state that develops technology such as Iran is engulfed in intolerable level of human rights abuses to enforce religious codes. Nigeria must hands off religion and persistent quarrel on how to share resources. It must shift from religion-based to a knowledge-based society. This is the only way in which it can embrace digital revolution and join the civilized world in the search for human good.</strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->', '<strong>ISSUES IN NIGERIAN POLITICS</strong>', '', 'publish', 'open', 'closed', '', 'issues-in-nigerian-politics', '', '', '2022-12-13 11:01:42', '2022-12-13 11:01:42', '', 0, 'https://boldtimes.com.ng/?p=1078', 0, 'post', '', 0),
(1045, 1, '2022-11-01 09:04:47', '2022-11-01 09:04:47', '{\"version\": 2, \"isGlobalStylesUserThemeJSON\": true }', 'Custom Styles', '', 'publish', 'closed', 'closed', '', 'wp-global-styles-newslay-2', '', '', '2022-11-01 09:04:47', '2022-11-01 09:04:47', '', 0, 'https://boldtimes.com.ng/2022/11/01/wp-global-styles-newslay-2/', 0, 'wp_global_styles', '', 0),
(1046, 1, '2022-11-01 16:16:53', '2022-11-01 16:16:53', '<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>WHO EXACTLY IS NORTH?<br>by Chris O. O. Biose,<br>chrisoobiose@gmail.com</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The idea of North as a political category features prominently in Nigerian politics. The question arises as to how far this all-pervading political North can be reasonably identified as a geographical, ethnic or religious concept.<br>In view of the fact that the defunct Northern Region consists of hundreds of ethnic groups as well as different religious groups, it is pertinent to interrogate the concept of North. One would wish to know exactly who northern elders refer to when they talk of North and whether “North” is truly a coherent and identifiable political unit that can be linked to political decision-making in the old Northern Region and the country as a whole.<br>In the run-up to the 2011 presidential election, some northern elders insisted that it was the turn of the north to produce the president of Nigeria in 2011. For this reason they argued, former President Goodluck Jonathan who is from Bayelsa state in South-South Nigeria, must not be allowed to contest the 2011 presidential election. The northern elders posed as protectors of “northern interest”.<br>On July 28, 2010, late Mallam Adamu Ciroma, who was then a leading member of the Peoples’ Democratic Party (PDP), led a delegation of northern elders to visit then Speaker of the House of Representatives, Hon. Dimeji Bankole at the National Assembly on the zoning controversy in the party. Other members of the delegation included Ambassador Yahaya Kwande, Senator Daniel Saror, Mrs. Rhoda Akor, Alhaji Abubakar Mahdi, Alhaji Usman Alhaji, Professor Isa Mohammed and B. B. Farouk.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The group claimed that “Northern political leaders were alarmed” by the prospect of Dr. Jonathan contesting the 2011 presidential election. Mallam Ciroma said that the insistence of Northern Elders’ Political Forum to have the Presidency zoned to north was aimed at “safeguarding the interests of the North as a vital and integral component of this country and the peaceful coexistence of the various components of the country.” (Ciroma, others to Bankole: North insists on zoning, THE NATION, July 29, 2010, front page and p 2)<br>As the 2023 presidential election approaches, the highly regarded former Vice President of Nigeria, Alhaji Atiku Abubakar, who is presidential candidate of the PDP for the 2023 presidential election was in Kaduna on October 15, 2022, to appeal for support of the same “North” to help him win the Nigerian presidency. Atiku went further than late Mallam Adamu Ciroma about 13 years earlier, when he told an assemblage of venerable Northern leaders not to vote for Yoruba or Igbo candidates, emphasising that North “doesn’t need a Yoruba or Igbo candidate. (Ifreke Inyang, 2023: Northerners don’t need Yoruba, Igbo candidates – Atiku, DAILY POST online, October 15,2022)<br>In addition to overwhelming political influence of North, a lot is said about monopolistic control of the Nigerian economy by the same North. For example, sometime in 2013, Senator Ita Solomon Enang, representing Akwa Ibom North East Senatorial District and who was also Chairman of the Senate Committee on Rules and Business in the Senate, alleged that Northerners owned 83 per cent of oil blocs in the Niger Delta. (83% of Nigerian oil blocs owned by Northerners – Senate Committee chair, PREMIUM TIMES, March 6, 2013).</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Could this economic and political Leviathan called North include 12 million Almajirai, a class of homeless children, abandoned by their parents and northern state governments, to face extremely harsh struggle for bare survival by begging or being recruited to fight for their political oppressors?<br>Could this North include the Tiv of Middle Belt who have been at the receiving end of unrelenting feudal oppression since the Tiv crisis (1960 to 1966), renewed by the even more ferocious onslaught by armed Fulani herdsmen which has persisted since the Agatu massacres of February 2016?<br>Does this North include the long-suffering Atyap of Zangon-Kataf of Southern Kaduna, and their leaders such as Major General Zamani Lekwot (retd.) who, along with 16 of his Atyap kinsmen, was once sentenced to death by a kangaroo Special Military Tribunal, without fair hearing and without right of appeal? The military junta later commuted his death sentence to five years imprisonment before he was granted state pardon by late General Sani Abacha.<br>How far can several oppressed ethnic minorities in Southern Kaduna State who have been subjected to serial murders and massacres, which intensified since 2015, be included in North?<br>Does this North include millions of Christians in that sub-region who are victims of unmitigated discrimination in admission to schools and political patronage? “North” is an area where over 11 500 Christians were killed between June 2015 and April 2020, according to Patrick Tyrell of Magaret Thatcher Centre for Freedom, citing Genocide Watch (Patrick Tyrell, The Horrific Killing of Christians in Nigeria, The Heritage Foundation online, December 2, 2020).<br>In spite of the fact that some Middle Belt elite are regularly recruited to join the ranks of feudal lords that call the shots in Nigeria, how far does it make sense to continue to include the Middle Belt as part of North?<br>Linguistic and cultural diversity apart, the vast majority of people of the Middle Belt does not share the same worldview with the ruling class in the core Northern area, which derives essentially from Islamic culture. The resistance of some Middle Belt ethnic groups against submerging them into Islamic culture is one of the most enduring chapters in Nigerian history.<br>The hegemonic political power in Northern Nigeria takes it for granted that the Tivs, Katafs, Nupes, Jukuns, Igalas, Berom, Idomas and hundreds of other minorities in the Middle Belt have no choice but to remain subservient parts of Northern Emirates where a tiny but coherent ruling class that controls the levers of political power will always lord it over disunited minorities.<br>The demand for self-determination by the Tivs, Bchamas, Berom, Angas, Junkuns, Katafs and Gwaris in the old North marked a decisive stage of minority struggle for self-determination in the country. It is on record that the first-ever motion for the creation of a separate state in the Northern Region was tabled on Tuesday, March 6, 1956, on a motion by Mallam Ibrahim Imam, a Kanuri man who represented Tiv Division in the Northern House of Assembly, for the creation of Middle Belt State. The motion was defeated by 68 votes to 2, with 5 abstentions.<br>Again, on Tuesday, February 26, 1957, a second motion was tabled by Bitrus R. Pam of Jos for the creation of a Central region. It was defeated by 98 votes to 2, with 3 abstentions.<br>The struggle of Middle Belt minorities for a space under the sun in their homelands continues to make news today with daily reports of horrendous attacks on Katafs, Tivs, Biroms and others by hostile groups who desire to take over the Tiv homeland in Benue State. Under this condition, how can a Tiv man or anybody from Kogi State call himself “North”? Whether in a geographical, ethnographic or cultural sense, such classification does not make sense.<br>It is particularly instructive that when 12 Northern states of Zamfara, Sokoto, Katsina, Kano, Jigawa, Yobe, Borno, Kebbi, Kaduna, Gombe, and Bauchi adopted Shari’a Law as state religion between January 2000 and June 2001, the Middle Belt states were not involved, except Niger State.<br>This is very significant because it shows that in spite of the Fulani jihad of 1804 to 1809 that conquered the Hausa states, and over 100 years of jurisdictional pressure from the Sokoto Caliphate that controls the Northern area, the Middle Belt remains largely outside the social, cultural and ideological orbit of the Islamic-Arab world as represented by Islamic theocratic movement in Nigeria.<br>Clearly, the Middle Belt is linguistically, culturally and politically distant from Sharia states of the core Northern Area. The crisis over continued inclusion of Middle Belt in North broke into the open once again when Northern elders consistently included the Middle Belt as part of North that viciously opposed the emergence of Dr Goodluck Jonathan as President of Nigeria in 2011.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>In the run up to the 2015 presidential election, the Middle Belt bluntly refused to join Northern elders in their persistent attacks on former President Jonathan. Rather, the region sought to establish a separate identity from the political myth of “one North”. According to THISDAY, the name Middle Belt “is a stinging rejection of the entire behemoth called Northern Nigeria, with all its oppressive glory.” (Emmanuel Bello, Nigeria: North v Middle Belt – Royal Rumble 2015, THISDAY online)</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>A foundation member of the PDP and former Military Governor of old Plateau State, Major General Lawrence Onoja (retd) was categorical. In answer to a question by THE NATION reporter whether he would support a Northern candidate in 2015, the retired Major General said:<br>“There is a misconception. The North of the sixties when Sarduana was alive is no longer the same North today. Let me correct you that misconception. I am from the Middle Belt, which is a geographical reality today. I am an active member of the Congress for Equality and change, which is a non-political association. I am also a member of the Middle Belt Forum. So, if you say I am from the North, and you are referring to the old North yes, I am from the North. But now, the Middle Belt is a geographical reality and I am a member of the Middle Belt. I am not from the core North”. (Emmanuel Oladesun, Middle Belt’ll not support power shift to North, THE NATION, August 20, 2013)</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Several Middle Belt groups have rejected the title North-Central and chosen to be referred to as Middle Belt and insisted that they would sustain an independent political course. The appellation also reflects the wishes of the people of the region. In this wise, the President General of the United Middle Belt Youth Congress (UMBYC), Abuka Onalo Omo-Baba, firmly declared:</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>“We have insisted that we are Middle Belt Youth people and will remain Middle Belt. Nobody is going to give us a name other than the name we have chosen for ourselves. We did not give Arewa, we did not give Odua, we did not give Ndigbo, our own name is Middle Belt and if our governors do not feel that after their tenure as governors in the Middle Belt that their own colleagues in Kano and Sokoto have more right to the presidency, then they are mistaken</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>”…When they say power to the North, none of the governors past or present from the Middle Belt can present themselves. They will either point their colleagues in Sokoto, Kebbi or Bauchi. So they have continued to enslave the Middle Belt people and this why the United Middle Belt Congress, says no.” (THREAT TO DUMP JONATHAN IN 2015: Northern govs bluffing – Middle Belt groups, Vanguard, April 16, 2013, pp 1 and 5).</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>An extremely crafty and heartless set of feudalists consistently deceive the masses by preaching “One North” while actively impoverishing the commoners in that region and creating the highest concentration of human beings afflicted by extreme poverty on the surface of the earth. A World Bank Report entitled Advancing social protection in a dynamic Nigeria released on January 28, 2020 stated in part: “Almost half of all the poor lived in the North-West and the North accounts for 87 per cent of all the poor in the country in 2016.”<br>Apparently, a tiny but powerful and hegemonic class of feudal lords usurps the concept of North to intimidate and manipulate the bulk of the population of the old Northern Region and through that means, the entire Nigerian political class. This is a classic political ruse purposely designed to confuse and win over the unwary in that part of this unfortunate country.<br>Nigeria needs political leaders with capacity to empower the masses with access to liberal education and skills which are prerequisite for development and productivity. A genuine leader will endeavor to build national unity, not exploiting primordial sentiments to win undeserved support from a predominantly illiterate the impoverished population.<br>A genuine presidential candidate is obliged to meet authentic representatives of 371 ethnic nationalities that make up Nigeria as listed in the 1976 Federal Government ethnographic survey of the country. He must meet and identify with interests of significant groups in the country such as youths, students, women, farmers, professionals, teachers, health practitioners, workers in general. Oppressive Nigerian masses must wake up. Enough of the mass deceit!</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->', 'WHO EXACTLY IS NORTH?', '', 'publish', 'open', 'closed', '', 'who-exactly-is-north', '', '', '2022-11-01 16:16:53', '2022-11-01 16:16:53', '', 0, 'https://boldtimes.com.ng/?p=1046', 0, 'post', '', 0),
(1049, 1, '2022-11-09 21:46:01', '2022-11-09 21:46:01', '<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Publishers: BOLD Publishers Ltd, Asaba<br>Date Published: August 7, 2022<br>Cover Price: N20,000.00<br>Pages: 476<br>ISBN: 978-978-49936-5-4</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>ZONING DOCTRINE AND THE NATIONAL QUESTION IN NIGERIA (2022) is a companion of my work entitled ZONING DEBATE IN NIGERIAN POLITICS published in December 2021. This book interrogates the rationale of the practice whereby party lords in Nigeria share elective positions, such as Presidents, Governors, Senators, Members of House of Representatives, Council Chairmen and Councillors, to particular geo-political areas in advance of actual voting at party primaries to nominate candidates, thereby necessarily excluding aspirants from areas that are not favoured by zoning on each occasion. The book examines the relevance of this practice to resolution of the national question in Nigeria which pertains to how citizens of a multi-national and multi-cultural country can coexist for peaceful development in one nation-state.<br>The economic and political consequences of the doctrine are discussed within the wider context of ‘sharing ideology’ which has dominated the political thinking of Nigerian leaders since the transition from military to civil rule in May 1999. The presentation is detailed, and copiously illustrated with case studies from contemporary Nigerian economic and political history.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->', 'ZONING DOCTRINE AND THE NATIONAL QUESTION IN NIGERIA (2022)', '', 'publish', 'open', 'closed', '', 'zoning-doctrine-and-the-national-question-in-nigeria-2022', '', '', '2022-11-29 09:16:50', '2022-11-29 09:16:50', '', 0, 'https://boldtimes.com.ng/?p=1049', 0, 'post', '', 0),
(1050, 1, '2022-11-09 21:42:29', '2022-11-09 21:42:29', '', 'Screenshot_20221109-223154', '', 'inherit', 'open', 'closed', '', 'screenshot_20221109-223154', '', '', '2022-11-09 21:42:29', '2022-11-09 21:42:29', '', 1049, 'http://boldtimes.com.ng/wp-content/uploads/2022/11/Screenshot_20221109-223154.jpg', 0, 'attachment', 'image/jpeg', 0);
INSERT INTO `wp5g_posts` (`ID`, `post_author`, `post_date`, `post_date_gmt`, `post_content`, `post_title`, `post_excerpt`, `post_status`, `comment_status`, `ping_status`, `post_password`, `post_name`, `to_ping`, `pinged`, `post_modified`, `post_modified_gmt`, `post_content_filtered`, `post_parent`, `guid`, `menu_order`, `post_type`, `post_mime_type`, `comment_count`) VALUES
(1053, 1, '2022-11-29 09:09:02', '0000-00-00 00:00:00', '', '<strong>GAS FLARING IN THE NIGER DELTA</strong>', '', 'draft', 'open', 'closed', '', '', '', '', '2022-11-29 09:09:02', '2022-11-29 09:09:02', '', 0, 'https://boldtimes.com.ng/?p=1053', 0, 'post', '', 0),
(1054, 1, '2022-11-29 09:13:09', '0000-00-00 00:00:00', '<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>GAS FLARING IN THE NIGER DELTA</strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->', 'GAS FLARING IN THE NIGER DELTA', '', 'draft', 'open', 'closed', '', '', '', '', '2022-11-29 09:13:09', '2022-11-29 09:13:09', '', 0, 'https://boldtimes.com.ng/?p=1054', 0, 'post', '', 0),
(1083, 1, '2023-01-02 11:18:06', '2023-01-02 11:18:06', '<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>By Chris O. O. Biose,</strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><a href=\"mailto:chrisoobiose@gmail.com\"><strong>chrisoobiose@gmail.com</strong></a><strong></strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>It is generally recognized</strong><strong> that the missing link in Nigeria’s development is visionary and disciplined political leadership. </strong><strong>The country’s precarious social, economic and political situation needs to be arrested.</strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>The 2023 presidential election presents citizens an opportunity to make a fresh start. The electorate must make rational choices rather than those based on financial inducement or sentiments of religion and ethnicity.</strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>Nigeria needs a leadership that would mobilize all its human and material resources to achieve the goals of the state rather than private, ethnic or theocratic goals. It needs competent leadership that builds rather than divides along gender, ethnic and religious lines.</strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>For some weeks now, some rich presidential candidates bombard the country’s airwaves as well as the print and social media, with lists of infrastructural projects which they wish to implement if elected. The ongoing political campaign for the 2023 presidential election reveals some troubling gaps.</strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>First, the manifestoes presented by presidential candidates of the two major political parties, the ruling All Progressives Congress (APC) and major opposition Peoples Democratic Party (PDP), sidetrack the fundamental issue of THE NATIONAL QUESTION IN NIGERIA. This pertains to how 371 national groups that constitute Nigeria as documented by the Federal Government National Ethnographic Survey, 1976, can live together in peace and prosperity.</strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>Second, the manifestoes of the two political parties lack ideological focus. Practical politics is a struggle for power or control of government. Fundamentally, it is also a battle of ideas, involving a process whereby men impose their world views on others.</strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>According to the Gemran philosopher, sociologist and socialist revolutionary, Karl Marx (1818-1883), ideology is a set of related ideas and values which reflects the interests of a group. It arises when ideas are deployed as weapons to defend socially valued interests.</strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>Ideology provides a philosophical and rational justification for a preferred line of action. It serves to set socially desirable goals or a road map for the development of societies.</strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>Every intelligent traveler should at all times be able to say where he is coming from and where he is going to, or the objects of his travels. Like the intelligent traveller, a political party desiring to take control of the government of a country needs to fashion out and specify a definite path to development, either of its own or among available ideological options.</strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>Both the APC and PDP presidential candidates fail to make clear the kind of Nigerian society that they have in mind. Is it more of the same rapacious and heartless exploitation of the masses or a fundamental shift in the direction of the society? There is absolute need for clear shift from the existing feudal-theocratic mode of governance to democratic and republican ethos of equality of opportunity.</strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>In spite of all the noise and fury, the APC and PDP fail to present clear vision. The presidential candidates of the two parties merely made lists of projects they intend to implement if they win. This is not good enough because any school boy can make a list.</strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>The critical issue to be considered in electing a Nigerian president in 2023 is not list of projects. In the run-up to the 2015 presidential election, Major General Muhammadu Buhari (retd) provided exhaustive list of promises, including devolution of powers, provision of electricity, fighting corruption, fixing the economy and fighting insecurity. After seven and half years in power as President, Commander-in-Chief of the Armed Forces of the Federal Republic of Nigeria, there is no devolution of powers in sight, electric power remains a mirage, insecurity multiplied and extended throughout the country, corruption became institutionalised while the economy plunged to abysmal levels. Worst of all, Nigerians became divided along ethnic and religious lines more than at any time in their history.</strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>A fundamental issue that underlies series of crises in the country is whether Nigeria is a Republic, Theocracy or Monarchy. It cannot be all three at the same time. It must choose one and not vacillate between those variant forms of government. It is not necessarily about whether one form of government is better than the other. It is about honesty of purpose, being true to oneself and to democratic norms.</strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>Theocracies such as Iran and Pakistan provide infrastructure for their citizens. Monarchies such as United Arab Emirates (UAE) and Saudi Arabia also provide excellent infrastructure in those countries. The highly regimented communist China is now the second largest economy in the world.&nbsp;</strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>Almost all economic and political systems can build roads and provide infrastructure.&nbsp; If it was about infrastructure, there would have been no need for Nigerian nationalists to ask colonialists to leave because they built better and more durable roads.</strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>It is sad that, ideologically speaking, Nigeria is still where it was in 1960. In his address entitled Philosophy for Independent Nigeria, a lecture delivered to Nigerian Students at Conway Hall, London, on September 3, 1961, the leader of the Action Group (AG), Chief Obafemi Awolowo declared:</strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>“Every vestige and every channel of undue influence of Britain and her allies in and on Nigeria should be totally eradicated.” (Philosophy for Independent Nigeria, p 21)</strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>In addition, the economic and stratification systems in the country were to undergo comprehensive change. This entails revolutionary empowerment of citizens through universal access to education and health facilities. A true integrated national economy, designed to generate an internal dynamism of its own, was in view.</strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>This Awoist vision for Nigeria that has remained unfulfilled in the past 62 years is the central ideological challenge that confronts Nigerians in the 2023 presidential election, not diversionary tactic of saying that my list is better than your list of projects.</strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>At the end of May 2018, the World Poverty Clock published by the Vienna-based Data Lab disclosed that six Nigerians fall into extreme poverty every minute while poverty in other parts of the world continues to fall.</strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>In 2019, the New York based Brooklings Institution reported that Nigeria had overtaken India as the country with the highest number of extremely poor people in the world.</strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>The result of a of Multidimensional Poverty Index (MPI) survey carried out by the National Bureau of Statistics (NBS) in collaboration with several international agencies, published in November 2022, showed that 133 million people living in Nigeria (63 per cent of the population) are multi-dimensionally poor.</strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>Something must be fundamentally wrong with an economic and political system that serves to impoverish more and more citizens in spite of abundant human and material resources.</strong><strong> All Nigerians that truly love their country must therefore join hands to abolish the existing system that sends six Nigerian citizens into extreme poverty every minute,</strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>Nigeria cannot afford to continue with a system that thrives on dishonesty, social discrimination on ethnic and religious grounds, grand corruption, pervasive insecurity, disregard of rule of law and man’s inhumanity to man.</strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>Moreover, the presidential candidates of the two major political parties that held power since 1999, need to let Nigerians into how they intend to purge themselves of those defects of character that brought this immensely endowed country to its knees.</strong><strong></strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>The Nigerian electorate, particularly the youth and women segments of it, have a historic duty to remove this cancerous political class from power with their votes in February and March 2023. This is the only way to bring about a humane, egalitarian and just society that provides opportunities for women, youths, the economically underprivileged, ethnic minorities and other traditionally oppressed classes in Nigeria.</strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->', 'A FRESH START FOR NIGERIA IN 2023', '', 'publish', 'open', 'closed', '', 'a-fresh-start-for-nigeria-in-2023', '', '', '2023-01-02 11:18:06', '2023-01-02 11:18:06', '', 0, 'https://boldtimes.com.ng/?p=1083', 0, 'post', '', 0),
(1080, 1, '2022-12-13 11:20:14', '2022-12-13 11:20:14', '<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>MUST READ</strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong><u>Omo Omoruyi, <em>An appeal to President Olusegun Obasanjo: Nigeria Neither an Islamic nor a Christian Community, in</em> NIGERIA WORLD FEATURE (Mar.7 2001), at http://www.nigeriaworld.com</u></strong><strong><u></u></strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><a href=\"https://issuesinngr.wordpress.com/2013/03/14/nigeria-neither-an-islamic-nor-a-christian-country%ef%bf%bc-by-prof-omo-omoruyi/\"><strong>March 14, 2013</strong></a><strong>&nbsp;</strong><a href=\"https://issuesinngr.wordpress.com/author/issuesinngr/\"><strong>Editor</strong></a><strong>&nbsp;</strong><a href=\"https://issuesinngr.wordpress.com/category/uncategorized/\"><strong>Uncategorized</strong></a><strong></strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>AN APPEAL TO (THEN) PRESIDENT OLUSEGUN OBASANJO: Nigeria: Neither an Islamic nor a Christian Country</strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>￼</strong><strong><br>Prof. Omo Omoruyi</strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>Wednesday, March 7, 2001</strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>Research Fellow<br>African Studies Center<br>Boston University<br>Boston, MA, USA</strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>It is obvious that the pressure is too much for President Obasanjo. This is another reason why Nigerians of goodwill including, Chief Emeka, Anyaoku, Alhaji Babatunde Jose and Professor Adebayo Adedeji are calling for a National Conference to tackle the lingering political problems including the place of religion in the politics of Nigeria.</strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>The denial of the status of Nigeria in D8 by the Federal Government forced me to write this essay. I am arguing that the recent attendance of President Matthew Obasanjo, a Christian as President of Nigeria at the D8 Summit of Islamic countries at Cairo was not an isolated incidence. It is the culmination of the creeping Islamization of Nigeria, which started in 1975 under General Murtala Muhammad.</strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>The military leaders of Nigeria who got Nigeria into these various Islamic organizations had one thing in mind as to the qualification of Nigerian leadership. They had in mind that Muslims would permanently rule Nigeria as elected Presidents or military Heads of State. Is the emergence of ‘a Matthew’ in President Obasanjo an aberration?</strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>There is no doubt in the minds of General Ibrahim Badamasi Babangida (IBB) when he converted the Nigerian involvement from that of an Observer to that of a Full Member of the OIC in 1986. It was meant to stop the gimmick of that nebulous concept, ‘observer status’ and reenact the plan of General Murtala Muhammed to make Nigeria an Islamic Republic in the fashion of Libya.</strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>It was his untimely death that interrupted the plan. Murtala’s death did not kill the plan, as his followers in the military and in civilian wings of the geo-ethno-military-clique that is the northern leadership were aware of the plan and pushed it to the Constitution Drafting Committee (CDC).</strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>Those who sought membership for Nigeria in the Organization of Islamic Conference (OIC) and later in the D8 knew that the Presidents of Nigeria, by election or by coup or by appointment would or should always be Muslims. Is this not an issue, which Nigerians should discuss?</strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>SHARIA EPISODE IN 1977/78</strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>CDC PLAN A REENACTMENT OF MURTALA’S PLAN</strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>It is part of the history of Shariazation of Nigeria that it was the Murtala’s plan for the Islamization of Nigeria, which formed the basis of the Sharia provision in the Draft Constitution.</strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>But the determination of the members of Constituent Assembly from the Middle-belt working with the members from the Southern minorities of then Bendel, Rivers and Cross River States that successfully put a check on the planned Islamization of Nigeria after the death of General Murtala.</strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>SOUTHERN LAWYERS UNAWARE OF THE POLITICS OF SHARIA</strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>Nigerians of today are faced with the Sharia crisis. But they should note that for whatever reasons, Southern Lawyers failed to warn their people of the implications.</strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>Do the southerners know that it was the Constitution Drafting Committee (CDC) headed by the eminent Lawyer, Chief Rotimi Williams that first translated Murtala’s plan into what appeared in the Draft Constitution?</strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>It is a matter of record that the South had eminent lawyers in the leadership of the CDC. From the record of the CDC, how many Nigerians realize that in addition to Chief Rotimi Williams, there were Professor Ben Nwabueze, Chief Richard Akinjide and Chief Bola Ige in the CDC who are concerned about the Sharia today?</strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>What did they have in mind when they yielded to the pressure and introduced the Sharia at the Federal level into the Draft Constitution in 1977?</strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>PAUL UNONGO AND SOLOMON D. LAR HERO OF SECULARISM</strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>It should be noted that it was only one CDC member, Paul Unongo from Benue who opposed the Sharia in the CDC. But he could not stop the inclusion of the Sharia provision in the Draft Constitution. Having failed at the CDC, he sought election to the Constituent Assembly from Benue along with others from the Middle-Belt on the anti-Sharia platform.</strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>ACCORD BETWEEN MIDDLE-BELT CAUCUS AND SOUTHERN MINORITIES</strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>How the Middle-Belt caucus under the leadership of Chief Solomon D Lar worked out a deal with the Southern minorities in the Constituent Assembly is something, which the new politicians of today should emulate. The Middle-Belt members and the members from the southern minorities in the Constituent Assembly were able to build a consensus on the Sharia, the Revenue Allocation and the Presidential System.</strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>This is a subject of my forthcoming book, Beyond the Tripod in Nigerian Politics. Those who are talking of accord between the Middle-Belt and the South-South would have some lessons from the past efforts in my forthcoming book.</strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>SOVEREIGNTY NOT WITH THE CONSTITUENT ASSEMBLY</strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>The crisis over Sharia in the Constituent Assembly was resolved in the Constituent Assembly. But because the Constituent Assembly was not sovereign, that that it did not have a final say on the decision on Sharia.</strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>Its decision was subject to the overriding approval of the Supreme Military Council, hence the Sharia provision in the final Constitution was fundamentally amended by the Supreme Military Council under the chairmanship of General Obasanjo as Alhaji Maitama Sule recently told Nigeria.</strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>This is why advocates of the Sovereign National Conference are agitating for some understanding that no one would tamper with any decision arrived at by a National Conference or an Assembly of Nigerian peoples and their leaders if it meets for the purpose of resolving the lingering political problems afflicting this country.</strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>SUPREME MILITARY COUNCIL UNDER OBASANJO WAS SPLIT</strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>It is part of the history of Nigeria, that General Olusegun Obasanjo, as the successor of General Murtala had problems with pushing through, the Islamization Plan of Murtala.</strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>The believers in Murtala’s Islamization Plan in the Supreme Military Council, Shehu Yar’Adua, Babangida and Buhari were not powerful enough to push the plan through.</strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>They had problems with the avid defenders of the secularism in the Supreme Military Council led by the Service Chiefs {General TY Danjunma (Army), Commodore Isa Doko (Airforce) and Rear Admiral Adelanwa (Navy)}.</strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>Tinkling with what the Constituent Assembly approved was what General Obasanjo’s regime could do and it did precisely that.</strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>TY DANJUMA LONE COORDINATOR AGAINST SHARIA OF CDC</strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>Why and how some of us in the Constituent Assembly were able to withstand the pressure of the pro-Sharia in the military has not been told. It is part of the history of Sharia episode in the Constituent Assembly that General TY Danjuma held a meeting with some of us from the Constituent Assembly to plead with us to stay on course.</strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>He was particularly worried that some of the Igbo members who originally supported the motion ably and successfully moved by Dr. Mudiaga Odje for the deletion of the Sharia provision in the Constitution were wavering.</strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>The Igbo members were worried after some northern members walked out of the Assembly. Why were some of the Igbo members wavering?</strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>DILEMMA FACING IGBO MEMBERS IN THE ASSEMBLY</strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>The Igbo members came to the Constituent Assembly with two conflicting mandates. One was from the religious leaders that they should do everything to stop the Islamization of Nigeria by supporting the deletion of the Sharia provision in the Constitution.</strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>The other was from the political leaders of thought, which mandated the members from the Igbo land to the Constituent Assembly to do everything to work with the Hausa/Fulani in the tradition of the old NPC and NCNC alliance in post-Independence Nigeria (1960-66).</strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>How the various Igbo political leaders coped with this dilemma during and after the Constituent Assembly will be discussed in my forthcoming book. The Igbo leaders are still facing this dilemma today. To the Igbo leaders there can be compartmentalization; to the northern leaders they are joined and inseparable.</strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>GENERAL DANJUMA RESOLVED THE DILEMMA FACING IGBO</strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>It was General TY Danjuma who pleaded with the Igbo members of the Constituent Assembly not to yield to the pressure orchestrated by General Shehu Musa Yar’Adua, the Chief of Staff, Supreme Headquarters. He assured them that nothing would happen to them if they stayed the course.</strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>It was part of the scare tactics then mounted on the Igbo members of the Constituent Assembly that a second war was in the offing if they the Igbo leaders would not cooperate with the northern l4aders on the Sharia issue.</strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>The Igbo leaders were told that the losers would not be the Yorubas or the Southern minorities and that the winners in another war would certainly be the Yorubas and the southern minorities.</strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>This was very dangerous campaign in addition of some financial and office consideration. This was hard for the Igbo leaders to comprehend as the Igbo people were still battling the aftermath of the Civil War.</strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>This was why the meeting arranged by Paul Unongo with General Danjuma was hurriedly meant to reassure the Igbo members of the Constituent Assembly to discount the pressure and veiled threat from General Yar’Adua. What was the position of the then Head of State, General Obasanjo?</strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>GENERAL OBASANJO SUPPORTED THE CDC POSITION</strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>General Obasanjo then did not see anything wrong with the provision as propounded by the Pro-Sharia zealots. He did not see anything wrong with having the Sharia Court up to the highest level of the federal judiciary with a Grand Mufti as the head of the Federal Supreme Sharia Court.</strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>He was not opposed to the plan to have parity between the Chief Justice of the Supreme Court and the Grand Mufti of the Supreme Sharia Court.</strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>IMPLICATION OF PARITY BETWEEN GRAND MUFTI AND CJ</strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>For the interest of those who did not know of what I am talking about, under the provision in the Draft Constitution, the Grand Mufti was to have the same power like the Chief Justice of the Federation to swear in the President, Commander in Chief.</strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>Of course, the protagonists of the plan knew that the President would always be a Muslim. When some of us fought to delete the provision from the Draft Constitution in the Constituent Assembly in 1978, we knew that we were only postponing the evil day.</strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>I say this because the plan of the government of General Obasanjo was in violation of the decision of the Constituent Assembly. It was obvious to us then that the successor regime to the military whether civilian or military would whittle down the gains we made in the Constituent Assembly.</strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>PRESIDENT SHAGARI AND ISLAMIZATION</strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>President Shehu Shagari was a major exponent of federalizing the Sharia in the Constituent Assembly. When Alhaji Shagari became the President in 1978, the dynamics of the civilian politics did not make it possible for the new civilian to push for a full scale Islamization of the country between 1979 and 1983.</strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>BUHARI AND ISLAMIZATION</strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>General Muhammadu Buhari was one of the disciples of General Murtala. From what he did recently it is obvious that he is a strong believer in the political Sharia.</strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>But his regime (1983-1985) was too short to know what he too would have done with Islamization of the country, if he were to spend a longer period as the military Head of State.</strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>BABANGIDA AND ISLAMIZATION</strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>ORGANIZATION OF ISLAMIC CONFERENCE (OIC)</strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>But the period of General Babangida (1985-1993) represented the beginning of unmitigated Islamization of Nigeria. General Babangida was one of the loyal followers of General Murtala.</strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>This was why General Babangida took the fateful decision in 1986 to face the Nigerian Christians in particular and Nigerians in general with the plan of Muslims. He without the approval of the governing organs of the Federal Military Government organized a mission led by a non-member of the Government, the Sultan of Sokoto to formally admit Nigeria into the Organization of Islamic Conference (OIC).</strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>When the Chief of General Staff, Commodore Ubitu Ukiwe complained that he knew nothing about it and definitely said that he could not remember the matter coming to the Armed Forces Ruling Council (AFRC), he was shown his way out.</strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>The Christians took it. The Christian leaders bought the plan of the Military President to keep them talking under the auspices of a 20-man panel to ‘examine the implications of the country’s full membership of the organization’. This was a diversion and the Christians were victims. This was not all.</strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>ADVISORY COUNCIL ON IRELIGIOUS AFFAIRS</strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>Later President Babangida set up an Advisory Council on Religious Affairs. The Council as the name implied was advisory. One was not surprised that this Council was not privy to General Babangida’s decision to take Nigeria further down the road of Islamization.</strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>SHAREHOLDER OF ISLAMIC DEVELOPMENT BANK</strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>General Babangida unilaterally made Nigeria a Shareholder of the Islamic Development Bank in January 1988. Nigeria at the time of becoming a Shareholder held 0.32% shares. Nigeria is still a Shareholder of this Bank today.</strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>RELIGIOUS CLEANSING OF REGIME</strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>Nigeria would recall the hue and cry over the religious cleansing of the military regime in December 20, 1989 of Christian officers. How many Nigerians still recall how the commanding heights of the IBB regime were Islamized? This was by far the most far-reaching change under the IBB administration, which had five main implications.</strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>That it reenacted the regional dominance of the country;</strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>That it reenacted the Muslim religious dominance of Nigeria;</strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>That it dislodged the Langtang Mafia with the humiliation of General Bali out of office and the removal of General Dongoyaro from command and influence;</strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>That it empowered General Abacha by making him the Chief of Defense Staff, a more enhanced position than the one held by General Bali who was just Chairman, Joint Chief of Staff.</strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>CHRISTIAN LEADERS’VIEWS OF IBB</strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>Despite the protest of many Christian organizations, I was not surprised that just the way he treated Christians during the OIC protest, IBB ignored the prelate who carried placards with the following inscriptions:</strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>Forceful Islamization Impossible; Christians Have no Confidence in IBB and His Administration; No Arab Colonization; Maradona, Enough of Your Hypocrisy. (See African Concord of January 22, 1990).</strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>If the foregoing are no attributes of an Islamic Country, I do not know what is an Islamic Country.</strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>ABACHA AND ISLAMIZATION OF NIGERIA.</strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>PARTICIPATION IN OIC AFFAIRS OPENLY</strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>That Nigeria is a full member of the OIC was no longer in dispute as Nigerian Islamic leaders were freely and openly participating in all the activities of the organisation.</strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>The Nigerian Christians and their leaders resigned themselves to that fact with the Sultan of Sokoto flying in and out of one OIC affair or the other in different parts of the Islamic world.</strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>MEMBERSHIP OF D8</strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>General Abacha took another fateful step when he got Nigeria into the D8, an organization of eight Islamic countries in 1997.</strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>The eight countries are<br>Egypt,<br>Malaysia,<br>Indonesia,<br>Iran,<br>Nigeria,<br>Turkey,<br>Pakistan, and<br>Bangladesh.</strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>OBASANJO AND ISLAMIZATION</strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>SHARIA, A POLITICAL FACT.</strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>Under President Obasanjo, the Shariasation of the northern part of Nigeria, which commenced in one location in Gusau in Zamfara State, is now an established practice in most of the states in the north.</strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>SUMMITRY OF D8</strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>Under President Obasanjo Nigeria openly participated at the<br>President’s level in the activities of the D8 with the attendance of the President Olusegun Obasanjo at 3rd Summit of D8 in Egypt on February 24, 2001. One should ask some questions for the President of Nigeria to answer.</strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>What was going through President Obasanjo’s mind when he took off for Cairo that day?</strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>Where did President Obasanjo tell Nigerians he was going when he took off for Cairo?</strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>How comfortable was President Obasanjo when the other Islamic leaders surrounded him from Bangladesh, Turkey, Egypt, Iran, Pakistan, Indonesia, and Malaysia?</strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>Did President Obasanjo, a born again Christian see himself as one of the Islamic leaders?</strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>Did President Obasanjo see Nigeria as an Islamic country to qualify to be a member?</strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>MINISTER OF FOREIGN AFFAIRS WAS WRONG</strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>Why should the Federal Government issue a statement credited to the Foreign Minister, Alhaji Sule Lamido, that D8 is not an Islamic organization and that Nigeria’s participation does not mean that Nigeria is an Islamic country.</strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>Haba!, Comrade Sule, why should you engage in disputing what is so obvious internationally? As a Foreign Minister, you should be aware of what is known internationally.</strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>If Alhaji Sule Lamido is not aware, he should read the news reports in the international media since the inauguration of the D8 through the successive Summits.</strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>He would come to one conclusion that the D8 is an organization of Islamic Countries and that membership of the organization defined by Islam.</strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>D8 IS AN ISLAMIC ORGANIZATION</strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>May I appeal to Alhaji Lamido to stop deceiving or confusing the Nigerian people. Does the Minister know that the CNN World news had this headline: ‘Islamic Leaders Debate Responses to Globalization’?</strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>This news was web posted at 10:47 am on February 25, 2001. Does the Minister think that these international media are ignorant of the developments in the world, as the Minister would want us believe?</strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>D8 IRRELEVANT TO PROBLEMS OF DEBT RELIEF</strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>I do not want to discuss the merit of using this forum to attack the problems of globalization. Nigeria was in the wrong place if debt crisis is her problem.</strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>I do not want to discuss the lack of commonality among the countries except that they are all supposed to be Muslim Countries run supposedly by Islamic leaders.</strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>Nigerians were right in asking if Nigeria is a Muslim country and if President Obasanjo is an Islamic leader?</strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>CREDIBILITY OF MINISTER AN ISSUE</strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>May I with humility call on my former comrade in progressive movement in Nigeria, Alhaji Lamido to perform two tasks in the interest of his credibility as a Foreign Minister of Nigeria.</strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>He should search for more news items on the Web on the D8 since 1997; He should let Nigerians know what he found out.</strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>OBASANJO, AN ISLAMIC LEADER OF AN ISLAMIC COUNTRY?</strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>As a loyal Nigerian, may I let the Hon. Minister know what I found out from my exercise. I found out three things.</strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>That the D8 is made up of Islamic Countries;</strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>That Nigeria’s membership is derived from the fact the military leader who took Nigeria to D8 knew that Nigeria is an Islamic country; and</strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>That the attendance of President Obasanjo at the last meeting confers on him the envious title of an Islamic leader.</strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>CONCLUSION: A NATIONAL CONFERENCE</strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>Finally, granted that General Olusegun Obasanjo had no alternative than to follow the footstep of General Murtala his former boss who was assassinated on February 13, 1976 in supporting the Murtala’s Shariasation of Nigeria in 1977/78.</strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>But President Obasanjo has no reason whatsoever to follow the footsteps of Generals Babangida and Abacha in turning Nigeria into an Islamic State by becoming an Islamic leader in the process.</strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>A Summit of Islamic leaders, which General Abacha could not attend even though he got Nigeria into it was graced by President Obasanjo!</strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>It is obvious that the pressure is too much for President Obasanjo. This is another reason why Nigerians of goodwill including, Chief Emeka, Anyaoku, Alhaji Babatunde Jose and Professor Adebayo Adedeji are calling for a National Conference to tackle the lingering political problems including the place of religion in the politics of Nigeria.</strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->', 'NIGERIA: NEITHER AN ISLAMIC NOR A CHRISTIAN COUNTRY By Professor Omo-Omoruyi', '', 'publish', 'open', 'closed', '', 'nigeria-neither-an-islamic-nor-a-christian-country-by-professor-omo-omoruyi', '', '\nhttps://issuesinngr.wordpress.com/2013/03/14/nigeria-neither-an-islamic-nor-a-christian-country%ef%bf%bc-by-prof-omo-omoruyi/', '2022-12-13 11:20:14', '2022-12-13 11:20:14', '', 0, 'https://boldtimes.com.ng/?p=1080', 0, 'post', '', 0),
(1093, 1, '2023-04-11 22:01:25', '2023-04-11 22:01:25', '<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>MISS ALBERTINE DOIBO FOR LP GUBERNATORIAL PRIMARIES<br>AN APPEAL TO LP MEMBERS/DELEGATES IN BAYELSA STATE</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>We wish to appreciate the Screening Committee of the Labour Party (LP) for successfully screening gubernatorial aspirants in our great party from Bayelsa State. OBIdients in Nigeria and the Diaspora are delighted to learn that Miss Albertine Doibo is among the successful applicants.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>One of the cardinal principles of the LP is greater inclusiveness of hitherto excluded social forces in the country. This entails increased effective participation of women and youths in political and economic process of Nigeria. This is a departure from entrenched interests in erstwhile leading parties, PDP and APC, that exist to perpetuate the status quo in the country.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The LP Gubernatorial Primaries in Bayelsa State is the first Primaries, preparatory to the first Gubernatorial Election in Nigeria after the General Election held in February and March this year. It presents a unique opportunity for LP to live up to its national billing as the Party to build a new Nigeria that works for all rather a hegemonic few.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>We, the OBIdients, hereby respectfully appeal to the LP members, officials and Party Delegates for the Primaries in Bayelsa State to nominate Miss Albertine Doibo as LP candidate for the election. This is the best way to address existing gender inequity, female and youth exclusion from governance in Nigeria.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>It is also the surest way to galvanise the OBIdient Movement nationwide and internationally once again, to passionately involve in the Gubernatorial contest in Bayelsa State.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>OBIdients are not asking for Miss Albertine Doibo as Governor of Bayelsa State merely because of her gender. She is an accomplished professional who has displayed requisite character, competence, compassion as well as high principles, impeccable personal integrity and capacity to maximally harness abundant human and material resources of the state for the benefit of all.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>She has already placed before the Party and Bayelsans, her commitment to implement LP programmes, and drawing on her oil industry background, suitably modify them to address specific environmental challenges of a predominantly riverine state.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Dear LP Delegates, please nominate Miss Albertine Doibo as LP Gubernatorial candidate for Bayelsa State and OBIdients in Nigeria and the Diaspora will join you totally to take the contest from there.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>God Bless Labour Party, God Bless OBIdients, God Bless Bayelsa State, God bless Nigeria.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Chris O. O. Biose,<br>National Co-ordinator,<br>TEAM NIGERIA FOR PETER OBI</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->', 'MISS ALBERTINE DOIBO FOR LP GUBERNATORIAL PRIMARIESAN APPEAL TO LP MEMBERS/DELEGATES IN BAYELSA STATE', '', 'publish', 'open', 'closed', '', 'miss-albertine-doibo-for-lp-gubernatorial-primariesan-appeal-to-lp-members-delegates-in-bayelsa-state', '', '', '2023-04-11 22:19:28', '2023-04-11 22:19:28', '', 0, 'https://boldtimes.com.ng/?p=1093', 0, 'post', '', 0),
