(1101, 1, '2023-06-02 05:26:51', '2023-06-02 05:26:51', '<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>TINUBU IS 6TH PRESIDENT OF NGIERIA NOT 16TH<br>By Chris O. O. Biose,<br>chrisoobiose@gmail.com</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Since his inauguration as President, Commander-in-Chief of the Armed Forces of the Federal Republic of Nigeria on Monday, May 29, 2023, some Nigerian news media, have been announcing that Asiwaju Bola Ahmed Tinubu is the 16th President of Nigeria. That is quite wrong and needs to be corrected very quickly.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Mr. Bola Ahmed Tinubu is the 6th President of Nigeria after Presidents Shagari (October 1, 1979-December 31,1983), Olusegun Obasanjo (May 29, 1999-May 29, 2007), Umaru Musa Yar’Adua (May 29, 2007-May 5,2010), Goodluck Jonathan (May 5, 2010-May 29, 2015) and Muhammadu Buhari (May 29, 2015-May 29, 2023).</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Those referring to President Tinubu as 16th President of Nigeria are probably lumping together into one category, all those who have ruled Nigeria since 1960, including the First Prime Minister, Alhaji Sir Abubakar Tafawa Balewa, the long line of violent Military Dictators, and the Military-appointed Head of the Interim National Government in 1993.<br>The idea of President of a democratic country connotes civility and due legal process in attaining political power. In contrast, becoming a military ruler involves violent or treacherous overthrow of existing authorities by means of coup de tat.<br>It is generally recognized that teamwork is emphasised in achieving military goals but this does not apply to the business of coup making. As one time Nigerian Chief of Army Staff who later served as Minister of Defence, Lt. General Theophillus Y. Danjuma (retd.) explained in a lecture which he delivered in 1986:<br>“A military coup is an act of revolt usually planned and executed in extreme secrecy by a small group. The bulk of the armed forces are not normally consulted and therefore not involved in the revolt. So, the first casualty of most coups is not the constituted civilian government, but the destabilisation of and overthrow of the military set-up.” Danjuma concluded that “coup making is not an act of bravery or gallantry but a function of opportunism.”</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>It is bothersome to imagine that this picture vividly painted by the former Nigerian Chief of Army Staff is that of a Nigerian President.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>However, as soon as the successful coup-maker assumes office, everybody must obey him. There is no room for polemics. A costly product of military ethics is “You can’t question the boss.” Rather than bring any form of progress, the advent of prolonged military in Nigerian public life served to institutionalise the command mentality. Increasingly, most people failed to see anything wrong with dictatorships. Consequently, a whole generation of Nigerian youths was acculturated to trust in brawn rather than the brain.<br>The position of President of a democratic country is qualitatively distinct from that of military ruler. One entails rule of law while the other thrives on murder, treachery, arbitrary power and other unwholesome practices. To presume that the two are the same or should be known by the same descriptive title, amounts to grievous disregard for democratic values.<br>Military coup de tat is not only unconstitutional and illegal, it involves the most heinous offences against the state. It follows from this that coupists should be called upon to answer for their crimes irrespective of the number of years they enjoyed the benefit of their crimes.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Although most former military dictators in Nigeria acquired fame and stupendous wealth, those are not measures of constitutional rule. It should not be the role of the Nigerian media to launder the image of this class of rulers. They socialized a whole generation of Nigerian children and youths that force makes right and this pernicious social ideology is at the root of most of the ills that have plagued the country since the formal end of military rule in May 1999.<br>The act of calling different types of rulers of Nigeria by the same name or title does irremediable damage to Nigerian political history. It does equally immeasurable injustice to those Nigerian leaders who assumed political power by legitimate means as distinct from those who seized it by felonious and unconstitutional tactics.<br>A generalized title of President to both elected leaders who scrupulously embrace the rule of law and violent impostors who ruled by the gun, serves to mislead the younger generation of Nigerians. It gives the wrong impression that military dictators who came to power by subverting the constitution or by killing their fellow men, are in the same political bracket or categorisation with democratically elected Presidents freely chosen by the unfettered will of Nigerian citizens.<br>Those who came to power by foul means are only enjoying the benefits of their misdeeds because Nigeria is a place where mighty criminals go unpunished or are even rewarded<br>For example, on November 3, 2010, the Nigerian Senate approved new bumper package for former Heads of State in the country. This included offices of President, Head of Government of the Federation, Prime Minister, Vice President, President of the Senate, Speaker of the House of Representatives, Chief Justice of Nigeria, Deputy Senate President, Deputy Speaker of the House of Representatives under the 1999 Constitution.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Curiously, the pension was also extended to former master coup plotters and military dictators who came to office by force of arms. Everyone seems to presume that the coup plotters were above the law since they had friends in the Army whom they could use to cause trouble.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>A significant section of the Nigerian media all along euologised former coup plotters, calling them President, elder states man and the like. The only former military dictator that most of the media men describe appropriately is late General Sani Abacha, because he is dead. If he was alive, media practitioners would not dare to call him thief.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>No democratically elected President is known to have granted State Pardon to any of the offenders against the state as late President Shehu Shagari did to late ex-Biafran leader, General Chukwuemeka Odumegwu-Ojukwu on May 18, 1982, for his role in the Nigeria-Biafra War (1967 to 1970) and he returned to Nigeria on June 20, 1982, as a free citizen after about twelve years in exile.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>There has been no true Nigerian leader with courage to address fundamental issues, face reality or do justice in the polity. Nigeria needs courage to right the wrongs of the past, based on equity and justice and the media has a vital role to play in this sacred and social cleansing project. Heavens will not fall when justice is done in Nigeria. On the contrary, Heaven will bless those who have courage to do right.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>I have never seen a country where the law and Constitution says that violent overthrow of government is a crime and then the law makers of the country go ahead to remunerate those who overthrew the law and the Constitution of that same country. If that be considered right, equity demands that lesser offenders need to be equally compensated according to the enormity of their crimes.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The hefty pensions awarded to former coup plotters and their families by David Mark-led National Assembly, himself a retired Brigadier General, served to legitimise the illegality of coups in Nigeria. At the present time, nobody know how many persons in this category earn multiple pensions from the Nigerian treasury because some of them acquired legitimate political positions after retiring from military service.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>What message is the National Assembly and Nigerian media sending to the younger generation and all rational minds? Carry out whatever heinous crime you can. Just make sure you succeed because you will enjoy the fruits of your illegality ever after.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>It is demeaning and unjust to place duly elected Presidents of Nigeria in the same pedestal as military dictators. It signifies disregard for democratic values. It places military rule in the same category as democratic governance and that is really tragic.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>All the above are reinforced by the provisions of Subsection (2) of Section 1 of the Constitution of the Federal Republic of Nigeria (Promulgation), 1999, Decree No. 24, which states: “The Federal Republic of Nigeria shall not be governed, nor shall any person or group of persons take control of Nigeria or any part thereof, except in accordance with the provisions of this Constitution.”<br>Chapter 6, Part 1, Section 132 of that Decree which currently serves as the Nigerian Constitution, sets out .clear processes for the election of a President of that Federal Republic of Nigeria. Carrying out a military coup is not one of the processes.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Section 14(1) of the same Decree states: “(1) The Federal Republic of Nigeria shall be a state based on principles of democracy and social justice. (2) It is hereby accordingly declared that (a) sovereignty belongs to the people of Nigeria from whom government through this constitution derives its powers and authority.”</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>It follows from the above that Presidency of Nigeria is not a title to be frivolously awarded to anyone by the Independent National Electoral Commission (INEC) or conferred on former military dictators by some over-zealous media practitioners.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>It is for this reason that a large number of well-meaning and intensely patriotic Nigerians are currently withholding their recognition of the declaration of Asiwaju Bola Ahmed Tinubu as President of Nigeria because of serious irregularities and alleged fraud that attended the disputed February 25, 2023 presidential election until the Nigerian judiciary confirms the true winner of the election. The holder of the esteemed position of President of Nigeria must merit and earn it by electoral due process.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->', 'TINUBU IS 6TH PRESIDENT OF NGIERIA NOT 16TH', '', 'publish', 'open', 'closed', '', 'tinubu-is-6th-president-of-ngieria-not-16th', '', '', '2023-06-02 05:28:08', '2023-06-02 05:28:08', '', 0, 'https://boldtimes.com.ng/?p=1101', 0, 'post', '', 0),
(1106, 1, '2023-08-21 14:06:10', '2023-08-21 14:06:10', '<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>NIGERIA’S HYPOCRISY ABOUT MILITARY COUP IN NIGER REPUBLIC<br>By Chris O. O. Biose,<br>chrisoobiose@gmail.com</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>“Many leaders as you see dem,<br>Na inside disguise dem dey,<br>Animal in human skin,<br>Animal put on suit,<br>Animal wear agbada.”<br>Fela Anikulapo-Kuti (1938-1997)<br>I have observed with tremendous solicitude how some prominent Nigerian politicians toast coup leaders in their country but vehemently deprecate the same phenomenon in other countries. The hostile reaction of ECOWAS under the chairmanship of President Bola Ahmed Tinubu to the military coup that ousted former President Mohammed Bazoum on July 26, 2023, and installed General Abdourahmane Tchani as new leader of Niger, is a case in point.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>30 Years of Military Barbarism in Nigeria<br>Nigerians celebrate their own coup masters that seize political power by force. Their stock in trade is treachery and as the country’s former Chief of Army Staff and ex-Minister of Defence, Lt. General Theophillus Y. Danjuma (retd.) explained in 1986, “coup making is not an act of bravery or gallantry but a function of opportunism.” (The Guardian, Friday, 21 March 1986, p 19).</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>These opportunistic soldiers visited permanent damage to Nigeria. They instigated civil unrest and massacre of civil population, culminating in the Nigeria-Biafra War (July 1967 to January 1970). This war led to about 100,000 military casualties while between 500,000 and 2 million Biafrans died of starvation.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Beyond flagrant abuse of human rights, torture, extra-judicial execution of citizens and advertising blood letting in the civil population, Nigerian soldiers arrested democratic growth of the country in about 30 years of barbaric rule.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>In its desire to seek legitimacy and economic power outside the citizenry, General Ibrahim Babangida’s military junta embraced IMF-inspired Structural Adjustment Programme (SAP), saddled with “conditionalities”. It was couched in highly loaded and deceptive terminologies such as trade liberalization or deregulation (including payments or currency deregulation), rationalization or commercialization.<br>General Babangida’s deregulation compelled Nigeria to open its economic border to aggressive exploitation of Western powers. He dismantled Nigeria’s sovereign defences when the country had not set up facilities to enable it to compete in the world market.<br>The destruction of the economic foundation of the Nigerian state that resulted from the measures was swift, catastrophic and irreversible. In an analysis of Nigeria’s post-SAP economic condition carried out by Professor Tijani Yesufu in 1992, he found that after about five years of SAP which started in 1986, “the Naira depreciated from N0.8924 to US$1 in 1985 to N9.725 to US$1 in 1991 and N45 to US$1 in December 1993, equivalent to a devaluation of l,090% and 2,801% by 1993. In effect, the purchasing power parity of the Nigerian Naira against the American dollar “worsened to the extent that in 1993, it would be required for a Nigerian to work over 2,800 times as hard as he worked in 1985 to purchase the same basket of food.”<br>Yesufu observed that the Nigerian economy ostensibly recorded impressive growth by reference to published official statistics. But it was equally clear, he concluded, that these statistics constituted “a veritable farce”. Not only had there been little or no meaningful development, “the reality is a picture of unfathomable decline, if not decay.” (Tijani Yesufu, The Nigerian Economy: Growth Without Development, (1991-92) Convocation Lecture, University of Benin, Benin City, April 1993)</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Worse than SAP in its security implications, was the clandestine enrolment of Nigeria as Full Member of the Organisation of Islamic Conference (now Organisation for Islamic Cooperation (OIC) in January 1986. The action portrayed Nigeria as an Islamic state and sparked open demand for Islamic rule in the country. It sowed seeds of incubation of violent extremist sects determined to establish Isamic Caliphate in Nigeria as well as increasing politicisation of religion in the country.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Nigerian military leaders introduced unchallenged looting of the national treasury in a manner never experienced before the advent of military rule. First Republic politicians were afraid of stealing public funds; during military rule, it was done with impunity because no one could challenge armed men in power.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The Nigerian military capped their atrocities against democracy and rule of law with brazen annulment of the June 12, 1993 presidential election, adjudged by local and foreign observers as the freest and fairest election ever held in the country.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Clearly, the major social problems that currently plague Nigeria are directly traceable to acculturation of a whole generation of Nigerians in anti-social and anti-nationalistic behaviour during period of prolonged military misrule.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Military Messiahnism in Nigeria<br>It was bad enough that military tyrants bestrode the Nigerian political space for decades. But it was totally intolerable that these tyrants who posed as messiahs were hero-worshipped by the Nigerian elite..</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>For example, when Nigeria’s 7th Military Dictator, General Sani Abacha was alive, first class traditional rulers, bishops, erudite scholars, business men and professionals made regular pilgrimages to the maximum dictator, begging him to rule. Leaders of the five registered political parties (which Chief Bola Ige, SAN, famously referred to as “five fingers of a leprous hand”), all nominated the military strongman as sole candidate for presidential election.<br>The youthful Daniel Kanu facilitated an organization christened Youths Earnestly Ask for Abacha (YEAA). Under that platform, he organized a massive pro-Abacha two-million-man march from all local government areas in all states of the country to Abuja from March 3-4, 1998, in support of General Abacha’s self-succession plot.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The gold medal for Abacha panegyrist must be reserved for Anambra-born billionaire who demonstrated unrivalled sophistry, artistry and imagination in his support for the military dictator. In an article entitled Deification of a General, notable Nigerian essayist and former Minister of Aviation, Femi Fani-Kayode, drew attention to an interview granted to TheNews Magazine of April 14, 1997, at page A7. Giving reasons why Abacha should succeed himself, the praise-singer said, among other things:<br>“If Abacha is not there, that is the end of democracy.”<br>“Only Abacha can control the dangers we have.”<br>“Only Abacha can monitor the Army.”<br>“Abacha is the symbol of Nigerian unity.”<br>“Abacha provides security and stability.”<br>“Political economic and social stability is with Abacha.”<br>“Only Abacha can be there and if you challenge him, you are challenging God.”</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>“If Abacha does not want to be president, I will leave the country because there will be civil war.”</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>When General Abacha died suddenly on June 8, 1998, a few weeks before the onset of his seemingly assured civilian presidency, the businessman did not only fail to keep his promise of leaving Nigeria, he quickly made new political friends to keep his business alive.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>It was only after the death of General Abacha that some people started calling him “thief” and the story of “Abacha loot” is all over the media. I marvel at the hypocrisy of the Nigerian elite. If Abacha was alive, media practitioners would not dare to call him a thief. He would have been enjoying his loot as his living colleagues are doing today. Nobody is asking billionnaire Generals what legitimate salaries and allowances they earned while in service or what inventions they made to earn billions of Naira to build their mansions and estates in diverse parts of the world and live in apparent opulence.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Call Things by Their Names.<br>Nigeria is a country whose top political space brims with coup plotters and beneficiaries of military coups. But the Nigerian elite consistently misinforms the younger generation about the status of the movers and shakers of the Nigerian society. The media unfortunately joins in extolling former military dictators who killed or violently removed elected civilian leaders or their military colleagues in order to acquire political power. These traitorous characters are presented to Nigerians as “heroes” rather than villains which they really are. We call them “former Head of State”, “President”, “statesman” or the like. If we call a coup plotter a President, what shall we call Ahaji Shehu Shagari or Dr. Goodluck Jonathan?<br>No legitimate or democratically elected President is known to have granted State Pardon to any of the offenders against the state as late President Shehu Shagari did to late ex-Biafran leader, General Chukwuemeka Odumegwu-Ojukwu on May 18, 1982, for his role in the Nigeria-Biafra war. The state pardon granted by President Shagari enabled General Odumegwu-Ojukwu to return to Nigeria on June 20, 1982, as a free citizen after about twelve years in exile.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Nigerian coup masters go about with an air of triumph over Nigeria because no true Nigerian leader has had courage to address fundamental issues, face reality or do justice in the polity.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>During the recently concluded 2023 General Election, almost every presidential aspirant of note visited successful coup masters to seek their support and blessing. What kind of blessing does one expect from a coup plotter? How can one say that he is a democrat who does not support coup making and yet depends on successful coup leaders to bless him for a democratic election?</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Civil Society Organisations (CSOs) need to do the younger generation a favour by constantly making them understand the truth about those that are regarded as leaders or role models in the country. The most charitable and factual description of a former military ruler is “former Military Dictator”. It is a grave injustice that the biggest felons in the history of the country do not only escape justice but are also presented as valorous role models. This sends the wrong signals and teaches the wrong lesson to the younger generation.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>President Bola Tinubu has no moral justification to lead ECOWAS to criticize, not to speak of waging war against Niger coup leaders because Nigeria celebrates its own coup masters and has done nothing to purge itself of the coup mentality. Indeed, that mentality has spread to nearly all aspects of the social life of the country, namely, violent or fraudulent acquisition without remorse by the perpetrators and without question by the authorities.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Nigeria’s Charity Should Begin at Home<br>I suggest that the charity of Nigerian political elite about hatred of military coups should begin at home here in in our own country and not in Niger Republic. In this regard, I pray the Nigerian government to revisit the comprehensive and wide-ranging proposals made by Chief Anthony Enahoro on how to punish coup plotters. It involves coordinated national and internation action by the Nigerian state, the sub-regional body ECOWAS, the African Union and the United Nations Organisation (UNO).</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The older generation of pro-democracy activists in Nigeria (which presumably includes President Tinubu), have full details of these proposals. The facts are however in the public domain because Chief Enahoro summarised them as Guest Lecturer at the 1980 Press Week of the Oyo State Council of the Nigerian Union of Journalists. (Drastic solution to coups, NIGERIAN TRIBUNE, Saturday, November 10, 1980, pages 4 and 13).</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>If these proposals had been implemented and the necessary structures put in place, all the Federal Government needed to do was to invoke existing protocols and procedures. It would not have been necessary to engage in panicky and seemingly vindictive, if not illegal measures such as unwarranted violation of binding international agreement to provide electric power to Niger Republic. The war cries of President Tinubu-led ECOWAS has unfortunately attracted support for Niger not only from some West African countries but more ominously, from some hawkish international powers such as USA, France and Russia.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Chief Enahoro however pointed out that not all military dictators are culpable. He emphasized that his “drastic” recommendations pertained mainly to what he called “primary coups” or “coups of first instance” whereby soldiers “seize power from an elected government and succession by coup leaders.”</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>For example, Nigeria’s 4th Military Dictator, General Olusegun Obasanjo, is respected in Nigeria and internationally because when military leadership of the country was fortuitously entrusted to him in February 1976, he conscientiously implemented a genuine transition to civil rule programme and handed over power to a constitutionally elected President in in October1979. “</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>In the words of Chief Enahoro, “power was thrust upon him by historical forces beyond his control and he handed over to an elected government with requisite speed.” Therefore, such a person could not be ranked as a coup plotter. Indeed, he could qualify as one who made what Chief Enahoro described as a “supreme act of patriotism”. The same applies to Nigeria’s 8th and last Military Dictator, General Abdulsalami Abubakar (retd.).</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Enahoro also distinguished a coup designed to remove from office a “sit-tight” government or “for the express purpose of holding free and fair elections and restoring sovereignty to the people”, especially if the “secondary coup plotters” did not demonstrably benefit personally in any manner from the venture.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>It is indefensible to embrace the most hardened and vicious indigenous coup plotters with open arms as Nigeria’s economic and political elites do and then turn round to condemn those fighting to free their country from enslaving neocolonialists. President Tinubu-led ECOWAS has not provided a rational basis for choosing which coup plotters to cherish and which to condemn.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->', 'NIGERIA’S HYPOCRISY ABOUT MILITARY COUP IN NIGER REPUBLIC', '', 'publish', 'open', 'closed', '', 'nigerias-hypocrisy-about-military-coup-in-niger-republic', '', '', '2023-08-21 14:06:10', '2023-08-21 14:06:10', '', 0, 'https://boldtimes.com.ng/?p=1106', 0, 'post', '', 0),
(1107, 1, '2023-08-21 13:59:05', '2023-08-21 13:59:05', '', 'images', '', 'inherit', 'open', 'closed', '', 'images-3', '', '', '2023-08-21 13:59:05', '2023-08-21 13:59:05', '', 1106, 'https://boldtimes.com.ng/wp-content/uploads/2023/08/images.jpeg', 0, 'attachment', 'image/jpeg', 0);
INSERT INTO `wp5g_posts` (`ID`, `post_author`, `post_date`, `post_date_gmt`, `post_content`, `post_title`, `post_excerpt`, `post_status`, `comment_status`, `ping_status`, `post_password`, `post_name`, `to_ping`, `pinged`, `post_modified`, `post_modified_gmt`, `post_content_filtered`, `post_parent`, `guid`, `menu_order`, `post_type`, `post_mime_type`, `comment_count`) VALUES
(1088, 1, '2023-01-12 09:12:49', '2023-01-12 09:12:49', '<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>Speech delivered by Chief Chris O. O. Biose at the 2022 ANNUAL NATIONAL CONVENTION of the Old Boys Association of Immaculate Conception College, Benin City, hosted by Asaba Branch, with the Theme SPLENDOUR OF THE NIGER, holding at Orchid Hotels and Event Centre, Asaba, on November 19, 2022.</strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>Protocols</strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>(i) Preamble</strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>I wish to thank the leadership of Asaba Branch of the Old Boys Association of Immaculate Conception College, Benin City, for inviting me to speak on this special occasion of the NATIONAL CONVENTION of this famous educational institution holding here in the Delta State capital, Asaba. The College was founded in 1944 by His Lordship Bishop Patrick Joseph Kelly, S.M.A. (1894-1991) as a Catholic boarding school for boys with Rev. Andrew O’Rouke, S.M.A., as the first Principal of the college. Bishop Kelly was appointed Vicar-Apostolic of Asaba-Benin in 1943 and Bishop of the Catholic Archdiocese of Benin City from 1950 to 1973 and during his missionary career, he aided in construction of dozens of schools.</strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>To the Glory of God, the ICC has produced thousands of eminent men who are making significant contributions in all fields of human endeavor and the galaxy of distinguished personalities present here today is abundant testimony to that fact. It is therefore a prodigious honour to me to be here and I feel really humbled.</strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>My first close connection with ICC was 1981, nearly ten years after the compulsory take-over of voluntary agency schools by the military junta in 1972. In spite of general fall in standard of education occasioned by the military misadventure into management of educational institutions, ICC still retained much of its old glory.</strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>The Edo State Government later saw reason and returned the College to its original owners, the Catholic Mission on April 30, 2004 and the Mission quickly restored the old discipline that placed the institution first among equals in Nigeria.</strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>For the purpose of this speech, the River Niger includes all of its major tributaries, physical characteristics, hydrology, climatic factors, plant and animal life it sustains as well as the influence it has on the people who live nearby and how they adapt to it.</strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>The human factor is important in discussing the Splendour of the River Niger because physical realities are reflected and take form in the human consciousness, where they are endowed with meaning and it is also through human action that physical realities can be modified for greater human good or what is commonly referred to as development.</strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>The ideas that come to mind when one contemplates this topic are so extensive that it is difficult to do them justice in the 60 minutes allotted to me. As a result, I have divided my speech into twenty-one (21) micro concepts and will speak for roughly three minutes on each one.</strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>(ii) Asaba and the River Niger</strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>I think it is fair to say that the historical significance of Asaba stems in large part from its location at the Western bank of the River Niger. Asaba oral tradition relates that the town was founded by a man called Nnebisi, from Iteje, east of the River Niger.</strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>The National African Company Limited (NAC), the premier British explorer, trader and imperialist organisation in the 19th century, selected Asaba as its capital. Dr. F</strong><strong>elix </strong><strong>Nwanze-Obi provides extensive data in his 1976 book, <em>The British Occupation of the Niger Territories (1830-1914)</em> about how NAC agents annexed Asaba and coerced the natives to sign an agreement with them in 1884, to govern the territory politically and economically.</strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>Having met conditions stipulated by the British Government, the NAC changed its name to Royal Niger Company (RNC). In 1886, Her Majesty’s Government granted a Charter to the RNC, givin it unlimited power to administer the entire area known as the Niger Territories, over which it had gained trading pre-eminence over rival French companies. Having entrenched themselves as <em>de facto </em>as well as <em>de jure</em> government in the area, the colonial administrators established a Supreme Court, a high security prison, hospital, botanical and experimental gardens, military base, police constabulary, barracks, cemetery and administrative office at the capital city, Asaba, which also housed a retinue of high-ranking British officials.</strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>That was how Asaba became the capital city of the Niger Territories until the RNC formally lost her charter on January 1, 1900, and its flag bearing <em>ARS, JUX PAS</em> was lowered at Asaba and Lokoja giving way to the Union Jack, the official flag of Her Majesty’s Government.</strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>Following this event, the British Government temporarily relocated the territory’s administrative centre from Asaba to Calabar and subsequently to Lagos. However, General Murtala Mohammed’s military junta changed the capital of Nigeria from Lagos to Abuja in 1976 and this change only took effect on December 12, 1991.</strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>(iii) Nigeria and the River Niger</strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>The exploration of the River Niger by foreign explorers, slave traders, merchants and colonialists is inextricably related to the history of Nigeria as a political entity.</strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>British and French explorers had penetrated the hinterland by the 1770s and had set up trading posts along the River Niger all the way from Akassa in the Delta to Lokoja</strong><strong>.</strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>According to historical records, the Scottish surgeon and explorer Mungo Park (1771-1806) was the first to pinpoint the River Niger’s source. </strong><strong>The </strong><strong>passionate explorer is reported to have exclaimed:</strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>“I saw with infinite pleasure, the great object of my mission, the long sought majestic Niger, glittering to the morning sun.”</strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>During his second journey to map the route of the River Niger, he drowned in Bussa in 1806, just before the rapids. Lake Kainji which was constructed in 1968 as a component of the Kainji Dam now covers the former location of Bussa.</strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>The Lander Brothers (Richard Lemon Lander and John Lander) continued further exploration of the River Niger in 1830 and followed the lower Niger from Bussa to the sea. They were particularly fascinated by the Y-shape of the confluence of the River Niger and River Benue, which is captured in the Nigerian coat-of-arms.</strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>Another notable occurrence in the exploring adventures of foreigners along the River Niger took place in the 1830s when Obi Ossai of Aboh jailed the Lander Brothers for entering his realm without his permission although they were ultimately released unharmed.</strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>As noted in Paragraph (ii) of this address, the NAC established its capital at Asaba in 1884. </strong><strong>At the Berlin Conference from 1884 to 1885, where European powers shared Africa among themselves as their possessions, Britain was able to convince the French, German and others that it had established preeminent and paramount interests in the Niger Territories, precisely as a result of the activities of the NAC (renamed RNC in 1886). </strong><strong>The British Government took over the Niger Territories from the RNC in 1900. The Government revoked the Charter it granted to RNC to administer the Territories and paid the company the sum of BP865,000 (eight hundred and sixty-five thousand pounds) as compensation. But this was not before Nigeria had derived its name from the River Niger.</strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>According to a British anthropologist, author, and one-time Government Anthropologist in Northern Nigeria, Charles Kingsley Meek (1960), the name Nigeria first appeared in print in an article published in the Times of London issue of January 8, 1897, written by a British female journalist, Miss Flora Shaw, who at that time, wrote about the colonies. Miss Shaw dismissed existing nomenclatures such as Royal Niger Company’s Territories or Central Sudan as misleading and inconvenient to use.</strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>She suggested that</strong><strong> “the agglomeration of pagan and Mohammedan states” and </strong><strong>the collection of protectorates and colonies around the River Niger should be called Niger Area, or Nigeria.” That was how the country got a name.</strong><strong> (“The Times, London, 8 January, 1897)</strong><strong></strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>(iv) &nbsp;Physical Features of the River Niger</strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>The&nbsp;River Niger&nbsp;is the most important river&nbsp;in West Africa and the third-longest river in Africa, trailing only the Nile&nbsp;and Congo.</strong><strong></strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>The River Niger rises in Guinea, near the Sierra Leona border on the south-eastern edge of the Fouta Djallon highlands at 9°05′N and 10°47′W. It travels in a crescent shape and reaches its upper course near the Malian capital, Bamako, before turning south, </strong><strong>snaking its 4,180 km route through nine sub-Saharan countries before emptying into the Atlantic Ocean at the Gulf of Guinea via a huge delta known as the Niger Delta.</strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>A region of lakes, streams and backwaters known as the River Niger’s “internal delta” is an interesting aspect of the river’s upper course. These lakes are mostly on the left bank and are linked to the river via channels that change direction, depending on the season. When the water level rises, most of the lakes become inundated.</strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>The River Niger reaches its lower course downstream of Jebba (Kwara State), Nigeria, running south-east over a vast and shallow valley 8 to 16 km wide.</strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>The River Niger from Source to the Gulf of Guinea</strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>Source: www.wikimedia.org</strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>From Lokoja downstream to Idah in Kogi State, the River Niger flows in a narrow valley surrounded by hills and flanked by 45-metre-high sandstone cliffs.</strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>Between Idah and Onitsha, the banks are lower and the country flatter. At Onitsha, the valley narrows as the river flows through what is probably a fault in the area’s sandstone. It emerges at Aboh (Delta State), splitting into many branches before reaching the Gulf of Guinea&nbsp;via Africa’s largest delta.</strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>(v) Major Tributaries, the River Niger</strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>Without its tributaries, the River Niger would lose much of its volume and splendor. This makes them crucial.</strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>The Bani River is River Niger’s largest tributary in Mali. It is created by the&nbsp;</strong><a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Confluence\"><strong>confluence</strong></a><strong>&nbsp;of the Baoule&nbsp;and Bagoe&nbsp;rivers, 160&nbsp;km east of&nbsp;</strong><a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bamako\"><strong>Bamako</strong></a><strong>&nbsp;and it joins with the River Niger close to&nbsp;</strong><strong><a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mopti\">Mopti</a></strong><strong>. It is approximately 1,100&nbsp;km long.</strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>The Mekrou River, anther tributary of the River Nigeria, originates in Benin, north of Kounde and flows for 250 kilometres through the&nbsp;</strong><a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/W_National_Park\"><strong>W National Park</strong></a><strong>, which spans parts of Niger, Benin and Burkina Faso.&nbsp;</strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>The Sokoto&nbsp;River which originates south of Katsina State in Funtua in northwestern Nigeria is the next. It flows north-west through Gusau in Zamfara State where the Gusau Dam creates a reservoir that supplies water to the city. It is roughly 275 kilometres long.</strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>The Rima River joins the Sokoto River farther down; it then flows through Sokoto before turning south and passing into Birnin-Kebbi&nbsp;in Kebbi State.</strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>It merges with the River Niger about 120 kilometres south of Birnin-Kebbi.</strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>The&nbsp;Bakalori </strong><a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bakolori_Dam\"><strong>Dam</strong></a><strong> is an important reservoir on the Sokoto River and is located about 100 kilometres upstream of Sokoto. It has a substantial impact on flood plain farming downstream.</strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>The plains around the Sokoto River are widely cultivated. It is a source of irrigation and also an important means of transport.</strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>The Kaduna River joins the River Niger about 110 km from Jebba, contributing about one-fourth of the river’s annual discharge below the Niger-Kaduna confluence. It begins&nbsp;on the Jos Plateau,&nbsp;29 kilometres southwest of Jos, flows 550 kilometres through Kaduna State&nbsp;and its capital Kaduna, before joining the River Niger in Niger State. The majority of its path is across open savanna woodland, although its bottom half carves many canyons above its entrance into the vast River Niger floodplains.</strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>The next and biggest is the River Benue (meaning “Mother of Water” in the Batta language). It joins the River Niger at Lokoja. It rises approximately 1,350 metres above sea level on the Adamawa Plateau in North Cameroon.</strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>The river is approximately 1,400 kilometres long and is almost totally navigable during the summer months. As a result, it is an essential transportation route in the areas through which it flows. The size of its basin is 319,000&nbsp;km<sup>2</sup>. </strong><strong></strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>At the point of confluence, the Benue exceeds the Niger by volume and thereby approximately doubling the volume of River Niger’s annual discharge.</strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>The&nbsp;Anambra River (Igbo: Omambala) in Anambra State is the most important feeder of the River Niger below Lokoja. It travels 210 kilometres before emptying into the Atlantic via different outlets in the Niger Delta region.</strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>(vi) The Niger Basin</strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>The Republic of Benin, Burkina Faso, Cote de Voire, Chad, Cameroon, Guinea, Mali, Niger and Nigeria are all parts of the Niger Basin. Together with its tributaries, the River Niger drains a total area of about 2,117,700&nbsp;km<sup>2</sup>.</strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>(vii) Climatic Conditions of the Niger Basin</strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>Climate varies greatly within the Niger Basin. While sparce rainfall </strong><strong>is a reality of life in the Sahara desert’s southern border, seasonal flooding is common in the plains of the middle and southern belts. In September and October 2022, 33 of 36 states in Nigeria experienced catastrophic flooding, killing over 600 people, displacing over 1.5 million others and destroying farmland and property worth several hundred billion Naira.</strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>Unplanned human growth, including the obstruction of natural water routes, exacerbates the situation as does climate change. A substantial percentage of land in the Niger Delta’s riverine areas is progressively being submerged below sea level.</strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>The delta area has over 160 inches (4,100 mm) of yearly precipitation while Timbuktu has less than 10 inches (250 mm).</strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>Precipitation reduces significantly in the middle Niger region, and the greatest quantity of evaporation occurs here as well. It is believed that the River Niger loses almost two-thirds of the annual volume of discharge that runs past Mopti in the lake region through evaporation or seepage.</strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>(viii) Hydrology of the River Niger</strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>Due to climatic differences, the annual river flood&nbsp;does not occur at the same time in different areas of the basin due to water movement in relation to land around the River Niger.</strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>The upper Niger has a high-water discharge season in June and a low-water season in December.</strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>In the middle course, the&nbsp;white flood (so named because of the light sediment content of the river) occurs immediately after the rainy season between July and October.</strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>A second rise – the&nbsp;black flood (so called because of the greater sediment content), begins in December with the arrival of floodwaters from upstream. May and June are the low-water months.</strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>On the Benue there is only one high-water season. Because of the Benue’s more southerly location, this normally occurs from May to October - earlier than on the middle Niger.</strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>(ix) Plant and Animal Life</strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>The River Niger runs across all of West Africa’s vegetational zones.</strong><strong> The Fouta Djallon plateau, where it rises, is covered in sedge vegetation made up of tiny, wire-like tufts intermingled with bare rock surfaces.</strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>The River Niger flows primarily through savanna grassland terrain from the Fouta Djallon until well below its confluence with the Benue. The grass in the north is short and discontinuous, with thorny shrub and acacia woods.</strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>Tall, tussocky grass is intermingled with dense woody growth in the grassland region’s south.</strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>The river enters the high rain forest zone around Onitsha, where it blends with the mangrove swamps’ flora of the delta below Aboh. The woodland belt’s relatively mild weather attracts a wide range of birds and other creatures.</strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>The River Niger and its tributaries are home to a diverse range of fish, the most important of which</strong><strong> </strong><strong>are catfish, carp and Nile perch. There are at least three varieties of crocodiles (including the dreaded Nile crocodile) and a variety of lizards.</strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>There is a wide range of birds. Geese can be spotted in the lake region, as can herons, egrets, and storks on the river and surrounding lakes. The eye-catching crown&nbsp;bird can be found everywhere there is open ground in the grassland zone, while pelicans and flamingos are especially common in the upper Benue area.</strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>White-headed plovers, crocodiles, birds, sand pipers, curlews, and green-red shanks are among the smaller riverine species.</strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>Hippopotamuses can also be found in some regions, particularly in the “inner delta” region’s slow-moving waters and lake region. On November 19, 2014, The Guardian (International Edition), reported that “twelve children and one adult died after a hippopotamus overturned a boat near the Niger&nbsp;capital, Niamey.” The boat was carrying school children across the River Niger when the sad event occurred.</strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>(x) Ecology of the Niger Delta</strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>The Niger Delta portion of the Niger Basin deserves special mention in a discussion of the Splendour of the Niger. It</strong><strong> is the area where the River Niger and its tributaries pour into the Atlantic Ocean.</strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>The term ecology, according to Oxford Languages online, is “a branch of biology that deals with the relation of organisms to one another and to their physical surroundings.”</strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>The Nun River&nbsp;is said to be a direct continuation of the River Niger. Other significant channels in the delta area include Forcados River, Brass River, Sambreiro River, Bonny River and Escravos. However, the Escravos River has displaced the River Forcados, which itself, replaced&nbsp;the Nun as the most traveled channel in the early twentieth century.</strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>The three major ecosystems of the Niger Delta are lowland rain forest, freshwater forest and the mangrove swamp.</strong><strong></strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>The lowland rain forest zone in the Niger Delta is replete with diverse species. The zone is characterized by tall trees (1<sup>st</sup>&nbsp;layer/stratum) which is characterized by thick and dense forest with smooth bark trees of about 40 to 50 metres tall and often, epiphytes and lianas are attached to the bark of the tree; big trees with canopy (2<sup>nd</sup>&nbsp;layer/stratum) which are plants with high branch, 20–35 metres tall and can provide shade; lower trees with bare trunks (3<sup>rd</sup>&nbsp;layer/stratum) which are plants of about 20 metres tall; while 4<sup>th</sup>&nbsp;layer are with mosses, small stemmed shrubs, lichens, herbs and ferns.</strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>The vegetation found in rain forest is mainly used for timber, firewood, saw wood, particle board, pulp/paper, poles and traditional medicine.</strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>The freshwater swamp of the Niger delta is situated between the lowland rain forest in the north and the mangrove swamp in the south.&nbsp;It covers an area of about 14,500 km<sup>2</sup>.</strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>Freshwater are water resources that are mostly used for domestic purposes including drinking, bathing and cooking.</strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>Within the freshwater swamps, other habitats such as riparian and arable farmland are common.</strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>The swamps provide a transition zone between the two ecosystems and a passageway for the migration of biodiversity.&nbsp;This zone is silt laden from the River Niger. It is essential habitat for fisheries particularly during the flooding period. The freshwater is also an important habitat for crayfish, prawn, crabs and crocodiles.</strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>The vegetation of freshwater forest is also characterized by tall trees. Majority of the plant species in the area is used as fuel, food, medicine, boat carving and it also protects the shoreline.</strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>According to Paula Caballera, The Nature Conservancy (TNC) Regional Managing Director for Latin America, the Niger Delta forms a natural dam for the River Niger. She says that “if the delta did not exist, it is likely we’d be brainstorming ways to build a man-made structure to do the work the delta does naturally.” (Paula Caballera, The Niger River Delta – A Strategic Asset in Africa’s Sahel Region, October 20, 2014)</strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; &nbsp;Freshwater and Mangrove Swamp Zones</strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;of the Niger Delta</strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>The mangrove swamp</strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>of the Niger Delta</strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>consists &nbsp;of vast</strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>interlocking network</strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>of mangrove</strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>swamps, rivers, rivulets</strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>and creeks covering</strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>about 6,000 km<sup>2</sup></strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>within the&nbsp; estuary of the</strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>River Niger.</strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>This makes&nbsp; it</strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>the third largest in</strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>the world and the most &nbsp;</strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>extensive lowland</strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>tropical and fresh</strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>water forests, aquatic</strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>ecosystems and</strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>biodiversity in West Africa.</strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>Source: www.polymer.github.io/LICENSE.</strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>The Willink’s Minorities’ Commission Report, 1958, recognizes the Niger Delta wetlands as “one of the most fragile ecosystems of the world”, requiring special developmental attention.</strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>The mouths of the channels are almost all obstructed by sandbars. In this way, the delta is being gradually extended seaward by the increments of silt&nbsp;brought down by the river, and mangrove swamps extend beyond its outer edge.</strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>(xi) Ancient Empires of the River Niger</strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>The River Niger was the site of some early empires in West Africa. One of these was the Mali Empire which was a trading empire that flourished from the 13<sup>th</sup> to the 16<sup>th</sup> centuries. It was located on the upper River Niger east of the Fouta Djallon. At the heights of its power, it controlled the land of the middle Niger and absorbed the trading cities of Timbuctu and Gao.&nbsp;</strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>Another great trading empire which was located on the middle reaches of the River Niger is Songhai in present day central Mali. The Songhai Empire expanded tremendously and dominated the Western Sahel/Sudan in the 15<sup>th</sup> and 16<sup>th</sup> centuries.&nbsp;</strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>(xii) Ethnic Groups and Principal Cities of the River Niger</strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>The River Niger hosts an estimated 110 million people from diverse ethnic groups who live along its banks in hundreds of small and medium size communities as well as important cities.</strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>The ethno-linguistic trend along the river’s is somewhat complicated. The Malinke-speaking people, for example, are spread across numerous West African countries including Guinea and Mali.</strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>The Songhai-speaking people of Gao and Timbuctu, the Bambara-speaking people of Bamako and the Zarma (also called Zerma, Djerm) people of Niger and Burkina Faso are other prominent ethno-linguistic groups located in the upper stretch of the River Niger.</strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>In Nigeria, the Yoruba and Nupe speaking inhabitants of Jeba dwell beside the River Niger. Others include the Igbirra (Ebira) speaking people of Lokoja, the Igala speaking people of Idah, the Igbo-speaking people of the commercial metropolis of Onitsha on the eastern bank and Asaba on the western side, the Ukwani speaking people of Aboh and the Itsekiri of Koko are all on the Niger. The Ijaw (Izon) speaking people occupy the creeks of the delta.</strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>(xiii) Effects of River Niger on Livelihood and Culture of Communities</strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>For those who live on either side of its banks, the River Niger is a vital source of economic support. The uses of the River Niger environment to enhance the quality of life may be discussed under several heads, including domestic, fishing, agriculture (farming); hunting and gathering; trading and transportation, pastoral cattle ranching and cultural activities.</strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>(a) Domestic: For centuries, communities living along the Niger took water directly from the river for drinking, cooking, bathing and other domestic use.</strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>(b) Hunting and Agriculture: The bank of the river is frequented by various categories of animals that come to the river to drink water, particularly during dry periods. The banks therefore become an important area of hunting.</strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>(c) Fishing: Along the whole length of the river system and its tributaries, fishing is a significant activity, particularly during the dry season. For several group of people, such as the Bozo and Somono in the lake region, the&nbsp;Sorko&nbsp;on the middle Niger, the&nbsp;Kede&nbsp;and the Kakanda between Jebba and&nbsp;Lokoja and the Wurbo and the&nbsp;Jukun&nbsp;on the Benue, river fishing is a specialist vocation.</strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>(d) Trading and Transportation: The River Niger is an important source of water transportation. More than three-quarters of the total length of the river is used for commercial transportation.</strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>The middle course of the River Niger is navigable to small craft during high water as far downstream as Ansongo (a small town in the Gao region of eastern Mali) - some 1,770 km in all. Below Ansongo, 690 km downstream from Timbucktu, navigability is interrupted by a series of defiles and rapids. The river becomes navigable to small vessels again at the river port village of Labbezanga - from which it flows into Niger - and continues to be navigable up to the Atlantic Ocean.</strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>From Onitsha to the confluence of the River Benue and River Niger, large vessels can move for 10 months of the year (June–March).</strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>From Lokoja to Jebba,&nbsp;the River Niger is navigable to all craft only from October to mid-November. Above Jebba the River Niger is navigable only to smaller craft and this is dependent locally on periods when water levels are adequate. Thus, Jebba is in effect the head of navigation of the Niger waterway. Extreme fluctuation in water level at times constitutes&nbsp;a major handicap to vessels plying beyond Lokoja.</strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>Navigation in the lower stretch is made possible by the influx of water from the Benue River which is at its high level in June.</strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>(e) Pastoral Cattle Rearing: Livestock raising is one of the principal occupations in rural settlements along the upper and middle courses of the River Niger and one of the main sources of income. Due to climatic fluctuations, nomadic cattle herders move their herds of cattle and sheep to the northern Sahel during the rainy season and down to the southern parts in search of grazing land and drinking water during the dry season.</strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>&nbsp;(f) Environment and Culture</strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>The culture, traditions and spiritual life of riverine settlements are also influenced by their environment. For instance, the Ogiamien title of the Itsekiri Kingdom monarchs has something to do with water. The boat regatta is also one of the kingdom’s most impressive cultural events.</strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>For centuries, t</strong><strong>he Niger Delta’s Ijaw, Ogoni and other people coexisted peacefully with one another and their neighbors. In <em>temperament, </em>they were generally easy going and the tranquil surroundings kept them away from hinterland aside for trade and inter-marriage.</strong><strong></strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>This<em> was </em>until contact with Europeans and when the region became part of Nigeria in 1914. The discovery and large-scale exploitation of crude oil in 1956 the area shattered the peaceful life of the people.</strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>(xiv) Problems of the River Niger</strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>The River Niger is plagued by two significant issues that detract from its natural beauty. These are natural and man-made factors.</strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>The first is a climatic condition that causes</strong><strong> d</strong><strong>rought such as what happened in 1985 and 1990, when some areas of the river dried up almost completely.</strong><strong> </strong><strong>Water scientists believe that the River Niger’s volume has dropped by one third in the previous thirty years.</strong><strong></strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>The second main factor is Africa’s fast </strong><strong>increasing population and pollution caused by unregulated human activities.</strong><strong> Urbanization, industrialization, intensive farming practices, deforestation, use of chemical fertilizers and pesticides and emission from the consumption of fossil fuel, all contribute considerably to the degradation of the River Niger environment.</strong><strong></strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>In 2004, representatives of nine member-states of the Niger Basin Authority (NBA), held a two-day summit in Paris, France, and signed a blueprint for protection of the bio-diversity and ecosystem of the River Niger and its tributaries.</strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>On pollution, the Paris summit noted that the Malian capital, Bamako, lies on the banks of the River Niger. Nearly all the city’s commercial and residential effluent drains into the river, untreated. Other Malian towns further east follow suit. The same situation occurs in Niamey, capital of Niger, through Burkina Faso and the commercial city of Onitsha in Nigeria.</strong><strong></strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>Pollution poses severe hazards, particularly water-borne diseases such as diarrhea which spreads through contaminated water.</strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>Moreover, as the water table shrinks due to an annually decreasing rainfall, pollutants become increasingly concentrated in the river.</strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>Another element that has had a negative influence is the spread of water hyacinth. This floating, aquatic weed originates in South America’s Amazon basin and was first introduces to Africa as an ornamental plant. It thrives in stagnant and filthy streams, depriving river water of oxygen, destroying fish and aquatic life. Water hyacinth spread on the River Niger has had major environmental repercussions as it has in East and Southern Africa.</strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>Plastic debris has also presented a significant threat in the River Niger, causing pollution and amphibian suffocation.</strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>The River Niger is likewise being choked by silt (from runoffs) at an alarming rate.</strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>As a combined result of increasing population, drought and shrinking water volume, river fishing communities have become increasingly vulnerable as fish stocks have dwindled.</strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>One of the grave social problems that has arisen from pattern of adaptation of communities to the River Niger environment is widespread conflicts between nomadic Fulani cattle herders and farming communities.</strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>A frightening dimension was added to the crisis with emergence of armed Fulani herdsmen, who constantly launch attacks on farming communities and violently seize land from farmers to make way for their cattle. The security challenge posed by this category of herders stretches from Middle Belt states in Nigeria such as Benue and Taraba to most states in South-East, South-South and South-West Nigeria.</strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>A report by Jose Luis Baran, an independent researcher based in Brussels, Belgium, revealed that between 2017 and May 2020, armed Fulani herdsmen killed 2,539 people in 654 attacks in Nigeria. (Bayo Akinloye, Report: Fulani Herdsmen Killed 2,539 Nigerians in 654 Attacks, THISDAY online).</strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>It is claimed that some of the criminals carrying out the attacks are aliens from neighboring countries. However, the ethnically and religiously skewed structure of the Nigerian security apparatus makes it impossible for farming communities to get the protection they need from the Federal Government. </strong><strong></strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>Nine countries share the Niger River Basin. Multiple uses and possible trade-offs need to be considered and assessed along this transboundary watershed, especially against the backdrop of climate change and demographic trends.</strong><strong></strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>&nbsp;(xv) Scramble for the Wealth of the Niger Basin</strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>From the 16<sup>th</sup> century onwards, the rich and huge natural resources of the Niger Basin drew international competition to exploit them. Although the Portuguese were the first Europeans to discover &nbsp;&nbsp;“the land of the Blacks” in the mid-14th century, the British outwitted other European nations in manipulative and brutal conquest and exploitation of the Niger Territories’ human and natural resources.</strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>When the British Government prohibited slave trading in the British Empire in 1833, British explorers and slave merchants switched to what they termed “legitimate trade”. The Niger Delta was abundantly endowed with palm oil production which British and other European countries required to power Europe’s budding industrial revolution.</strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>The export of palm oil was monopolized by European countries. They determined the prices at which they would purchase palm fruit from Niger Delta residents in exchange for worthless commodities such as hot beverages (dry gin), coloured beads, mirrors, hats and walking sticks which they referred to as “free trade”.</strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>Thus, the First Scramble for the Niger Delta involved competition among European powers for a share of the palm produce of the Niger Delta, an area which they named the “Oil Rivers”.</strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>Another leg of the First Scramble was intensive and extensive exploitation of timber in the rain forest region with its abundance of tall trees.</strong><strong></strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>For nearly 100 years, covering the period of 50 years before and another 50 years after Nigerian Independence, the virgin forest of the lower forest zone of the Niger Delta which Mother Nature endowed her since time began was mercilessly exploited by timber loggers for expot to Europe.&nbsp;</strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>Indeed, the first experience of a moving motor vehicle which I personally ever had in my village was that of heavy-duty lorries known as “motor-<em>igedu” </em>which ferried logs of wood away from forests in my area in the late 1940s and early 1950s.</strong><strong></strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>(xvi) Paradox of the Splendour of the River Niger</strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>The second scramble for the natural resources of the River Niger is more entrenched and ongoing. Unlike the First Scramble, the discovery and large-scale exploitation of crude oil and natural gas in the Niger Delta is carried out by multinational corporations in such a way that people have no knowledge of what is going on and only experience the consequences.</strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>Foreign exchange gains from crude oil sales were projected to dramatically improve Nigeria’s standard of living. On the contrary, it became a harbinger of ruin for the surrounding communities, comparable to what Chief Obafemi Awolowo once called “<em>damnosa hereditas</em>”, an accursed heritage involving misery.</strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>In the Niger Delta, indigenes suffer, foreigners enjoy. The nearer you are to sources of wealth (crude oil), the poorer you are. This is the paradox of the Niger Delta. Outsiders gain, insiders lose.</strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>This applies not only to the oil bearing areas that suffer the brunt of oil doom in relation to the Nigerian state but also to the country as a whole where foreigners and their corrupt local agents gain the larger benefit than honest toiling citizens of Nigeria.</strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>Surely, foreign banks keep more of proceeds of sale of &nbsp;Nigeria’s crude oil and natural gas than Nigerian banks, courtesy of big looters and oil thieves.</strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>Most Nigerian billionaires keep the illicit money they make from crude oil theft in foreign banks. The foreigners then lend the money back to the country, saddled with “conditionalities”,</strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>Perhaps, the most notable examples of the paradox briefly described here are Oloibiri and </strong><strong>adjoining communities of the local government areas where crude oil was first struck in commercial quantities</strong><strong> by Shell in 1956 and Ogoni land where Shell started drilling crude oil shortly after Oloibiri.</strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>Oloibiri is in total decay.&nbsp; The site of the oil wells is now wasteland. Shell and the Federal Government of Nigeria extracted the oil, devastated the environment and deserted the place. All that remains are polluted streams infested with fat mosquitoes, unproductive farmlands and capped empty wells while oil companies have long moved to new sites where they serially carry out similar devastations.</strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>The tragedy of Oloibiri is not merely that Shell and Nigerian Government stole the oil and left the place as wasteland.</strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>2008 picture of Oloibiri Oil Well 1 and 2</strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>&nbsp;which Shell left about 1977</strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>Source: Nairaland Forum</strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>Shell and the Federal Government of Nigeria left the place worse than they met it. The means of subsistence of the people (farming and fishing) was destroyed without any replacement. In order to develop Oloibiri, one has first to undo the colossal environmental damage done to the land and creeks by Shell and the Nigerian Government before one could think of how to start developing the place.</strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>The construction of a Specialist Hospital in the area which was billed to be the nucleus of the Rivers State University Teaching Hospital was abandoned when the military junta led by Major General Mohammadu Buhari (retd.) sacked the civilian government on December 31, 1983.</strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>Even the foundation for a National Oil Museum (NOM) laid by late President Shehu Shagari in 1980 remains abandoned.</strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>The major function of police posts in the area is to harass innocent and peaceful villagers and arrest people who produce native gin which government labels “illicit”.</strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>Given the mindless greed of the Nigerian ruling elite, it is feared that the fate of Oloibiri awaits much of the oil-bearing areas of the Niger Delta.</strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>If Nigeria presents a clear case of intense human suffering characterised by much labour without commensurate rewards, the Niger Delta epitomises the Nigerian paradox.</strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>(xvii) Developmental Projects Based on the River Niger</strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>Development refers to a process of planned material or social change that conduces to life sustenance, self-esteem and freedom. It is a measure of the intrinsic welfare of citizens.</strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>Since the middle of the 20<sup>th</sup> century, more sophisticated technology has been used in the construction of dams for irrigation, hydroelectric power and modern transportation systems along the River Niger.</strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>Mali, Niger and Burkina Faso are three of the world’s most destitute landlocked nations and improved management of the River Niger’s resources is a difficulty they face on a daily basis.</strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>Over the years, river navigation along the River Niger has significantly improved. The main River Niger ports in Mali are located at Koulikoro and Timbuktu while those in Nigeria are located at Onitsha, Burutu and Koko,</strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>There are rail bridges over the River Niger at Koulikoro in Mali, Jebba in Nigeria and another over the River Benue at Makurdi,</strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>Road bridges over the River Niger include those at Bamako, Segao, in Mali, Niamey in Niger, Malanville in Benin and Kainji, Jebba, Lokoja, and Onitsha in Nigeria. There are also road bridges over the River Benue at Makurdi, Numan and Yola. Ferries cross the Niger at Bamako, Gao, Niamey, Yelwa and Idah; there are also ferry crossings of the Benue at Garoua.</strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>Among large scale power station and irrigation systems developed on the River Niger and some of its tributaries are:</strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:list -->\n<ul><!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><strong>Fomi Dam in Guinea (hydroelectric and irrigation potential of Guinea and Mali);</strong></li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><strong>Selingue hydro-electric dam, Mali;</strong></li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><strong>Markala dam (irrigation) in Mali;</strong></li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><strong>Sotuba Hydro-Electric Power Plant in Mali;</strong></li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><strong>Goronyo dam on Rima River Sokoto State (irrigation);</strong></li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><strong>Bakolori dam, on Sokoto River (irrigation);</strong></li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><strong>Gusau dam (on Sokoto River), Zamfara State;</strong></li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><strong>Zungeru Power Station (near completion)</strong></li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><strong>Kainji hydro-electric dam, Borgu LGA, Niger State, Nigeria;</strong></li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><strong>Jebba hydro-electric power station across the Niger River on the border between Niger State and Kwara State;</strong></li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><strong>Dadin Kowa dam on Gongola River situated in Yamaltu Deba LGA of Gombe State;</strong></li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><strong>Lagdo reservoir on the River Benue in Cameroon (intended for hydro-electric power and irrigation);</strong></li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><strong>Nigeria has about 38 power generating plants (bothhyrdo and thermal).</strong></li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item --></ul>\n<!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>The&nbsp;Niger River Commission was formed in 1963 to coordinate multinational cooperation in developing the River Niger and its tributaries. On November 21, 1980, it was refounded as the Niger Basin Authority (NBA) but its success has been mediocre.</strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>(xviii) Present Condition of Niger Basin Communities</strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>The Niger Basin seems to have a low level of technological progress and this reflects in the low standard of living of the population. Mismanagement of economic infrastructure, political instability and a high level of insecurity are other problems.</strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>The majority of the region\'s hydro-electric and thermal power plants are either closed or running at considerably below capacity. As a result, just 35</strong><strong> per cent of the population has access to electricity.</strong><strong></strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>Despite the enormous water resources of the River Niger and its tributaries, 30 per cent of the area’s population has no access to improved water supply and 75 per cent lack access to improved sanitation.</strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>Furthermore, the level of insecurity is concerning. For instance, the Nigerian Security Tracker, an initiative of the Council on Foreign Relations of the USA, revealed in October 2022 that 53,418 people were killed by non-state actors in Nigeria under the watch of Major General Muhammadu Buhari (retd.) between May 29, 2015 and October 14, 2022. The former military dictator was elected as President and Commander-in-Chief of the Armed Forces of the Federal Republic of Nigeria in 2015, promising to combat insecurity. (Leke Baiyewu, Tope Omogbolagun, Solomon Odeniyi, Sami Tunji, Deborah Tolu-Kolawole, Damilola Aina and Justin Tyupuusu, N’East leads as terrorists, others kill 53,418 under Buhari, PUNCH NEWSPAPERS, October 23, 2022).</strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>According to the UNDP Human Development Index (2018), nine countries of the Niger Basin are at the worst third of 189 countries.&nbsp; (Water-energy-food.org, Niger Basin).</strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>(xix) The Curse of Crude Oil and Gas Exploitation in the Niger Delta</strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>Taking the River Niger to represent the physical environment and its people, the Splendour of the Niger is subjected to considerable stress by poor quality of political leadership in the region. Nowhere is this debility more starkly exemplified than the unbelievable level of ineptitude brought into the management of Nigeria’s huge crude oil and natural gas endowments.</strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>There are about 94 oil producing countries in the world and Nigeria is among the top ten flarers of gas. Approximately 75 per cent of total gas produced is flared while about 95 per cent of associated gas used as by-product is also flared.</strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>Gas flares cause acid rain, which damages crops and vegetation and acidifies rivers and streams. It reduces farm yields and harms human health by raising the risk of respiratory conditions such as asthma, chronic bronchitis and lung conditions like cancer as well as other conditions like blindness, impotence, miscarriages and early deaths.</strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>The case of Ogoni land in Gokana LGA of Rivers State serves as a stark illustration. </strong><strong>Environmental Assessment of Ogoni territory, a Report from the United Nations Environment Programme (UNEP) in August 2011, on the consequences of oil exploitation in Ogoni region, reveals that the operation of oil firms left the land in turmoil and an obvious environmental disaster.</strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>In order to determine how much the ecosystem has been degraded and how prevalent it is, a UN team evaluated every component of the environment as well as the health of the local population. They looked for health issues among the locals that might be caused by petroleum throughout their health examination. According to their findings, the soil, vegetation, surface water and ground water have all been seriously damaged.</strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>Permanent heat and lack of darkness at night have affected human, animal and plant life in the area incalculably. Thick sooth covers the affected areas, making rain water unfit for human consumption. Palm plants become scorched in the heat while rubber trees stop producing. This poses a serious threat to the local population’s subsistence farming, ecology, veggies, dwellings and metallic minerals.</strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>Since Shell started drilling oil wells in Ogoni land in 1958, the entire landscape was taken over by oil rigs and intricate network of pipelines.</strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>Oil&nbsp; Industry Infrastructures in Ogoni land (UNEP, 2011)</strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>The UNEP Report criticized Shell Petroleum Development Company’s (SPDC) handling of environmental degradation in Ogoni area. It discovered that the world’s largest ever clean up would be required, with an initial cost of $1 billion (about N500 billion) and a clean-up time of 30 years.</strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>The region is plagued by “administrative neglect, crumbling social infrastructure and services, high unemployment, social deprivation, abject poverty, filth and squalor, and endemic conflict”, according to the UNEP report.</strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>Crude oil exploitation does not only devastate the environment and livelihood of the people, it also devastates other economic, cultural and social activities.&nbsp; “The majority of the people of the Niger Delta do not have adequate access to clean water or health-care.”</strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>The UNEP draws attention to a further threat to communities in the Niger Delta that results from the negligence of those in charge of the country’s oil sector.</strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>“In the Niger Delta, oil spills, garbage dumping and gas flaring are widespread. The land, water and air quality have all been harmed by this pollution, which has been present in the area for decades.”</strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>&nbsp;The report went on to say that “hundreds of thousands of people are affected, notably the poorest and those who depend on traditional livelihoods like fishing and agriculture.”</strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>The Nigerian National Petroleum Corporation (NNPC), now NNPC Limited, estimates that approximately 2,300 cubic metres of crude oil are spilled in 300 separate incidents annually. Also, between 1970 and 2000, between 9million and 13million barrels of crude oil was spilled in the region. The World Bank however states that actual volume of spills may be ten times greater as most spills are not reported. (Yosef Mahmoud, The State of the Niger Delta Oil Spill – The Borgen Project online)</strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading {\"level\":1} -->\n<h1><strong>In the last 20 years, about 2,000 Niger Deltans have been killed in pipeline related explosions and accidents in the Region. One of the most horrific incidents occurred in Jesse, near Sapele, Delta State on October 17, 1998. People had been taking fuel where it was gushing out from a pipeline which they later hawk in jerry cans along Benin-Sapele Road. An account of the incident by Wale Fawale (The Guardian, October 24, 1998, p 5), reveals that over 1,000 people were roasted to death in that incident.</strong><strong></strong></h1>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>(xx) Enhancing the Splendour of the River Niger</strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>Mother Nature gave River Niger Basin residents a plethora of natural riches that is the envy of many other countries. </strong><strong></strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>The greater part of Nigeria’s 923,768km<sup>2</sup> area encompass fertile agricultural terrain suited for the growth of a wide range of food crops including yam, cassava, cocoa-yam, maize, onion, melon, okra and many kinds of vegetables, as well as palm trees, cocoa, rubber, cotton, groundnut and millets.</strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>A wide range of timber including iroko, mahogany, which are now severely reduced due to relentless cutting over the past 100 years, is found in the vast evergreen rain forest region.</strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>Access to sea food is ensured by the River Niger as well as its extensive coastal waters that border the Atlantic.</strong><strong></strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>Metal, ceramic, chemical, metallurgical and refractory, industrial and mineral fuel deposits are found in the subsoil of the area.</strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>Thinking of enhancing the splendor of the Niger, many ideas of innovative projects spring to mind. These include:</strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:list -->\n<ul><!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><strong>Restoration of river banks;</strong></li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><strong>Opening natural river channels blocked by unplanned human habitation;</strong></li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><strong>Harnessing the tourism potentials of the River Niger/Benue confluence and other areas;</strong></li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><strong>Dredging and development of shipping ports to accommodate larger vessels that will facilitate export of farm produce from the hinterland; such ports will reduce frequency of heavy-duty vehicles moving goods over long distances and cut down current high rate of accidents and also make it easier to maintain existing roads;</strong></li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><strong>The Koko Port is a viable additional port for ocean going vessels and this would relieve the perennial congestion at Apapa Port in Lagos;</strong></li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><strong>The River Niger and its tributaries are also apparently sources of additional energy;</strong></li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><strong>Joint stake-holders engagement in remediation of the Niger Delta environment which has been destroyed by Shell and Nigerian Government;</strong></li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><strong>Boat building industry.</strong></li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item --></ul>\n<!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>The Niger Delta also has potential for a thriving palm wine industry but this potential is not exploited. The fishing potential is also under-exploited.</strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>One however realises that there has never been shortage of suggestions for improving the environment. The critical issue has always been the human factor. For example, the dredging project of the River Niger commenced by late President Umaru Musa Yar-Adua’s administration in 2009 has been neglected by the Federal Government.</strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>The level and quality of development in any society is dependent on the level and quality of leadership. In turn, the quality of leadership is dependent on quality of minds that choose leaders. Both are intricately inter-related.</strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>The ingredients of democratic governance such as rule of law and periodic elections are necessary but not sufficient conditions for development. In his Presidential Address entitled <em>Charter of Stability and Progress which he </em>delivered at the Second Annual Congress of the UPN at Eko Hotel, Lagos, on Tuesday, December 11, 1979, Chief Awolowo declared: <u></u></strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>“What is required is that every Government of the day must recognise the ultimate purpose of the state - which is the welfare of the entire people without discrimination and having recognised that purpose, to set about making plans for its attainment. But no plan for the welfare of the people will ever succeed unless the very first principle in the pursuit of this ultimate purpose is fully recognized.</strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>“The first principle is that: man is the sole creative and purposive in nature: everything else by comparison is inert.&nbsp; In all the spheres of production, distribution, exchange and consumption, he is the initiator and accelerator of every form of human progress: he is the generator of every initial impulse to human evolution.</strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>“Any plan for progress therefore, which places man in a secondary place is basically in error, antithetic to the natural order of human endeavours, and is bound to fail, as well as a bane rather than a blessing to the people.” </strong>&nbsp;<strong>(Charter of Stability and Progress, p 24-6).</strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>For the above reasons, Awoist vision of a prosperous Nigerian nation-state lays utmost emphasis on investment in man with a view to increasing human power over the challenges of the physical and social environment.&nbsp; Children inherit the biological endowments of their parents which define body characteristics and intelligence.&nbsp; However, intelligence and acquisition of relevant skills are nurtured in a social environment which may stimulate or stultify it.</strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>At the first Guardian Lecture marking the newspaper’s fifth anniversary in July 1988, Mr. Michael Manley, former Jamaican Prime Minister asserted:</strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>“Third world societies cannot seriously tackle their major structural problems without the broadest involvement of their populations.”</strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>He further observed that:</strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>“Economic independence begins and ends with the skills of the people who wish to enjoy it.”</strong><strong> (The Guardian, Tuesday, July 12, 1988, p 17).</strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>Enhancing the Splendour of the Niger presents a human challenge rather than a technical or technological one. The world has the technology to address its fundamental developmental issues. Examples of nations that have surmounted environmental constraints to provide their citizens with excellent levels of living include Israel, Japan and United Arab Emirates (UAE). The main issue in enhancing the Splendor of the Niger therefore, is how to create a visionary and disciplined leadership that is focused on the real development of the sub-region.</strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>It is obvious that a government that places less emphasis on investing in people and permits students in federal tertiary educational institutions in Nigeria to stay at home for 8 months due to misplaced priorities and a refusal to uphold minimum standards in tertiary educational institutions cannot enhance the splendor of the Niger, taking the Niger to symbolize the environment and people of Nigeria or the Niger Basin.</strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>(xxi) The Core Message of The Splendour of the Niger</strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>In concluding this address, Ladies and Gentlemen, kindly permit me to present what I consider to be the core message of The Splendour of the Niger. Nature has a silent and unobtrusive way of impressing vital lessons on man if only he cares to look. The River Niger and its tributaries and indeed the natural world, constitute an abiding object lesson in building up, conservation and self-preservation through the law of cycles.</strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>The River Niger is magnificent because it is constantly replenished by its tributaries to become the gigantic natural phenomenon that it is. If it should stand alone without its tributaries, it would probably dry up rather than increase tremendously before the end of its 4,000plus kilometre journey from the Fouta Djalon highlands to the Atlantic Ocean.</strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>Therefore, the River Niger teaches us the value of co-operation and team-work in producing great results. As far back as the 4th century BCE, the ancient Greek philosopher Aristotle (384-322 BCE) posited that “the whole is greater than the sum of its parts.”&nbsp; For example, if each item required to build this edifice is placed alone, each would most certainly not serve as much as when they are combined to build it. The same goes for modern technological equipment.</strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>Development failure in Nigeria is ample demonstration of the fact that large land mass, huge population and abundant natural and human resources do not necessarily add up to wellbeing of citizens or development.</strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>Here comes the critical factor of what has been called “intangible assets” in terms of human and institutional strength in any geographical space. In practical terms, these translate to attitudinal factors, positive values, common goals, unity of purpose and other factors which enable any society to deploy the skills of its citizens for the achievement of group goals. Indisputably, relevant technical competence of individual role occupants and availability of material resources are indispensable but they are not sufficient to achieve development.</strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>There is a more telling dimension of backwardness which is more invidious, yet less amenable to measurement. It is the structure of private and social thinking about the world, about society and governance which some social psychologists refer to as Group Intelligence (GI).</strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>Nigeria is a country replete with individual intelligence but little or no “group intelligence”. This explains why Nigerians perform brilliantly abroad but fall short of their capabilities in their own country. This is a place where, in most cases, individual intelligence is applied to frustrate and checkmate group objectives, thereby depleting rather than replenishing the Splendor of the Niger.</strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>Africans and the entire world are waiting for Nigeria to lead the way in restoring the dignity of the Black Man in the world. A great Nigerian nation-state will reverberate across Africa as a model of the new Africa.</strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>I respectfully submit that the missing link in the Splendor of the Niger, which I equate with the development of the Niger Basin, is visionary and disciplined political leadership that builds rather than divides. In other words, Nigeria needs a leadership that fosters Group Intelligence by mobilizing all the human and material resources in the country to achieve the goals of the state rather than private, ethnic or theocratic goals.</strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>The 2023 presidential election offers each and every one of us an opportunity to contribute to enhancement of the Splendour of the Niger, taken to represent the bigger Nigerian state or the Niger Basin.</strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>In this respect, any politician can reel out an impressive array or list of developmental projects. The pertinent question then is the vision of such a leader or his ideological orientation. What kind of society does he have in mind? Is it more of the same or a fundamental shift in the direction of the society? The capacity of such a politician and his credibility, based on his or her antecedents also come to play.</strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>A politician, for example, whose personal and business life is opaque or shrouded in secrecy, who cannot be transparent about his personal life, or who looks the other way when heinous crimes are committed by his religious or ethnic group without speaking out boldly, is most unlikely to be a credible candidate to lead the largest Black Country in the world.</strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>I further submit that character of leaders is key because the Nigerian experience has shown that whatever the resources available in a state, if the human beings that occupy that space are debased, the place is thereby debased. The effect is more crippling if the debased constitute the ruling class in the society. This is the core of the Nigerian predicament.</strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>On this score, I wish to appreciate the Old Boys Association of ICC for </strong><strong>keeping alive the spirit of this great citadel of nurturing human intellect and character beyond the walls of that noble institution. By </strong><strong>applying group intelligence to organize this Annual Convention, you preserve in perpetuity, the objectives for which the college was founded for the advancement of human civilization.</strong><strong></strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>By virtue of this, I pray with the Psalmist that the ICC and all its alumni “shall be like a tree planted by the rivers of water, that bringeth forth his fruit in its season; its leaves also shall not wither; and whatsoever he doeth shall prosper.” Psalm 1:3 (KJV)</strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>Mr. Chairman, distinguished ladies and gentlemen, I believe that each of us here today can play a vital role in enhancing the Splendour of the Niger and I so entrust you with this sacred responsibility.</strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>I thank you very much for listening.</strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>References</strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>Awolowo, Obafemi. Charter of Stability and Progress, Presidential Address delivered at the Second Annual Congress of the Unity Party of Nigeria at Eko Hotel, Lagos, on Tuesday, 11<sup>th</sup> December, 1979, p 24-6.</strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>Caballera, Paula. The Niger River Delta – A Strategic Asset in Africa’s Sahel Region, October 20, 2014</strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>Meek, Charles Kingsley. The History of a Name, The Journal of African History, Vol. 1, No. 1, 1960</strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>Nwanze-Obi, Felix. The British Occupation of the Niger Territories (1830-1914), Mercury (Overseas) Press, Great Britain, 1976</strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>United Nations Environmental Programme (UNEP). Environment Assessment of Ogoniland, (2011),&nbsp; ISBN: 978-92-807-3130-9 Job No.: DEP/1337/GE</strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->', 'SPLENDOUR OF THE NIGER', '', 'publish', 'open', 'closed', '', 'splendour-of-the-niger', '', '', '2023-01-12 09:12:49', '2023-01-12 09:12:49', '', 0, 'https://boldtimes.com.ng/?p=1088', 0, 'post', '', 0);
INSERT INTO `wp5g_posts` (`ID`, `post_author`, `post_date`, `post_date_gmt`, `post_content`, `post_title`, `post_excerpt`, `post_status`, `comment_status`, `ping_status`, `post_password`, `post_name`, `to_ping`, `pinged`, `post_modified`, `post_modified_gmt`, `post_content_filtered`, `post_parent`, `guid`, `menu_order`, `post_type`, `post_mime_type`, `comment_count`) VALUES
(1145, 1, '2024-09-19 16:47:53', '2024-09-19 16:47:53', '<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>THE CONCEPT OF ANIOMA STATE<br>By Chris O. O. Biose<br>chrisoobiose@gmail.com</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The main issue in the current debate over creation of Anioma State is not whether the people of the area desire such a state. That was settled over 50 years ago in several petitions for the creation of the state. As I wrote at page 150 of my book entitled Anioma: Path to Political Emancipation, BOLD Publishers Limited, Lagos, 2011;</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>“No segment of the Nigerian society has had a longer or more justifiable demand for state creation than Anioma. From 1938, through 1975 and 1980 to date, the Aniomas consistently clamoured for a state of their own. Unfortunately, the vagaries of political power structures in Nigeria have prevented the realization of this dream.”</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Meaning of ANIOMA<br>The name Anioma is an acronym for the four local government areas of Aniocha, Ndokwa, Ika and Oshimili in the defunct Bendel State, formerly Mid-Western State, which was carved out of Western Region on August 9, 1963.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The name is made up of the first letters of these four local government areas, namely A, N, I, and O. In the common language of the area, Ani means land while Anio… means, “land of”. The suffix ‘MA’ or ‘oma’ means good. Hence, Anioma means “the good land” or “the land of good people”.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>According to mystical tradition, the sound ‘MA’ signifies the mothering force in nature. Hence, the spiritual significance of the name Anioma is that of a land where Mother Nature provides good in prolific abundance.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The coinage Anioma is attributed to the late Chief Dennis C. Osadebay, Premier of the defunct Mid-western region.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The Anioma people inhabit the area west of the lower Niger River. In current political grouping, it is located in the South-South geo-political zone or South-South Region of Nigeria. In Delta State, it is called Delta North Senatorial District.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Anioma Culture Area<br>Anioma is a part of the subculture of Western Igbo civilization within the socio-geographical area west of the River Niger best described as Anioma land. These include Enuani, Ika and Ndokwa people, all of whom collectively make up Anioma, but they have different dialects.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Map of Anioma Culture Area</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Source: Wikipedia Online</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>According to Professor M. Angulu Onwuejeogwu (1972), the anthropological concept of culture area is “based on empirical observation that cultures have spatial or geographical distribution traits, complexes and patterns at any one given period.” A culture area is made up of geographically discreet regions wherein comparable culture complexes are found.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Culture consists of the way of life of a particular people. It is a social rather than a physiological heritage. Every people have their culture and a memory of the ways their forefathers had done things.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Language is a dominant theme in culture. It is one of the principal ways of identifying an ethnic, cultural or subcultural group.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>In defining Anioma subculture within the Igbo culture area in Africa, Professor Onwuejeogwu states:</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>“People living within the Igbo culture area, apart from the two known enclaves, speak the same language and so constitute what linguists call a speech community. Within this speech community, there are scores of regional dialects. Amongst West Niger Igbo, there are three dialect clusters, Ika dialects, Ukwuani dialects and Enuani dialects.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>“All dialects of the same language share a common structure and a common written form amongst other things. Dialects of the same language may differ from each other in pronunciation, grammatical construction and vocabulary, but communication between speakers of different dialects remains possible. Hence, the peoples of Agbor and Umunede of the Ika dialect of West Niger Igbo can communicate after sometime with peoples of Owerri dialect or Nsukka dialect or Etche dialect or Ikwerre dialect of the East Niger Igbo, while the Ika may not communicate with their nearest neighbours, the Ishan, except one happens to understand the language of the other.”</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Anioma dialects are basically part of Igbo language group. Most Anioma people have Igbo names. In addition, most Anioma towns and villages also have Igbo names. Clearly, the various Anioma people are part of the Igbo Culture Area.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Distinctive Characteristics of Anioma Civilisation<br>Clearly, language lends considerable support to the construction of history and ethnology but it is not wholly determinative. In spite of indisputable historical and cultural affinities in the distant past, present day Anioma has grown increasingly distinct from Eastern Igbo culture as differences in social institutions and attitude to life widened over the centuries. Anioma people continuously evolved a distinct cultural identity which increasingly marks them out in the African world. Therefore, the fact that Anioma land is historically and culturally a part of the Igbo Culture Area does not in any way mean that Anioma people are on all fours with present-day Igbos East of the River Niger.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Civilisation has to do with how a people try to cope with their existential problems. Although Anioma is contiguous to several neighbours, it is united by cultural homogeneity and value consensus about its universe of experience. In addition to similarities in material culture such as mode of dressing, arts and crafts, Anioma people also developed certain cultural values, norms, mores and taboos that distinguish them as one people. This has to do with social ethics, concepts and notions of good and bad, of fairness and justice, which are fairly similar within the culture area. These are enshrined in the form of proverbs and anecdotes commonly shared by the people.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Anioma is an authentic cultural expression of the African people. The fundamental value ideal in Anioma culture is ezi madu (a good person) or nwa ezi madu (child of a good person). The values upon which this ideal is built include ezi-okwu (truthfulness), trust, honour, hard work, bravery and personal integrity. These are indispensable components of what constitutes ezi madu.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The social ideology of the people is egalitarian rather than individualistic and exploitative. This social ideology entails a meritocracy made up of those who are worthy of praise, men who are best in their fields as judged by their fellowmen.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Anioma has deep respect for character and integrity as well as deference to elders and women. The Aniomas are known to be peaceful, law-abiding, considerate and accommodating.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>One of the most significant cultural symbols of Anioma is akwa-ocha. (home woven white cloth). It was produced and worn by the people and this marked them out in any cultural show. Households cultivated cotton in their farms. These were harvested, dried and processed to produce the soft fibre for spinning in their local looms. Crafts women utilised these to produce all-white wrapper famously known as akwa-ocha. The final product was also known as ogbenye-apani, which literally means ‘not to be lifted by the poor’: figuratively, the maxim indicates that akwa-ocha is ‘beyond the means of paupers’, due to the rigorous process of its production.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The soil in most parts of Enuani and Ika as well as some parts of Ukwuani is laterite red colour, yet the people delight in and scrupulously maintain akwa-ocha. The choice of white is a symbol of the general social aspiration of the people to live unblemished lives. Anioma people place high value on cleanliness of mind and body. Anything filthy is abhorrent among the people.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Another significant cultural symbol is ekpele (flute) which is used to greet or praise chiefs and royalty.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Anioma Resistance to Forces of Oppression<br>Anioma civilisation is age-old. The people however suffered violent and severe distortions inflicted by external forces over the past 500 years.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>From the 16th and 17th centuries, Anioma fought wars of resistance against Benin expansionist proclivity.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>As many other nationalities close to the sea on the West Coast of Africa, the Anioma nation suffered the pangs of the slave trade from the 18th century up to the 19th century.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>At the turn of the 19th century, Anioma people fought gallantly to resist desecration of their culture by colonial adventurers as evident in the Ekumeku Resistance Movement which lasted from 1894-1910.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>In January 1914, the British colonialists amalgamated the Protectorate of Northern Nigeria with the Colony and Protectorate of Southern Nigeria to form one country christened Protectorate of Nigeria. The colonialists split the large territory into administrative units such as Regions, Provinces, Divisions and Districts.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>In this process, Anioma was split into parts and joined to alien groups who then acquired political precedence over Anioma people in spite of the fact that the people lived in unity before the territory that later became Nigeria was colonised by the British.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Asaba Division was joined to Benin Province while Aboh Division was joined to Urhobos, Ijaw and Itsekiri in Warri Province. For decades, most of Ndokwa land was placed under Warri Province. During this long period, the Ndokwas looked to the Urhobos, not only as neighbours but as political leaders. Thus, the colonialists did incalculable violence to the unity of Anioma people by balkanizing them between the then Benin and Warri provinces. There was no referendum. It was purely British administrative convenience.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>In 1922, Anioma leaders sent a delegation to London to correct the anomaly. Another protest letter was officially lodged by the Obi of Aboh in 1937.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Again in 1951, Chief Dennis Osadebay (Oshimili), Chief F. H. Utomi (Aniocha), Obi of Akumazi (Ika), Chief Frank Oputa Otutu and Mr. J. A. P. Oki (both of Ndokwa), jointly moved a motion in the then Western House of Assembly to seek “a separate province” for Anioma people who had been balkanized and merged with Benin and Warri provinces by the colonialists.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>After prolonged protests by Ndokwa leaders, the area was finally joined administratively to their brethren in other parts of Anioma land. The people enjoyed relative peace until the catastrophe of the Nigeria-Biafra war and its aftermath.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Anioma War Travails<br>Perhaps, the climax of atrocities and macabre brutality of the Nigeria-Biafra war was the Asaba massacre, aptly described by Emma Okocha in his epoch-making book, Blood on the Niger: The First Black-on-Black Genocide (Revised Edition 2010).</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The Asaba massacre was followed by several months of systematic massacres in several nearby villages notably, Isheagu. The Isheagu Massacre was led by Lt. Col. Samuel Osaigbovo Ogbemudia (as he then was). The troops arrived Isheagu at about 8:00pm, on May 2, 1968, rounded up the King, His Royal Majesty Agbogidi Obi John Onyema 1, and his chiefs and summarily executed them. Emma Okocha gave names of several males in the town who were similarly picked up and shot.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>For much of the period of the war and several years after, Anioma land was occupied by hostile troops during which period Anioma farmlands and farm produce were indiscriminately destroyed. The People suffered inestimable losses. Indigenes were shooting targets for Nigerian soldiers. They were among other Igbos who were routinely dehumanised, mutilated and murdered by Federal troops.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>During this period, hundreds of Anioma ladies were raped and taken away for forced marriage by hostile soldiers from alien nationalities.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Anioma State Movement<br>The people of Anioma recognized their unique identity in the Nigerian experiment and started demand for recognition during the era of colonial rule. At a mammoth rally held at Amai (in present day Ukwuani Local Government Area), in August 1956, they expressed their desire in a resolution passed at that rally but at that time, it was not called Anioma State. Rather, they demanded for the creation of a separate province out of the then Western Region to be known as the West Niger Province.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The proposed West Niger Province was to be formed by the then Asaba and Aboh Divisions. A headquarters for the new Province was proposed to be sited at the meeting point of the two or three components of the province. The objective was to put the Anioma people on equal footing with the rest of the Benin Province and Delta Province, rather than balkanizing Anioma between these two provinces. The resolution was forwarded to the then Colonial Secretary, Rt. Hon Lennox Boyd.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>In 1957, the British Government set up the Minorities Commission under the Chairmanship of Sir Henry Willink, QC, to examine the problems of the Minorities throughout Nigeria. The Report of the Commission however did not meet the demands of Anioma people.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Soon after Nigeria’s independence in 1960, the people of Anioma who were then in the Western Region, propelled the Mid-West State Movement and were therefore among the first to popularize and raise to national consciousness, the need for state creation in Nigeria. The Movement became a unifying force which reconcile erstwhile political parties in the Midwest, mainly the AG, NCNC, BOC and UPP, all of which worked happily towards freedom of fatherland. Erstwhile political foes become friends for the purpose of achieving an overriding political objective.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The struggle for creation of Mid-West Region lasted for 16 years before the eventual creation of the state in August 1963, renamed Bendel State by the Gowonian military junta in March 1976.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>After some time, the dynamics of Bendel society once again compelled the people of Anioma to demand a state of their own, this time properly called ANIOMA State.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The Anioma State Movement presented its petition in 1975, this time, through Comrade Nduka Eze.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Justice Ayo Irikefe, then Chairman of the Federal Committee on States, described the Anioma petition as “the most scientific proposal” of all in the country.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The principal motivators of the movement at this point in time included, Dr. George O. Orewa, Chairman, Central Working Committee, Anioma State Movement, Senator Nosike Ikpo, Prof. B. I. C. Ijomah and Chief P. O. C. Ozieh, The Olikeze of Ogwashi-Ukwu.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Again in 1977/78, Anioma leaders circulated a petition among the members of the Constituent Assembly calling for the creation of Anioma State.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>On March 14, 1980, the leaders of the Movement filed another request for the creation of Anioma State with the National Assembly. The signatories to the request were the elected representatives of Aniocha, Ndokwa, Ika and Oshimili in the councils of the four local government areas.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Between October 24 and 28, 1980, the House of Representatives Committee on the Creation of States under the chairmanship of Hon. Tuggar paid official visits to Aniocha, Ndokwa, Ika and Oshimili to verify the authenticity of the signatories to the petition for the creation of Anioma State.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The Anioma State Movement was launched in various Nigerian cities. The Lagos Branch of Anioma State Movement was launched on May 30, 1981 while Kaduna State chapter was launched on the same day.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Aberrant Joiner of Anioma With South-East Igbo<br>Some Igbo-speaking people, East of the River Niger seem to favour administrative unity with Igbo-speaking people of Enuani, Ika and Ndokwa, West of the River Niger.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>For example, on 6th January, 1978, the people of Onitsha Local Government Area and Ogbaru of Anambra State, sought to be included in the then proposed Anioma or Oshimili or Lower Niger State. In a unilateral memo submitted to the State Creation Committee, the people averred: “Our desire to be included in the proposed Anioma or Oshimili or Lower Niger State is founded on our long-standing and fervent yearning to exercise our natural right of self-determination and of association with our kith and kin with whom we share common origin, common outlook, common social values, common heritage and cultural affinity.”</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The petition was signed by H. R. H. Obi Ofala Okagbue, The Obi of Onitsha, Chief Philip O. Anatogu, Onowu-Iyasele of Onitsha, Chief J. A. Ukpabi, Ajie-Ukadiugwu, Isagba of Onitsha, Chief I. A. Mbanefo, Odu-Osodi of Onitsha, Chief J. N. Egwuatu, Ogene-Onira of Onitsha, Mr. Same Ifeka, Chairman, Onitsha Local Government for and on behalf of the people of Onitsha.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Those who signed for and on behalf of the people of Ogbaru were H.R.H. R.R. Olisa, The Atamanya of Ossomari, H.R.H. Chief J. Oduah II, The Igwe of Akili Ozizo, H.R.H. Chief Chukwudebe Ezike, The Igwe of Atani, H.R.H. Chief Philipp Ochije, The Igwe of Mputu, H.R.H. Chief F. B. C. Isamade, The Okakwu of Odekpe, H.R.H. Chief Obumselu, The Igwe of Onita, Councillor Jude K. N. Ezezie, Secretary, Ogbaru Youths Association and Chief D. C. Nworah, The Oduah of Ochuche.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Again, on November 18, 1980, the people of Onitsha and Ogbaru presented another request to be included in the then proposed Anioma State, even though no representative of that area was party to the initial request. These were however neither considered nor approved by the Anioma people.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Incongruity of Anioma and South-East Igbo<br>Ab initio, the concept of Anioma does not include any part of the geographical and cultural South-East of Nigeria.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The River Niger is a natural boundary that encouraged different directions in cultural emphasis between Anioma and South-East Igbos over the centuries. This is evident in widened differences in attitude to life.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The sum total of the issues dissected above is that Anioma is culturally and sociologically miles apart from the Eastern Igbos. Spatial separation accentuates social differentiation.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Anioma national character is defined by amiable disposition, satiated personality, tolerance, hospitability and good-neighbourliness. In this is to be found the true genius of Anioma civilization.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Except to defend themselves against aggression from expansionist machinations of belligerent kings in historical times, Anioma has not been known to go to war with neighbours in recent times.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>In traditional political affairs, Igbo enwe eze (Igbos have no kings) whereas Igbos West of the Niger had borrowed and become acculturated into the kingship tradition for centuries.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Warrant chiefs among South-East Igbos were British impositions. Kingship in those areas is largely devoid of traditional roots; it is more often purchased. The emphasis is on money – the financial power to purchase kingship. This is antithetical to Anioma culture and concept of traditional governance represented in the kingship institution.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>In spite of indisputable linguistic unity as well as historical and cultural affinities in the distant past, many present day Anioma People cannot reconcile themselves with the extreme individualism and mercantile mentality which are the hallmark of most of their Igbo brethren East of the Niger.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Most Anioma strongly resent and abhor the seeming dog-eat-dog social philosophy of South-East Igbo life style. Extreme individualism has a corrosive effect on social well-being and well-meaning Igbo leaders acknowledge this characteristic of Igbo life which is inimical to social cohesion.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Arthur A. Nwankwo (Nigeria: The Challenge of Biafra, Fourth Dimension Publishers, 1972, p 57) was on point when he suggested that “the principle of Biafran brotherhood was a distant dream in a nation where trampling on everybody else like maggots in the struggle for personal survival, was deeply ingrained in the Igbo national psyche.”</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>It will do great damage to Anioma civilization if the Eastern and Western wings of the Igbo nation, as two diverging camps of Igbo culture are brought together into one administrative unit by coercion or any form of legislative fiat rather than by mutual understanding or choice by the people.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Political Destiny of Anioma<br>The long-standing demand for Anioma State is defined in terms of the authentic yearnings of the Enuani, Ika and Ndokwa peoples of Delta North Senatorial District of Delta State. South-East Igbo has never been part of Anioma. Any suggestion by some politicians that Anioma is not economically viable without South-East Igbo is untenable and an affront on past, present and future generations of Anioma People.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Anioma constitutes an organic unity that sets it apart from other peoples on the surface of the earth and does not need to look elsewhere for inspiration and identity. As Dr. Kunirum Osia (2001) once put it, “Anioma provides for us the physical and cultural space that defines the necessary locus of our highest fulfillment as a people.” This distinct and unified cultural space and identity is recognised by its neighbours.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Anioma is imbued with natural sense of equity and social justice which is not based on legal prescriptions. For example, when Chief Dennis Osadebay became Premier of Midwestern Region in 1963, he helped in fashioning a Constitution that made provision for representation of minorities in the Region. He also ensured equitable distribution of major industries in the Region among Edo, Urhobo and Anioma.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Political arrangements need to respect the sentiments and sensibilities of communities and groups involved.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Anioma culture is a worthy bearer of humane values that must be preserved and defended. Some elite think that values do not matter; all they care for is power and wealth. This is the basis of the Nigerian predicament.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>For example, the distortion of Asaba Master Plan and absence of planning of towns and villages in Anioma is not due to non-availability of resources or skill manpower. It is a function of the greedy disposition of the political and bureaucratic elite that dominated Delta State since its inception in August 1991.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>A genuine Anioma State, not one corrupted with un-Anioma values, would present a golden opportunity to regain and cultivate a true Anioma civilization.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Imperative of Referendum or Plebiscite<br>The present Federal administration in Nigeria that was inaugurated on May 29, 2023, is not a military dictatorship. Therefore, it has no mandate to create any new state in Nigeria by executive fiat.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>It is expected that before the creation of a new state can be considered, the demand must be made formally by two-thirds majority of members representing the area in the Senate, House of Representatives, House of Assembly and all local government councils in the area demanding the new state. Section 8(1)(a) of the Constitution of the Federal Republic of Nigeria (Promulgation), 1999, Decree No. 24, refers.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>After due legislative process, the proposal must be approved in a referendum by two-thirds majority of the people of the area demanding creation of the new state. This is necessary to ascertain that the proposal is in line with the wishes of the people of the area that demand the state. Ultimately, the will of the people should prevail.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Chris O. O. Biose,<br>(The Ode-Uri)</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->', 'THE CONCEPT OF ANIOMA STATE', '', 'publish', 'open', 'closed', '', 'the-concept-of-anioma-state', '', '', '2024-09-19 16:47:53', '2024-09-19 16:47:53', '', 0, 'https://boldtimes.net.ng/?p=1145', 0, 'post', '', 0),
(1130, 1, '2024-07-08 23:01:59', '2024-07-08 23:01:59', '<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>STOP ELECTION STEALING IN DELTA STATE<br>By Chris O. O. Biose,<br>chrisoobiose@gmail.com</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>“Authority stealing pass armed robbery.” – Fela Anikulapo Kuti</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The local government election scheduled to hold in Delta State on July 13, 2024, presents a golden opportunity for Deltans to exercise their democratic rights and enthrone electoral integrity at the grassroots level in the state.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>For free and fair elections, there are at least five critical groups that must play their roles creditably. These are the Government in power, the ruling political party, the electoral body, security agencies and the electorate. Of these, the government in power has the greatest capacity to subvert the electoral process.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Anyone who is in doubt of this fact needs to read the graphic blow by blow account of ‘How Governors Rig Elections’ by former Governor of Cross River State, Mr. Donald Duke (available online).</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>It is also well known by election observers and all sincere Nigerians that Delta State is one of the states where election malfeasance has been institutionalised. High officials of state that are expected to observe the electoral law, disregard it with impunity. If in doubt about this fact, please refer to the judgement of Honourable Justice Monica Dongban-Mensah, at the Court of Appeal, sitting in Benin City on November 9, 2010, cited as Chief Great Ovedje Ogboru &amp; Anor v Dr. Emmanuel Ewetan Uduaghan &amp; Ors CA/B/EPT/38/10.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>About three and half years after the action challenging the purported election of Dr. Emmanuel Uduaghan was initiated, a full panel of the Court of Appeal sitting in Benin City finally pronounced its verdict. The Court of Appeal established that no governorship elections were held on April 14, 2007, but corrupt INEC officials, in collaboration with like-minded political party operatives in the state, illegally generated results to put Dr. Uduaghan in the Delta State Government House. The result declared by the then Resident Electoral Commissioner in Delta State, Alhaji Ismaila Abdulkareem on Form EC 8, when there was no election on April 14, 2007, was not a result of “electoral due process” and was therefore false.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>This shameless practice has festered in Delta State since that verdict. Criminal infractions of the electoral process that should attract severe punishment in sane climes are presented as heroism by habitual law breakers in high places.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Political parties have unequal finance, official power or access to instruments of private violence which are the main tools for subverting the will of the electorate.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Of the five most significant actors in the electoral process, security agencies, particularly the Nigeria Police Force NPF and Department of State Services DSS, have the greatest capacity to checkmate electoral malfeasors. Prevention of rigged election is better than its uncertain cure at election petition tribunals. Electoral infractions can be significantly curtailed by vigilant and proactive Security Services.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The mangled mindset of an electoral thief that is criminally fixated on rigging elections to acquire political power is fundamentally and ideologically incapable of connecting and unlocking the infinite creative genius and innate potentials of Nigerian youths. It is incapable of unleashing technological innovations and skills of the populace which are requisite for development of the country.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>An election rigger by definition lacks character or noble self image; he is most probably a dissembler and dubious personality, presenting false front before the unsuspecting public. This set of people is largely responsible for the Nigerian developmental tragedy.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Someone who steals your votes in broad day light is most likely to steal public funds without qualms. Any citizen who goes to the house of an election robber to celebrate a successful robbery inexorably shares in the karmic debt of the criminal. Both electoral thieves and their patronisers are public enemies Number One.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Nigeria is a place where many citizens adore such big rogues who happen to occupy glamorous public positions. Unfortunately, the ambition of some youths is “to be like” the real big robbers in the society. Under this condition, there is no hope for transformation of the country into a peaceful and prosperous nation-state.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>As Mohandas Karamchad Gandhi (The Mahatma or Great Soul) of India once said: “Impure means results in an impure end.” He said that “the means may be likened to a seed, the end to a tree; there is just the same inviolable connection between the means and the end as there is between the seed and the tree.” According to Gandhi, “to say that we can get good by sewing evil is the same thing as saying “that we can get rose by planting a noxious weed.”</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Corrupt judicial officials may absolve electoral malfeasors from criminal liability and punishment. But that cannot free them from social sanctions such as stigma which cannot be erased by their ill-gotten wealth.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Elders, women and youths need to call out electoral thieves in their wards and local government areas. It is a perversion of African culture to celebrate thieves. Yorubas call them ole. Hausa calls them barawo. Igbo calls them oshi. In most Nigerian cultures, thieves are booed when they appear in public. Electoral thieves deserve worse.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>It will be a moral and political revolution if the present political class in Delta state currently under the leadership of Governor Sheriff Oborevwori, can turn a new and glorious page in the political history of Nigeria by recording the first free, fair and credible local government election in the state on July 13, 2024. An unprecedented achievement of this nature will go down in the electoral history of Delta State, possibly Nigeria as a whole,</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Such a fundamental shift in ideological focus will entail electoral due process and the dawn of electoral integrity at the grassroots since return from military to civil rule in 1999. It will reverberate across the country and possibly set the tone for a new and Better Nigeria. a</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->', 'STOP ELECTION STEALING IN DELTA STATE', '', 'publish', 'open', 'closed', '', 'stop-election-stealing-in-delta-state', '', '', '2024-07-08 23:01:59', '2024-07-08 23:01:59', '', 0, 'https://boldtimes.net.ng/?p=1130', 0, 'post', '', 0),
(1132, 1, '2024-07-10 05:42:46', '2024-07-10 05:42:46', '<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>FLAWED ELECTIONS AND SOCIAL DISORDER IN NIGERIA<br>By Chris O. O. Biose,<br>chrisoobiose@gmail.com</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>“The very curse of an evil deed is that it must always continue to engender evil. - Schiller</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The local government polls scheduled to hold in Delta State on July 13, 2024, calls for sober reflection by the Government in power in the state, the political class, security agencies and the electorate.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>A situation where a dare-devil armed gang invades a bank and removes several million Naira is generally considered to be extremely terrible. Consider another scenario where a criminal gang that go by the name “politicians” or “Independent Electoral Body” invade several polling units and collation centres on Election Day, write fake election results to get the “boss” declared a Local Government Council Chairman which enables the beneficiary of the criminal enterprise to preside over disbursement of Federal Allocations of several hundred million Naira every month for several years which he proceeds to share as he pleases. Which of these two is the greater robber?</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>It is clear from the above hypothetical illustration that election rigging is the chief source of most social evils that beset Nigeria. It is the source of disregard of the rule of law, mismanagement of public funds, grand corruption, deficit in social and core economic infrastructure and continued underdevelopment of the country.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Electoral malfeasance also has a high potential for escalating violence, social disorder and tumult. Ultimately, it is correlated with heightened insecurity and threat to the corporate existence of the country.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Election Rigging and Military Coups in Nigeria<br>Election rigging has resulted in major disasters which threatened the very foundations and continued existence of Nigeria.<br>The blatant rigging of the Western Region Parliamentary Election in August 1965 and the attempt to foist an unwanted Premier on the people of Western Region by the NPC Government generated widespread protests in the region. The spontaneous and sporadic protests took a horrific turn which came to be known in Nigerian political history as “operation wetie”.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>This involved setting alight political opponents and suspected electoral malefactors and burning them alive. It also involved burning the vehicles, buildings or other valuable property of perceived political traitors.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>There was widespread murder, arson and looting in many parts of the Western Region during the period. Conservative estimates indicate that about 160 people, including some electoral officers, were killed in the uprising while thousands of houses were burnt.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The crisis set the stage for the first military coup in the country in January 1966. The coup and the resultant Nigeria–Biafra war (1967–1970) were distant consequences of the l965 Western Region Parliamentary Election crisis.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The military coup of December 31, 1983 was also a result of the massive rigging of the 1983 presidential and gubernatorial elections by the NPN.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>In old Ondo State (present day Ondo and Ekiti states), the NPN gubernatorial candidate at the 1983 election, Chief Akin Omoboriowo could not obtain winning votes at the election but the electoral body increased his scores and reduced that of his UPN opponent, Governor Adekunle Ajasin, to give victory to Omoboriowo.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>On August 16, l983, wide spread civil disorder broke out in Ondo State following the declaration of Chief Omoboriowo as Governor-elect of the State when majority of the people actually voted for Chief Adekunle Ajasin. In the ensuing riots, scores of high and low perished as human beings were burnt along with property.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Notable personalities killed during the riot included an NPN leader named Agbayewa, Barrister Tunde Agunbiade for allegedly celebrating the electoral robbery openly and was decapitated by the irate mob. A prominent member of the UPN who defected to the NPN and who was also a member of the House of Representatives, Hon. Olaiya Fagbamigbe (1025-1983, was among the victims. Hon. Fagbamigbe’s case was particularly pathetic because he was for long a loyalist of Chief Awolowo. His name is etched in Nigerian progressivist history as owner of Fagbamigbe Publishers that published three of Awolowo’s books, namely, Voice of Reason, Voice of Wisdom and Voice of Courage. The rioters also razed FEDECO office on Oba Adesida Road, Akure.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The Delta State Experience<br>Following announcement of false results which gave former Governor James Onanefe Ibori a second term by a landslide of votes in 2003, hell was let loose in several parts of Delta State. The epicentre of the electoral war was the area around Warri where INEC awarded the incumbent ridiculously high votes.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>On the day that Delta State Security Council meeting was scheduled for Warri, some warlords invaded the town and embarked on sporadic shooting. Not less than ten buildings, most of them belonging to PDP chieftains in Ogaga Street and JJC area of Effurun were burnt. Mobile Policemen gunned down a young boy identified as Henry who went to bath at Effurun River, mistaking him for an arsonist.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>According to Vanguard columnist, Ochereome Nnann, “In Warri, the police admitted the killing of six people by Sunday morning.” Independent reports put the number of dead much higher. Joel Bisiner of Warri-based Niger Delta Professionals for development told VANGUARD that “no fewer than 10 people have been killed.” Armed youths mounted on motorcycles paraded the town waving AD flags.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Soon after the battles occasioned by the April 2003 elections which was fought with sophisticated weapons by agents of the Delta State Government, political thugs who had not been settled unleashed a reign of terror on the citizenry. Robberies and hostage taking in Delta State, traceable to weapons placed in the hands of youths by politicians assumed alarming proportions. There were broad daylight robberies in hitherto peaceful and serene environments.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Following escalation of violence in Warri and environs, frightened residents of the oil city started fleeing the town in their hundreds. According to Emmanuel Ijewere, who was then President of the Nigerian Red Cross Society, “about 320 people are injured and about 12,000 residents were displaced” in the Warri area due to the election crisis. (Vanguard, May 15, 2003)</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The then Delta State Police Commissioner, Mr. Charles Akaya hit the nail on the head when he said while briefing his men attached to Ekpan Police Station in Uvwie Local Government Area of the state that “the rampaging bandits were thugs armed by politicians for election purposes.” The police boss regretted that “politicians failed to disarm their private army after the election.”</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Elaborating, Mr. Akaya said:<br>“When there was politics, when there was campaigns, I mean politicking, there were many of these prominent politicians who bought arms and engaged some men to be guiding them, they call these men thugs, that is what they call them and that is what they are.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>“Election has come and gone, those who won the election have won, those who were defeated have been defeated but politicians failed to withdraw the sophisticated weapons in the hands of the thugs. Unfortunately for us, the huge money they used to give to these thugs are gone and the thugs want to maintain that kind of life they were used to and the quickest way to make money with arms in their possession, they feel, is robbery.”</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>“But with the nature of our jobs, we are not expected to permit them to commit robbery and when they want to do this and you are around, they want to finish you first.” (Sola Adebayo, Police Commissioner blames rising crime wave on politicians, Vanguard, Thursday, June 19, 2003, p 6).</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Indirectly, police became victims of electoral robberies carried out by politicians. The robberies, kidnappings and heightened insecurity, which followed the April 2003 elections, persisted three years after the election, coming to a head during the April 2007 phantom elections in the state, enforced with guns. Indeed, April 2003 and 2007 left a permanent imprint on Delta State for the worse.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Again in 2007, following announcement of false election results, which awarded Dr. Uduaghan gubernatorial victory in an election that never took place, widespread arson and killings reigned in the state. See Sunday Vanguard, Delta: Killings, arson reign, April 15, 2007, p 7.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>On Tuesday following the gubernatorial election, a supporter of the ruling PDP and his family were burnt alive in their house by youths suspected to be sympathisers of opposition AD. In response, PDP supporters also burnt alive an AD supporter and his family. Reports put the number of people killed at 25 or more. (VANGUARD, Elections: 3 burnt to death In Commissioner’s house, April 17, 2007, p 8).</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The 2007 election crisis also exacerbated tensions which were then rife between Itsekiri and Urhobo ethnic groups in Delta State. (Emma Amaize, …As soldiers barricade Effurun, Ekpan, Enerhen, VANGUARD, April 17, 2007, p 9).</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The episodes recounted above have their counterparts in several states in Nigeria. The result is that the entire gamut of Nigerian society is in fundamental disarray as significant publics and groups protest and refuse to give their loyalty to the rule of force and fraud.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The fact that there were no convulsive riots to protest the scientifically rigged February 25, 2023 presidential elections in no way implied that all was well or that the electorate accepted the cheating by electoral rogues.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>At this point in Nigeria’s troubled historical experience, the Delta State political elite should not trigger the anger of impoverished and hungry youths by brazenly stealing their votes and denying them the right to choose their leaders by due electoral process at the grassroots level.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->', 'FLAWED ELECTIONS AND SOCIAL DISORDER IN NIGERIA', '', 'publish', 'open', 'closed', '', 'flawed-elections-and-social-disorder-in-nigeria', '', '', '2024-07-10 05:42:46', '2024-07-10 05:42:46', '', 0, 'https://boldtimes.net.ng/?p=1132', 0, 'post', '', 0),
(1133, 1, '2024-07-10 05:41:14', '2024-07-10 05:41:14', '', 'Screenshot_20240708-124950_1', '', 'inherit', 'open', 'closed', '', 'screenshot_20240708-124950_1', '', '', '2024-07-10 05:41:14', '2024-07-10 05:41:14', '', 1132, 'http://boldtimes.net.ng/wp-content/uploads/2024/07/Screenshot_20240708-124950_1.jpg', 0, 'attachment', 'image/jpeg', 0);
INSERT INTO `wp5g_posts` (`ID`, `post_author`, `post_date`, `post_date_gmt`, `post_content`, `post_title`, `post_excerpt`, `post_status`, `comment_status`, `ping_status`, `post_password`, `post_name`, `to_ping`, `pinged`, `post_modified`, `post_modified_gmt`, `post_content_filtered`, `post_parent`, `guid`, `menu_order`, `post_type`, `post_mime_type`, `comment_count`) VALUES
(1135, 1, '2024-07-17 22:48:46', '2024-07-17 22:48:46', '<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>By Chris O. O. Biose.<br>chrisoobiose@gmail.com</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>There were numerous unresolved issues about Alhaji Bola Ahmed Tinubu, as a public officer prior to the February 25, 2023 presidential election. These included questions about his true age, his educational record, alleged connection with narcotic under-world when he lived in the USA as well as undisclosed sources of his stupendous wealth.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>In spite of these, he was widely credited with an admirable degree of administrative acuity. Nobody expected the depth of dereliction that has characterised his administration since he was inaugurated as President, Commander-in-Chief of Nigerian Armed Forces on May 29, 2023. It has been one mis-step after another, leading the country to its worse economic crisis since independence in 1960.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>His precipitate removal of fuel subsidy during his inauguration speech, without prior plan on how to cushion the harsh consequences of the action was received with consternation. He followed with equally unplanned floating of the national currency, the Naira, when he had done nothing to shore up the productive base of the country’s economy to ensure that Nigeria can compete with other countries in the international market.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>His bloated cabinet of 48 ministers, the highest ever in the country did not take account of the harsh economic realities of the country. His government engaged in humongous expenditure on items of conspicuous consumption such as purchase of SUVs for officials, unwarranted renovation of residence of the Vice President at a whooping cost of N21 billion and expenditure of N90 billion for religious ceremonies in March 2024.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>He deepened Nigeria’s status as a debtor country by borrowing additional N24.33 trillion naira in the past one year to add to already unbearable debt burden of N77 trillion left by his much criticized predecessor.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>President Tinubu seems to have imbibed the intensely exploitative feudal ethos of the Northern ruling elite. His policies since assuming office betray the dominant political tradition of the Western Region since 1954 which was based on democratic socialist ideology. It is totally out of tune with the egalitarian political philosophy of the the Action Group (AG) in the old Western Region and Unity Party of Nigeria (UPN) in South-West States in the Second Republic (1979-1983).</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Although President Tinubu promised to continue the policies of his predecessor in office, I personally never imagined that this would extend to ex-President Buhari’s Fulanisation agenda.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>With the inauguration of Rtd Major General Muhammadu Buhari as President, Commander-in-Chief of the Armed Forces of the Federal Republic of Nigeria on May 29, 2015, armed Fulani herdsmen quickly became one of the most deadly terrorist organisations in Nigeria. The Global Terrorism Index (GTI) named armed Fulani herdsmen as the “fourth deadliest terrorist group in the world”, after Boko Haram, ISIS and al-Shaba. (Rose Troup Buchanan, Global Terrorism Index: Nigerian Fulani milititants named fourth deadliest terrorist group in the world, November 2015).</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Between 2017 and 2018, herdsmen attacks increased from 411 to 562 in Nigeria. In fact, in 2018 alone, it was reported that Fulani herdsmen killed more than 1,300 people.<br>Attacks in various parts of Benue and Plateau states were most horrendous. In June 2022, The Association of Kwande Academia (AKA) stated that no fewer than 2,728 persons including women and children, had been murdered in Turan community in Kwanda local government area of Benue state.<br>Kwande Academia concluded that “the game plan of the alien Fulani herdsmen in Turan area of Kwande LGA is to annihilate the entire people and occupy their God’s given land and inheritance.” (Peter Duru, Makurdi, over 2,728 persons killed by Herders in Benue-LG, Vanguard Nigeria, June 23, 2022, p.1).</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Speaking to Seun Okinbaloye of Channels TV on Friday April 12, 2024, the Speaker of the Plateau State House of Assembly, Rt. Hon. Gabriel Dewan described continued killings in the Plateau as “ethnic cleansing” and “conspiracy against the people” of Plateau State”. He revealed that 40 communities had been systematically wiped out and replaced with cattle herders.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>As at June 2024, indigenous youths groups in Plateau State also revealed that 151 villages were occupied by armed foreign herdesmen.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Many other parts of Plateau, Benue as well as several other states in Nigeria have their tales of woes inflicted on them by the same terrorist group.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>On July, 9, 2024, President Tinubu announced that he had established a Ministry of Livestock Development which in effect, is a revival of the infamous RUGA Settlement Scheme of his predecessor in office. This is surely Presdent Tinubu’s most insensitive, dangerous and anti-nationalist policy measure thus far.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Any plan, by whatever name it is called, that involves annexation of ancestral land of indigenous peoples in any part of Middle Belt and Southern Nigeria must be seen as a revival of the infamous Buhari RUGA Settlement scheme. RUGA is Hausa word for cow.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The policy seeks to sweep under the carpet, all the atrocities committed by armed herders during eight years of former President Buhari. According to Nigerian Security Tracker, about 4,068 persons were killed by herdsmen between 2015 and 2019.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>More than one year into the Tinubu Presidency, the most productive farming communities in Nigeria, Benue and Plateau states, are still ravaged by armed herders. Majority of citizens in these states cannot go to their farms while thousands are in IDP camps. His administration has done nothing to reclaim their land and resettle them.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Two million persons displaced from their homes are in IDP camps in the affected zones. To leave these innocent men, women and children in the IDP camps while allowing the armed herdsmen to keep the land which they ceased, is not only unconscionable, it amounts to aiding and abetting criminality of armed herders.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Like they pass budgets within two months and the Bill changing the National Anthem within one week, the Executive and Legislature could pass a Bill to enable states establish police forces to protect their land.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Agriculture should be a state affair and not a federal business. The Federal Government has no land anywhere in Nigeria on which to embark on livestock development.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Acquisition of modern skills and construction of infrastructure to improve livestock production is absolutely laudable. The million dollar question is why the project must involve taking over land from Southern and Middle Belt states. There must be something sinister in such plan that goes beyond agricultural training and infrastructure.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The Sambisa Forest in North-East Nigeria stretches across Borno, Yobe, Gombe and Bauchi states up to some parts of Kano State. The Forest is believed to have a land area of about 60,000 square kilometres.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>It’s pertinent to mention that the defunct Northern Regional government of Nigeria did not maintain the tourist facility and as a result it deteriorated and went into decay. The forest reserve was handed to the Federal Government in 1991 during the period of military dictatorship via the National Park with an area of about 2,250km2. The Forest has boundaries with Chibok, Gwoza, Konduga, Askiru, Bama, Dikwa, Damboa, among others.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Boko Haram terrorists took control of the entire Sambisa Forest and made it their headquarters. The insurgents destroyed traces of game in the forest and erected make-shift camps from where they constantly unleashed terror on adjoining villages and towns.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Since the Nigerian Army have reportedly cleared Sambisa Forest of terrorists, all genuine Fulani herdsmen could be taken to that place. The Tinubu administration could assist respective and willing state governments concerned to use that land mass to effect any agricultural development scheme he wishes and leave the Southern and middle- belt lands alone.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Fulani herders do not need to come to Delta State which has only 17,698 km2, less than 30 per cent of the land in that Forest alone, not speaking of the whole North-East and North-West. Any herder who produces beef or cow milk from the Sambisa Forest base is free to transport these finished products to point of sale in other states. This would surely not involve use of AK 47 rifles.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Most of the frozen fish and chicken in Nigeria are imported from Asia yet, the Asians did not need to come to occupy rivers in the Niger Delta to make their products available in Nigeria.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>According to Wikipedia online, Borno state has a total area of over 70,898 km2 and is the second largest state in Nigeria, after Niger State which has a total land area of 74,363 km2.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>As the presidential candidate of the Labour Party (LP) in the February 25, 2023, presidential election, Mr Peter Obi, said repeatedly, Niger State alone can feed the whole of West Africa, if their land resources are managed by innovative competent leaders.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The total land mass of the entire South-East and South-South geopolitical zones is about 29,388 km2 and 84, 587 km2 respectively, with the latter just a little more than that of Bornu Sate.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>In spite of this obvious fact, the Federal Government wants to take land from those that have less to give to those that have more. What kind of logic is that?</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>What is the rationale for somebody that has several hundred thousand hectares of land to come and violently grab that of somebody who has only a few hundred hectares? What is the true reason of the Buhari-Tinubu APC administrations pursuing less endowed states to give their lands to those that have more?</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>It was relatively easy for Boko Haram terrorists to occupy the Sambisa Forest mainly because it was so vast and was abandoned for several years. If the place is not utilised for productive purposes such as cattle grazing or ranches, the probability is high that the area will remain a refuge for questionable characters. Instead of pursuing other nationalities that have less, it is wise to make use of the Sambisa Forest for whatever agricultural development the state governments propose to pursue.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>President Tinubu may as well lure some greedy and unpatriotic Southern State Governors with financial packages to concede implantation of permanent Fulani enclaves in their states. If he succeeds with this anti-people design, this will be the foundation of the biggest armed conflict in Nigeria in the foreseeable future.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->', 'BIGGEST SURPRISE ABOUT PRESIDENT TINUBU', '', 'publish', 'open', 'closed', '', 'biggest-surprise-about-president-tinubu', '', '', '2024-07-17 22:48:46', '2024-07-17 22:48:46', '', 0, 'https://boldtimes.net.ng/?p=1135', 0, 'post', '', 0),
(1137, 1, '2024-08-01 06:39:18', '2024-08-01 06:39:18', '<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>KILLING THE YOUTH IN NIGERIAN YOUTHS<br>By Chris O. O. Biose,<br>chrisoobiose@gmail.com</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>“Every generation, out of relative obscurity, must find its mission, and either fulfill it or betray it.” -Frantz Fanon (1925-1961)</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Nigerian youths are waking up to the challenges of their unacceptable situation by organizing a nation-wide protest, scheduled to commence on August 1, 2024, with the hashtag #EndBadGovernance. The purpose is to compel the Nigerian government to bring about positive reforms in public affairs.<br>Most of the demands of the youths are quite reasonable and if implemented, would conduce to sustainable development of the country. These include drastic reduction in cost of governance, bridging the yawning gap between emoluments of political office holders and that of other public servants, elimination of grand corruption, resumption of subsidy in the price of fuel, bringing Nigerian petroleum refineries to working condition and universal access to basic education.<br>Prudent and judicious use of state resources as demanded by the youths would generate productive economic activity that would rescue youths from roaming the streets in search of non-existent jobs. It would release funds for social services such as education, health care and welfare for the aged.<br>Population explosion without viable plan or provision of education for children and youths posits unspeakable danger for the stability of this backward, predominantly illiterate, ex-colonial country.<br>Nigerians need freedom from superstitious beliefs and social prejudices based on ethnic and religious differentiation. One of the best ways to achieve this is universal access to liberal education. The country is backward and in turmoil because her self-centered leaders prefer to line their pockets than prepare children and youths to build a peaceful, productive and prosperous society.<br>The country cannot start to address its developmental problems until a free environment is created for that purpose.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>It is therefore bothersome that some traditional and religious elite are colluding with the ruling class to kill the buoyancy of Nigerian youth by asking them not to express their dissatisfaction with the situation in the country by means of peaceful protest.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Perceived Fears About The Protest<br>Organizers of the #EndBadGovernance protest fear that it could be hijacked by Nigerian security forces as witnessed during the Lekki Toll gate massacre of patriotic and unarmed peaceful youth protesters in Lagos on October 20, 2021.<br>The Jama’atu Nasril Islam (JNI), Christian Association of Nigeria (CAN), Pan-Niger Delta Forum (PANDEF), Ijaw National Congress (INC), Movement for the Survival of Ogoni People (MOSOP) and Enugu Innovative Youths all argued that the protest could lead to violence, destruction of property and disruption of public peace.<br>In particular, the Imeobi Igbo Forum (IIF) asked Igbo youths to shun the protest claiming that it was belated since they failed to protest the selection called election on February 25, 2023 presidential election. On their part, Igbos in Lagos said they would not participate in the protest based on the well-founded fear that their businesses could be targeted for looting by hoodlums. Pro-Biafra groups also alleged that the protest was a subtle plot to lure Ndigbo youths to their early graves.<br>This advisory from various quarters is directed to the wrong persons. Critics of the protests should direct their persuasion, criticisms or verbal attacks as the case may be to those who target youth protesters rather than asking the youths not to express their constitutional rights of protest when injured.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Qualities of Youths<br>The youthful period of life is characterized by generation and expenditure of tremendous energy. Such youthful energy and activism is a great asset to any community or nation. Without youths, sports would lose its zest and allure.<br>Indeed, no human society would be glad to be saddled with a docile and complacent youth population, without inner fire to challenge the ills of society or ward off external and internal threats to its survival. Such a youth population would be a sure recipe for national suicide.<br>Youths are bold, daring, often more altruistic, evince greater honesty, and sincerity of purpose and pursue their objectives with greater enthusiasm as opposed to the often legalistic hypocrisy of the adult world.<br>Being the most active segment of the population, youths are more adventurous, mobile and prone to move from one area to another while the aged generally tend to suffer inertia. In contemporary Nigeria, youths are not only constantly on the move from one part of the country to another, but also across the globe.<br>Evidence from several cultures shows clearly that youths are more likely to resist aggression than the aged. During the period of inter-tribal wars, youths formed the bulk of the field fighting forces while elders provided the strategy, tactics, and logistics. In modern states, youths form the bulk of the Army, Air Force, Navy, Police and other security personnel.<br>Indeed, it can be said that youths are the true wealth of a country. They constitute the real social insurance policy for a country’s tomorrow, not the amounts hidden in foreign accounts by kleptocratic leaders or foreign account balance that do not translate to the welfare of the citizens.<br>However, due to intensity of the physical and emotional activity, the young person may be prone to both physical and psychological trauma, if left without proper guidance.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Reawakening of Youth Patriotism in Nigeria<br>The abject failure of the Buhari regime between May 2015 and May 2023 and the cocktail of woes which it visited on the population brought the Nigerian crisis to a head. Living in Nigeria became a daily ordeal.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Bearing in mind that the behavior of youths is central to a nation’s survival, the perverse and degenerate practices of the Nigerian ruling class constituted a veritable recipe for hell on earth. Youths imbibed the negative subterranean values in the wider society and improve on them. These eventually pose serious problems, not only for youths, but for the entire society.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Extreme situation may challenge people and societies to reinvent themselves. The unprecedented levels of suffering brought upon the masses by the Buhari regime triggered varied reactions from diverse sections of the Nigerian society.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>It elicited unwavering determination among significant sections of Nigerian youths to take their destiny in their own hands. They took the initiative to make a change in the country through the democratic process. Unfortunately, their magnificent efforts at self-mobilisation for political change were subverted by the Professor Mahmood Yakubu-led Independent National Electoral Commission (INEC) that failed the country at the last moment. He claimed a fictitious glitch in the transmission of the result of the February 25, 2023, presidential election without providing evidence.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Most youth organisations possess the intellect and willpower to fight injustice but are in no financial position to reach out to fellow youths and the general public. In this regard, the social media provides effective communication channels that enable them organize unobtrusively.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Why Youths Must Get Involved<br>Nigerian youths need to brace up for a struggle against the possible emergence of any monstrous (civilian) administration. Whether youths take part in the social, economic and political process or not, they will inevitably be affected by decisions made daily by impostors lurking around to foist themselves on Nigerians from time to time.<br>The theme of the International Youth Day celebration on August 12, 2022 – WHEN YOUTHS ARE SHUT OUT, THE NATION SUFFERS succinctly captures the essence of youth participation in state affairs.<br>If youths fail to act now, the devils would continue to entrench themselves and thereby there might be less and less opportunity for future positive change.<br>Youth bodies as conceptualized in this discourse are change catalysts. A catalyst is a variable that causes change without itself changing. It is an agent that initiates, speeds up or slows down change in development process. Such a catalyst should have communication skills, infectious enthusiasm which leaves permanent motivation.<br>Youth organisations may serve to direct latent or dormant social capital among themselves and the wider society (time, human energy, technical and organizational skills) and investing the same, to a given purpose.<br>Youths have a stake where politics in their country is reduced to privatized power play among contending political gladiators who wish to ensure that the system does not threaten their ill-gotten positions. If Nigeria needs a clean break from its ugly past, youths must vehemently disown impostors and looters masquerading as leaders including those who come from their villages or claim to steal on their behalf.<br>If youths remain aloof, the resulting national paralysis may condemn a whole generation to mediocrity.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Youths Who Changed The World<br>When great ideas take root in their impressionable minds, youths may spend their life time working them out or giving effect to them.<br>In the early 1730s, a group of Oxford University students led by John and Charles Wesley got the nickname “Methodists” because of their methodical discipline in holy living. Today, Methodism is used to refer to the teachings and practices of John Benjamin Welsey and those Christian denominations which sprang from his ministry.<br>The German sociologist and political philosopher, Karl Marx (1818-1883) was only 24 years old when the Communist League commissioned him to draw up the Communist Manifesto in 1847. With the assistance of his 27 years old friend, Friedrich Engels, these two idealistic but angry young men drafted and edited the Communist Manifesto.<br>Seventy years later, (November 7, 1917), Nikolai Lenin led the Bolsheviks and a number of key groups to capture most government offices in Russia. This began a staggering series of events tagged ‘the October Revolution” which brought a new kind of government to Czarist Russia. This has affected lives of generations.<br>Jean Henry Dunant, a young Swiss banker was on summer holidays in Italy from his native Switzerland in 1859 when he beheld the brutalization of the war between Italy and Austria. Young Henri rushed before the astounded Austrians crying “Stop, stop! We are all brothers!” He immediately recruited helpers to carry casualties inside a church, got straw, food, water and spent his money for medicines to care for the wounded soldiers. The story of the International Red Cross had begun. Henry was only 31 years old. He later became the first person to receive the Nobel Peace Prize in 1901, the proceeds of which he turned over to the Red Cross and other charities.<br>The historic Self-Government motion by the youthful Mr. Anthony Enahoro on the floor of the Federal House of Representatives in Lagos on March 31, 1953, set in motion a chain of events and constitutional conferences that culminated in Nigeria’s independence on October 1, 1960.<br>The question one would like each Nigerian youth to answer is this: What ideas are at present occupying your mind regarding Nigeria’s greatness and how do you seek to actualize these in your life time?<br>Youths as Youths<br>Nigerian youths need to embrace new and positive values as outlets for their courage and creativity. They need to organize to defend the interests of youth, as youths, not Arewa youths, Igbo youths, Yoruba youths or to defend other parochial interests.<br>It is time to hear about youths united against corruption, youths united against oppression, against man’s inhumanity to man. It is time for Nigerian youths to unite against economic, political and spiritual wickedness in high places. They must unite for noble causes.<br>Youths must become agents of drastic reformation rather than slavish conformists to a decadent social order in which they are essentially losers.<br>Energetic parades, smart uniforms and catchy slogans are not enough unless these are informed by powerful new ideals.<br>Nigerian youths must not be distracted by those who preach division, ethnicity and religious hatred. They must remain united for noble causes and embrace that which truly defines youths all over the world – altruism.<br>Youth Empowerment Schemes<br>Nigeria needs a people and youth-friendly government. Absence of youth empowerment schemes turn youths to pirates, pipeline vandals and ethnic militias.<br>An example of a genuine youth empowerment scheme was the Universal Primary Education Programme instituted by Chief Obafemi Awolow’s administration in Western Nigeria which took effect from January 17, 1955. The Free Education Programme was also extended to the States controlled by the Unity Party of Nigeria (UPN) from 1979 to 1983. Since the end of the Second Republic, Awolowo’s free education programme has remained the largest programme of youth empowerment in Nigeria and indeed in African history.<br>It was not selective. A child did not have to have rich parents to benefit from the programme. Moreover, it was not a programme for children of one ethnic group or of one socio-economic class. It was targeted at children and young people in general.<br>Awolowo provided a rational basis for popular access to education and free health services for Nigerian children. According to the sage,<br>“No plan for the welfare of the people will ever succeed unless the very first principle in the pursuit of this ultimate purpose is fully recognized.<br>“The first principle is that man is the sole creative and purposive in nature; everything else by comparison is inert. In all the spheres of production, distribution, exchange and consumption, he is the initiator and accelerator of every form of human progress; he is the generator of every initial impulse to human evolution.<br>“Any plan for progress therefore, which places man in a secondary place is basically in error, antithetic to the natural order of human endeavours, and is bound to fail, as well as a bane rather than a blessing to the people.”<br>Based on this reasoning, Awolowo insisted that the government must give utmost priority to investment in man with a view to increasing human power over the challenges of the physical and social environment. Children inherit the biological endowments of their parents, which define body characteristics and intelligence. However, intelligence and acquisition of relevant skills are nurtured in a social environment, which may stimulate or stultify them.<br>Education is a fundamental human right which should be extended to all Nigerian children because without it, one cannot develop the attributes of his humanness in the modern world. It is needed for intelligent participation in civic affairs. This is a critical aspect where egalitarian ideologies differ from feudal, conservative and reactionary polices that have pervaded Nigeria since return to civil rule in May 1999.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->', 'KILLING THE YOUTH IN NIGERIAN YOUTHS', '', 'publish', 'open', 'closed', '', 'killing-the-youth-in-nigerian-youths', '', '', '2024-08-01 06:39:18', '2024-08-01 06:39:18', '', 0, 'https://boldtimes.net.ng/?p=1137', 0, 'post', '', 0),
(1139, 1, '2024-08-02 06:56:55', '2024-08-02 06:56:55', '<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>IMPERATIVE OF STATE AND COMMUNITY POLICING<br>By Chris O. O. Biose,<br>chrisoobiose@gmail.com</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>One of the major challenges in contemporary Nigeria is heightening insecurity which has overwhelmed federal security agencies. Many innocent citizens are killed every day by unknown persons. Every responsible government is duty- bound to protect the lives and property of its citizens as utmost priority. Therefore, it is imperative that each level of government should be constitutionally empowered to maintain peace in its area of jurisdiction through a decentralized policing system.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>In a speech at the Resumed Constitutional Conference on Nigeria held at Lancaster House, London, in September 1958, Chief Obafemi Awolowo, who was then leader of the Action Group and Premier of Western Region, outlined the position of his delegation on several constitutional issues, including regionalisation of the Nigeria Police. He presented several logical and factual grounds for establishment of Regional police, but for reasons of space, I will cite only three in this discourse:</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>First, said Chief Awolowo, it is<br>“wrong in principle to vest a Government with responsibility for the maintenance of law and order and, at the same time, refuse to provide that Government with the necessary executive instruments for discharging its responsibility.”</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The sage also said that centralisation of Police</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>“has no parallel or precedent in any part of the British Commonwealth with a Federal Constitution, save Malaya, where a perpetual state of emergency has necessitated an extraordinary policy in police matters.”</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>On the claim by colonial administrators, which continues to be parroted by some Nigerian politicians that state police would be politically biased, the late sage averred,<br>“I consider it a gratuitous insult to Nigerian leaders for high-ranking British officials in Nigeria and British spokesmen in Britain to suggest that, if the Police are regionalised, they would be used as instruments of oppression against political opponents. It seems to me grossly illogical and untenable to maintain, as British protagonists of centralisation of Police have done, that while political leaders in the Regions are incapable of making responsible use of the Police Force, these same leaders, if they happen to be in the Central Government, would automatically undergo some inexplicable metamorphosis, which would transform them into new beings sufficiently responsible to administer the very subject which has been denied them in the Regions.”</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Nigeria is a vast country covering diverse geographical zones and cultural groups and it is absolutely impossible for the Federal Police in Abuja to ensure security in all nooks and crannies in all parts of the country. No amount of vain and hypocritical exhortations by politically-biased IGPs will bring about the much-desired peace and security in Nigeria. By its consistent refusal to facilitate establishment of regional, state and community policing, the Federal Government constitutes itself into the chief promoter of insecurity in Nigeria.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Moreover, it is futile to invite police officers to fight crime one thousand kilometres away in a country that lacks efficient communication and transport system. It is dysfunctional to post officers from other cultural groups to perform policing functions in states where cultural gaps create difficulties for understanding local issues by those who were acculturated outside those areas.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Decentralisation of policing as practised in other countries with a federal system of government is the panacea to encroaching insecurity. No amount of deployment of soldiers to perform policing duties will promote security in the country.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Drastic reduction in crime rate in the country will only be achieved when those closest to the scene of crime are allowed to fight it. With state and local policing in place, it would have been very difficult for the Boko Haram terrorist sect to incubate and build up its forces in Sambisa Forest over a period of several years without being detected by the security apparatus in Borno State. Their organisational apparatus would have been nipped in the bud.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Borno State spans an area of 70,898 km2 and more than double the land area of five South-East states put together. Even though most of the land is unoccupied, it is unrealistic to expect the ill-staffed, ill-equipped and ill motivated Federal Police at Abuja or the state command of the Federal Police at Maiduguri, to effectively monitor the vast area.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>With state policing, the constant massacre of peaceful villagers in Benue, Taraba and some other states by Fulani herdsmen militia would not be possible.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>With state police, failure of security would no longer be blamed on Federal Government. It would no longer be tenable to accuse Nigerian soldiers and Police of colluding with Fulani militia to kill people in various parts of the country.<br>Attention would shift to how well state Governors provide security for their states.<br>Under that condition, no one would reasonably blame a President for failing to provide security in states. The Nigerian Armed Forces would concentrate on its constitutional duty of defence of the country rather than be deployed to intimidate road users at roadblocks, on election duties and as security guards for politicians and rich people.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Based on the above premises, I submit that the first step to bringing about a reasonable level of security in the country is the establishment of regional, state and community policing. This will place enforcement of laws in the hands of states and officers who know the terrain and who are closer to the people.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Extract from pages 289-292 of my book:\\ entitled STOP ZONING; RESTRUCTURE NIGERIA, published in December 2023.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->', 'IMPERATIVE OF STATE AND COMMUNITY POLICING', '', 'publish', 'open', 'closed', '', 'imperative-of-state-and-community-policing', '', '', '2024-08-02 06:56:55', '2024-08-02 06:56:55', '', 0, 'https://boldtimes.net.ng/?p=1139', 0, 'post', '', 0),
(1141, 1, '2024-08-03 13:54:24', '2024-08-03 13:54:24', '<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>ISSUES FROM #ENDBADGOVERNANCE PROTEST<br>By Chris O. O. Biose<br>chrisoobiose@gmail.com</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The nation-wide protest against bad governance in Nigeria which took place on August 1 and 2, 2024, demonstrated a commendable determination on the part of Nigerian youths to overcome the false propaganda of the Federal Government and their agencies to lure them into lukewarmness and inaction. The fact that the youth population largely ignored the hypocritical appeals by traditional rulers and religious elite to dissuade them from the protest is evidence of the disconnect between these institutions and the younger generation in the country.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>It is instructive to note that the most serious incidents of violence recorded during two-days of protests occurred in parts of Northern Nigeria such as Kano, Kaduna, Katsina, Jigawa, Yobe, Kebbi and Gombe that have no culture of civil protests. These areas have historically been associated with violent disturbances involving arson and killing of innocents in the past 45 years. The lesson to learn from these episodes is intensification of liberal education and public enlightenment to inculcate verbal articulation of disagreement and grievances. Those who have not cultivated this habit or skill are most likely to resort to physical aggression or belligerence in conflict situations.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>However, the protest appears to be directed almost entirely to highlight the failures of the Federal Government and demands for prudent use of national resources. While this is worthwhile, it is pertinent to note that Nigeria is a Federation consisting of 36 federating units, the Federal Capital Territory (FCT), Abuja, and the Federal Government. Just as the citizens elect their President, so also they elect their Governors, Senators, Members of the House of Representatives, Members of State Houses of Assembly, Chairmen of Local Government Councils and Councilors. All these elected officers bear responsibility to citizens at various levels.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Apart from ignominious handling of fuel subsidy regime and thoughtless floating of the national currency which are exclusive preserve of the Federal Government, all other grave ills that beset the country such as grand corruption, large-scale misuse of public funds, extravagance of public office holders, diversion of public funds for private use and dilapidated infrastructure are even more blatant in many states of the country.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Governors and local government councils seem to regard their areas as prebends. They play roles akin to those of prebends of feudal Europe where members of clergy felt entitled by right to revenue of a Cathedral.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Professor Richard Joseph (1993) defines prebend as “an office of state that is granted by a ruler to an individual in return for loyalty, the support of a popular constituency, and a willingness to participate in the privatizing of public resources. It rests on the clientelistic system that runs from the head of state down to the village and ward levels.” (Richard Joseph, Restoring Hope, in Liberty, Quarterly Journal of the Civil Liberties Organisation, Vol. 4, No. 3, August-October 1993, ISSN 1115-8522, p 25).</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Although the Federal Government takes a lion share of 52.69 per cent from the Federation Account every month, states and local governments receive a sizeable percentage of 26.72 and 20.60 respectively. In June 2024, the total distributable revenue was N2.32 trillion. Of this, the Federal Government received N365.81 billion, state governments received N388.42 billion while local governments collected N282.48 billion. In addition, the sum of N106.50 billion was shared among oil producing states from the 13 per cent Derivation Fund. States also keep 100 per cent of their Internally Generated Revenue.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The revenue generating agencies comprising Nigeria Customs Service (NCS), Federal Inland Revenue Service (FIRS), and Nigerian Up stream Development Petroleum Regulatory Commission (NUPRC) received N20.85 billion, N36.33 billion and N19.46 billion respectively as cost of revenue collections.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>A total net amount of N232.82 billion was disbursed to the FGN consolidated revenue account; N5.58 billion was received as a share of derivation and ecology; N2.79 billion as stabilization fund; N9.38 billion for the development of natural resources; and N10.12 billion to the Federal Capital Territory (FCT), Abuja.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Nigeria is awash with Naira but nobody seems to know what the Federal Government and their agencies, states and local governments do with these monies. There is no state in the Federation that has a basic amenity such as functional public water works to supply potable water to households. To varying the grease, education and health facilities are in shambles, compelling citizens who have the means to do so to withdraw their children from public schools to private schools. Intra-city roads, as well as states and local roads are in sorry situation while refuse disposal constitutes a nightmare.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The yawning gap between elite and mass in Nigeria that defines the Federal Government is replicated with damning enormity at state and local government levels. Workers in states and local governments can barely survive on their monthly salaries compelling them to resort to bribery and corrupt practices to make ends meet while political office holders live large.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Under this condition, there is absolute need for citizens to demand accountability from their states and local governments. This should include full details of monthly receipt from the Federation Account, Internally Generated Revenue (IGR), 13 per cent derivation (where applicable), loans, Value Added Tax (VAT) and other sources of revenue as well as how these monies are spent. This is particularly necessary in a situation where state houses of assembly that are constitutionally empowered to act as checks on state governments, are mere rubber stamps and conspirators in mismanagement of public funds leading to lack of development at state and grassroot levels throughout the country.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Nigeria’s woes are compounded by lame-duck National Assembly (NASS) and state assemblies. Like the Nigerian Presidency and various Government Houses, national and state legislatures since 1999 have been colossal disasters that appear not to realize the purpose of their existence. They regard their positions as opportunities to share in looting of public funds.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Whereas the legislature in a presidential system is designed to act as check on the executive, it does absolutely nothing of the sort in Nigeria. Rather, it is hand in gloves with executive excesses and in most cases, take directives from the executive, at national and state levels.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Because of these ‘zombie’ legislatures, the electorate loses benefit of democratic governance. Representatives who are expected to speak and defend the interests of the electorate actually represent and defend the interests of godfathers and other oppressor groups, which are directly antithetical to the interests and well-being of the electorate.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>With the clear exception of oppressive feudal system in some parts of Northern Nigeria, most Nigerian societies are community-centred. The increasing feudalization and personalization of government is one of the most dangerous and corrupting influences in the country and unless this is effectively checked, Nigeria may slide from military dictatorship to civilian autocracy.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>As a pre-condition for being placed in their positions, legislators are duty bound to give regular account of their legislative activities to the constituencies that elected them. Where this is not done, citizens must demand such accountability.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>In addition to their huge emoluments, Senators and Representatives are entitled to between N75 million and N500 million per legislator per annum for constituency projects, depending on their ranking. The most controversial of these constituency projects in the 2024 budget were concentrated in Akwa-Ibom (North-West Senatorial District), the Senatorial District of the Senate President, Godswill Akpabio. It included N18.5 billion for construction of rural roads and markets in Akwa-Ibom (North-West Senatorial District) which was strangely inserted in the budget for the Federal Ministry of Agriculture. Another sum of N2.5 billion for provision of deep freezers and generators to be distributed in the Senate President’s constituency was inserted in the budget of the Federal Ministry of Women Affairs.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The self-centred legislators that receive these humongous financial benefits from the public purse are the same ones that insist that N70,000 per month (about USD 46.7 at the rate of N1,500 to $1) is good enough as minimum wage for public servants in Nigeria.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>After two days of nation-wide protest, President Tinubu had failed to address the youths and suffering masses or respond to any of their demands. Only three State Governors out of 36, were reported to have come out of their cosy Government Houses to speak to the youths. Neither the Federal Government nor any state government has laid out any policy measure to resolve the travails of the protesters.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>This disdainful attitude of Nigerian governments reinforces the imperative for citizens to intensify their demand for accountability from their President, State Governors and Local Government Chairmen.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Nigerian youths at state and local government levels need to beam their searchlight on public affairs in their areas with a view to ensuring that public funds are judiciously utilised for the benefit of citizens. They must show their anger in constitutionally or socially accepted means but must never cooperate with thieves and oppressors.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>In African justice system, where there are no prisons, thieves and other malfeasors are subjected to social sanctions. Women and children boo them if they have the temerity to appear in public places and they hide their faces in shame. That is the minimum sanction. A situation where citizens sing and dance for reputed thieves is unheard of.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>If bad governance must be brought to an end in Nigeria, citizens must ceaselessly and persistently repudiate it in any contact with their oppressors. It should not be left to civil society organisations (CSOs), or human rights and pro-democracy groups. Agitation for good governance should be a job for all patriotic citizens. Failure to do so, political hawks will continue to prey on the society and everyone suffers.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The bad governance habit of the Nigerian ruling class may not be obliterated in one fell swoop of a two-day protest. In view of their seeming intransigence, organisers of the #EndBadGovernance campaign may need to institutionalise it to teach citizens how to conduct civil protests. They may also need to make it a regular activity, say, once in a month, similar to the weekly Monday sit-at-home non-violent protest by South-East. Each protest outing may be concentrated on a particular aspects of concern at each point in time.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Sustainable development of a deeply corrupted country such as Nigeria necessitates sustained momentum of a permanent nature from patriots to mount pressure on government officials to change for good. It requires intelligent and vigilant involvement of all citizens in all ways, at all times and at all levels of government in the country.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->', 'ISSUES FROM #ENDBADGOVERNANCE PROTEST', '', 'publish', 'open', 'closed', '', 'issues-from-endbadgovernance-protest', '', '', '2024-08-03 13:54:24', '2024-08-03 13:54:24', '', 0, 'https://boldtimes.net.ng/?p=1141', 0, 'post', '', 0);
INSERT INTO `wp5g_posts` (`ID`, `post_author`, `post_date`, `post_date_gmt`, `post_content`, `post_title`, `post_excerpt`, `post_status`, `comment_status`, `ping_status`, `post_password`, `post_name`, `to_ping`, `pinged`, `post_modified`, `post_modified_gmt`, `post_content_filtered`, `post_parent`, `guid`, `menu_order`, `post_type`, `post_mime_type`, `comment_count`) VALUES
(1143, 1, '2024-08-10 09:50:50', '2024-08-10 09:50:50', '<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The nation-wide hunger protest in Nigeria with the hashtag #EndBadGovernance which started on August 1, 2024, demonstrated a commendable determination on the part of Nigerian youths to overcome the intimidation and propaganda of the Tinubu-led Federal Government and their agencies to lure them into lukewarmness and inaction. The fact that the youth population ignored the hypocritical pleas by traditional rulers and religious elite to dissuade them from the protest is abundant evidence of the disconnect between these institutions and the younger generation of Nigerian citizens.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The charter of demands of the youths are designed to bring about a better, more livable and just Nigerian state. These include drastic reduction in cost of fuel (PMS) and cost of governance, bridging the yawning gap between emoluments of political office holders and that of other public servants, elimination of grand corruption, bringing Nigerian petroleum refineries to working condition and universal access to basic education.<br>It is regrettable that the civil protest degenerated into violence, looting of property and destruction of public facilities in some parts of the country. The police reportedly responded with live bullets, killing about 40 citizens, according to the Nigerian Labour Congress (NLC). The question is whether there is no other method of crowd control open to the Nigeria Police than shooting people to death.<br>The complaints by government functionaries and police about unruly behaviour of some protesters have their root cause in the political culture and social practices which the Federal Government refused to address and in most cases, actually encourage for its political gains. Nobody learns or forms a bad habit overnight.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>For example, the Governor of Borno State, Professor Babagana Zulum, complained that over 95 per cent of protesters in Maiduguri were under age. The erudite gentleman could not claim ignorance of widespread under-age voting which had been the norm in Borno, Kano, Katsina, and parts of Kaduna State for decades. Voting is meant for adults but when children are induced to vote at elections, such children who are treated as adults by their political leaders could reasonably regard themselves as equally entitled to civil protests. Unfortunately, most of them misbehave and the culpability rests on adults that lure them to play illegitimate roles.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>For example, a little speck of the widespread electoral evil of child-voting accidentally came to light in Kano State in 2018 during the APC administration. Soon after the LGA election held in Kano State on February 10, 2018, video evidence emerged on social media showing large-scale child-voting at the election. Although this practice had long existed in the state, that was the first time that people openly criticised the practice on social media. Remarkably, the Nigerian print media studiously refrained from publishing the facts for fear of reprisal from powerful quarters with vested interests in perpetuating the malpractice.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The Chairman of INEC, Professor Mahmood Yakubu conducted an investigation into it. He exonerated his Commission by claiming that the Kano State Independent Electoral Commission (KSIEC) failed to use the INEC Electoral Register for its elections. (ALLEGED KANO UNDERAGE VOTERS: Our story by Yakubu, INEC Chairman, SUNDAY VANGUARD, May 6, 2018).</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Some of the President’s men complained that the protest involved political demands. They fail to understand that the political structure of a country is pivotal to its economy. Both are inseparable.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>After three days of the protest, President Bola Ahmed Tinubu made a nation-wide broadcast at 7am on Sunday, August 4, 2024. He condemned the violent turn of the protests in some parts of the country and reeled out some of the economic measures taken by his government, urging hungry citizens to suspend the protest.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The President acknowledged the severe suffering of the masses but failed completely to present any fresh policy measure to respond to any of their demands of the protesters.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>On a general note, we find it shocking that President Tinubu failed to address the issue of horrendous killings of crop farmers in many parts of the country by armed Fulani herdsmen. It is unreasonable to believe that he does not know that this phenomenon is at the root of inability of Nigerian farmers to produce sufficient food for local consumption in the past few years.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Nigerians accuse President Tinubu’s administration of lack of accountability, profligacy, expenditure on frivolous items while majority of Nigerians were suffering from intense hunger but he failed to address these issues. He was silent about rampaging oil thieves that run down the economy. Worse still, he made no mention of investigating brutal killing of Nigerian citizens by his police men.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The fact that the President could read such an insensitive speech to toiling Nigerians at such a moment of great expectations from him shows that he is out of touch with the reality in his country. It also shows that he has all the wrong people surrounding him.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The factors that consistently generate increasing poverty in Nigeria are systemic, requiring fundamental reforms. These are exactly what the President sought to avoid in his speech. His attitude in this regard emphasizes the need for continuous struggle of patriots to build a just and egalitarian society.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>With respect to the Niger Delta of which Delta State is a part, President Tinubu evinced a total lack of concern for the peculiar problems of the region. All the problems accentuated by his insensitive policies such as unbearable cost of transportation and food are infinitely multiplied in the Niger Delta due to difficulties of water transportation. Unlike land areas of the country, cost of fuel to power boats to move goods to markets in riverine areas of the Niger Delta range between N1,500 and N2,000 per litre. This in turn reflects on cost of food items.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>In spite of sea water all around, riverine communities have no access to clean potable water due to heavy environmental pollution caused by mindless exploitation of crude oil by International Oil Companies (IOCs) who are protected against rural communities by the Federal Government.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The case of Ogoni land in Gokana LGA of Rivers State serves as a stark illustration of the Niger Delta condition. Environmental Assessment of Ogoni territory, a Report from the United Nations Environment Programme (UNEP) in August 2011, on the consequences of oil exploitation in Ogoni land, reveals that the operation of oil firms left the land in turmoil and an obvious environmental disaster.<br>According to the UNEP report, the Niger Delta region is plagued by “administrative neglect, crumbling social infrastructure and services, high unemployment, social deprivation, abject poverty, filth and squalor, and endemic conflict”. In spite of the Report of the UNEP, the Federal Government has done next to nothing in the past 13 years to ameliorate these harmful effects.<br>Permanent heat and lack of darkness at night have affected human, animal and plant life in the area incalculably. Thick sooth covers the affected areas, making rain water unfit for human consumption. Palm plants become scorched in the heat while rubber trees stop producing. This poses a serious threat to the local population’s subsistence farming, ecology, veggies, dwellings and metallic minerals.<br>Gas flares cause acid rain, which damages crops and vegetation and acidifies rivers and streams. It reduces farm yields and harms human health by increasing the risk of respiratory and lung conditions such as asthma and chronic bronchitis. Other conditions like blindness, impotence, miscarriages, cancer and early deaths are also consequences of gas flares in the Niger Delta.<br>During the past 4 days of the protest so far, only three State Governors out of 36, were reported to have come out of their cozy Government Houses to speak to the youths. Neither the Federal Government nor any state government has laid out any policy measure to resolve the demands of the protesters.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>It is shocking that an area that produces over 80 per cent of the resources which the elite use to purchase luxury items for their private enjoyment received not even the slightest mention in a policy statement by the President of the country.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>This disdainful attitude of the Tinubu administration reinforces the imperative for citizens to intensify their demand for accountability from their political office holders.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>We, the Delta Obidient Elders’ Council hereby respectfully urge President Tinubu to desist from his grandstanding and address realistic issues that affect the masses. We implore him to urgently address the concerns of Ijaw Youth Council (IYC) and other patriotic organisations in the Niger Delta. If he fails to do so, empty self-adulating television broadcasts may not assuage the righteous anger of long-suffering Niger Delta youths.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>It is instructive to note that the most serious incidents of violence recorded during the first three days of protests occurred in parts of Northern Nigeria such as Borno, Kano, Kaduna, Katsina, Jigawa, Yobe, Kebbi and Gombe states that have no culture of civil protests. These areas have historically been associated with violent disturbances involving arson and killing of innocents in the past 45 years.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>These episodes call attention to the urgency for intensification of liberal education and public enlightenment to inculcate verbal articulation of disagreement and grievances. Those who have not cultivated this habit or skill are most likely to resort to physical aggression or belligerence in conflict situations.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Moreever, the protest appears to be directed almost entirely to highlight the failures of the Federal Government and demands for prudent use of national resources. While this is worthwhile, it is pertinent to note that Nigeria is a Federation consisting of 36 federating units, the Federal Capital Territory (FCT), Abuja, and the Federal Government. Just as the citizens elect their President, so also they elect their Governors, Senators, Members of the House of Representatives, Members of State Houses of Assembly, Chairmen of Local Government Councils and Councilors. All these elected officials bear responsibility to citizens at various levels.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Apart from ignominious handling of fuel subsidy regime and thoughtless floating of the national currency which are exclusive preserve of the Federal Government, all other grave ills that impoverish the citizens are even more blatant in many states of the country. These include grand corruption, large-scale misuse of public funds, extravagance of public office holders, bloated numbers of PAs, SAs, SSAs and other underserved appointments with bogus salaries and allowances that do not add value to the country’s ailing economy, diversion of public funds for private use and dilapidated infrastructure.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Governors and local government council chairmen seem to regard their areas as prebends. They play roles akin to prebends in feudal Europe where members of clergy felt entitled by right to revenue of a Cathedral.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Professor Richard Joseph (1993) defines prebend as “an office of state that is granted by a ruler to an individual in return for loyalty, the support of a popular constituency, and a willingness to participate in the privatizing of public resources. It rests on the clientelistic system that runs from the head of state down to the village and ward levels.” (Richard Joseph, Restoring Hope, in Liberty, Quarterly Journal of the Civil Liberties Organisation, Vol. 4, No. 3, August-October 1993, ISSN 1115-8522, p 25).</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Although the Federal Government takes a lion share of 52.69 per cent from the Federation Account every month, states and local governments receive a sizeable percentage of 26.72 and 20.60 respectively. In June 2024, the total distributable revenue was N2.32 trillion. Of this, the Federal Government received N365.81 billion, state governments received N388.42 billion while local governments collected N282.48 billion. In addition, the sum of N106.50 billion was shared among oil producing states from the 13 per cent Derivation Fund. States also keep 100 per cent of their Internally Generated Revenue.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The revenue generating agencies comprising Nigeria Customs Service (NCS), Federal Inland Revenue Service (FIRS), and Nigerian Up stream Development Petroleum Regulatory Commission (NUPRC) received N20.85 billion, N36.33 billion and N19.46 billion respectively as cost of revenue collections.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>A total net amount of N232.82 billion was disbursed to the FGN consolidated revenue account; N5.58 billion was received as a share of derivation and ecology; N2.79 billion as stabilization fund; N9.38 billion for the development of natural resources; and N10.12 billion to the Federal Capital Territory (FCT), Abuja.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Nigeria is awash with Naira but nobody seems to know what the Federal Government and their agencies, states and local governments do with these monies. There is no state in the Federation that has a basic amenity such as functional public water works to supply potable water to households. To varying degrees, education and health facilities are in shambles, compelling citizens who have the means to do so to withdraw their children from public schools to private schools. Intra-city roads, as well as states and local roads are in sorry situation while refuse disposal constitutes a nightmare.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The yawning gap between elite and mass in Nigeria that defines the Federal Government is replicated with damning enormity at state and local government levels. Workers in states and local governments can barely survive on their monthly salaries compelling them to resort to bribery and corrupt practices to make ends meet while political office holders live large.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Under this condition, there is absolute need for citizens to demand accountability from their states and local governments. This should include full details of monthly receipt from the Federation Account, Internally Generated Revenue (IGR), 13 per cent derivation (where applicable), loans, Value Added Tax (VAT) and other sources of revenue as well as how these monies are spent.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>This is particularly necessary in a situation where state houses of assembly that are constitutionally empowered to act as checks on state governments, are mere rubber stamps and conspirators in mismanagement of public funds leading to lack of development at state and grassroot levels throughout the country.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Nigeria’s woes are compounded by lame-duck National Assembly (NASS) and state assemblies. Like the Nigerian Presidency and various Government Houses, national and state legislatures since 1999 have been colossal disasters that appear not to realize the purpose of their existence. They regard their positions as opportunities to share in looting of public funds.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Whereas the legislature in a presidential system is designed to act as check on the executive, it does absolutely nothing of the sort in Nigeria. Rather, it is hand in gloves with executive excesses and in most cases, take directives from the executive, at national and state levels.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Because of these ‘zombie’ legislatures, the electorate loses benefit of democratic governance. Representatives who are expected to speak and defend the interests of the electorate actually represent and defend the interests of godfathers and other oppressor groups, which are directly antithetical to the interests and well-being of the electorate.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>With the clear exception of oppressive feudal system in some parts of Northern Nigeria, most Nigerian societies are community-centred. The increasing feudalization and personalization of government is one of the most dangerous and corrupting influences in the country and unless this is effectively checked, Nigeria may slide from military dictatorship to civilian autocracy.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>As a pre-condition for being placed in their positions, legislators are duty bound to give regular account of their legislative activities to the constituencies that elected them. Where this is not done, citizens must demand such accountability.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>In addition to their huge emoluments and allowances, Senators and Representatives are said to be entitled to between N75 million and N500 million per legislator annually for constituency projects, depending on their ranking. The most controversial of these constituency projects in the 2024 budget occurred in Akwa-Ibom (North-West Senatorial District), the Senatorial District of the Senate President, Godswill Akpabio. It included N18.5 billion for construction of rural roads and markets in Akwa-Ibom (North-West Senatorial District) which was strangely inserted in the budget for the Federal Ministry of Agriculture. Another sum of N2.5 billion for provision of deep freezers and generators to be distributed in the Senate President’s constituency was also included in the budget of the Federal Ministry of Women Affairs.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The self-centred legislators that receive these financial benefits from the public purse are the same ones that insist that N70,000 per month (about USD46.7 at the rate of N1,500 to $1) is good enough as minimum wage for non-political public servants.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Nigerian youths at state and local government levels need to beam their searchlight on public affairs in their areas with a view to ensuring that public funds are judiciously utilised for the benefit of citizens. They must show their anger in constitutionally or socially accepted means but must never cooperate with thieves and oppressors.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>In African justice system, where there are no prisons, thieves and other malfeasors are subjected to social sanctions. Women and children boo them if they have the temerity to appear in public places and they hide their faces in shame. That is the minimum sanction. A situation where citizens sing and dance for reputed thieves is unheard of.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>If bad governance must be brought to an end in Nigeria, citizens must ceaselessly and persistently repudiate it in any contact with their oppressors. It should not be left to civil society organisations (CSOs), or human rights and pro-democracy groups. Agitation for good governance should be a job for all patriotic citizens. Failure to do so, political hawks will continue to prey on the society and everyone suffers.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The bad governance habit of the Nigerian ruling class may not be obliterated in one fell swoop of a few days of protest. In view of this fact, organisers of the #EndBadGovernance campaign may need to institutionalise it. They may need to make it a regular activity, say, once in a month, similar to the weekly Monday sit-at-home non-violent protest in the South-East. Each protest outing may be concentrated on a particular aspect of concern at each point in time.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>This line of action poses a major organisational challenge particularly when some well-meaning participants may interpret the concept of civil protest in varying ways. Since people do not form new habits overnight, they are most likely to conceptualise it in terms of their traditional ways of reacting to perceived injustice.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>For this reason, organisers have to undertake a massive project of teaching citizens how to conduct civil protests, using social media, town halls and face to face contacts. This approach poses the problem that government does not want civilized ways of protest and prefer to keep people in the dark about its activities, hence indirectly facilitating anti-social avenues for expressing social grievances.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The protesters must also insist on the amendment of the Electoral Act to include as follows:</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>i. Compulsory use of BVAS for accreditation and transmission of results from the units.<br>ii. Collation of transmitted election result from the units to the IREV should be done electronically which should be on display nationwide.<br>iii. Conducting both Federal and State elections in one day.<br>iv. Local Government elections should be conducted by INEC and not State Electoral Commision.<br>v. The appointment of INEC Chairman and Commissioners should be devoid of the Executive input for it to be purely independent.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Sustainable development in a deeply corrupt country such as Nigeria necessitates relentless momentum of a permanent nature from patriots to mount pressure on government officials to change for good. It requires intelligent and vigilant involvement of all citizens in all ways at all times and at all levels of government in the country.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Chris O. O. Biose, Chief Solomon Akeni,<br>Protem Chairman, Protem Secretary.<br>(a.k.a. OBIDIENT DADDY).<br>FOR AND ON BEHALF OF DELTA OBIDIENT ELDERS’ COUNCIL</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->', 'ISSUES FROM #ENDBADGOVERNANCE PROTEST, PRESS RELEASE BY DELTA OBIDIENT ELDERS’ COUNCIL, ASABA, DELTA STATE.', '', 'publish', 'open', 'closed', '', 'issues-from-endbadgovernance-protest-press-release-by-delta-obidient-elders-council-asaba-delta-state', '', '', '2024-08-10 09:50:50', '2024-08-10 09:50:50', '', 0, 'https://boldtimes.net.ng/?p=1143', 0, 'post', '', 0),
(1159, 1, '2025-01-04 17:09:24', '2025-01-04 17:09:24', '<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>GLORIFICATION OF MILITARY DICTATORS IN NIGERIA<br>By Chris O. O. Biose,<br>chrisoobiose@gmail.com</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The proposed renaming of the University of Abuja to Yakubu Gowon University is yet another misstep by President Bola Ahmed Tinubu. It is totally uncalled for. No reason has been given for the proposal and no benefit is expected to accrue to the university for the proposed change.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>This is not an issue on which the Senator Godswill Akpabio led 10th Senate would display its usual habit of “rubber-stamp”. A situation where holders of degrees from University of Abuja would suddenly find themselves holding degrees named after an African military dictator whom Nobel Laureate, Professor Wole Soyinka once described as “progenies of the abnormal phenomenon of military adventurism in the African continent” leaves much to be desired. It could accentuate credibility gap of such degree holders and create reputational challenges especially in international academia and global employment market.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The major social problems that currently plague Nigeria are directly traceable to acculturation of a whole generation of Nigerians in anti-social and anti-nationalistic behaviour during period of prolonged military misrule. It was bad enough that military tyrants bestrode the Nigerian political space for decades. But it is totally intolerable that these tyrants pose as messiahs and are hero-worshipped by the Nigerian elite.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Celebration of Coup Masters<br>The patronizing manner in which Nigerian politicians regard former military dictators must be a matter of grave concern to democrats. They celebrate adepts in coup-making that seized political power by force for about three decades. The stock in trade of coupists is treachery. As a former Chief of Army Staff and ex-Minister of Defence, Lt. General Theophillus Y. Danjuma (retd.) explained in 1986, “coup making is not an act of bravery or gallantry but a function of opportunism.” (The Guardian, Friday, 21 March 1986, p 19).</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>These treacherous soldiers perpetrated permanent damage on Nigeria. They institutionalised brutalities, even among their own compatriots as illustrated by Col. Dr. Bello Fadile in his recent book entitled “NINE LIVES: The Bello-Fadile Memoirs”.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The soldiers instigated civil unrest and massacre of civil population, culminating in the Nigeria-Biafra War (July 1967 to January 1970). The war led to about 100,000 military casualties while between 500,000 and 2 million Biafrans died of starvation.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Beyond flagrant abuse of human rights, torture, extra-judicial execution of citizens and advertising blood letting in the civil population, Nigerian junta leaders introduced unchallenged looting of the national treasury in a manner never experienced before the advent of military rule. First Republic politicians were afraid of stealing public funds. During military rule, it was done with impunity because no one could challenge armed men in power.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The Nigerian military capped their atrocities against democracy and rule of law with brazen annulment of the June 12, 1993 presidential election, adjudged by local and foreign observers as the freest and fairest election ever held in the country.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Nigerians Worship Evil Rulers<br>When Nigeria’s 7th Military Dictator, General Sani Abacha was alive, first class traditional rulers, bishops, erudite scholars, business men and professionals made regular pilgrimages to the maximum dictator, begging him to rule. Leaders of the five registered political parties at that time (which Chief Bola Ige, SAN, famously referred to as “five fingers of a leprous hand”), all nominated the military strongman as sole candidate for presidential election.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The youthful Daniel Kanu facilitated an organization christened Youths Earnestly Ask for Abacha (YEAA). Under that platform, he organized a massive pro-Abacha two-million-man march from all local government areas in all states of the country to Abuja from March 3-4, 1998, in support of General Abacha’s self-succession plot.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The gold medal for Abacha panegyrist must be reserved for Anambra-born billionaire who demonstrated unrivalled sophistry, artistry and imagination in his support for the military dictator. In an article entitled Deification of a General, notable Nigerian essayist and former Minister of Aviation, Femi Fani-Kayode, drew attention to an interview granted to TheNews Magazine of April 14, 1997, at page A7. Giving reasons why Abacha should succeed himself, the praise-singer said, among other things:<br>“If Abacha is not there, that is the end of democracy.”<br>“Only Abacha can control the dangers we have.”<br>“Only Abacha can monitor the Army.”<br>“Abacha is the symbol of Nigerian unity.”<br>“Abacha provides security and stability.”<br>“Political economic and social stability is with Abacha.”<br>“Only Abacha can be there and if you challenge him, you are challenging God.”</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>“If Abacha does not want to be president, I will leave the country because there will be civil war.”</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>When General Abacha died suddenly on June 8, 1998, a few weeks before the onset of his seemingly assured civilian presidency, the businessman did not only fail to keep his promise of leaving Nigeria, he quickly made new political friends to keep his business alive.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>It was only after the death of General Abacha that some people started calling him “thief” and the story of “Abacha loot” is all over the media. I marvel at the hypocrisy of the Nigerian elite. If Abacha was alive, media practitioners would not dare to call him a thief. He would have been enjoying his loot as his living colleagues are doing today.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Nobody is asking billionnaire Generals what legitimate salaries and allowances they earned while in service or what inventions they made to earn billions of Naira to build their mansions and estates in diverse parts of the world and live in apparent opulence.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Nigerian coup masters go about with an air of triumph over Nigeria because no true Nigerian leader has had courage to address fundamental issues, face reality or do justice in the polity.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Call Things by Their Names<br>Nigeria is a country whose top political space brims with coup plotters, beneficiaries of military coups and “fantastically corrupt” public officials. But the Nigerian elite consistently misinforms the younger generation about the status of the movers and shakers of the Nigerian society. The media unfortunately joins in extolling former military dictators who killed or violently removed elected civilian leaders or their military colleagues in order to acquire political power.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>These duplicitous characters are presented to Nigerians as “heroes” rather than villains which they really are. We call them “former Head of State”, “President”, “statesman” or the like. If we call a coup plotter a President, what shall we call Alhaji Abubakar Tafawa Balewa, Alhaji Shehu Shagari or Dr. Goodluck Jonathan?</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>No legitimate or democratically elected President is known to have granted State Pardon to those coup plotters, offenders against the state as late President Shehu Shagari did to late ex-Biafran leader, General Chukwuemeka Odumegwu-Ojukwu on May 18, 1982, for his role in the Nigeria-Biafra war. The state pardon granted by President Shagari enabled General Odumegwu-Ojukwu to return to Nigeria on June 20, 1982, as a free citizen after about twelve years in exile.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>In the run-up to the 2023 General Election, almost every presidential aspirant of note visited former coup masters to seek their support and blessing. What kind of blessing does one expect from a coup plotter? How can one say that he is a democrat who does not support coup making and yet depends on successful coup leaders to bless him for a democratic election?</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Civil Society Organisations (CSOs) need to do the younger generation a favour by constantly making them understand the truth about those that are falsely regarded as leaders or role models in the country.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The most charitable and factual description of a former military ruler is “former Military Dictator”. It is a grave injustice that the biggest felons in the history of the country do not only escape justice but are also presented as valorous role models. This sends the wrong signals and teaches the wrong lesson to the younger generation.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Naming a major educational institution after a former military dictator is an indirect recommendation of that brutish system of government. How could anyone who calls himself a democrat eulogise military dictatorship?</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>By worshipping former coup masters, Nigeria has done nothing to purge itself of the coup mentality. Indeed, that mentality has spread to nearly all aspects of the social life of the country, especially violent or fraudulent acquisition of political power without remorse under the pretext of democratic elections.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>General Gowon Must Atone For His Misdeeds<br>This is not a time to heap undeserved honours on former military dictators in Nigeria. Here is President Tinubu, criticizing military dictators in Mali, Burkina Faso and Niger Republic and at the same time honouring former military dictators in his own country. This is a contradiction or more realistically, an expression of his deep love for military tyrants.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Rather than receiving honours, it is time for General Yakubu Gowon to atone for his misdeeds in the course of his 8 years dictatorship. He laid foundation for balkanisation of Nigeria into unviable states when he unilaterally created 12 states in 1967 to protect his personal power and advance Fulani hegemony in Nigeria.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The clear objective of the July 29, 1966 coup plotters was to Divide Nigeria as indicated by their battle cry, Araba (Hausa word for Divide It). His later mantra of “One Nigeria” was but a rationalization of selfish ends because of advice received from their British friends.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>General Gowon’s illegitimate military junta also destroyed fiscal federalism in Nigeria with the abolition of fiscal federalism and imposition of fiscal unitarism. His Petroleum Decree No. 51, 1967 provided in Schedule 1 that:<br>“the entire ownership and control of all petroleum in, under or upon any land out to the 200 miles limit of the exclusive economic zone is vested in the Federal Republic of Nigeria.” He expropriated all petroleum resources and claimed absolute ownership and control of all petroleum resources in the Niger Delta.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Unknown to Niger Deltans and the generality of Nigerians, from then on, the Nigeria-Biafra war became effectively a war to control the vast oil resources of the Niger Delta. This was viewed in inner military circles as “war booty”. This is the mentality (sharing oil money) that has being inherited from the military by contemporary Nigerian politicians</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>General Gowon may rightly be regarded as the direct cause of the agitation of Niger Delta militants for economic justice in the Niger Delta. If he did not seize the oil wealth of the Niger Delta, there would have been no need for thousands of young Niger Deltans to die in pursuit of economic justice which still eludes the region.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Apparently, President Tinubu lacks ideas to move Nigeria forward and appears too self-concerned to implement measures that would liberate the masses from the exploitative hegemony that seeks to enslave them. Rather, he prefers to launder his unflattering image by playing with the destiny of national institutions and thereby, the destiny of future generations of Nigerians.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>General Gowon has one duty yet to fulfil to Nigerians in his life. As the military dictator who promulgated the string of military decrees that dispossessed the Niger Delta of its land and resources, he must be involved in the restoration of these lands and resources to the rightful owners.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Although he could not reasonably be expected to singlehandedly undo the evil he has done, he has a moral burden to come out boldly and courageously to tell the National Assembly and the entire world, this is why I did it. The situation is no longer the same and the decrees are no longer tenable. NASS, please remove the decrees and return the situation to what it was or to something fair for the people.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>This is a greater, more urgent and useful duty than his pet project of “Nigeria Prays”. Prayer without works is dead. Prayer without doing what one knows to be right is worse than useless.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->', 'GLORIFICATION OF MILITARY DICTATORS IN NIGERIA', '', 'publish', 'open', 'closed', '', 'glorification-of-military-dictators-in-nigeria', '', '', '2025-01-04 17:09:24', '2025-01-04 17:09:24', '', 0, 'https://boldtimes.net.ng/?p=1159', 0, 'post', '', 0),
(1161, 1, '2025-01-04 17:20:21', '2025-01-04 17:20:21', '', 'IMG-20241231-WA0033~2', '', 'inherit', 'open', 'closed', '', 'img-20241231-wa00332', '', '', '2025-01-04 17:20:21', '2025-01-04 17:20:21', '', 0, 'http://boldtimes.net.ng/wp-content/uploads/2025/01/IMG-20241231-WA00332.jpg', 0, 'attachment', 'image/jpeg', 0);
INSERT INTO `wp5g_posts` (`ID`, `post_author`, `post_date`, `post_date_gmt`, `post_content`, `post_title`, `post_excerpt`, `post_status`, `comment_status`, `ping_status`, `post_password`, `post_name`, `to_ping`, `pinged`, `post_modified`, `post_modified_gmt`, `post_content_filtered`, `post_parent`, `guid`, `menu_order`, `post_type`, `post_mime_type`, `comment_count`) VALUES
(1162, 1, '2025-01-04 17:27:03', '2025-01-04 17:27:03', '<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>KEYNOTE ADDRESS ON<br>IMPERATIVE OF PROPER POLITICAL LEADERSHIP RECRUITMENT<br>FOR GOOD GOVERNANCE</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Delivered By<br>CHRIS O. O. BIOSE (ODE-URI),<br>CEO, BOLD PUBLISHERS LTD., ASABA, DELTA STATE, NIGERIA</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Organised By<br>NIGERIAN UNION OF JOURNALISTS (NUJ), DELTA STATE COUNCIL, ASABA, DELTA STATE</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Holding At<br>NUJ PRESS CENTRE, MARYAM BABANGIDA WAY, ASABA, DELTA STATE</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>ON<br>MONDAY, DECEMBER 30, 2024, AT 11.00AM</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Protocols</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>“Authority stealing pass armed robbery.”</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:list -->\n<ul class=\"wp-block-list\"><!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li>Fela Anikulapo Kuti (1938 – -1997)</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item --></ul>\n<!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>(1) Introduction<br>I thank the Chairman and Members of Council of the Nigerian Union of Journalists, Delta State, for inviting me to speak on this important occasion of the NUJ Delta State Council 2024 Press Week. The role of the media is central in any social democracy and the theme of this Press Week is most apt, bearing in mind the leadership challenges that plague our dear country.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>(2) Perspective of This Presentation<br>In addressing the challenge of leadership recruitment in Nigeria, it is necessary to understand its root causes. Towards this objective, this presentation adopts a historical approach and attempts to periodise notable political developments in Nigeria and their interaction with private biography of individuals.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Most Nigerian youths are not aware of the root cause of the social and security challenges posed by ethnic and religious politics that turn their lives upside down. It is instructive to take a glance back in time and understand that the present is an outgrowth of the past as well as it portends the future.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>(3) Role of Political Parties in Leadership Recruitment<br>A political party is an organised group of people, united by common interest or ideology and who engage themselves in power struggle for the purpose of controlling the personnel, policies and machinery of government. A viable party system is the foundational basis of sustainable social democracy.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Political parties are the most efficient and effective means of articulating and aggregating the wide diversity of interests in a given society. It is also the principal mechanism for recruitment of political leaders and the platform on which its members stand to access government.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The political party is the principal source of choice of political heads of government departments, The quality of political party leadership therefore informs administrative capacity to formulate, implement and monitor socio-economic policies.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Efficient party organisation solidifies consensus of minds on fundamental issues. The party is then better able to fulfill its function of awakening and sustaining political consciousness among the people, thereby helping to build a virile political culture.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>(4) Credible Elections and Proper Leadership Recruitment<br>The critical issue in a democracy is not whether a person is in power but the manner of coming to power. Elections are institutionalised procedures for choosing office holders by members of a group.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Election constitutes the primary method of recruiting political leaders. It is regarded as the best procedure for setting up new governments and ensuring orderly succession of governments. In a functional social democracy, power resides in the electorate.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Free and fair election means that all registered voters who are able and willing to vote have unfettered opportunities to cast their votes without let or hindrance or undue obstacles placed on their way. The electorate is organised to choose its preferred representatives from nomination of party candidates to the general election.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>A government that emerges from a free and fair election is regarded as legitimate. This refers to exercise of powers by a government which possesses the right to govern and where the governed recognize that right.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>(5) Post-Independence Leadership (1960-1966)<br>As a teenager in 1960, I shared the general euphoria of Nigeria gaining political independence on October 1, 1960. Most developed countries were organised as nation-state and it was hoped that Nigeria too would gain the benefit of state organisation as an independent country. Post-independence administration was expected to build indigenous institutions that serve the citizens rather than foreign interests.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The relationship between the Nigerian state and the citizens was put to test soon after independence resulting in a series of crises. Notable among these were the Tiv crisis, the Census Crisis of 1962, repeated in 1963 without being resolved, the Action Group crisis 1962 and the Western Region Parliamentary Election Crisis of 1965. These events precipitated military take over on January 15, 1966 and the Nigeria-Biafra war, which was followed by strings of military coups.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>(6) Leadership Recruitment Under Military Rule (1966-1979 and 1984-1999)<br>Men of the Nigerian Army overthrew the civilian government on January 15, 1966 and introduced a new concept of leadership recruitment based on undisguised violence and treachery.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>In a system of military rule, there is no room for debates or rhetorics. One must obey the boss. This period of Nigerian history left the following imprint on the Nigerian national psyche:</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>• Bloodshed was regularly advertised in the public space;<br>• Freedom of speech was suspended and this occasioned closure of media houses and imprisonment of journalists without trial;<br>• Introduction of religion into Nigerian politics vide enrolment of the country as Full Member of the Organisation of Islamic Conference (OIC) in January 1986. This emboldened some Muslims to started asking for full Sharia in the country;<br>• Destruction of the economic base of the country through imposition of Structural Adjustment Programme (SAP) and imprisonment of those who opposed it;<br>• Brazen annulment of the June 12, 1993 presidential election, adjudged by local and international observers as the freest and fairest election in Nigerian history;<br>• Retroactive penal Decrees (e.g. Decree 4, 1984);<br>• Lawlessness as a way of life of the military ruling Professionals and intellectuals were routinely recruited to paint lawless acts of military tyrants with aura of intellectual prestige.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>After 30 years of military rule, a generation of Nigerians who were born and nurtured within the context of military dictatorship had been socialized to believe that force makes right and saw military dictatorship as normal. They did not see anything wrong with autocratic leadership - barking orders, brigandage and violence, including official and unofficial murders and assassinations which became practical means of gaining political power. One gains the benefit of his heinous crime as long as there was no one more powerful to hold him to account.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>(7) Upsurge of Pro-Democracy Activism in Nigeria<br>One of the significant but unanticipated consequences of sustained high-handedness of the Babangida and Abacha military juntas was the emergence of pro-democracy and human rights movements that sprang up to protest continued military dictatorship. The extreme brutalities of military juntas had the unforeseen consequence of conscientising and radicalising a large crop of young lawyers as manifested in the explosion of civil rights groups and non-governmental organisations from the Babangida era onwards.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Prominent among these were Civil Liberties Organisation (CLO), Campaign for Democracy (CD), Committee for Defence of Human Rights (CDHR), Constitutional Rights Project (CRP), CP, NCP, National Association of Democratic Lawyers (NADL). and National Democratic Coalition (NADECO).</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>I joined the pro-democracy movement in Nigeria shortly after the annulment of the June 12, 1993 presidential election.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>(8) My Initiation Into Partisan Politics in 1979<br>By the end of the first stanza of military rule, I came to the conclusion that while building a knowledge-based society was fundamental to nation-building and development, the most critical factor at that point in time was deploying knowledge to bring about political and economic transformation of a backward society.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Based on my reading of the manifestoes of the five registered political parties at that time (NPN, GNPP, NPP, PRP and UPN), as well as their leadership and organisational structure at that time, I enlisted in political party struggle to build a great Nigerian nation-state under the platform of the Unity Party of Nigeria (UPN).</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>(9) Beginning of Imposed Leadership in 1983<br>The 1983 General Election marked the beginning of imposition of governors and heads of state on Nigerian citizens under the pretense of conducting democratic elections. Prior to the election, opposition political parties were in control of the capitals of old regions, Ibadan, Kaduna, Benin City, Enugu. The ruling party at the national level, NPN, wanted to demonstrate that it was indeed national and mapped out definite plan to acquire control of those states by rigging the elections. The party carried out the plan but the social consequence proved to be extremely costly, not only for the party but for the country and generations to come.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>For example, at the end of the gubernatorial election held in old Ondo State on August 13, 1983, the candidate of the NPN, Chief Akin Omoboriowo scored 703,792 votes but the then Federal Electoral Commission (FEDECO) changed his score to 1,228,981. The electoral body also reduced the scores of Governor Michael Adekunle Ajasin from 1,563,377 to 1,015,385. This enabled the Federal Government to falsely declare Omoboriowo as winner of the election and Governor-elect on the platform of the then ruling NPN.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Since neither the Federal Electoral Commission (FEDECO) that perpetrated the electoral fraud nor the Sunday Adewus-led Nigeria Police Force that aided the massive rigging could be relied upon to exercise their duties in accordance with the laws of the land, the masses took the law into their own hands.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>On August 16, 1983, wide spread civil disorder broke out in Ondo State following the announcement of Chief Akin Omoboriowo’s name as Governor-elect of the State when the people actually voted for Chief Adekunle Ajasin.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Unable to withstand the violent protests, Omoboriowo fled Ondo State in disguise to Lagos while the returning officer for the election was escorted away to his home in a military armoured tank.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>In the ensuing riots, scores of high and low perished as human beings were burnt along with property. Notable personalities killed during the riot included a popular member of the House of Representatives, Olaiya Fagbamigbe, Akure NPN leader, Agbayewa and member of House of Assembly, Barrister Tunde Agunbiade who started celebrating the electoral robbery openly and was decapitated by the irate mob. The rioters razed FEDECO office on Oba Adesida Road, Akure.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The rigging of the 1965 parliamentary election in Western Region angered some idealistic soldiers that staged the January 1966 military coup. In the same manner, the widespread rigging of the 1983 general election instigated the military coup on the night of December 31, 198 that brought Major General Muhammadu Buhari to power as Nigeria’s 5th Military Dictator.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Summarising the 1983 electoral fraud, Alhaji Ahmed Kurfi, who was the Chief Electoral Officer of FEDECO for the 1979 federal election stated:</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>“All sorts of strategies and stratagems including manipulation of the ballot or “rigging” were employed in order to win elections. Each of the opposition parties used its local power of incumbency to retain power and/or to improve its position vis-à-vis other contenders. However, federal might was used to dislodge state governors in Anambra, Oyo, Kaduna, Gongola and Borno states, thus raising NPN’s tally of governorships from seven to twelve.” (1983 election (Ahmed Kurfi, On strategies and strategems of rigging, 2005,97)</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>(10) Obnoxious Military Constitution<br>The document entitled Constitution of the Federal Republic of Nigeria (Promulgation) 1999, Decree No. 24, which serves as the Nigerian constitution is a consolidation of the treachery that defines Nigeria.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The document opens with the following words:<br>“WE, THE PEOPLE of the Federal Republic of Nigeria;</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>“HAVING firmly and solemnly resolved;</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>“TO LIVE in unity and harmony as one indivisible and indisolluble sovereign Nation under God….</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>“DO HEREBY MAKE AND GIVE TO OURSELVES the following Constitution.”</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The claim in the above-quoted opening words of the Decree that “We, the people of the Federal Republic of Nigeria…firmly and solemnly resolved” to make that Constitution is absolutely false. At no time did the people of Nigeria or their accredited representatives make or approve that Decree as their Constitution.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The document was signed by the country’s 8th and last Military Dictator, General Abdulsalami Abubakar (retd.), two weeks before he handed power to a former military dictator of Nigeria, General Olusegun Obasanjo (retd.) as a civilian President of Nigeria on May 29, 1999. The document has never been ratified by the Nigerian people through a referendum.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Whatever the extent of patch-work or serial amendments. It remains a Military Decree. What is germane to the constitution of a country is not the title but the process that goes into its making. A head of government may conceivably ask a professor or assemble a number of professors in a University and ask them to write a beautiful document and call it the constitution of a country. If citizens of the country concerned are not consulted, did not participate in making the document and did not ratify it in a referendum, the document may represent an excellent academic exercise but cannot reasonably be regarded by citizens as their constitution The constitution of a democratic state must derive from citizens of the country concerned and that cannot be said of Decree 24, 1999.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>(11) Leadership Recruitment in Post-Military Nigeria<br>The out-going military junta in 1999 had a vested interest in foisting a former military dictator as President. Anyone who has any doubt about how the 1999 presidential election was rigged may wish to read Final Report of the Carter Center on the 1999 Election in Nigeria.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Shortly after assuming office, former President Obasanjo quickly shoved off the civilian National Chairman of his party, Chief Solomon Lar, and installed a retired colonel of the Nigerian Army, Col. Ahmadu Alli (retd.), as Chairman of the ruling party. This set the tone for instability of political party structures and executive control of political parties in post-military rule in Nigeria.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The patriotism of General Olusegun Obasanjo (retd) was never in doubt but his brash authoritarianism was equally indisputable.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The 2003 general election was the most comprehensively rigged in Nigeria’s electoral history.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>It was followed by the 2007 election when the Professor Maurice Iwu-led INEC simply wrote results without regard to voting. Till date, detailed results of the election have not been published. In the Report which it submitted to late President Umaru Musa Yar’Adua on December 11, 2008, the Chairman of the Electoral Reform Commission (ERC), Hon. Justice Mohammed Uwais described it as “the worst since the first elections in Nigeria in 1923”.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The 2011 general elections were relatively free and fair due mainly to the personality of the then President, Dr. Goodluck Jonathan, not due to change in the behaviour of the political class.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>(12) Undemocratic Political Parties Since 1999<br>Political parties formed in Nigeria before independence in 1960 and up to the end of the First Republic, defined their objectives and tactics in clear terms. However, since the beginning of the Fourth Republic in 1999, political parties were formed primarily to access political power, not to achieve clearly defined national objectives.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The two political parties that controlled the Federal Government of Nigeria since May 1999 (PDP and APC) are two sides of the same coin. They lack political ideology. Both are committed to protection of feudal privileges.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>They both failed to perform functions of political parties as earlier enunciated in this presentation.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>They failed to take principled positions on burning issues of the day, let alone, educate their members on such issues and the factual or rational basis of party positions. They only wait for election time to print posters to deface the environment, disturb the peace of the communities with riotous rallies and share ill-gotten money to corrupt the electorate.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Rather than consolidating democratic culture, Nigeria steadily degenerated into a neo-feudal society where a small set of individuals determine who would contest election or get what elective position. It started with the idea of power shift in 1999. It deteriorated with doctrine of zoning political positions to ethnic or geographical areas in advance of voting.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Nigeria became a place where unscrupulous men impose themselves as mouthpiece of their clans, local government areas and states or champions of their ethnic and religious groups.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Consequently, Nigeria in 2024 is politically in a far worse and more precarious position than it was at independence. In 1960, the ruling Northern People Congress (NPC) did not mince words about its Northernisation agenda. It held proudly and tenaciously to its slogan of “One North, One Constitution, One Destiny”. It did not even bother to field candidates to contest elections in other Regions of the country. In contrast, the Opposition Action Group took every opportunity to parade its socialist credentials and programmes.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>During the 4-years Shagari inter-regnum (1979-1983) that punctuated about 30 years of military dictatorship (1966-1999), the ruling NPN (a reincarnation of the NPC) was unrepentantly conservative. The Opposition UPN on the other hand was core democratic socialist in ideology and its famous “4-Cardinal Programmes” was a model in ideological packaging.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>But from 1999, the country came to be ruled by a disparate collection of military apologists, nominees of political potentates (illegitimate products of zoning), religious fanatics and ethnic jingoists masquerading as Representatives, Senators, Governors and Presidents. The country is held to ransom by an incongruous collection of political misfits and celebrated crooks, most of who are neither responsible nor accountable to society but to faceless cliques. Under this condition, corruption, greed and infighting sap national energies and deprived the country of national character.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>(13) Leadership Recruitment Pattern Since 2015<br>The 2015 general election brought to the fore a new social force in the electoral process in the form of explicit religiously loaded campaign as well as systematic enforcement or denial of voting. While voting was based on accreditation in most Southern states, there was free thumb printing by adults and children alike in areas favourable to the APC presidential candidate. The result of the election showed that there were far more unaccredited voters than those accredited to vote. (See How 2015 election was rigged in the North by Alhaji Tanko Yakasai, available online). The 2019 election was militarized and lack credibility.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The most outstanding feature of 8 years of the tenure of former President Muhammadu Buhari (2015 to 2023) is revitalization of Fulani Jihad (1804-1809), this time through the Jihadist movement and armed Fulani herdsmen that physically took large swath of the Middle Belt and Southern Nigeria by means of unchecked violence.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>(14) Religionisation of Political Leadership Recruitment in Nigeria<br>One of the underlying and unspoken factors in leadership recruitment in Nigeria is religion. The religionisation of the Nigerian political space which started with enrolment of Nigeria as Full Member of the Organisation of Islamic Conference (OIC) in 1986, came to full maturity in 2011, when over 800 citizens were killed in post-election riots by religious fanatics in North-East Nigeria. In 2015, religion became a visible factor in federal election as religious fanatics openly stopped some citizens from voting in their areas of influence.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Totally frustrated with the abject failure of former President Buhari to fulfil his major electoral promises (security, economy and devolution of power) as well as heightened insecurity across the country, veteran Northern politician and one of the strongest opponents of President Goodluck Jonathan in the 2011 and 2015 presidential elections, Alhaji Tanko Yakasai, let the cat out of the bag and publicly described how the 2015 presidential election was rigged in the Northern Area. (Akin Alofetekun, How 2015 poll was rigged in North – Tanko Yakassai, THE GUARDIAN, April 16, 2018).</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The religious forces that brought former President Muhammadu Buhari to power in 2015 and sustained his inglorious 8-years of misrule was intensified to ensure continuation of Muslim rule in 2023. In furtherance of its religious empire building design laid by the Buhari presidency, the leadership of the APC decided at the end of President Buhari’s second term of office in 2023, to foist a Muslim President and Muslim Vice President on the country.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>From this time, community mission in the Republic became increasingly missing. Politics was no more about competing ideas to foster public good. It was about how a feudal-theocratic hegemony shared the wealth of the country.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>At the present time, all arms of government, including agencies such as the security services, police and judiciary that are expected to protect public interest are actually deployed to serve the interest of the supreme feudal ruler who pretends to be serving the interests of the masses.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>(15) Commercialisation of Voting as Leadership Recruitment<br>The next stage of the bastardisation of leadership recruitment in Nigeria is commercialisation of the electoral process from nomination of party candidates to general election. Where these fail, the electoral body is known to engage in outright writing and announcement of fake results that have no bearing with voting as recorded in several parts of the country over the years. (See Judgement of the Court of Appeal, Hon. Justice Monica Dongban-Mensah in the case cited as Chief Great Ovedje Ogboru and Anor v Dr. Emmanuel Ewetan Uduaghan &amp; Ors CA/B/EPT/38/10)</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Ultimately, the judiciary is available to award judgment for the highest bidder in what notable eminent constitutional lawyer and human right activist, Mike Ozechome, SAN, described as “judocracy”. (Steve Oko, Nigerian Practices “Judocracy” and not Democracy, Vanguard Online, December 15, 2024).</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>One of the most bizarre examples of this pernicious manner of recruiting political leaders in Nigeria took place during the Ondo State governorship election held on November 16, 2024. It was an open rehearsal of vote buying and selling. Prince Adewole Adebayo, presidential candidate of the Social Democratic Party (SDP) in the 2023 presidential election who is from Ondo State and who participated in the election as a voter, told Seun Okinbaloye of Channels Television on Monday, November 19, 2023 (POLITICS TODAY) that some candidates collected account numbers of voters for the purpose of paying them for votes. There was open haggling for price of votes. The candidate of one of the main parties was paying only N10,000 per vote and voters openly shunned that party and opted for the party that was paying N25,000 for one vote. Apparently, the highest bidder for votes purchased the election.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>(16) President Bola Ahmed Tinubu Administration<br>It is impossible to speak of leadership recruitment in Nigeria without commenting on the current state of leadership in the country.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>President Bola Ahmed Tinubu succeeded the most divisive Nigerian leader ever, far worse than late General Abacha whose wickedness was mainly restricted to Ogoni nationality and his military constituency.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The most vital need of the country today is to try to commence the process of building one nation-state out of hundreds of disparate and antagonistic groups that constitute the country. President Tinubu seems to have completely ignored this fundamental responsibility of leadership.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The global scenario of conflict of cultures depicted by the American political scientist, Samuel Huttington in his book entitled Clash of Civilizations and The Remaking of World Order (2011) is playing out gruesomely in Africa’s most populous country, Nigeria where Islam and Christianity are distributed in almost equal proportion in the population. This results in two opposed, almost irreconcilable religiously-based world-views about what the world and the country should be.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Unlike the balance of military power between Western and Islamic nations, the balance in Nigeria seems to favour the Muslims due mainly to their domination of the armed forces. The desire of political leaders to maintain this advantage is also at the root of seeming inability of some leaders to address security issues squarely. The perceived nepotism encountered in recruitment and posting of military personnel in turn plague the security apparatus of the state and impinges on its effectiveness.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Education is a key factor in nation building and leadership. Chief Obafemi Awolowo and his Action Group colleagues established Universal Primary Education (UPE) in Western Region (present-day Lagos, Ogun, Oyo, Osun, Ondo, Ekiti, Edo and Delta states) in 1955. As Nigeria’s 4th Military dictator, General Olusegun Obasanjo extended UPE to the entire country in 1976. He returned as civilian President to extended universal education to cover nine years of basic education for all Nigerian children from 2004 onwards. Northern leaders refused to implement the scheme. It fell to former President Goodluck Jonathan to reinvigorate the programme and extend it to almajirai children in 2012 but again former President Buhari’s administration crashed it. The question is: what has President Tinubu done to encourage universal basic education of Nigerian children.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Another critical issue is security of life and property. President Tinubu has so far failed to address the issue of violent seizure of ancestral land by Fulani herdsmen during the tenure of former President Buhari, particularly in the Middle Belt.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Speaking to Seun Okinbaloye of Channels TV on Friday, April 12, 2024, the Speaker of the Plateau State House of Assembly, Rt. Hon. Gabriel Dewan described continued killings in the Plateau as “ethnic cleansing” and “conspiracy against the people” of Plateau State.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>As at June 2024, indigenous youth groups in Plateau State revealed that 151 villages had been forcibly occupied and renamed by armed Fulani herdsmen. The original owners of these communities are left to roam.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The Middle Belt is one of the chief suppliers of crop produce in Nigeria. Hundreds of thousands of these people have been displaced for years, aggravating the hunger crisis in the country.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Does the Tinubu administration mean that the victims of violent land grabbing have lost their ancestral land for ever to the invaders?<br>The environmental degradation of the Niger Delta by international oil companies in collaboration with the Federal Government has not received any mention by the President.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Nigeria has not been able to produce an accurate census of the number of people living in its territory due to moral morbidity of its political and bureaucratic elite. It is not even in the 2025 budget.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The almagiri phenomenon in Northern Nigeria is a classic case of the deliberate pauperisation of a class of Nigerian citizens by the Nigerian ruling class. These are children who live in the streets abandoned by their families and their governments.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>They have no homes, no parental protection, no access to education and health services and no technological or marketable skills. There is no social security and no safety nets. All doors of social mobility are closed against them.<br>Not only has President Tinubu failed to address the social problems posed by the social system that produces this class of citizens, the policies of his administration has resulted in increasing almagirisation of the entire Nigerian populace. During the Christmas celebration last week, social media was awash with beggars, reminiscent of almagiri, flooding the streets near Boudillon residence of President Tinubu. The pauperisation of the Nigerian population is a danger signal.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Two sharply opposed classes, oppressor and oppressed, have become clearly visible in contemporary Nigeria. The interest of oppressor and oppressed are totally irreconcilable. The oppressor is totally divorced from citizens and procures fake support of his victims through purchase of votes, intimidation, manipulation and fraud.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Of all basic problems facing Nigeria, the only one currently in the works is decentralisation of policing, through introduction of state and community policing. Even then, the debilitating and self-destructive level of corruption, indiscipline and lawlessness of the Nigeria Police Force is such that if it is not addressed, a larger number of police personnel will only increase the misery of Nigerians.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>From the above, it is abundantly clear that President Tinubu has not demonstrated the requisite level of leadership expected from a caring president of this country.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Suffice it to say that the attitude of the current leadership to pressing national issues is not an aberration. It is a natural product of the social forces that propel the country. Therefore, the actions and inactions of the current administration is a reflection of that reality.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>(17) Non-Punishment for Electoral Offences<br>Electoral malfeasors are at the root of poor leadership recruitment in Nigeria. They continue to dominate the Nigerian political space because of the country’s weak law enforcement mechanism. The Nigeria Police Force and judiciary are in the pockets of politicians that appoint them.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>As far back as 2011, Hon. Justice Mohammed Uwais Chairman of the Electoral Reform Committee (ERC), recommended the establishment of a special Electoral Offences Court to deal with electoral offences but politicians who are beneficiaries of electoral heist failed to do so. Rather, they speedily made laws to gag the media to stop citizens from exposing their crimes.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>(18) The Challenge of Post-Tinubu Presidency<br>The first challenge facing Nigeria is that the political recruitment system that brought President Tinubu to power in 2023 is most likely to be reinforced in 2027. This foreboding is heightened by two facts. One, the President has said nothing about reforming the flawed electoral system. Next is the Second Term Syndrome whereby second term elections of incumbents tend to be far worse than that which brought them to power.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Second Term Syndrome consists of a set of anti-democratic behaviour patterns adopted by ineffective governments to subvert the electoral process in order to remain in office. In this regard, the second term election of Alhaji Sir Abubakar Tafawa Balewa in 1964 was the most critical in Nigerian history. It set the stage for military destabilisation of constitutional rule in Nigeria.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>To borrow the words of Mr. Peter Obi, presidential candidate of the Labour Party in the 2023 presidential election, the historic task of this new generation of freedom fighters is to “dismantle the present system of criminality” and establish proper democratic leadership in Nigeria.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Nigeria must return to the drawing board. Like nationalist leaders that fought for Nigerian independence in 1960, there is absolute need for a coalition of new crop of leaders.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The battle for a New Nigeria will be fought on two fronts, namely, moral rearmament of the new generation of freedom fighters and constitutional change that guarantees participatory democracy.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Leaders of the New Nigeria must build institutions that work for citizens. The most critical is political restructuring which simply means return to parliamentary system of government. Zoning of elective positions in advance of voting is abrogated.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>If a good number of persons who listen to or read this message should consistently and persistently propagate the ideas within and outside your social circle, this idea will gain increasing social visibility and therefore social validity. This is one method to build a critical mass of persons, sufficiently influential to make a positive change in the society. Clearly, the media has a key role to play in building this critical mass.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>(19) Qualities of A National Leader<br>At the present historical juncture of its development, the political elite is the pivot of political, economic and social change in Nigeria. The action or inaction of members of this class at various levels culminate in group karma that affects both the living and unborn generations.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The fact that someone occupies a top position, irrespective of the means of securing that position, and irrespective of what he does with that position, does not make him a leader.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Competence, founded on integrity, knowledge, honesty and character are indispensable qualities of a good leader. Such a person must endow himself with Vision and actionable plan for development of the country. He must be incorruptible. Age and maturity to ensure orderly social processes are also indispensable.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>A national leader must combine stern discipline with a humane disposition. A leader of a potentially great country such as Nigeria must exude patriotism, commitment to stated goals and accepted norms of conduct of state affairs, fairness, vision and ability to conceive social and economic projects of lasting value. Those in authority must merit their positions in terms of temperament, trustworthiness and popular choice of the electorate in a free and fair election.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>A national leader is expected to put the interest of all citizens above his personal or ethnic interest.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>A leader of a multi-national and multi-cultural country like Nigeria needs capacity to build national consensus for political and economic emancipation of the masses. His goal must be to build a Nigerian nation-state, not merely a North or South ethno-religious hegemony.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>It is a function of leadership to demonstrate clear understanding of basic issues and reconcile or integrate varying perceptions through continuous dialogue. In this way, a genuine spirit of nationalism could emerge.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Nationalism is like a seed planted and nurtured to maturity when it can withstand the vagaries of political, economic and social weather conditions.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>A leader leads politically and ideologically. His power represents the interests of the overwhelming majority of the people of the country. His very presence is a negation of injustice, oppression, inequality and antagonistic social divisions. He does not side with his class or state or ethnic group or state of origin, either overtly or covertly. Such a leadership speaks the language of the country’s discontented masses.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Any person that occupies a position of political leadership in a multi-national, multi-cultural and multi-religious country such as Nigeria must be religiously neutral in his management of state affairs. He must restrict his religion to his private life and not foist his private religious convictions on the state.<br>(20) Presidential System is Unsuitable for Nigeria<br>After about a quarter of a century of presidential system of government in Nigeria, there is wide consensus among unbiased observers that the system has failed Nigerians.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The presidential system introduced by military rulers in 1979 is an aberration compared to the workings of the American presidential system on which it is fashioned. The Nigerian variant of the presidential system encourages executive dictatorship. The president has a misleading sense of power.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>For example, on June 30, 2019, the Muhammadu Buhari presidency announced that the Federal Government had gazetted establishment of RUGA Settlements in 36 states of the country. Ruga is a Hausa word for cow settlement.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The Scheme was however firmly rejected by almost all state governments in the Middle Belt, South-East, South-South, and South-West geo-political zones Southern and Middle Belt Leaders Forum (SMBLF), Tiv Professionals and most ethnic nationalities in those zones strongly opposed Buhari’s Gazette.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>President Tinubu appears to be indirectly reviving Buhari’s Ruga with his Ministry of Livestock Development. The Federal Government has no land anywhere in Nigeria, except the Federal Capital Territory (FCT), federal secretariats in states and barracks. One is yet to see how the Tinubu administration will undertake establishment of livestock businesses for the Fulani herders.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The presidential system is inordinately expensive. Due to huge expenditure involved in electioneering, the presidential system does not encourage grassroot political development. Many credible people cannot afford election, hence, the system is exclusionary rather than participatory.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>(21) Leadership Recruitment in Parliamentary System<br>The parliamentary system which I advocate in my book entitled STOP ZONING: RESTRUCTURE NIGERIA, is better adapted to withstand internal crises and external political forces. There is no need for expensive governorship and presidential elections that only corrupts the electorate.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The parliamentary system entails an entirely different and more participatory system of leadership recruitment. There is no Executive Governor but simply Governor. All the 36 overbearing Executive Governors in Nigeria will be swept away, so also is the wasteful, oppressive and unproductive Presidency. There is one Representative from each Federal Constituency. The Representatives form the Federal Parliament that elects one among themselves as Prime Minister (who functions as first among equals).</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>As Dr. Edwin Madunagu pointed out in June 1990, the Nigerian crisis can only be resolved in the same way in which it was created, namely, by direct political action (Dr. Edwin Madunagu, Whose national conference?, THE GUARDIAN, Thursday, June 21, 1990, p 9). To free federating units in the country from the enslaving and suffocating political environment brought upon them by military dictators, military structure must be dissolved and federal system restored. This is the principal mechanism for bringing about social, economic and political justice in the country. It will be aimed at correcting the deficits that detract from the concept of a federation. The guiding objective is to devolve powers to states or regions as it was in the First Republic.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>(22) Leadership Training in the Parliamentary System<br>Chief Obafemi Awolowo recommended that each member of the party Executive Committee should study a problem affecting a particular department so as to have first-hand grasp of the problem as it affects his or her ward, local government area, state or the country as a whole. Floor members of the party may choose or may be chosen to study a particular subject that interests him or her, study them exhaustively and deliver a discussion or a learned or policy paper on it. This is then subjected to analysis and general discussion.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>In this way, the party trains and builds up men and women who are versed with the problems with which the party has to deal with from time to time, particularly if voted into power. This is an effective tool of leadership training. You build up a core of leaders in several fields with adequate knowledge and shrewd judgement of men and matters.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Whether at local, state or federal levels, the party would not be short of men of practical competence in various fields or departments of government. Based on their experience, such men and women are also able to make input into party programmes.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>It also becomes easier to draw up a list of candidates for elections at various levels or make recommendations for appointment into local councils, board and departments of state. Such men are surely square pegs in square holes for prompt and efficient execution of party policies and programmes.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>(23) Huge Disappointment<br>I am saddened to observe that majority of my fellow elders at local levels and on the national scene are failing in their duty to speak truth to the authorities and the younger generation. The most significant things in life such as telling plain truth, may actually cost less in terms of cash but lead to much gain in terms of impact.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The role of some elders that saw Nigeria when it was fairly workable is hugely disappointing. Most of the older members of the military, political, economic and judicial elite seem to be united in suppression of social justice in Nigeria. There are too few voices in support of social justice in contemporary country.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>I feel ashamed that in most cases, a lot of Nigerian elders (septuagenarians, octogenarians and nonagenarians), join the oppressive ruling class, zombie generation and other rascals to encourage opaqueness in public affairs or refuse to speak out against oppression in the country.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>I am however encouraged by the unwavering and steadfast support for good causes by nonegenarian, Chief Ayo Adebanjo. His robust support for Mr. Peter Obi at the 2023 presidential election is worthy of commendation. As leader of the Pan-Yoruba socio-political organisation, Afenifere, he shunned ethnicity and chose the best candidate for Nigeria. He also spoke out boldly against recent persecution of human rights lawyer Mr. Dele Farotimi, by the Nigeria Police Force.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Here in Delta State, our political elder, Chief Edwin Kiagbodo Clark, former Minister of Information in the 1970s is an irrepressible voice for truth and justice. He continues to speak out with vigour, unmistakable clarity and forthrightness against oppression of Niger Delta, Middle Belt, and other vulnerable groups in the country.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>(24) Role of Civil Society Organisations (CSOs) in Leadership Change<br>Society is an integrated system of roles and it is impossible to isolate leadership and mark it out for change while ignoring the larger body of the society.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>It is not enough that political impostors manipulate the electoral process. It takes a docile citizenry and corrupt bureaucracy to complement and bring about the leadership failure that has brought Nigeria to its knees</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>CSOs may be likened to change catalysts. This is a variable that causes change without itself changing. It is an agent that causes, speeds up or slows down change in a development process. Such a catalyst should have communication skills, infectious enthusiasm which leaves permanent motivation.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The most outstanding CSOs that joined the struggle for social justice in Nigeria include the Nigerian Press, the Labour Movement, professional associations, notably the Nigerian Bar Association (NBA), civil rights groups notably the CLO, pro-democracy movements, non-governmental associations Yiaga Africa and pressure groups.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Every country needs a vibrant civil society as a counter balance to government. Even in a democratic setting, empowerment of the masses is the ultimate check to authoritarianism.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The critical custodians of democracy, economic development and fundamental human freedoms in the New Nigeria will be an empowered civil society, not the President and his ministers, not even parliament or party chiefs.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Through their actions, they set the tone for the wider society, particularly the youths who are ever more patriotic, altruistic and impressionistic.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>In established democracies such as UK and USA, civil society organisations act as watchdog of social, economic, political and judicial system.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>An important section of civil society is student organisation, particularly those in tertiary institutions. For decades, they remained highly patriotic.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>No country attains greatness without going through challenges.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>(25) Historic Role of Nigerian Journalists in Leadership Recruitment<br>The principal role of journalists is to keep journal or record of information in the society. They educate, inform, entertain and set values and agenda for public discourse and action. In this way, they wield social and political influence and positively impact public decisions and policy. This is why you are recognised as the Fourth estate of the realm.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Journalists played key role in the anti-colonial struggle that led to freedom from colonial rule in October 1960. Notable among this class were Nnamdi Azikiwe and his West African Pilot newspaper, Obafemi Awolowo and Anthony Enahoro.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Other prominent journalists in the Nigerian national life were Isa Kaita, Dan Agbase, Usifoh Ovie-Wiskey, Chris Okolie, Chris Anyawu, Bayo Ohu, Bayo Onauga, to mention but a few</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The Nigerian press bore the brunt of brutal repression during the most trying years of Buhari, Babangida and Abacha dictatorships. According to a report published by the Constitutional Rights Project (CRP) titled “The Crisis of Press Freedom in Nigeria”, the period also “marked the darkest period in Nigerian journalism”.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>During this period, some Nigerian journalists paid the supreme price in pursuit of truth and social justice. Notable among these are Dele Giwa of Newswatch Magazine and Bagauda Kaltho, ace reporter of TheNews Magazine who disappeared after being arrested by security operatives during the Abacha regime.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>(26) My Humble Request From Delta State NUJ<br>I wish to take this opportunity to make two requests from NUJ, Delta State Council, which I believe is one of the most vibrant, sophisticated and patriotic branches of the union in Nigeria today.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>I would like the Delta State Council of the NUJ to take more robust steps to build up its Library to international standards. You can take the lead in storing important or historic documents in Nigeria. Your service may also be made available online as time goes on.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Secondly, I wish to ask the Nigerian Union of Journalists to conduct thorough investigation or research and publish comprehensive and unbiased report of Okuama Tragedy in Ughelli South Local Government Area which took place in March 2024.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>(27) Salute to Courage and Patriotism<br>In concluding this address, I wish to take this opportunity to pay public tribute to shining lights of the human rights community in Nigeria – professionals, academics, lawyers, journalists, student leaders, all those who have been in the forefront of the crusade of social justice in Nigeria. These are the true heroes of our country, not thieves that parade as leaders.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>It is important to understand that the wicked souls are moral and spiritual cowards. They fear the light of truth and that is why they draw their swords against innocent and patriotic citizens whose only offence is that they bear the light. Patriots must remain courageous and steadfast in the battle for a Better Nigeria by continuing to shine the light of truth brilliantly on the society.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>I salute the memory of indefatigable Gani Fawehinmi, SAN, Dr. Beko Ransome Kuti and all those who fought the good fight for a Better Nigeria.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>I salute my erstwhile leaders in the Civil Liberties Organisation (CLO), Dr. Olisa Agbakoba, SAN, Arc. Nimo Bassey, Oronto Douglas and others.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>I doff my hat for Senator Shehu Sani, Femi Falana, SAN and Mike Ozekhome, SAN. I thank the young human rights lawyer, Effiong Inibehe who stood by the children arrested and detained in inhuman conditions in August this year and later charged with treason by IGP Egbetokun.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>I solemnly pray that the Grand Architect of the Universe, Grant the Nigerian Union of Journalist (NUJ), Delta State Council and all Nigerian journalists, the inspiration to continue to propagate ideas that will conduce to proper leadership recruitment for good governance in Nigeria.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Distinguished Senators, Honourable Representatives, Your Royal Majesty, Distinguished Ladies and Gentlemen, Thank you very much for listening to me.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>REFERENCES<br>Awolowo, Obafemi (1960). AWO: The Autobiography of Chief Obafemi Awolowo: Cambridge University Press.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Biose, Chris O. O. (2023). STOP ZONING: RESTRUCTURE NIGERIA, BOLD Publishers Limited, Lagos.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->', 'KEYNOTE ADDRESS ON IMPERATIVE OF PROPER POLITICAL LEADERSHIP RECRUITMENT FOR GOOD GOVERNANCE', '', 'publish', 'open', 'closed', '', 'keynote-address-on-imperative-of-proper-political-leadership-recruitment-for-good-governance', '', '', '2025-01-04 17:29:17', '2025-01-04 17:29:17', '', 0, 'https://boldtimes.net.ng/?p=1162', 0, 'post', '', 0);
INSERT INTO `wp5g_posts` (`ID`, `post_author`, `post_date`, `post_date_gmt`, `post_content`, `post_title`, `post_excerpt`, `post_status`, `comment_status`, `ping_status`, `post_password`, `post_name`, `to_ping`, `pinged`, `post_modified`, `post_modified_gmt`, `post_content_filtered`, `post_parent`, `guid`, `menu_order`, `post_type`, `post_mime_type`, `comment_count`) VALUES
(1186, 1, '2026-02-26 10:00:03', '0000-00-00 00:00:00', '<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>THE ASSASSINATION ATTEMPT ON ADC LEADERS IN BENIN CITY<br>By Chris O. O. Biose, Chrisoobiose@gmail.com</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>It is widely reported in the print and electronic media that armed political thugs attacked some leaders of the African Democratic Congress (ADC) in Benin City on Tuesday, February 24, 2026 during a political meeting. The leaders, included former Governor of Edo State, Chief John Odigie-Oyegun, who is also a former National Chairman of the All Progressives Congress (APC) and former Governor of Anambra State, Mr. Peter Obi, who was also Presidential candidate of the Labour Party (LP) in the February 2023 presidential election.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The incident occurred during the formal reception of Mr. Olumide Akpata, former National Chairman of the Nigerian Bar Association (NBA) and gubernatorial candidate of the LP in the September 2024 governorship election in Edo State that produced Monday Okpebolo as Governor of the State. The event followed Mr. Olumide’s Defection to the African Democratic Congress (ADC) from the Labour Party.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>It was reported that the attackers arrived at the Secretariat of the party in about 10 vehicles without plate numbers and immediately opened fire indiscrimately and vandalized party property. Some members of the party reportedly sustained injuries.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The assailants finally drove off with confidence, to the residence of Chief Odigie-Oyegun along Reservation Road, Benin City, where they destroyed the gate of the well-fortified residence to gain entry, shot sporadically in several directions, and destroying several vehicles in the compound of the former governor.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The question arises as to how such brazen violence could take place in broad day light in the centre of Benin City where there is a government and police. The answer lies with the type of governments in place in Nigeria and the close relationship of government with the criminal underworld in the country.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>It is generally recognized that in Nigeria, deadly political violence is the stock in trade of powerful politicians. During the first decade of return to civil rule since 1999, there were over 100 unresolved assassinations of politically exposed persons. Prominent among these were the famous Lagos politician Engr Funsho Williams, the then Minister of Justice and attorney-General of the Federation, Chief Bola Ige, Harry Marshall a top notcher of the ANPP and A.K. Dikibo, PDP National vice-Chairman, South-South.<br>During the 2003 gubernatorial election in Anambra State, the existence of a para-military organisation of storm troopers came to light. According to Dr. Edwin Madunagu, the operational tactics of storm troopers in Anambra State were comparable to that formed by the late German Dictator, Adolf Hitler in 1923. As Dr. Madunagu made clear, during the officially sanctioned rampage that took place in Anambra State between 2003 and 2004, the relationship between these private armies and regular security forces of the state may not be cordial but \"they all served the same strategic objectives - the maintenance of the fascist state by the crushing of all enemies, actual and potential.\"<br>“Although not listed in the Constitution, the fascist storm troopers were \"protected and assisted by strategic state institutions, including the police, the army and the judiciary.\"</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Madunagu concluded that Nigeria was witnessing the evolution of \"a new form of state\" characterized by \"de facto one party system, a merger of the ruling party and the state, the appearance of state sponsored armed institutions that are not in the constitution or in any statute.\" They operate \"above the law\" which he called \"fascist regimentation.\" (The Guardian, Monday, December 13, 2004).</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The massive deployment of lethal weapons to destroy political opponents and other sources of resistance to election marauders could not, by any stretch of the imagination, be procured by much maligned cultist indigent students from higher institutions of learning or village folk deployed for these robberies. Peddling deadly weapons is the exclusive preserve of the high and mighty in Nigerian society, particularly the political, business and military elite. Indeed the cost of one AK 47 riffle is far beyond the economic reach of the average citizen. These evil deeds are planned and funded by those same people that lead the country.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>State governors have played key roles in political violence in Nigeria since return to civil rule in May 1999. In August 2006, the Upper Chamber of the National Assembly invited the then Director General of the State Security Service (SSS), Col. Kayode Are (Rtd.), and the then Inspector General of Police, Mr. Sunday Ehindero, to appear before the Senate to shed light on the worrisome spate of political violence in the country. Col. Are (Rtd.) told senate on August 24, 2006 that they were “killer squads” in nearly every state of the country. (Emmanuel Aziken &amp; Ayodele Adegbuyi “Nigeria: SSS DG Fingers Govs in Political Killings”. Vanguard online, 25 August 2006.)</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>When he appeared before the senate on September 6, 2006, the then IGP, Mr. Sunday Ehindero, corroborated the submission of the then SSS Director. He added that state governments often frustrated the prosecution of arrested perpetrators of political violence. Mr. Ehindero said that many politicians funded thugs and illegal gangs, leading to proliferation of arms. He however submitted that the SSS had failed to share intelligence information on “killer squads” with the Police.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>To take another specific case, His Excellency, Seriake Dickson, who was then the Governor of Bayelsa State gave a factual and detailed account of the situation in his state which confirmed electoral wars in some Niger Delta states. In a candid interview which he granted Emmanuel Aziken, Political Editor, Vanguard newspaper, In May 2012, the Governor declared:<br>“We had a system where a terror squad was put together and funded by government over the years. You may recall that the day preceding my declaration, five persons were gunned down just to scare me and my supporters from coming. “But we dared them. In fact, what we did was a suicide mission.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>“Instead of giving the boys and girls education, instead of building infrastructure and investment, they were giving young people drugs, AK-47, they were giving them dynamite to explode and kill people so that there will be an impression; any attempt to take anybody out will be resisted.”</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The security challenges arising from desire of Governors to protect ill-gotten positions, as described by Governor Dickson applied mutatis mutandis to states in South-East, South-South and South-West. (Emmanuel Aziken, Challenging Sylva was a suicide mission – Dickson, Vanguard, May 23, 2012, p 44).</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>This pattern of violence has continued under the Tinubu Presidency. In Kaduna State, sponsored political thugs constantly attacked and disrupted meetings of Opposition SDP and ADC. The Nigeria Police joined the ruling party in suppressing opposition by placing a ban on political meeting in the state. Opposition parties successfully challenged the unlawful action of the Nigeria Police court and a Federal High Court sitting in Kaduna. On October 15, 2025, the court awarded N15 million in total damages against the police, covering the “arbitrary suspension” of the ADC meeting, the “unlawful ex-parte injunction” obtained without due procedure, and the “failure to investigate the August 30 violence.” (Ibrahim Hassan-Wuyo: “Court slams Kaduna Police over ban on political meetings, orders N15m damages to ADC, SDP”. Vanguard Online, October 15, 2025)</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>What happened in Benin City on February 24 was a continuation of what took place in Lagos on September 7, 2025 during the planned defection of Gbadebo Rhodes-Vivour, gubernatorial candidate of LP in the 2023 governorship election in Lagos State to ADC. Sponsored political thugs disrupted the event, injuring party members and destroying properties while Police looked on. Indeed, political thugs that constitute a special wing of the ruling class in Lagos State take it for granted that they could attack opponents of the APC and get police protection. This was exactly what happened during the 2023 presidential and gubernatorial elections in Lagos State.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The attack on ADC members in Benin City took place on a day that the IGP Kayode Egbetokun, who had a dismal human rights record, was handing over to newly appointed IGP, Mr. Tunji Disu. An effective Police Force is the first line of defence on security in a democratic state<br>Section 14 (2) (b) OF THE Constitution of Nigeria states that “the security and welfare of the people shall be the primary purpose of government.”</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Nigerians would wish to see a definite directive from the new IGP to State Police Commands, particularly, Lagos, Kaduna and Edo States, to protect political opponents as well as all citizens in compliance with Section 4 of the Police Act Cap P19 Laws of the Federation, 2004. The perceived collaboration of Police with high profile criminals is one of the weakest points in the security apparatus of the country.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->', '', '', 'draft', 'open', 'closed', '', '', '', '', '2026-02-26 10:00:03', '2026-02-26 10:00:03', '', 0, 'https://boldtimes.net.ng/?p=1186', 0, 'post', '', 0),
(1166, 1, '2025-03-20 17:49:44', '2025-03-20 17:49:44', '<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>PRESIDENT TINUBU’S COUP IN RIVERS STATE<br>By Chris O. O. Biose,<br>chrisoobiose@gmail.com</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>“The very curse of an evil deed is that it must always continue to engender evil.” – Johann Christoph Friedrich von Schiller (1759–1805).</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>A large part of the Nigerian crisis arises from the fact that a few individuals, military or civilian, violently and or fraudulently seize the instruments of government, subvert the state, assume power of the state and enforce their private will on the population, for their private or sectarian profit.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>On Tuesday, March 18, 2025, President Bola Ahmed Tinubu declared a State of Emergency in Rivers State, citing Section 305 of the Constitution of the Federal Republic of Nigeria (Promulgation) 1999 Decree No. 24, as amended. He suspended the elected Governor of Rivers State, Siminalayi Fubara, his Deputy, Professor Ngozi Odu, and members of the Rivers State House of Assembly from their positions for six months.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Fundamentally, the President’s action in abrogating the democratically elected government of Rivers State runs against the letter and spirit of democratic governance guaranteed by Section 1(2) which states that “the Federal Republic of Nigeria shall not be governed nor shall any person or group of persons take control of the government of Nigeria or any part thereof except in accordance with the provisions of this constitution.”</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Article 21(3) of the Universal Declaration of Human Rights (UDHR) of which Nigeria is a state party states:<br>“The will of the people shall be the basis of the authority of government; this will shall be expressed in periodic and genuine elections which shall be by universal and equal suffrage and shall be held by secret vote or by equivalent free voting procedures. (UN General Assembly Resolution 217 A (III), December 10, 1948)</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The idea of democratic governance as a human right goes beyond rights conferred and protected by legal enactments. It is inherent in individual citizens, inalienable and no person or authority in a state can legitimately remove it.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>From this point of view, Tinubu’s State of Emergency in Rivers State is an affront against democratic governance and human civilisation. It is unconscionable, totally unjustified, arbitrary, unconstitutional and in bad faith. It is calculated to achieve political and economic objectives of some Federal Government functionaries rather than peace and prosperity of the people of Rivers State.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>It has absolutely nothing to do with constitutionalism but everything to do with economic pillage of Rivers State and the Niger Delta in general. Since British colonialists incorporated the Niger Delta into the Colony and Protectorate of Nigeria on January 1, 1914, the relationship between the Nigerian State and the people of Niger Delta has been parasitic.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>This state of affairs was aggravated by military seizure of crude oil and natural gas resources of the Region vide the Petroleum Decree No. 51, 1969. Contrary to the 50:30:20 Derivation Formula enshrined in Section 140(6) of the Constitution of the Federal Republic of Nigeria, 1963, the Decree provided in Schedule 1 that:<br>“the entire ownership and control of all petroleum in, under or upon any land out to the 200 miles limit of the exclusive economic zone is vested in the Federal Republic of Nigeria.”</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>To sustain illegitimate advantages gained by means of obnoxious military decrees, successive military juntas and succeeding civilian regimes resorted to policy of repression, wars, state terrorism occasioning serial massacres to silence the people of the Region. The Federal Government of Nigeria turned the Niger Delta, the once serene wonder of nature into a killing field and frightening place in which to live or grow up.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Tinubu’s declaration of State of Emergency in Rivers State is a continuation of violent military take-over of crude oil and natural gas resources of the Niger Delta by unconstitutional and illegitimate military juntas. This is the historical context in which Tinubu’s declaration of State of Emergency in Rivers State can be understood. There is no other rational explanation.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Viewed from this perspective, the emergency declaration amounts to a civilian version of a coup. The Collins New English Dictionary defines a coup as “a stroke or blow, then, a successful stroke or move, the successful result of a sudden and unforeseen strategic move, a forceful master stroke.”</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The Dictionary also defines a coup d’etat as “a stroke of state; a sudden and revolutionary change of government achieved by force, any sudden and decisive political move involving an abuse of authority” while the New Lexicon Webster’s Encyclopedic Dictionary of the English Language defines it as “an attempt to overthrow a government by sudden violent, illegal means.”</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The defining characteristics of coup d’etat are illegal removal of Legitimate Political Authority. The illegal action must relate to government. Hence, a sudden and forceful change of leadership of board of a company is not a coup d’etat.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The action of President Tinubu is tantamount to a coup because Decree 24, 1999, which operates as the Constitution of Nigeria does not confer power on the President to remove or suspend a State Governor. Section 188(2) makes explicit provisions on how a State Governor may be removed from office and it is as follows;<br>(a) Impeachment;<br>(b) Resignation;<br>(c) Permanent incapacity; or<br>(d) Death.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>No part of the Nigerian Constitution grants power to the President to disband a constitutionally elected state government. Tinubu’s action is a serious breach of his oath to uphold the constitution of the country and therefore constitutes an impeachable offence.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Aside procedural flaws in his declaration as provided in Section 305(2), the President did not give the Senate and House of Representatives the opportunity to “consider the situation and decide whether or not to pass a resolution approving the proclamation” as provided by the constitution before giving effect to his desire. He instantly appointed a Military Administrator, former Chief of Naval Staff, Vice Admiral Ibokette Ibas (retd.), who took over the government of Rivers State on the same day. Ibas was hastily sworn in the next day. This dictatorial stance is a brutal assault on the letter and spirit of Nigeria’s democracy.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Section 305(3) states conditions under which a President may declare a State of Emergency, namely”<br>(a) The Federation is at war;<br>(b) The Federation is in imminent danger of invasion or involvement in a state of war;<br>(c) There is actual breakdown of public order and public safety in the Federation or any part thereof to such extent as to require extra-ordinary measures to restore peace and security;<br>(d) There is a clear and present danger of an actual breakdown of public order and public safety in the Federation or any part thereof requiring extra-ordinary measures to avert such danger;<br>(e) There is an occurrence or imminent danger, or the occurrence of any disaster or natural calamity, affecting the community or a section of the community in the Federation;<br>(f) There is any other public danger which clearly constitutes a threat to the existence of the Federation; or<br>(g) The President receives a request to do so in accordance with the provision of subsection (4) of this section.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>None of the above constitutionally stipulated conditions was met before President Tinubu declared his emergency. The Governor of Rivers State did not request for declaration of State of Emergency in the state. The state had been relatively peaceful. The citizens abide by the law and there is no breakdown of law and order by any stretch of the imagination.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The President’s claim in his broadcast that the state was “at a standstill” is contradicted by the unprecedented level of development of the educational, health and road infrastructure projects in all parts of the state as well as highly improved welfare of public servants under the tenure of Governor Siminalayi Fubara.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The President apparently took sides with the self-proclaimed political godfather of Rivers State, Mr. Nyesom Wike, whom he appointed as Minister of the Federal Capital Territory (FCT). At a New Year luncheon organised in his country home, Rumueprikom, Obio/Akpor Local Government Area, Rivers State, Mr. Nyesom Wike, on Sunday, January 7, 2024, announced that he bought nomination forms for the Governor, Senatorial Candidates, Members of the House of Representative and House of Assembly in his State for the 2023 election. (Seun Opejobi, Rivers: No regrets making Fubara governor: I bought his nomination, DAILY POST Online, January 8, 2024). For this reason, Wike claimed that the political party structure in Rivers State belong to him.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>There is no gainsaying the fact that President Tinubu desires to gain political mileage in Rivers State and Wike’s stooges in the National Assembly. It was apparently prudential for the President to accuse Governor Fubara of demolishing the Rivers State House of Assembly and failing to rebuild it while remaining silent on the torching of four local government council buildings in the state by thugs allegedly loyal to Nyesom Wike.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The President was also silent on the fact that when Governor Siminalayi Fubara went to House of Assembly to present the Appropriation Act, 2025, as ordered by the Supreme Court, the House of Assembly members that defected to his party, locked the gates and prevented him from gaining access to perform that function.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The President’s reference to security report of threats by militants is disingenuous because the Nigerian Army, Air Force, Navy, Nigeria Police Force and 14 or more other security outfits in the country are completely under the control of the President. If he is unable to maintain security, he cannot blame anyone but himself.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>It is instructive that the President attempted to ground his missteps on decision of the Supreme Court pertaining to Rivers State. There is no doubt that both civilian and military dictators often attempt to premise their tyrannical conduct on judicial authority. For example, Nigeria’s 6th Military Dictator, General Ibrahim Babangida (retd.), attempted to use the Judiciary to truncate the June 12, 1993 presidential election.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The Supreme Court of Nigeria is generally perceived as complicit in elite conspiracy against the emergence of democratic culture in Nigeria. One of the high points of judicial mischief in the public domain was the declaration of Mr. Hope Uzodinma as Governor of Imo State when he came a distant fourth in the gubernatorial election in the State held on March 9, 2019. This brazen act of judicial recklessness marked the beginning of wide-scale breakdown of law and order and intensified insecurity in Imo State. One hopes and fervently prays that the Nigerian Supreme Court by its tainted judgements pertaining to Rivers State would not unleash a new chapter of terror in the long oppressed and disenchanted South-South Region of Nigeria.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>On February 28, 2025, this same Supreme Court set judicial anarchy in place in Nigeria when it decided that 27 members of the Rivers State House of Assembly who publicly defected to the Ruling APC from the Opposition PDP on whose platform they were elected, were still valid members of the Assembly. The defection of the 27 lawmakers in December 2023 was shown on national television. The presiding Speaker of the remaining members duly declared their seats vacant but the partisan electoral body in Nigeria refused to conduct elections to replace them. The defectors also swore to Affidavits confirming their defection.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The verdict of the Supreme Court did not only make nonsense of the express provision of Section 109(1)(g) of Decree 24, 1999, it brought the judiciary to a new low in the estimation of the Nigerian public. Section 109(1)(g) states clearly that a member of a House of Assembly shall vacate his seat in the House if “being a person whose election to the House of Assembly was sponsored by a political party, he becomes a member of another political party before the expiration of the period for which that House was elected”.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The treachery of the Supreme Court and the brazen tyranny of President Tinubu portends great danger to Nigeria’s fragile democracy. There are too many bad examples in the upper echelons of the Nigerian state apparatus, Legislature, Executive and Judiciary. When political leaders are steeped in violent assault on rule of law, where are citizens expected to get the good example to guide their behaviour. It is a remarkable credit to Nigerians that only a few citizens imitate the lawlessness of their leaders while the vast majority still remain law abiding. But the Supreme Court and the Tinubu Presidency appear to be pushing Nigerians to the wall.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:image -->\n<figure class=\"wp-block-image\"><img alt=\"\"/></figure>\n<!-- /wp:image -->\n\n<!-- wp:image -->\n<figure class=\"wp-block-image\"><img alt=\"\"/></figure>\n<!-- /wp:image -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\"></h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:image {\"id\":1170,\"sizeSlug\":\"full\",\"linkDestination\":\"none\"} -->\n<figure class=\"wp-block-image size-full\"><img src=\"https://boldtimes.net.ng/wp-content/uploads/2025/03/IMG-20250320-WA0047.jpg\" alt=\"\" class=\"wp-image-1170\"/></figure>\n<!-- /wp:image -->', 'PRESIDENT TINUBU’S COUP IN RIVERS STATE', '', 'publish', 'open', 'closed', '', 'president-tinubus-coup-in-rivers-state', '', '', '2025-03-20 17:56:48', '2025-03-20 17:56:48', '', 0, 'https://boldtimes.net.ng/?p=1166', 0, 'post', '', 1),
(1170, 1, '2025-03-20 17:51:57', '2025-03-20 17:51:57', '', 'IMG-20250320-WA0047', '', 'inherit', 'open', 'closed', '', 'img-20250320-wa0047', '', '', '2025-03-20 17:51:57', '2025-03-20 17:51:57', '', 1166, 'https://boldtimes.net.ng/wp-content/uploads/2025/03/IMG-20250320-WA0047.jpg', 0, 'attachment', 'image/jpeg', 0),
(1176, 1, '2025-05-30 05:23:36', '2025-05-30 05:23:36', '<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><em>DELTA STATE MUST RESTORE ITS HONOUR AND DIGNITY</em></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><em>PRESS BRIEFING BY OBIDIENT MOVEMENT, DELTA STATE, HOLDING AT NUJ PRESS CENTRE, MARYAM BABANGIDA WAY, ASABA, DELTA STATE, ON APRIL 25, 2025, AT 1.00PM</em></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Gentlemen of the Press,</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>We, the DELTA OBIDENTS, thank you most sincerely for honouring our invitation to this press briefing.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>As I informed you at the last briefing held in this Press Centre on June 29, 2024, Nigerian youths constitute the moving force of the Obidient Movement, but when there are serious challenges to the community or the state, the Elders are duty bound to speak out or take action.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>You are all aware, from reports in the print and electronic media that the former Governor of Delta State and Vice Presidential Candidate of the Peoples Democratic Party (PDP) in the 2023 presidential election, Dr. Ifeanyi Okowa, the incumbent Governor of Delta State, His Excellency, Sheriff Oborevwori, PDP members of the Delta State House of Assembly as well as all Chairmen and Councillors in 25 local government areas of the state defected from the PDP to the ruling All Progressives Congress (APC).</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>This is an issue that calls for serious concern by all well-meaning Deltans. The act of diverting the sacred mandate of Delta State electorate to another political party is a grievous betrayal of the electoral will of Deltans. It is an assault on party and the principle of democratic representation. Their action, not their words, tell Deltans what they really are. They stand for nothing, no principles, no ideology, no values and no ideals.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The claim of the defectors that the action of joining the APC is to foster development of the state is countermanded by the dismal record of that party at the national level in the past 10 years of its hold on the country. APC rule is marked by descent into economic chaos and untrammelled insecurity.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>There are numerous issues of effectiveness and transparency of governance which are being covered up. For example, here is an oil-rich Delta State where such a basic social amenity as potable water is inexistent. The streams are polluted with waste.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>In February 2023, the late Chief Edwin Clark, Leader of Pan Niger Delta Forum, PANDEF and Southern and Middle-Belt Leaders Forum (SMBLF) asked former Governor Okowa to give account for what he received as 13% derivation to Deltans before May 2023. This simple demand for transparency and accountability has been ignored to date.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Obidients in Delta State are not bothered one bit about fusion of PDP into APC in the state because they are disconnected from the electorate and cannot win a free and fair election in the state.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Our worry is not whether APC will win Delta State because they cannot win a free and fair election anywhere in Nigeria today. It is pertinent to remind Nigerians that at the presidential election held on February 25, 2023, Deltans gave our principal, Mr. Peter Obi, as presidential candidate of the Labour Party (LP), a decisive victory of 341,866 votes to 161,600 for the PDP presidential candidate, Alhaji Atiku Abubakar and 90,183 for the presidential candidate of the APC, Alhaji Bola Ahmed Tinubu. This shows that the combined votes of 251,783 (both PDP and APC) were far below that of the LP candidate.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>During the last local government election in Delta State, the PDP refused to conduct a genuine or free and fair democratic election. They were so afraid of the electorate that they resorted to writing results in secret places and announcing their forgeries as products of elections that did not take place. It was so shameless.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The treachery of the PDP in handing over the votes given to them by Deltans to the APC so that INEC will write results for them in 2027 is bad enough. By this wholesale defection, PDP leaders have gratuitously acquired vicarious liability for all the evils inflicted on Nigerians by the APC at the national level.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The real big worry of DELTA OBIDIENTS is the security implication of handing over Delta State to the APC. The most socially visible achievement of former President Muhammadu Buhari and his successor, President Bola Ahmed Tinubu is what former President Obasanjo referred to as Fulanisation of Nigeria in his speech during the second session of the Seventh Synod of the Anglican Communion held at Oleh, Delta State on May 18, 2019.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Since the APC took over governance at the Federal level in May 2015, a new strain of insecurity spear-headed by armed Fulani herders overtook the entire country. In the light of horrific massacres carried out across Nigeria by armed Fulani herders, the Global Terrorism Index (GTI) named it as the “fourth deadliest terrorist group in the world”, after Boko Haram, ISIS and al-Shaba. (Rose Troup Buchanan, Global Terrorism Index: Nigerian Fulani milititants named fourth deadliest terrorist group in the world, November 2015). In another report published in November 2020, the Global Terrorism Index (GTI) ranked Nigeria as the third most terrorised country in the world.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Given the unwillingness or inability of the Federal Government to protect citizens of Nigeria, well-meaning patriots advised citizens to arm themselves for self-defence. At a public event in Jalingo, Taraba State, on March 24, 2018, former Chief of Army Staff, and Minister of Defence under former President Obasanjo, Lt.-General Theophillus Y. Danjuma (retd) stated that the Nigerian Army was aiding armed Fulani herdsmen and other hoodlums to kill Nigerians and grab their lands. Urging citizens to defend themselves, he said among other things:</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>“This ethnic cleansing must stop in Taraba and it must stop in Nigeria. These killers have been protected by the military, they cover them and you must be watchful to guard yourselves because you have no other place to go.”</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Referring to the Nigerian Army, Danjuma stated pointedly that “they collude”. He warned his listeners not to depend on Nigerian Army to protect them, that individuals should hold on to their right to self-defence.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>TY Danjuma, is a leading member of the Nigerian military establishment. He is one of the spearhead of the second military coup on July 29,1966 that led to anti-Igbo pogrom in Northern Nigeria. He is one of the wealthiest Nigerians by virtue of oil blocks allocated to him by his military colleages who were junta leaders in the country.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Ten years on, the Nigerian Government has failed or neglected to reign in the terrorists. The genocidal massacres in Plateau, Benue, Taraba and Enugu states make national headlines. But here in Delta State, the militia men have been operating with impunity and little is written about their nefarious activities. Ubulu-Uku, Onicha-Ugbo, Ibusa, Ika, Ndokwa, Ughelli, Abraka, Uwheru in Isoko, even the riverine areas are not free from the murderous activities of these terrorists.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>More recently, the Director-General of the Department of State Services (DSS), Mr. Adeola Oluwatosin Ajayi, advise community to defend themselves because the Nigerian army and the Nigeria Police were over stretched and not capable of defending all communities in the country.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>At a time when Fulani terrorists are killing citizens of Nigeria with impunity in Plateau and Benue States, President Tinubu could afford the luxury of sending thousands of troops to Rivers State to secure an illegal overthrow of the constitutionally elected government of the state. That action speaks volumes about where he stands between security of lives and property of Nigerians and morbid quest for totalitarian political power.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Commenting on attacks on his people at Uwheru Kingdom, a distinguished son of Delta State and former Commissioner for Education in the state, Professor Muoboghare noted that “there must be something fundamental that we do not understand about the Nigerian government.” He spoke for all victimised crop farmers in Nigeria when he affirmed with confidence that “but for the protection they receive from the soldiers, most communities in Delta State can deal with the herdsmen.”</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Nigeria is fast slipping into a predatory autocracy where power is controlled by violence and sustained by a narrow elite rather than broad public support or economic performance. According to Effert et al (2003), such regimes act as “roving bandits” due to exploitation of public and private resources for the gain of the elite.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>In spite of seeming wholesale of Delta to APC, we are sure that there are still men and women of conscience in several theatres of Delta affairs, even within the PDP and APC fold.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>We hereby call on community leaders, youth groups, civil society organisations, professional bodies, Deltans and particularly the media to join hands in rejecting the sale of Delta State to the APC on the altar of political expediency for the benefits of few political merchants. It goes beyond well-known diversion of state resources to private pockets. It is a threat to life and survival of Deltans.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>We call on all men of conscience in Delta State and the country at large to join Obidients in requesting the Governor of Delta State, Sheriff Oborevwori to take immediate action to empower community vigilantes to protect communities from armed invaders who are destroying their farms and killing citizens.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The Obidient Movement constitute a category of Nigerians from all walks of life, both at home and in the Diaspora who strongly abhor injustice, man’s inhumanity to man, religious bigotry, grand corruption and nepotism and are resolute in fighting these debilitating vices that beset the Nigerian polity.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>We hereby call on men of integrity in PDP and APC who are unable to put up in good conscience with the shenanigans of their parties to join the Obidients to rescue Nigeria. The request of public servants to join Obidients will be treated with absolute confidence.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>As for popular acceptance, the PDP and APC do not have it. We defeated them roundly in February 2023. With the support of the common people and workers of delta state and Nigeria, we shall surely defeat them again in any future general election.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Thank you very much for listening to me.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><em>A New Nigeria is POssible.</em></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><em>Chris O. O. Biose, (a.k.a. Obidient Daddy)</em></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->', 'DELTA STATE MUST RESTORE ITS HONOUR AND DIGNITY', '', 'publish', 'open', 'closed', '', 'delta-state-must-restore-its-honour-and-dignity', '', '', '2025-05-30 05:23:36', '2025-05-30 05:23:36', '', 0, 'https://boldtimes.net.ng/?p=1176', 0, 'post', '', 0);
INSERT INTO `wp5g_posts` (`ID`, `post_author`, `post_date`, `post_date_gmt`, `post_content`, `post_title`, `post_excerpt`, `post_status`, `comment_status`, `ping_status`, `post_password`, `post_name`, `to_ping`, `pinged`, `post_modified`, `post_modified_gmt`, `post_content_filtered`, `post_parent`, `guid`, `menu_order`, `post_type`, `post_mime_type`, `comment_count`) VALUES
(1178, 1, '2025-05-30 05:30:29', '2025-05-30 05:30:29', '<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>UROMI KILLINGS RAISE THE NATIONAL QUESTION POIGNANTLY<br>By Chris O. O. Biose,<br>chrisoobiose@gmail.com</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The sad and painful murder of 16 Nigerian citizens from Kano State at Uromi in Edo State on March 27, 2025, who were ostensibly mistaken for kidnappers, was unlawful, barbaric, brutish, inhuman and unreservedly condemnable. The ethnic suspicion and distrust of security operatives which engendered resort to jungle justice raises a fundamental issue of the National Question in Nigeria which successive Federal administrations bluntly refused to address.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Prior to the Uromi tragedy, anyone that suggests that Nigeria is not yet a nation-state and that the leaders should pay attention to nation building is labeled as unpatriotic. The reality has dawned on everyone that this is not how compatriots who belong to the same commonwealth should behave towards each other.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>In an article titled “Whose national conference?” published in THE GUARDIAN, Thursday, June 21, 1990, p 9, Dr. Edwin Madunagu noted that the Nigerian society is increasingly structured against some social groups and classes. He defines the National Question as “the range of specific problems that arise when two or more ethnic groups are merged under one polity and governed by one state.” In the Nigerian context, the questions that arise under this rubric include “ethnic and religious questions” as well as “human rights”.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>A genuine National Question is that whose solution advances the interests of the working and toiling population –the overwhelming majority of the people.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Professor Omo Omoruyi had also called attention to two fundamental issues that were left unresolved by the Constitution of the Federation, 1960 and subsequent constitutions in the country. These are:<br>(a) How the various ethnic nationalities could live together in peace and harmony; and<br>(b) How to evolve a government to which all the various ethnic nationalities are committed as guaranteeing them justice.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The failure of successive Federal Administrations to address these two issues manifested in several other issues such as persecution of minorities, ethnicity, religious strife and most disturbingly, violent seizure of crude oil and natural gas resources of the Niger Delta by unconstitutional military juntas and inherited by hegemonic ruling class.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Horrendous Attacks on Farming Communities<br>The National Question in Nigeria was aggravated with the Fulanisation Programme of former President Muhammadu Buhari in 2015 with unprecedented tempo of horrendous attacks on farming communities in the country.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The first major warning shot was the massacre at Agatu community in Benue State in February 2016. Fulani militia men invaded the community and killed hundreds of villagers while several thousand others were rendered homeless. (Peter Duru and Levinus Nwabughiogu, 7,000 persons displaced in Fulani herdsmen, Agatu farmers’ clash in Benue, Vanguard, February 29, 2016; p 45).</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Early in the morning on April 25, 2016, Fulani militiamen numbering over 50 descended on the villages of Ukpabi/Nimbo, Ngwoko, Ugwuijoro, Ekwuru, Ebor and others in Uzo-Uwani LGA of Enugu State from several directions. They killed about 60 natives and wounded several others. The invaders had earlier sent a warning to the villagers who alerted security agencies and begged for protection but none was offered. (Chinenye Ozor, AFTERMATH OF INVASION, KILLINGS BY FULANI HERDSMEN: Tears, Fear, uncertainty rule Abbi community, Vanguard, February 26, 2016, p 20).</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Insensitivity of APC Leaders to Killings<br>After massacres by the Fulani militiamen in Benue and Enugu states in February and March 2016, Northern Governors held a meeting in Kaduna and condemned those who said that perpetrators of the massacres in Benue and Enugu States were criminals. Instead of condemning the murderers in unmistakable terms, the Northern Governors, through its Chairman, Dr. Kashim Shettima who was then Governor of Borno State, said that it was an insult to say that Fulani herdsmen were criminals.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Dr. Shettima said: “We want to unequivocally condemn the politicization, or permit me to say, the ethnicisation of the whole crisis. It goes beyond Fulani. If anything happens, they say Fulani herdsmen; to me, it is an insult.” The former Governor who became Vice President of Nigeria in 2023, argued that criminals existed among other ethnic groups and that the prefix Fulani should not precede the description of the murderers. (It’s an insult to label criminals as Fulanis – Northern Govs, Vanguard, April 30, 2016).In other words, the victims of the massacres were not even allowed to complain.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Since that time, killings by armed Fulani herdsmen continued unabated throughout the country. Nobel Laureate, Professor Wole Soyinka described the killings in Benue and Taraba states as “ethnic cleansing”. He made the statement during a courtesy call on Former Governor Samuel Ortom after attending activities marking the 35th anniversary of Senator Suemo Chia’s novel Adan Wade Kohol Ga, written in Tiv language.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>While calling on former President Buhari to check the “massacres going on in the region, especially Benue State”, Nobel Laureate, Wole Soyinka said:<br>“There’s no other word for it.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>“Let’s not play around with the euphemisms, it’s ethnic cleansing. There’s no other definition for what has been going on here. And it’s very sad to me personally to see that a nation like Nigeria with so much human talent, has failed to learn the lesson of the history of places like Rwanda.” (Dapo Akinrefon, Gbenga Oke and Peter Duru, BENUE KILLINGS: ETHNIC CLEANSING OR JIHAD? Soyinka, Northern leaders tango, Vanguard, May 25, 2018, p 5)</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The Federal Government paid deaf ears to the appeals by the Nobel Laureate and several other well meaning Nigerians and the massacres continued unabated.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>In June 2022, the Association of Kwande Academia (AKA) stated that no fewer than 2,728 persons including women and children had been murdered in Turan community of Kwande LGA of Benue State due to ceaseless herdsmen attacks on the community.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Speaking to Seun Okinbaloye of Channels TV on Friday, April 12, 2024, the Speaker of the Plateau State House of Assembly, Rt. Hon. Gabriel Dewan described continued killings in the Plateau as “ethnic cleansing” and “conspiracy against the people” of Plateau State. He revealed that over one hundred communities had been systematically wiped out and replaced with cattle herders.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>As at June 2024, indigenous youth groups in Plateau State revealed that 151 villages are occupied by armed foreign herdsmen.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Numerous communities in Edo and Delta States have suffered from persistent attacks by armed Fulani herders, occasioning kidnapping for ransom, destruction of farmlands, raping of women, maiming and killing of citizens, without check by the authorities.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Surprising Response of President Tinubu and Northern Governors<br>Given refusal of the Federal Government to respond to persistent demand of Nigerian farmers to be saved from there killers, one is surprised that President Tinubu and Northern Governors reacted swiftly to the Uromi killings.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The President found his voice to condemn wanton killing of Nigerian citizens by fellow Nigerians. Uromi Vigilante have succeeded in attracting attention of the Federal Government to unjustified killing of Nigerian citizens which decades of ethnic cleansing in Plateau and Benue states failed to attract.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Throughout 8 years of carnage during Buhari regime, Tinubu, who was then National Leader of the ruling APC, failed to condemn or initiate actions to stop the killings.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>If it takes Uromi tragedy to rouse the Presidency and Northern Governors to human concern for fellow citizens, the victims would be counted as martyrs and heroes of the Nigerian struggle for nationhood. That is only if the leaders would be honest enough to admit their failures and turn a new leaf for justice in the country.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Imperative of Justice For All Nigerians<br>While commiserating with relatives of victims of the Uromi murders, the demand for justice for the victims is absolutely necessary. They must be arrested, prosecuted and punishment meted out to those involved according to law.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>It is however fair to say that if the government desires to address fundamental issues, justice would not start and end with punishing Uromi. It would entail applying the same standard to everyone without discrimination, fear or favour.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>All victims of violent attacks who now live in IDP camps in Plateau, Benue, Taraba and other parts of the country must have their ancestral land restored to them as a matter of national priority. The usurpers must not be allowed to continue keeping the benefit of their crimes.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Now that President Tinubu and his Northern elite suddenly remember that justice is necessary in Nigeria, they are honour bound to extend the same justice to hundreds of thousands of Christian families whose relatives have been murdered in cold blood over the past 10 years of Buhari-Tinubu regime.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>As the Catholic Bishop of Sokoto Diocese, Matthew Hassan Kukah, lamented during the Silver Jubilee Anniversary of the Order of the Knights of St. Mulumba, Nigeria, Eko Subordinate Council, in August 2024, killing of Christians is the “only offence not punished” in Nigeria. Killers of Christians go scot free and prepare for more killings.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The concept of justice can be applied to all social groups, whether it is the home, the school, organisation or community. It can also be applied to states and international relations. In this respect, nationalism has to be properly defined. No sub nationality, or country, has the right to cheat and exploit other nations or wage unjust wars against them.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>An enlightened leader is an embodiment of justice and fair play at all times. His very presence is a negation of injustice, oppression, inequality and antagonistic social divisions. He does not side with his class, political friends, ethnic group or state of origin, either overtly or covertly. Such a leader speaks the language of the underprivileged masses. All truly great men are infused by thoughts of and unstoppable desire for justice, which animate their deeds.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The survival of a heterogeneous society like Nigeria must depend in the long run on the ability and willingness of powerful groups to submit to popular opinion and justice. It is necessary for citizens to seek and maintain a humanitarian content in the body politic. If leaders fail to do so, the society stands to lose its peace and cohesion.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The issue today is not whether Nigeria or the world can get justice. It has become a condition for human survival. The challenge of today is that justice is beyond individual, moral or private duty. It is becoming a social duty in so far as society cannot survive without justice.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->', 'UROMI KILLINGS RAISE THE NATIONAL QUESTION POIGNANTLY', '', 'publish', 'open', 'closed', '', 'uromi-killings-raise-the-national-question-poignantly', '', '', '2025-05-30 05:30:29', '2025-05-30 05:30:29', '', 0, 'https://boldtimes.net.ng/?p=1178', 0, 'post', '', 0),
(1182, 1, '2025-06-23 20:00:25', '2025-06-23 20:00:25', '<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>STOP PRESIDENT TINUBU’S CONTROVERSIAL RUGA REVIVAL SCHEME<br>By Chris O. O. Biose,<br>chrisoobiose@gmail.com</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The most outstanding feature of 8 years tenure of former President Muhammadu Buhari was the emergence of armed Fulani herders that engaged in maiming, killing, and driving away crop farmers with intent to occupy their victims’ land.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>In November 2015, the Global Terrorism Index published by the Institute for Economics and Peace, named armed Fulani herdsmen as the “fourth deadliest terrorist group in the world”, after Boko Haram, ISIS and al-Shaba. (GLOBAL TERRORISM INDEX: 2015, INSTITUTE FOR ECONOMICS AND PEACE)</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>A report published by Jose Luis Baran, an Independent researcher based in Brussels, Belgium, revealed that between 2017 and May 2020, armed Fulani herdsmen killed 2,539 people in 654 attacks in Nigeria. (Bayo Akinloye, Report: Fulani Herdsmen Killed 2,539 Nigerians in 654 Attacks, THISDAY online).</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>As far back as May 2018, Nobel Laureate, Professor Wole Soyimka, described the mass killings in Taraba, Benue and Plateau states as “ethnic cleansing”. He said “There’s no other word for it. Let’s not play around with the euphemisms, it’s ethnic cleansing. There’s no other definition for what has been going on here.” He asked former President Buhari to stop the killings. (Dapo Akinrefon, Gbenga Oke and Peter Duru, BENUE KILLINGS: ETHNIC CLEANINSING OR JIHAD? Soyinka, Northern leaders tango, Vanguard, May 25, 2018, p 5)</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Rather than deal decisively with the perpetrators of the heinous crimes as required by law, the Buhari administration mounted consistent and persistent pressure to secure land in all states of Nigeria for Fulani herdsmen. These attempts included the National Grazing Reserve Commission Bill, 2016, Cow Colony Project, National Livestock Transformation Plan (NLTP) and RUGA Settlement Scheme, 2019.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>On June 30, 2019, the Presidency announced that the Federal Government had gazetted establishment of RUGA Settlements in 36 states of the country. Ruga is Hausa word for cow settlement. The statement from the Presidency read in part:<br>“Ruga Settlement” that seeks to settle migrant pastoral families simply means rural settlement in which animal farmers, not just cattle herders, will be settled in an organized place with provision of necessary and adequate basic amenities such as schools, hospitals, road networks, vet clinics, markets and manufacturing entities that will process and add value to meats and animal products. Beneficiaries will include all persons in animal husbandry, not only Fulani herders.”</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The justifications proposed for RUGA by the Buhari administration was a ruse because no other ethnic nationality in Nigeria engages in cattle rearing business other than the nomadic Fulani. Hence, the then Governor of Benue State, Mr. Samuel Ortom, asked to know when the programme was gazetted and demanded proof of the existence of the gazette but the Presidency did not respond.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The Southern and Middle Belt Leaders Forum (SMBLF) described the RUGA Scheme as “repugnant, repulsive and provocative”. The group said the initiative was to “colonize the rest of Nigeria under the guise of promoting cattle rearing”. It asked for outright cancellation of the project. (Abdullateef Aliyu, Southern, Middle Belt Leaders Reject Ruga Settlements, Daily Trust online, June 30, 2019)</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The Yoruba socio-political organization, Afenifere rejected Ruga Settlements. The then National Publicity Secretary of the Organisation, late Mr. Yinka Odumakin said that the Yoruba nation would not concede an inch of its land for the programme because it is an “expansionist policy in furtherance of Fulanisation project. It seeks to use the might of the FG to take over land across and hand it over to herdsmen. If the project is allowed, non-Fulani communities would experience what Hausa experienced when Fulani walked into their space and took over all the institutions.” (Dapo Akinrefon, Why S’West rejects RUGA scheme, Vanguard, July 5, 2019, p 28).</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>OnTiv Professionals in the FCT, Abuja, warned the Federal Government that it would take legal action to prevent the Federal Government from ceding any part of its territory to the herdsmen. On its part, the Christian Association of Nigeria (CAN) threatened to drag President Buhari to the United Nations (UN) over the issue. (Dapo Akinrefon, Johnbosco Agbakwuru, Victoria Ojeme, Joseph Erunke &amp; Chinonso Alozie, CAN to drag Buhari to UN over RUGA policy, Vanguard online, July 1, 2019)</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Speaking to Seun Okinbaloye of Channels TV on Friday, April 12, 2024, the Speaker of the Plateau State House of Assembly, Rt. Hon. Gabriel Dewan described continued killings in the Plateau as “ethnic cleansing” and “conspiracy against the people” of Plateau State. He revealed that 40 communities in the state had been systematically wiped out and replaced with cattle herders. As at June 2024, indigenous youth groups in Plateau State also revealed that 151 villages were occupied by armed foreign herdsmen. Many other parts of Plateau, Benue as well as several other states in Nigeria have their tales of woes inflicted on them by the same terrorist group.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>About two years into the Tinubu Presidency, the most productive crop farming communities in Nigeria, Benue and Plateau states, are still ravaged by armed herders. Majority of citizens in these states cannot go to their farms while two million persons displaced from their homes are in IDP camps in the affected zones.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>On July 9, 2024, President Tinubu announced that he had established a Ministry of Livestock Development which in effect, is a revival of the infamous RUGA Settlement Scheme of his predecessor in office. President Tinubu had earlier said that he would continue where his predecessor stopped but few expected this to include the most treacherous and destabilizing policy of the former President.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>On the occasion of his visit to Benue State on June 18, 2025, following intensified massacre of Benue citizens by armed herders, the Tor Tiv V, James Ayatse, told President Tinubu;<br>“We do have grave concerns about the misinformation and misrepresentation of the security crisis in Benue State. Your Excellency, it’s not herders-farmers clashes, it’s not communal clashes, it’s not reprisal attacks or skirmishes.” It is a “calculated, well-planned, full-scale genocidal invasion and land-grabbing campaign.” (Adetutu Sobowale, Benue killings: ‘This is genocide, not a dispute’, Tor Tiv tells Tinubu, PUNCH online, June 18, 2025)</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>President Tinubu did one good thing on that occasion. He ordered the army and Police to arrest and prosecute the perpetrators of the mass killings. He also directed the National Intelligence Agency (NIA) and the Department of State Services (DSS) to gather actionable intelligence to prevent further attacks. (Hope Abah, Benue Killings: Arrest Perpetrators, Tinubu Orders Security Chiefs, Daily Trust online, June 19, 2025)<br>Surprisingly however, the President also asked the Governor of Benue State, Hyacinth Alia, to provide land in the state for ranching. (Johnbosco Agbakwuru, Benue Bloodletting: Tinubu tells Gov Alia to allocate land for ranching, Vanguard online, June 18, 2025). The relevant question that arises from President Tinubu’s directive is: ranching by who and on whose land? By what authority was the President making the request?</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Any request to the Benue State Governor by President Tinubu to provide land for ranching in the circumstances, which may be construed as a directive, is ultra vires the powers of the President because it negates the letter, spirit and concept of federalism on which the Nigerian state is founded. It underscores the urgency of political restructuring to redefine Nigerian federalism.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Ranching is a private business and it is puzzling that the Tinubu administration desires to spend public funds on it. Benue State is predominantly Christian. The people do not rear cattle, do not engage in ranching and never asked for it. Could the request therefore be for the terrorist land grabbers as described by the Tor Tiv? This is a question that needs to be answered because it baffles the citizens.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Acquisition of modern skills and construction of infrastructure to improve livestock production is absolutely laudable. The million dollar question is why the project must involve taking over ancestral land from Southern and Middle Belt states and other indigenous peoples. There must be something sinister in such plan that goes beyond livestock development which Nigerians need to know.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>It is also particularly bothersome because the Federal Government has no land anywhere in the country on which to embark on livestock development, except the Federal Capital Territory (FCT). Agriculture should be a state affair and not a federal business. Any plan, by whatever name it may be called, that involves Federal Government annexation of ancestral land of indigenous peoples in any part of Middle-Belt and Southern Nigeria must be seen as a revival of the infamous Buhari RUGA Settlement scheme which was roundly rejected by Southern and Middle Belt peoples.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>More seriously, President Tinubu’s Livestock Development Plan seeks to sweep under the carpet, all the atrocities committed by armed herders during eight years of former President Buhari. According to Nigerian Security Tracker, about 4,068 persons were killed by armed herdsmen and bandits between June 2015 and January 2019. Armed marauders killed hundreds of people in Christian communities in Benue State alone between April and June 14, 2025.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Under this condition, the first rational action for President Tinubu to take in checking the heightened insecurity posed by armed herders is to declare them as terrorists as the Institute for Economics and Peace has already done. This will enable security agencies to invoke the Anti-Terrorism Law of the country to deal with the situation. Secondly, those whose land has been violently seized in Benue, Plateau, Taraba and other states must be restored to their ancestral land. Such restitution is the irreducible minimum.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>President Tinubu’s approach to the Benue massacres also raises serious questions about the status of his political party as a progressive political formation because progressivism is antithetic to authoritarianism. Progressivism does not recognise any special or private privileges, rights or power beyond that stipulated by the law of the land. It strongly abhors injustice, man’s inhumanity to man, religious bigotry and violent extremism. It aims to neutralize and re-educate the carriers of these debilitating social vices with a view to enhancing overall social harmony. Its raison de tre is protection of vulnerable and under-privileged members of the society.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>It is the height of injustice to give or propose giving Tivland or the land of any other indigenous people of Nigeria to terrorists. Any plan that leaves the innocent victims of the armed herders in Internally Displaced Persons’ camps while allowing the armed herdsmen to keep the land which they seized, is not only heartless and unconscionable, but also amounts to aiding and abetting terrorism.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>If President Tinubu succeeds in making Governor Alia to give Tivland to terrorists, this is likely to encourage the invaders to intensify their violent campaign in other parts of the country in order to be compensated with land. This will amount to official advancement of the Fulanisation of Nigeria, which will inevitably spell the doom of the country.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The way to assist underprivileged citizens is not to give what rightfully belongs to others to them. The first step in the liberation of nomadic cattle herders from their historic exploitation is to give them access to formal and non-formal education to enhance their well-being and participation in the nation-building process. The National Commission for Nomadic Education (NCNE), was established in 1989, but the Fulani leaders of the target population shunned the schools established by the Commission and insisted on roaming with their cows. Basic education which is necessary for appropriate training and impartation of technical skills in modern trends of cattle rearing was denied the herders.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>When they are empowered with basic education and requisite skills, it becomes absolute unnecessary and uncalled for to locate Fulani herders in all the states of the federation. There is more than sufficient land in the Sambisa Forest in North-East Nigeria, believed to be about 60,000 square kilometers, mainly undeveloped.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Since the Nigerian Army has reportedly cleared Sambisa Forest of Boko Haram insurgents, all genuine Fulani cattle herders could be taken to such place. The Tinubu administration could assist willing state governments concerned to use such land mass to effect any livestock development scheme he wishes and leave the Southern and Middle-Belt states alone. The area can provide beef and dairy products for the whole of the West African subregion and beyond if properly managed.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The herders do not need to come to Southern and Middle-Belt States that have much less land. The argument that Fulani lack grazing fields in Northern states is not valid. As Professor Patrick Muoboghare once explained following protracted attacks and killing of his kinsmen by Fulani militia in Uwheru Kingdom of Delta State in March 2020, “the rice that we consume in Nigeria comes from the north, meaning their soil can sustain rice farming. Anywhere rice can grow, grass can grow there. All the carrots, tomatoes and onions come from the north. The northern soil is not sterile but fertile. It is just that there is an attempt to claim our land and it is a kind of Jihad.” (Perez Brisibe, DELTA KILLINGS: But for protection herdsmen receive, we can deal with them – Prof Muoboghare, Vanguard online, March 1, 2020)</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Any herder who produces beef or cow milk from the Sambisa Forest ranches should transport these finished products to point of sale in other parts of the country. For example, most of the frozen fish and other sea foods sold in Nigeria are imported from Asian countries. The Asians do not need to come to occupy rivers in the Niger Delta to make their products available in Nigeria.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>It is therefore undesirable for the Presidency to ask Benue State or any other state to surrender communal land of indigenous peoples to terrorists, who have no allegiance to the Nigerian state or its constitution and who do not share the same cultural values with the indigenes.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The APC administration has not addressed the fundamental issue of the acculturation process that produces the terrorist mind-set. It is dangerous in the extreme to distribute that destructive cultural virus in all states of the country.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The survival needs and peaceful development of indigenous peoples of Nigeria demand that they must resolutely reject the latest plan to sow seeds of discord in their territories. If the Presidency is able to lure some greedy and unpatriotic Southern State Governors with financial packages to concede implantation of permanent Fulani enclaves in their states, such a dangerous design will surely be the foundation of the biggest and most intractable armed conflict in Nigeria in the foreseeable future. A stitch in time saves nine.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Chris O. O. Biose (Human Rights Activist).</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->', 'STOP PRESIDENT TINUBU’S CONTROVERSIAL RUGA REVIVAL SCHEME', '', 'publish', 'open', 'closed', '', 'stop-president-tinubus-controversial-ruga-revival-scheme', '', '', '2025-06-23 20:00:25', '2025-06-23 20:00:25', '', 0, 'https://boldtimes.net.ng/?p=1182', 0, 'post', '', 0),
(1187, 1, '2026-02-26 10:06:58', '2026-02-26 10:06:58', '<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>THE ASSASSINATION ATTEMPT ON ADC LEADERS IN BENIN CITY<br>By Chris O. O. Biose, Chrisoobiose@gmail.com</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>It is widely reported in the print and electronic media that armed political thugs attacked some leaders of the African Democratic Congress (ADC) in Benin City on Tuesday, February 24, 2026 during a political meeting. The leaders, included former Governor of Edo State, Chief John Odigie-Oyegun, who is also a former National Chairman of the All Progressives Congress (APC) and former Governor of Anambra State, Mr. Peter Obi, who was also Presidential candidate of the Labour Party (LP) in the February 2023 presidential election.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The incident occurred during the formal reception of Mr. Olumide Akpata, former National Chairman of the Nigerian Bar Association (NBA) and gubernatorial candidate of the LP in the September 2024 governorship election in Edo State that produced Monday Okpebolo as Governor of the State. The event followed Mr. Olumide’s Defection to the African Democratic Congress (ADC) from the Labour Party.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>It was reported that the attackers arrived at the Secretariat of the party in about 10 vehicles without plate numbers and immediately opened fire indiscrimately and vandalized party property. Some members of the party reportedly sustained injuries.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The assailants finally drove off with confidence, to the residence of Chief Odigie-Oyegun along Reservation Road, Benin City, where they destroyed the gate of the well-fortified residence to gain entry, shot sporadically in several directions, and destroying several vehicles in the compound of the former governor.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The question arises as to how such brazen violence could take place in broad day light in the centre of Benin City where there is a government and police. The answer lies with the type of governments in place in Nigeria and the close relationship of government with the criminal underworld in the country.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>It is generally recognized that in Nigeria, deadly political violence is the stock in trade of powerful politicians. During the first decade of return to civil rule since 1999, there were over 100 unresolved assassinations of politically exposed persons. Prominent among these were the famous Lagos politician Engr Funsho Williams, the then Minister of Justice and attorney-General of the Federation, Chief Bola Ige, Harry Marshall a top notcher of the ANPP and A.K. Dikibo, PDP National vice-Chairman, South-South.<br>During the 2003 gubernatorial election in Anambra State, the existence of a para-military organisation of storm troopers came to light. According to Dr. Edwin Madunagu, the operational tactics of storm troopers in Anambra State were comparable to that formed by the late German Dictator, Adolf Hitler in 1923. As Dr. Madunagu made clear, during the officially sanctioned rampage that took place in Anambra State between 2003 and 2004, the relationship between these private armies and regular security forces of the state may not be cordial but \"they all served the same strategic objectives - the maintenance of the fascist state by the crushing of all enemies, actual and potential.\"<br>“Although not listed in the Constitution, the fascist storm troopers were \"protected and assisted by strategic state institutions, including the police, the army and the judiciary.\"</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Madunagu concluded that Nigeria was witnessing the evolution of \"a new form of state\" characterized by \"de facto one party system, a merger of the ruling party and the state, the appearance of state sponsored armed institutions that are not in the constitution or in any statute.\" They operate \"above the law\" which he called \"fascist regimentation.\" (The Guardian, Monday, December 13, 2004).</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The massive deployment of lethal weapons to destroy political opponents and other sources of resistance to election marauders could not, by any stretch of the imagination, be procured by much maligned cultist indigent students from higher institutions of learning or village folk deployed for these robberies. Peddling deadly weapons is the exclusive preserve of the high and mighty in Nigerian society, particularly the political, business and military elite. Indeed the cost of one AK 47 riffle is far beyond the economic reach of the average citizen. These evil deeds are planned and funded by those same people that lead the country.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>State governors have played key roles in political violence in Nigeria since return to civil rule in May 1999. In August 2006, the Upper Chamber of the National Assembly invited the then Director General of the State Security Service (SSS), Col. Kayode Are (Rtd.), and the then Inspector General of Police, Mr. Sunday Ehindero, to appear before the Senate to shed light on the worrisome spate of political violence in the country. Col. Are (Rtd.) told senate on August 24, 2006 that they were “killer squads” in nearly every state of the country. (Emmanuel Aziken &amp; Ayodele Adegbuyi “Nigeria: SSS DG Fingers Govs in Political Killings”. Vanguard online, 25 August 2006.)</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>When he appeared before the senate on September 6, 2006, the then IGP, Mr. Sunday Ehindero, corroborated the submission of the then SSS Director. He added that state governments often frustrated the prosecution of arrested perpetrators of political violence. Mr. Ehindero said that many politicians funded thugs and illegal gangs, leading to proliferation of arms. He however submitted that the SSS had failed to share intelligence information on “killer squads” with the Police.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>To take another specific case, His Excellency, Seriake Dickson, who was then the Governor of Bayelsa State gave a factual and detailed account of the situation in his state which confirmed electoral wars in some Niger Delta states. In a candid interview which he granted Emmanuel Aziken, Political Editor, Vanguard newspaper, In May 2012, the Governor declared:<br>“We had a system where a terror squad was put together and funded by government over the years. You may recall that the day preceding my declaration, five persons were gunned down just to scare me and my supporters from coming. “But we dared them. In fact, what we did was a suicide mission.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>“Instead of giving the boys and girls education, instead of building infrastructure and investment, they were giving young people drugs, AK-47, they were giving them dynamite to explode and kill people so that there will be an impression; any attempt to take anybody out will be resisted.”</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The security challenges arising from desire of Governors to protect ill-gotten positions, as described by Governor Dickson applied mutatis mutandis to states in South-East, South-South and South-West. (Emmanuel Aziken, Challenging Sylva was a suicide mission – Dickson, Vanguard, May 23, 2012, p 44).</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>This pattern of violence has continued under the Tinubu Presidency. In Kaduna State, sponsored political thugs constantly attacked and disrupted meetings of Opposition SDP and ADC. The Nigeria Police joined the ruling party in suppressing opposition by placing a ban on political meeting in the state. Opposition parties successfully challenged the unlawful action of the Nigeria Police court and a Federal High Court sitting in Kaduna. On October 15, 2025, the court awarded N15 million in total damages against the police, covering the “arbitrary suspension” of the ADC meeting, the “unlawful ex-parte injunction” obtained without due procedure, and the “failure to investigate the August 30 violence.” (Ibrahim Hassan-Wuyo: “Court slams Kaduna Police over ban on political meetings, orders N15m damages to ADC, SDP”. Vanguard Online, October 15, 2025)</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>What happened in Benin City on February 24 was a continuation of what took place in Lagos on September 7, 2025 during the planned defection of Gbadebo Rhodes-Vivour, gubernatorial candidate of LP in the 2023 governorship election in Lagos State to ADC. Sponsored political thugs disrupted the event, injuring party members and destroying properties while Police looked on. Indeed, political thugs that constitute a special wing of the ruling class in Lagos State take it for granted that they could attack opponents of the APC and get police protection. This was exactly what happened during the 2023 presidential and gubernatorial elections in Lagos State.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The attack on ADC members in Benin City took place on a day that the IGP Kayode Egbetokun, who had a dismal human rights record, was handing over to newly appointed IGP, Mr. Tunji Disu. An effective Police Force is the first line of defence on security in a democratic state<br>Section 14 (2) (b) OF THE Constitution of Nigeria states that “the security and welfare of the people shall be the primary purpose of government.”</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Nigerians would wish to see a definite directive from the new IGP to State Police Commands, particularly, Lagos, Kaduna and Edo States, to protect political opponents as well as all citizens in compliance with Section 4 of the Police Act Cap P19 Laws of the Federation, 2004. The perceived collaboration of Police with high profile criminals is one of the weakest points in the security apparatus of the country.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->', 'THE ASSASSINATION ATTEMPT ON ADC LEADERS IN BENIN CITY', '', 'publish', 'open', 'closed', '', 'the-assassination-attempt-on-adc-leaders-in-benin-city', '', '', '2026-02-26 10:06:58', '2026-02-26 10:06:58', '', 0, 'https://boldtimes.net.ng/?p=1187', 0, 'post', '', 0);
